window.LITUK_TESTS = [{"id":"test-1","name":"Test 1","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"Who were the first people to arrive in Britain in what we call the Stone Age?","o":["Farmers","Hunter-gatherers","Warriors","Pirates"],"a":[1],"e":"The first people to live in Britain were hunter-gatherers, in what we call the Stone Age."},{"q":"When did Britain become permanently separated from the continent by the Channel?","o":["10,000 years ago","50,000 years ago","15,000 years ago","18,000 years ago"],"a":[0],"e":"Britain only became permanently separated from the continent by the Channel about 10,000 years ago."},{"q":"The Commonwealth has no power over its members and it cannot suspend their membership:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"The Commonwealth has no power over its members, although it can suspend membership."},{"q":"Which of the following statements regarding the Black Death is NOT true?","o":["One third of the population of England died and a similar proportion in Scotland and Wales","It was one of the worst disasters ever to strike Britain","It affected children and old people only","Following the Black Death, there were labour shortages"],"a":[2],"e":"In 1348, a disease, probably a form of plague, came to Britain. This was known as the Black Death. One third of the population of England died and a similar proportion in Scotland and Wales. This was one of the worst disasters ever to strike Britain. Following the Black Death, the smaller population meant there was less need to grow cereal crops. There were labour shortages and peasants began to demand higher wages."},{"q":"Who was reigning in England when Wales became formally united with England by the Act for the Government of Wales?","o":["Henry VIII","Henry VII","Elizabeth I","James I"],"a":[0],"e":"During the reign of Henry VIII, Wales became formally united with England by the Act for the Government of Wales."},{"q":"The Elizabethan period is known for the richness of its poetry and drama, especially for the plays and poems of which playwright?","o":["Alexander Dumas","William Shakespeare","Charles Dickens","Thomas Hardy"],"a":[1],"e":"The Elizabethan period is remembered for the richness of its poetry and drama, especially the plays and poems of William Shakespeare."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a fundamental principle of British life?","o":["Tolerance of those with different faiths and beliefs","The rule of law","Autocracy","Democracy"],"a":[2],"e":"The fundamental principles of British life include: tolerance of those with different faiths and beliefs, the rule of law, democracy, individual liberty and participation in community life."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following are environmental charities?","o":["Crisis","The National Trust","Friends of the Earth","PDSA"],"a":[1,2],"e":"The National Trust and Friends of the Earth are environmental charities."},{"q":"Which flag has a diagonal red cross on a white ground?","o":["The cross of St George, patron saint of England","The cross of St Patrick, patron saint of Ireland","The cross of St David, patron saint of Wales","The cross of St Andrew, patron saint of Scotland"],"a":[1],"e":"The cross of St Patrick, patron saint of Ireland, is a diagonal red cross on a white ground."},{"q":"Where is the city of Swansea located?","o":["In England","In Scotland","In Wales","In the Isle of Man"],"a":[2],"e":"Swansea is located in Wales."},{"q":"When is Christmas Eve celebrated?","o":["24th of December","26th of December","25th of December","27th of December"],"a":[0],"e":"Christmas Eve is celebrated on the 24th of December."},{"q":"Which British sportsman won five consecutive gold medals at the Olympic Games in the rowing category?","o":["Sir Chris Hoy","Christopher Dean","Bradley Wiggins","Sir Steve Redgrave"],"a":[3],"e":"Sir Steve Redgrave won gold medals in rowing in five consecutive Olympic Games and is one of Britain’s greatest Olympians."},{"q":"What is the name of the centrepiece to the Remembrance Day service located in Whitehall, London?","o":["Cenotaph","Stonehenge","Grimsby","Scunthorpe"],"a":[0],"e":"The Cenotaph in Whitehall is the site of the annual Remembrance Day service attended by the King, politicians and foreign ambassadors."},{"q":"Who was the inventor of the World Wide Web?","o":["Sir Tim Berners-Lee","Sir Peter Mansfield","Sir Ian Wilmut","Sir Bernard Lovell"],"a":[0],"e":"The inventor of the World Wide Web, Sir Tim Berners-Lee (1955-), is British. Information was successfully transferred via the web for the first time on 25 December 1990."},{"q":"What does the UK offer to its residents or citizens (choose FIVE answers)?","o":["Freedom of belief and religion","Freedom of speech","Free university tuition fees","Freedom from unfair discrimination","A right to a fair trial","A right to join in the election of a government"],"a":[0,1,3,4,5],"e":"The UK offers to its residents or citizens: freedom of belief and religion, freedom of speech, freedom from unfair discrimination, a right to a fair trial and a right to join in the election of a government."},{"q":"When did the Wars of the Roses start?","o":["1388","1455","1462","1478"],"a":[1],"e":"In 1455, a civil war was begun to decide who should be king of England. It was fought between the supporters of two families: the House of Lancaster and the House of York. This war was called the Wars of the Roses, because the symbol of Lancaster was a red rose and the symbol of York was a white rose."},{"q":"How many members does the Scottish Parliament have?","o":["60","90","120","129"],"a":[3],"e":"There are 129 members of the Scottish Parliament (MSPs), elected by a form of proportional representation."},{"q":"What does the term ‘coalition’ refer to?","o":["If no political party wins a majority, a new election is held to vote for one of the top two parties only","The candidate who gets the most votes is elected","If no political party wins a majority, two parties may join and govern together","If an MP dies or resigns, there will be a fresh election"],"a":[2],"e":"The government is usually formed by the party that wins the majority of constituencies. If no party wins a majority, two parties may join together to form a coalition."},{"q":"The public can listen to debates in the Palace of Westminster from public galleries in both the House of Commons and the House of Lords.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"Pool and darts are traditional pub games.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"True."},{"q":"When did the UK formally leave the European Union?","o":["31 January 2019","30 January 2020","31 January 2020","30 January 2019"],"a":[2],"e":"The UK formally left the European Union on 31 January 2020."},{"q":"What is the minimum age requirement in the UK to drink wine or beer with a meal provided you are with someone over 18?","o":["14 years old","15 years old","16 years old","17 years old"],"a":[2],"e":"When they are 16, people can drink wine or beer with a meal in a hotel or restaurant (including eating areas in pubs) as long as they are with someone over 18."},{"q":"Who is the spiritual leader of the Church of England?","o":["The Pope","The Archbishop of Canterbury","St Augustine","St Columba"],"a":[1],"e":"The spiritual leader of the Church of England is the Archbishop of Canterbury."},{"q":"Civil servants cannot stand for public office:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"Most citizens of the UK, Ireland or the Commonwealth aged 18 or over can stand for public office. There are some exceptions, including: members of the armed forces, civil servants, people found guilty of certain criminal offences."}]},{"id":"test-2","name":"Test 2","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"Which Scottish city is known as the home of golf?","o":["Dundee","Edinburgh","Aberdeen","St Andrews"],"a":[3],"e":"St. Andrews in Scotland is known as the home of golf."},{"q":"Which court deals with the most serious cases of children aged 10 to 17 in England, Wales and Northern Ireland?","o":["The High Court","The Youth Court","The Magistrates’ Court","The Crown Court"],"a":[3],"e":"In England, Wales and Northern Ireland, if an accused person is aged 10 to 17, the case is normally heard in a Youth Court in front of up to three specially trained magistrates or a District Judge. The most serious cases will go the Crown Court."},{"q":"What is the name of the best preserved prehistoric village in northern Europe and which origin traces back to the Stone Age?","o":["Maiden","Skara Brae","Stonehenge","Bayeux"],"a":[1],"e":"Skara Brae on Orkney, off the north coast of Scotland, is the best preserved prehistoric village in northern Europe, and has helped archaeologists to understand more about how people lived near the end of the Stone Age."},{"q":"When was the National Trust founded?","o":["1890","1895","1980","1910"],"a":[1],"e":"The National Trust was founded in 1895 by three volunteers."},{"q":"After the Black Death, new social classes appeared in England, including owners of large areas of land known as:","o":["Judiciary","Gentry","Clans","Nobility"],"a":[1],"e":"Following the Black Death, the smaller population meant there was less need to grow cereal crops. There were labour shortages and peasants began to demand higher wages. New social classes appeared, including owners of large areas of land (later called the gentry ), and people left the countryside to live in the towns. In the towns, growing wealth led to the development of a strong middle class."},{"q":"Elizabeth I was the younger daughter of which King?","o":["James I","William the Great","Henry VII","Henry VIII"],"a":[3],"e":"Elizabeth I was the younger daughter of Henry VIII."},{"q":"Which of the following operas was written by Gilbert and Sullivan?","o":["The Phantom of the Opera","The Mikado","The Mousetrap","Cats"],"a":[1],"e":"In the 19th century, Gilbert and Sullivan wrote comic operas, often making fun of popular culture and politics. These operas include HMS Pinafore, The Pirates of Penzance and The Mikado."},{"q":"Where is Loch Lomond and the Trossachs National Park located?","o":["East of Wales","West of Wales","West of Scotland","East of Scotland"],"a":[2],"e":"Loch Lomond and the Trossachs National Park is located in the west of Scotland."},{"q":"Who is married to the King of the United Kingdom?","o":["Kate, the Princess of Wales","Camila, the Queen Consort","Camila, the Princess of Wales","Kate, the Queen Consort"],"a":[1],"e":"The King of the United Kingdom is married to Camila, the Queen Consort."},{"q":"By what TWO other names is the Church of England known?","o":["The Anglican Church","The Union Church","The Greatest Church","The Episcopal Church"],"a":[0,3],"e":"The official Church of the state is the Church of England (called the Anglican Church in other countries and the Episcopal Church in Scotland and the United States)."},{"q":"Who was the first British Prime Minister?","o":["Henry Pelham","Sir Robert Walpole","Admiral Nelson","Oliver Cromwell"],"a":[1],"e":"The first man to British Prime Minister was Sir Robert Walpole, who was Prime Minister from 1721 to 1742."},{"q":"What day does Lent start?","o":["On Shrove Tuesday","On Ash Wednesday","On Easter Monday","On Easter Sunday"],"a":[1],"e":"Lent begins on Ash Wednesday."},{"q":"How old do you need to be to apply for a free TV licence?","o":["Over 65","Over 60","Over 70","Over 75"],"a":[3],"e":"People over 75 can apply for a free TV licence and blind people can get a 50% discount."},{"q":"What time do pubs usually open on Sundays in the UK?","o":["10 am","11 am","12 pm","1 pm"],"a":[2],"e":"Pubs are usually open during the day from 11.00 am (12 noon on Sundays)."},{"q":"What is the official home of the Prime Minister?","o":["Buckingham Palace","Windsor Castle","10 Downing Street","21 Oxford Street"],"a":[2],"e":"The official home of the Prime Minister is 10 Downing Street, in central London, near the Houses of Parliament. He or she also has a country house outside London called Chequers."},{"q":"When did the Conservative government called a referendum on the UK’s membership of the European Union?","o":["23rd July 2016","23rd June 2016","23rd June 2015","23rd July 2015"],"a":[1],"e":"The Conservative government called a referendum on the UK’s membership of the European Union on the 23rd June 2016."},{"q":"Which British sportswoman won two gold medals for running in the 2004 Olympic Games?","o":["Dame Ellen MacArthur","Jayne Torvill","Dame Kelly Holmes","Jessica Ennis-Hill"],"a":[2],"e":"Dame Kelly Holmes won two gold medals for running in the 2004 Olympic Games. She has held a number of British and European records."},{"q":"What percentage of the total British population is located in England alone?","o":["84%","60%","58%","79%"],"a":[0],"e":"The population is very unequally distributed over the four parts of the UK. England more or less consistently makes up 84% of the total population, Wales around 5%, Scotland just over 8%, and Northern Ireland less than 3%."},{"q":"What was the population of the UK in 2010?","o":["Just over 50 million","Just over 62 million","Just under 50 million","Just under 60 million"],"a":[1],"e":"The population of the UK in 2010 was just over 62 million people."},{"q":"Which court deals with cases involving personal injury, family matters, breaches of contract and divorce in England and Wales?","o":["The Crown Court","The High Court","County Courts","The Sheriff Court"],"a":[2],"e":"County Courts deal with a wide range of civil disputes. These include people trying to get back money that is owed to them, cases involving personal injury, family matters, breaches of contract, and divorce. In Scotland, most of these matters are dealt with in the Sheriff Court."},{"q":"What is the name of the building where the Scottish Parliament meet?","o":["Holyrood","Senedd","Westminster","Stormont"],"a":[0],"e":"In Scotland the elected members, called MSPs, meet in the Scottish Parliament building at Holyrood in Edinburgh."},{"q":"Which flower is associated with England?","o":["Daffodil","Shamrock","Rose","Thistle"],"a":[2],"e":"The rose is the flower associated with England."},{"q":"Who was Robert Burns?","o":["A British musician","A Scottish poet","A Welsh scientist","An Irish engineer"],"a":[1],"e":"Known in Scotland as ‘The Bard’, Robert Burns was a Scottish poet."},{"q":"In which period did British Film studios flourish?","o":["1920s","1930s","1940s","1950s"],"a":[1],"e":"British film studios flourished in the 1930s."}]},{"id":"test-3","name":"Test 3","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"What is the capital city of Northern Ireland?","o":["Belfast","Dublin","Cork","Edinburgh"],"a":[0],"e":"The capital city of Northern Ireland is Belfast."},{"q":"What name is given to the day when people play jokes on each other until midday?","o":["Remembrance Day","April’s Fool Day","Halloween","Valentine’s Day"],"a":[1],"e":"April Fool’s Day, 1 April, is a day when people play jokes on each other until midday. The television and newspapers often have stories that are April Fool jokes."},{"q":"Who is the fastest person to have sailed around the world single-handed?","o":["Dame Kelly Holmes","Dame Ellen MacArthur","Jayne Torvill","Bradley Wiggins"],"a":[1],"e":"Dame Ellen MacArthur (1976-) is a yachtswoman and in 2004 became the fastest person to sail around the world singlehanded."},{"q":"When did the First World War end?","o":["9th of November 1918","11th of November 1918","9th of December 1918","11th of December 1918"],"a":[1],"e":"The First World War ended at 11.00 am on the 11th November 1918 with victory for Britain and its allies."},{"q":"What are Welsh cakes made of?","o":["Suet, onions and oatmeal","Flour, dried fruits and spices","Potatoes, vegetables, Yorkshire puddings","Bacon, eggs, sausage, black pudding and potato bread"],"a":[1],"e":"Welsh cakes are a traditional Welsh snack made from flour, dried fruits and spices, and served either hot or cold."},{"q":"Where is the London Eye situated?","o":["On the northern bank of the River Thames","On the southern bank of the River Thames","On the northern bank of the River Lea","On the southern bank of the River Lea"],"a":[1],"e":"The London Eye is situated on the southern bank of the River Thames and is a Ferris wheel that is 443 feet (135 metres) tall."},{"q":"What is the relationship between the monarch and the government?","o":["Approve the government’s decisions on government policies","Advise, warn and encourage the government’s decisions","Advise, encourage and approve the government’s decisions","Advise and approve the government’s decisions"],"a":[1],"e":"The monarch has regular meetings with the Prime Minister and can advise, warn and encourage, but the decisions on government policies are made by the Prime Minister and cabinet."},{"q":"The UK is a ‘constitutional monarchy’:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"The UK has a constitutional monarchy."},{"q":"What do you need to apply to become a permanent resident or citizen of the UK (Choose TWO options)?","o":["A British driving licence","To be able to speak and read English","A British university degree","A good understanding of life in the UK"],"a":[1,3],"e":"To apply to become a permanent resident or citizen of the UK, you will need to speak and read English and have a good understanding of life in the UK."},{"q":"Which British scientist was awarded a Nobel Prize for discovering the structure of the DNA molecule?","o":["Alan Turing","Sir Frank Whittle","Sir Christopher Cockrell","Francis Crick"],"a":[3],"e":"The structure of the DNA molecule was discovered in 1953 through work at British universities in London and Cambridge. This discovery contributed to many scientific advances, particularly in medicine and fighting crime. Francis Crick (1916-2004), one of those awarded the Nobel Prize for this discovery, was British."},{"q":"Who established the Church of England?","o":["King Charles III","The Archbishop of Canterbury","Henry VIII","Elizabeth I"],"a":[2],"e":"To divorce his first wife, Henry VIII needed the approval of the Pope. When the Pope refused, Henry established the Church of England. In this new church, the king, not the Pope, would have the power to appoint bishops and order how people should worship."},{"q":"When does Lent take place?","o":["40 days before Christmas","40 days after Christmas","40 days before Easter","40 days after Easter"],"a":[2],"e":"The 40 days before Easter are known as Lent. It is a time when Christians take time to reflect and prepare for Easter."},{"q":"Which of the following national galleries is NOT located in London?","o":["Tate Britain","The National Gallery","Tate Modern","The National Museum"],"a":[3],"e":"The National Museum is located in Cardiff, Wales."},{"q":"How much can you be fined if you watch TV but do not have a TV licence?","o":["Up to £500","Up to £1000","Up to £2000","Up to £3000"],"a":[1],"e":"You will receive a fine up to £1,000 if you watch TV but do not have a TV licence."},{"q":"How old is the clock at the Houses of Parliament in London, also known as ‘Big Ben’?","o":["100 years old","150 years old","200 years old","250 years old"],"a":[1],"e":"Big Ben is the nickname for the great bell of the clock at the House of Parliament in London. The clock is over 150 years old and is a popular tourist attraction."},{"q":"The UK was a full member of the European Union but did not use the Euro currency.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"The North Atlantic Treaty Organisation is a group of European and North American countries that was created with which TWO purposes:","o":["Protect and promote human rights in those countries","Help each other if they come under attack","Maintain peace between all its members","Promote free trade amongst those countries"],"a":[1,2],"e":"NATO is a group of European and North American countries that have agreed to help each other if they come under attack. It also aims to maintain peace between all of its members."},{"q":"The small claims procedure is used in England and Wales for minor disputes for:","o":["Claims of less than £15,000","Claims of less than £5,000","Claims of less than £3,500","Claims of less than £10,000"],"a":[3],"e":"This procedure is used for claims of less than £10,000 in England and Wales, £5,000 using the simple claims procedure in Scotland, and £5,000 through the small claims process in Northern Ireland."},{"q":"What name is given to the period in the 18th century when new ideas about politics, philosophy and science were developed?","o":["The Enlightenment","The Glorious Revolution","The Industrial Revolution","Elizabethan"],"a":[0],"e":"During the 18th century, new ideas about politics, philosophy and science were developed. This is often called ‘ the Enlightenment ’."},{"q":"What is the meaning of ‘Magna Carta’?","o":["The Big Charter","The Great Charter","The King’s Charter","The Small Charter"],"a":[1],"e":"The Magna Carta (which means the Great Charter ). The Magna Carta established the idea that even the king was subject to the law. It protected the rights of the nobility and restricted the king’s power to collect taxes or to make and change laws."},{"q":"When did the Wars of the Roses end?","o":["1455","1462","1478","1485"],"a":[3],"e":"The Wars of the Roses ended with the Battle of Bosworth Field in 1485."},{"q":"Which of the following is the responsibility of the Chancellor of the Exchequer?","o":["Education","Agriculture","Economy","Defence"],"a":[2],"e":"The Chancellor of the Exchequer is responsible for the economy."},{"q":"In which British university did Isaac Newton study?","o":["University of Bolton","University of Cambridge","Cranfield University","University of Dundee"],"a":[1],"e":"Isaac Newton studied at Cambridge University."},{"q":"Who invaded England after the Romans?","o":["The Vikings","The Anglo-Saxons","The Normans","The Scots"],"a":[1],"e":"The Roman army left Britain in AD 410 to defend other parts of the Roman Empire and never returned. Britain was again invaded by tribes from northern Europe: the Jutes, the Angles and the Saxons."}]},{"id":"test-4","name":"Test 4","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"When did people learn to make bronze?","o":["Around 3,000 years ago","Around 4,000 years ago","Around 5,000 years ago","Around 2,000 years ago"],"a":[1],"e":"Around 4,000 years ago, people learned to make bronze. We call this period the Bronze Age."},{"q":"When is the Jewish celebration known as Hanukkah normally celebrated?","o":["June or July","October or November","November or December","March or April"],"a":[2],"e":"Hanukkah is in November or December and is celebrated for eight days."},{"q":"What musical award is given during the Mercury Music Prize?","o":["Best album from the UK","Best album from the UK and Ireland","Best solo artist from the UK","Best solo artist the UK and Ireland"],"a":[1],"e":"The Mercury Music Prize is awarded each September for the best album from the UK and Ireland."},{"q":"Who was Boudicca?","o":["One of the tribal leaders who fought against the Romans","One of the architects who built Hadrian’s Wall","A Viking king","One of the tribal leaders who fought against the Vikings"],"a":[0],"e":"One of the tribal leaders who fought against the Romans was Boudicca, the queen of the Iceni in what is now eastern England."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following water sports are popular in the UK?","o":["Waterpolo","Rowing","Sailing","Surfing"],"a":[1,2],"e":"Sailing continues to be popular in the UK, reflecting its maritime heritage. Rowing is also popular, both as a leisure activity and as a competitive sport."},{"q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Forcing another person to marry is a civil offence","Forcing another person to marry is a criminal offence","Forcing another person to marry is not punished in the UK","Forcing another person to marry is common in the UK"],"a":[1],"e":"Forcing another person to marry is a criminal offence."},{"q":"In the UK, what should dogs wear in public places?","o":["A collar with the name and address of the owner","A collar with the name of the dog","A tracking device in case they get lost","Clothes to avoid the cold in the winter"],"a":[0],"e":"All dogs in public places must wear a collar showing the name and address of the owner."},{"q":"Where do UK residents need to register their car or motorcycle?","o":["At the Home Office","At the Ministry of Transport (MOT)","At the Driver and Vehicle Licensing Agency (DVLA)","At their Local Council"],"a":[2],"e":"If you are resident in the UK, your car or motor cycle must be registered at the Driver and Vehicle Licensing Agency (DVLA)."},{"q":"Popular social networking websites in the UK include:","o":["Drupal and Amazon","Facebook and Twitter","Dropbox and itunes","iCloud and itunes"],"a":[1],"e":"Social networking websites such as Facebook and Twitter are a popular in the UK."},{"q":"According to the 2011 Census, what percentage of the population identified themselves as Muslim?","o":["1%","2%","3%","4.8%"],"a":[3],"e":"In the 2011 Census, 59% of people identified themselves as Christian. Much smaller proportions identified themselves as Muslim (4.8%), Hindu (1.5%), Sikh (0.8%), Jewish or Buddhist (both less than 0.5%)."},{"q":"How often are elections held for the Welsh government?","o":["Every four years","Every three years","Every fix years","Every six years"],"a":[0],"e":"The Welsh government has 60 Senedd members (SMs) and elections are held every four years using a form of proportional representation."},{"q":"Who was the British Minister after Margaret Thatcher?","o":["Tony Blair","Harold Macmillan","Gordon Brown","John Major"],"a":[3],"e":"John Major was Prime Minister after Mrs Thatcher, and helped establish the Northern Ireland peace process."},{"q":"What name is given to the person appointed by local authorities to be the ceremonial leader of a particular council?","o":["Council candidate","Mayor","Peer","Magistrate"],"a":[1],"e":"Many local authorities appoint a mayor, who is the ceremonial leader of the council. In some towns, a mayor is elected to be the effective leader of the administration."},{"q":"What is the meaning of the term ‘first past the post’?","o":["In each constituency, the candidate who gets the most votes is elected","If no political party wins a majority, two parties may join and govern together","If an MP dies or resigns, there will be a fresh election","The Speaker will be chosen by other MPs in a secret ballot"],"a":[0],"e":"MPs are elected through a system called ‘first past the post’. In each constituency, the candidate who gets the most votes is elected."},{"q":"Who defeated Emperor Napoleon at the Battle of Waterloo in 1815?","o":["The Duke of Wellington","Admiral Nelson","Richard Arkwright","The Duke of Edinburgh"],"a":[0],"e":"In 1815, the French Wars ended with the defeat of the Emperor Napoleon by the Duke of Wellington at the Battle of Waterloo."},{"q":"Which court deals with the MOST serious cases in Scotland, such as murder?","o":["The Magistrates’ Court","The Sheriff Court","The Crown Court","The High Court"],"a":[3],"e":"In Scotland, serious cases are heard in a Sheriff Court with either a sheriff or a sheriff with a jury. The most serious cases in Scotland, such as murder, are heard at a High Court with a judge and jury."},{"q":"What was the symbol of the House of Tudor?","o":["A red rose with a white rose inside it","A red rose","A white rose","A white rose with a red rose inside it"],"a":[0],"e":"The symbol of the House of Tudor was a red rose with a white rose inside it as a sign that the Houses of York and Lancaster were now allies."},{"q":"What animal is represented in the Welsh flag?","o":["A lion","A dragon","A tiger","A cat"],"a":[1],"e":"There is also an official Welsh flag, which shows a Welsh dragon. The Welsh dragon does not appear on the Union Flag because, when the first Union Flag was created in 1606 from the flags of Scotland and England, the Principality of Wales was already untied with England."},{"q":"Which famous economist developed ideas about economics during the 18th century and which are still referred to today?","o":["Robert Dudley Baxter","David Hume","Adam Smith","Henry George"],"a":[2],"e":"During the 18th century, new ideas about politics, philosophy and science were developed. This is often called ‘the Enlightenment’. Many of the great thinkers of the Enlightenment were Scottish. Adam Smith developed ideas about economics which are still referred to today."},{"q":"Who is the ceremonial head of the Commonwealth?","o":["The British King","The Pope","The President of the United States","The Prime Minister of the UK"],"a":[0],"e":"The King is the ceremonial head of the Commonwealth."},{"q":"What time do pubs usually open during the week?","o":["9 am","10 am","11 am","12 pm"],"a":[2],"e":"Pubs are usually open during the day from 11.00 am (12 noon on Sundays)."},{"q":"In which TWO movie categories does Britain continue to be particularly strong at?","o":["Special effects movies","Action movies","Thrillers","Animation movies"],"a":[0,3],"e":"Britain continues to be particularly strong in special effects and animation movies."},{"q":"Which British artist was a portrait painter who often painted people in country or garden scenarios?","o":["Thomas Gainsborough","David Allan","Joseph Turner","John Constable"],"a":[0],"e":"Thomas Gainsborough (1727-88) was a portrait painter who often painted people in country or garden scenery."},{"q":"During which religious celebration do Muslims sacrifice an animal to eat?","o":["Eid ul Adha","Eid a-Fitr","Ramadan","Vaisakhi"],"a":[0],"e":"Many Muslims sacrifice an animal to eat during Eid ul Adha."}]},{"id":"test-5","name":"Test 5","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"How old do you need to be to be able to play the National Lottery?","o":["16 years old","18 years old","20 years old","21 years old"],"a":[1],"e":"People under 18 are not allowed to participate in the National Lottery."},{"q":"Where is the five-day race meeting attended by members of the Royal Family and known as Royal Ascot celebrated?","o":["In Luton","In Berkshire","In Salisbury","In Kent"],"a":[1],"e":"Royal Ascot, the five-day race meeting attended by members of the Royal Family, takes place in Berkshire."},{"q":"Who was the first person to lead a Roman invasion in Britain in 55 BC?","o":["Emperor Claudius","Julius Caesar","Napoleon","William Caxton"],"a":[1],"e":"Julius Caesar led a Roman invasion of Britain in 55 BC."},{"q":"Who wrote ‘The Lord of the Rings’?","o":["JK Rowling","JRR Tolkien","Sir William Golding","Jane Austen"],"a":[1],"e":"The Lord of the Rings was written by JRR Tolkien."},{"q":"Which court deals with minor criminal cases in England, Wales and Northern Ireland?","o":["The Justice of the Peace Court","The Magistrates’ Court","The High Court","The Crown Court"],"a":[1],"e":"In England, Wales and Northern Ireland, most minor criminal cases are dealt with in a Magistrates’ Court. In Scotland, minor criminal offences go to a Justice of the Peace Court."},{"q":"What name is given to the tombs where people buried their dead during the Bronze Age?","o":["Skara Brae","Hill forts","Round barrows","Canute"],"a":[2],"e":"During the Bronze Age people lived in roundhouses and buried their dead in tombs called round barrows."},{"q":"The Northern Ireland Assembly cannot make decisions on:","o":["Education","Defence","Agriculture","Health"],"a":[1],"e":"The Northern Ireland Assembly can make decisions on issues such as education, agriculture, the environment, health and social services. It cannot make decisions on defence."},{"q":"According to the 2011 Census, what percentage of the population identified themselves as Buddhist?","o":["Less than 0.5%","Less than 1%","Less than 2%","Less than 3%"],"a":[0],"e":"In the 2011 Census, 59% of people identified themselves as Christian. Much smaller proportions identified themselves as Muslim (4.8%), Hindu (1.5%), Sikh (0.8%), Jewish or Buddhist (both less than 0.5%)."},{"q":"In which category did Jayne Torvill and Christopher Dean win gold medals at the Olympic Games in 1984?","o":["Rowing","Marathon","Swimming","Ice dancing"],"a":[3],"e":"Jayne Torvill and Christopher Dean won gold medals for ice dancing at the Olympic Games in 1984 and is one of Britain’s greatest Olympians."},{"q":"What is the first verse of the National Anthem of the UK ‘God save the King’?","o":["Long to reign over us","God save the King!","Long live our noble King!","God save our gracious King!"],"a":[3],"e":"The first verse of the National Anthem of the UK is ‘God save our gracious King!."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a British invention of the 20th century?","o":["The radar","The Turing machine","The World Wide Web","Radioactivity"],"a":[3],"e":"Radioactivity is not a British invention."},{"q":"Which British Prime Minister was famous for the speech ‘I have nothing to offer but blood, toil, tears and sweat’?","o":["Clement Attlee","Anthony Eden","Winston Churchill","Harold Wilson"],"a":[2],"e":"During the Second World War, Winston Churchill made many famous speeches including lines which you may still hear: ‘I have nothing to offer but blood, toil, tears and sweat’. This was Churchill’s first speech to the House of Commons after he became Prime Minister, 1940."},{"q":"Which of the following countries did NOT belong to the Allies during World War I?","o":["France","Bulgaria","The UK","Australia"],"a":[1],"e":"Bulgaria was not part of the Allies during the First World War."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following charities work with homeless people?","o":["Friends of the Earth","Shelter","Crisis","Oxfam"],"a":[1,2],"e":"Charities that work with homeless people include Crisis and Shelter."},{"q":"Which flower is associated with Scotland?","o":["Rose","Thistle","Daffodil","Shamrock"],"a":[1],"e":"The thistle is the flower associated with Scotland."},{"q":"What is the money from TV licences used for?","o":["To pay actors and actresses","To pay for the British Broadcasting Corporation (BBC)","To pay for publicity","To pay for private channels"],"a":[1],"e":"The money from TV licences is used to pay for the British Broadcasting Corporation (BBC). This is a public service broadcaster providing television and radio programmes. The BBC is the largest broadcaster in the world. It is the only wholly state-funded media organisation that is independent of government."},{"q":"Members of the Welsh government are elected every four years on the basis of:","o":["Proportional representation","Personal achievements","Instant run-off","First past the post system (the candidate who gets the most votes)"],"a":[0],"e":"The Welsh government and the Senedd are based in Cardiff, the capital city of Wales. There are 60 members of the Senedd (SMs) and elections are held every four years using a form of proportional representation."},{"q":"What is the Commonwealth?","o":["An association of countries that support each other and work together towards shared goals in democracy and development","An organisation responsible for the protection and promotion of human rights in its member countries","An association of countries that promotes international peace and security","A group of European and North American countries that have agreed to help each other if they come under attack"],"a":[0],"e":"The Commonwealth is an association of countries that support each other and work together towards shared goals in democracy and development. Most member states were once part of the British Empire, although a few countries which were not have also joined."},{"q":"Which famous architect designed St Paul’s Cathedral after it was destroyed by a fire in 1666?","o":["Sir Christopher Wren","Isaac Newton","Robert Burns","Isambard Kingdom Brunel"],"a":[0],"e":"In 1666, a great fire destroyed much of the city of London, including many churches and St Paul’s Cathedral. London was rebuilt with a new St Paul’s, which was designed by a famous architect, Sir Christopher Wren."},{"q":"Which of the following is a fundamental principle of life in the UK?","o":["Rule of men","Individual liberty","Intolerance of those with different beliefs","Lack of social cohesion"],"a":[1],"e":"The fundamental principles of British life include: democracy, the rule of law, individual liberty, tolerance of those with different faiths and beliefs and participation in community life."},{"q":"Which of the following countries belongs to the Commonwealth?","o":["Morocco","Algeria","Mozambique","Mali"],"a":[2],"e":"Mozambique."},{"q":"Who has to pay national insurance contributions in the UK?","o":["Everybody under the age of 65","Almost everybody who is in paid work","Everybody under the age of 70","People with earnings over £18,000 per year"],"a":[1],"e":"Almost everybody in the UK who is in paid work, including self-employed people, must pay National Insurance Contributions."},{"q":"A good way to support your local community is to shop for products locally.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is TRUE."},{"q":"Where is the centrepiece to the Remembrance Day service known as Cenotaph located?","o":["Cardiff, Wales","Whitehall, London","Hounslow Heath, London","Piccadilly Circus, London"],"a":[1],"e":"The Cenotaph, the Centrepiece to the Remembrance Day service, is located in Whitehall, London."}]},{"id":"test-6","name":"Test 6","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"Which of the following territories does not belong to Great Britain?","o":["Scotland","Wales","Northern Ireland","England"],"a":[2],"e":"‘Great Britain’ refers only to England, Scotland and Wales, not to Northern Ireland. The official name of the country is the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland."},{"q":"Who was the first Briton to win the ‘Tour de France’?","o":["Sir Chris Hoy","Bradley Wiggins","Andy Murray","Sir Steve Redgrave"],"a":[1],"e":"Bradley Wiggins became the first Briton to win the ‘Tour de France’."},{"q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["The English language has no dialects","The English language has many accents and dialects","Gaelic is spoken in North Wales","In Northern Ireland people speak French"],"a":[1],"e":"The English language has many accents and dialects."},{"q":"How old do you need to be to go into betting shops or gambling clubs?","o":["16 years old","18 years old","20 years old","21 years old"],"a":[1],"e":"In the UK, you have to be 18 to go into betting shops or gambling clubs."},{"q":"What do Ian McEwan, Hilary Mantel and Julian Barnes have in common?","o":["They are British sports players","They were awarded with the Man Booker Prize for Fiction","They won a Nobel Prize in Literature","They were awarded with the Mercury Music Prize"],"a":[1],"e":"The Man Booker Prize for Fiction is awarded annually for the best fiction novel written by an author from the Commonwealth, Ireland or Zimbabwe. It has been awarded since 1968. Past winners include Ian McEwan, Hilary Mantel and Julian Barnes."},{"q":"In Northern Ireland members of the Legislative Assembly are elected on the basis of:","o":["Proportional representation","Personal achievements","First past the post system","Instant run-off"],"a":[0],"e":"The Northern Ireland Assembly has 90 elected members, known as MLAs (members of the Legislative Assembly). They are elected with a form of proportional representation."},{"q":"How can MPs be contacted (choose TWO options)?","o":["By letter","By going to your local council and asking for an appointment","By phoning their constituency office","Through facebook"],"a":[0,2],"e":"You can contact MPs by letter or telephone at their constituency office, or at their office in the House of Commons: The House of Commons, Westminster, London SW1A OAA, telephone 0207729 3000."},{"q":"What did the’Bill of Rights’ confirm in 1689?","o":["Women’s right to vote","The rights of Parliament and the limits of the king’s power","The supreme power of the king","The freedom of speech"],"a":[1],"e":"The Bill of Rights, 1689, confirmed the rights of Parliament and the limits of the king’s power. Parliament took control of who could be monarch and declared that the king or queen must be a Protestant."},{"q":"Who designed the Clifton Suspension Bridge located over the Avon George?","o":["Sir Norman Foster","Isambard Kingdom Brunel","Richard Arkwright","Robert Adam"],"a":[1],"e":"The Clifton Suspension Bridge was designed by Isambard Kingdom Brunel."},{"q":"Does Britain have a written constitution?","o":["Yes","No"],"a":[1],"e":"The British constitution is not written down in any single document, and therefore it is described as ‘unwritten’. This is mainly because the UK, unlike America or France, has never had a revolution which led permanently to a totally new system of government."},{"q":"What was the estimated population of the British Empire during the Victorian period?","o":["More than 300 million people","More than 400 million people","More than 450 million people","More than 500 million people"],"a":[1],"e":"During the Victorian period, the British Empire grew to cover all of India, Australia and large parts of Africa. It became the largest empire the world has ever seen, with an estimated population of more than 400 million people."},{"q":"Which of the following countries was NOT part of the Allied Powers during the First World War?","o":["France","Japan","Serbia","Bulgaria"],"a":[3],"e":"Bulgaria formed part of the Central Powers during the First World War."},{"q":"How many times have the Olympic Games been hosted in the UK?","o":["Two","Three","Four","Six"],"a":[1],"e":"The UK has hosted the Olympic games on three occasions: 1908, 1948 and 2012."},{"q":"When were films first shown publicly in the UK?","o":["In 1887","In 1896","In 1897","In 1886"],"a":[1],"e":"Films were first shown publicly in the UK in 1896 and film screenings very quickly became popular."},{"q":"What is the name of the building where the Welsh government members meet?","o":["Westminster","Stormont","Holyrood","Senedd"],"a":[3],"e":"Elected members of the Welsh government meet at the Senedd in Cardiff, the capital city of Wales."},{"q":"Where is the city of Southampton located?","o":["England","Wales","Northern Ireland","Scotland"],"a":[0],"e":"Southampton is located in England."},{"q":"When did the First World War finish?","o":["At 12 am on 11th November 1916","At 11 am on 11th November 1918","At 11 pm on 13th November 1916","At 12 am on 13th February 1918"],"a":[1],"e":"The First World War ended at 11.00 am on 11th November 1918 with victory for Britain and its allies."},{"q":"Florence Nightingale was known for her work on:","o":["Human rights","The development of nursing in the UK","The development of computer science","The development and manufacture of motor-sport technology"],"a":[1],"e":"Florence Nightingale is often regarded as the founder of modern nursing."},{"q":"Which Act abolished slavery throughout the British Empire in 1833?","o":["The Freedom Act","The Habeas Corpus Act","The Emancipation Act","The Slavery Act"],"a":[2],"e":"In 1833 the Emancipation Act abolished slavery throughout the British Empire. The Royal Navy stopped slave ships from other countries, freed the slaves and punished the slave traders."},{"q":"How long does Diwali last for?","o":["A weekend","40 days","Five days","A week"],"a":[2],"e":"Diwali normally falls in October or November and lasts for five days."},{"q":"Who became King of Britain when Queen Anne died in 1714?","o":["James II","George II","George I","Charles I"],"a":[2],"e":"When Queen Anne died in 1714, Parliament chose a German, George I, to be the next king, because he was Anne’s nearest Protestant relative."},{"q":"What was the population of the UK in 2005?","o":["Just under 50 million","Just under 60 million","Just over 50 million","Just under 62 million"],"a":[1],"e":"The population of the UK in 2005 was just under 60 million people."},{"q":"When did the ‘Habeas Corpus Act’ become law?","o":["In 1679","In 1669","In 1689","In 1709"],"a":[0],"e":"The Habeas Corpus Act became law in 1679."},{"q":"Who discovered the penicillin in 1928?","o":["Sir Robert Watson-Watt","Alexander Fleming","John McLeod","Howard Florey"],"a":[1],"e":"Alexander Fleming was researching influenza (the ‘flu’) in 1928 when he discovered penicillin."}]},{"id":"test-7","name":"Test 7","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"During the Middle Ages, England was an important trading nation and people came to England from abroad to trade and also to work. Where did glass manufacturers come from?","o":["Germany","Italy","Holland","France"],"a":[1],"e":"During the Middle Ages people came to England from abroad to trade and also to work, such as weavers from France, engineers from Germany, glass manufacturers from Italy and canal builders from Holland."},{"q":"Where can people facing domestic violence get help from?","o":["The NHS","The Citizens Advise Bureau","The Human Rights Commission","The RSPB"],"a":[1],"e":"People facing domestic violence can get help from a solicitor or the Citizens Advice Bureau."},{"q":"Which American President worked closely with Margaret Thatcher?","o":["Hillary Clinton","Ronald Reagan","Jimmy Carter","George Bush"],"a":[1],"e":"Margaret Thatcher worked closely with the United States President, Ronald Reagan, and was one of the first Western leaders to recognise and welcome the changes in the leadership of the Soviet Union which eventually led to the end of the Cold War."},{"q":"People might be taken to court if they owe money to someone.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"Who was the first Archbishop of Canterbury?","o":["St Patrick","St Columba","St Augustine","St Peter"],"a":[2],"e":"St Augustine led missionaries from Rome, who spread Christianity in the south. St Augustine became the first Archbishop of Canterbury."},{"q":"How many members does a jury have in England, Wales and Northern Ireland?","o":["12","14","15","18"],"a":[0],"e":"In England, Wales and Northern Ireland a jury has 12 members, and in Scotland a jury has 15 members."},{"q":"What is tested with a driving test (choose TWO options)?","o":["Your medical condition","Your knowledge","Your practical skills","Your physical condition"],"a":[1,2],"e":"To get a UK driving licence you must pass a driving test, which tests both your knowledge and your practical skills."},{"q":"What is the role of the Speaker?","o":["To keep order during political debates to make sure the rules are followed","To point out what they see as the government’s failures and weaknesses","To control important public appointments","To challenge the government and put forward alternative policies"],"a":[0],"e":"The Speaker keeps order during political debates to make sure the rules are followed."},{"q":"Which of the following statements is FALSE regarding Youth Courts?","o":["The parents or carers of the young person being judged are expected to attend the hearing","Members of the public are not allowed in them","They are used for trials of young people aged 10 to 18","The name or photographs of the accused young person cannot be published in newspapers or used by the media"],"a":[2],"e":"They are used for trials of young people aged 10 to 17."},{"q":"Which TWO films were directed by David Lean?","o":["Brief Encounter","The 39 Steps","Lawrence of Arabia","Chariots of Fire"],"a":[0,2],"e":"David Lean directed Brief Encounter (1945) and Lawrence of Arabia (1962)."},{"q":"Which of the following venues is located in Glasgow?","o":["The O2","Wembley Stadium","The SEC Centre","Buckingham Palace"],"a":[2],"e":"The Scottish Exhibition and Conference Centre (SEC Centre) is located in Glasgow."},{"q":"How many British casualties were recorded during the first day of the British attack of the Somme in July 1916 during the First World War?","o":["60,000","70,000","30,000","50,000"],"a":[0],"e":"The British attack of the Somme in July 1916, resulted in about 60,000 British casualties on the first day alone."},{"q":"The Welsh dragon on the Welsh flag does not appear on the Union Flag because, when the first Union Flag was created in 1606 from the flags of Scotland and England, the Principality of Wales was already untied with England:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"Which two countries developed the ‘Concorde’ aircraft?","o":["Germany and Italy","USA and Canada","Germany and Switzerland","France and Britain"],"a":[3],"e":"Britain and France developed the world’s only supersonic commercial airliner, Concorde."},{"q":"What was the population of the UK in 1801?","o":["5 million","8 million","10 million","12 million"],"a":[1],"e":"The population of the UK in 1801 was 8 million people."},{"q":"Where is there UK geographically located?","o":["In the north east of Europe","In the south east of Europe","In the north west of Europe","In the south west of Europe"],"a":[2],"e":"The UK is located in the north west of Europe."},{"q":"What is the main function of the Council of Europe?","o":["To protect and promote human rights","To promote international trade relationships","To maintain international peace","To recommend action when there are international crises and threats to peace"],"a":[0],"e":"The Council of Europe is responsible for the protection and promotion of human rights in its member countries."},{"q":"Sir Alfred Hitchcock was an important British film director","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"Eminent directors included Sir Alexander Korda and Sir Alfred Hitchcock, who later left for Hollywood and remained an important film director until his death in 1980."},{"q":"Which Scottish physician and researcher co-discovered the insulin used to treat diabetes?","o":["Francis Crick","Sir Robert Watson-Watt","John McLeod","John Logie Baird"],"a":[2],"e":"The Scottish physician and researcher John Macleod was the co-discoverer of insulin, used to treat diabetes."},{"q":"Who was voted the greatest Briton of all time in 2002?","o":["Clement Attlee","Winston Churchill","Anthony Eden","Harold Wilson"],"a":[1],"e":"Winston Churchill in 2002 was voted the greatest Briton of all time by the public."},{"q":"When is St George’s Day, patron of England?","o":["23rd of April","1st of March","17th of March","30th of November"],"a":[0],"e":"St George’s Day, patron of England is celebrated on the 23rd of April."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following responsibilities will you have as a permanent resident or citizen of the UK?","o":["To respect and obey the law","To go to church on Sundays","To look after the area in which you live and the environment","To do the military service"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Respect and obey the law and look after the area in which you live and the environment are the correct answers."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following social laws were liberalised during the ‘Swinging Sixties’?","o":["The law of abortion","The law of divorce","The law of immigration","The law of adoption"],"a":[0,1],"e":"The law of abortion and the law of divorce were liberalised during the ‘Swinging Sixties’."},{"q":"Who was the mother of Elizabeth I?","o":["Jane Seymour","Anne of Cleves","Catherine of Aragon","Anne Boleyn"],"a":[3],"e":"Anne Boleyn and Henry VIII had one daughter, Elizabeth I."}]},{"id":"test-8","name":"Test 8","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"Who was the first wife of Henry VIII?","o":["Anne Boleyn","Catherine of Aragon","Anne of Cleves","Catherine Howard"],"a":[1],"e":"Catherine of Aragon was the first wife of Henry VIII."},{"q":"Which of the following flags is not represented in the union flag?","o":["Welsh","Scottish","English","Irish"],"a":[0],"e":"The Welsh flag does not appear on the Union Flag because, when the first Union Flag was created in 1606 from the flags of Scotland and England, the Principality of Wales was already untied with England."},{"q":"When does the Edinburgh Festival take place?","o":["Every summer","Every spring","Every winter","Every autumn"],"a":[0],"e":"The Edinburgh Festival takes place in Edinburgh, Scotland, every summer."},{"q":"When did ‘the Concorde’ aircraft fly for the first time?","o":["In 1969","In 1970","In 1975","In 1976"],"a":[0],"e":"The ‘Concorde’, the world’s only supersonic passenger aircraft, first flew in 1969."},{"q":"By which other name is the Conservative Party also known as?","o":["The Labour Party","The Whigs","The Green Party","The Tories"],"a":[3],"e":"The modern Conservative Party is still sometimes referred to as ‘The Tories’."},{"q":"When was the Northern Ireland Parliament established for the first time?","o":["In 1922","In 1972","In 1998","In 1969"],"a":[0],"e":"The Northern Ireland Parliament was first established in 1922."},{"q":"What is the opposition?","o":["The second largest party in the House of Lords","The third largest party in the House of Commons","The second largest party in the House of Commons","The third largest party in the House of Lords"],"a":[2],"e":"The second-largest party in the House of Commons is called the opposition."},{"q":"Which two languages combined form the basis of the English language?","o":["French and Swedish","Latin and Celtic","Latin and Greek","Norman French and Anglo-Saxon"],"a":[3],"e":"After the Norman Conquest, the king and his noblemen had spoken Norman French and the peasants had continued to speak Anglo-Saxon. Gradually these two languages combined to become one English language."},{"q":"Which THREE of the following are known to be main parts of the British government?","o":["The cabinet","The judiciary","The local organizations","The police"],"a":[0,1,3],"e":"In the UK, there are several different parts of government. The main ones are: the monarchy, the Parliament (the House of Commons and the House of Lords), the Prime Minister, the cabinet, the judiciary (courts), the police, the civil service and the local government."},{"q":"When did Adolf Hitler come to power in Germany?","o":["In 1922","In 1931","In 1933","In 1942"],"a":[2],"e":"Adolf Hitler came to power in Germany in 1933."},{"q":"Who invented the hovercraft?","o":["Sir Frank Whittle","Sir Christopher Cockerell","John Logie Baird","Sir Robert Edwards"],"a":[1],"e":"Sir Christopher Cockerell (1910-99), a British inventor, invented the hovercraft in the 1950s."},{"q":"Which court deals with serious criminal offences in Wales?","o":["The Sheriff Court","The Crown Court","The High Court","The Magistrates’ Court"],"a":[1],"e":"In England, Wales and Northern Ireland, serious criminal offences are tried in front of a judge and a jury in a Crown Court. In Scotland, serious cases are heard in a Sheriff Court with either a sheriff or a sheriff with a jury."},{"q":"What is the minimum age requirement to buy alcohol in a pub or night club in the UK?","o":["16 years old","18 years old","20 years old","21 years old"],"a":[1],"e":"To buy alcohol in a pub or night club you must be 18 or over, but people under that age may be allowed in some pubs with an adult."},{"q":"What is the coin with the highest value in the UK?","o":["50p","£2","£5","£10"],"a":[1],"e":"The coin with the highest value in the UK is the £2 coin."},{"q":"When were Protection Orders to protect a person from being forced into a marriage introduced in Scotland?","o":["2007","2008","2010","2011"],"a":[3],"e":"Forced Marriage Protection Orders were introduced in 2008 for England, Wales and Northern Ireland under the Forced Marriage (Civil Protection) Act 2007. Court orders can be obtained to protect a person from being forced into a marriage, or to protect a person in a forced marriage. Similar Protection Orders were introduced in Scotland in November 2011."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following are famous horse racing events in the UK?","o":["The Major Race","The Grand National","Royal Ascot","Six Nations Championship"],"a":[1,2],"e":"Famous horse-racing events in the UK include Royal Ascot and the Grand National."},{"q":"Which of the following events relates to the German bombing of London and other British cities at night time during the World War II?","o":["The Bombing","The German raid","The Blitz","The Axis"],"a":[2],"e":"The Blitz relates to the German bombing of London and other British cities at night time during the World War II"},{"q":"Who wrote an oratorio called ‘Messiah’, which is regularly sung by choirs at Easter time?","o":["George Frederick Handel","Sir Edward Elgar","Gustav Holst","Henry Purcell"],"a":[0],"e":"George Frederick Handel wrote an oratorio, Messiah, which is sung regularly by choirs, often at Easter time."},{"q":"When did hereditary peers lose the automatic right to attend the House of Lords?","o":["1985","1995","1989","1999"],"a":[3],"e":"Since 1999, hereditary peers have lost the automatic right to attend the House of Lords."},{"q":"Which of the following is England’s largest national park?","o":["The Lake District","Snowdonia","South Downs","Loch Lomond and the Trossachs"],"a":[0],"e":"The Lake District is England’s largest national park."},{"q":"Which of the following social changes took place during the ‘Swinging Sixties’?","o":["The position of women in the workplace was improved","Everyone could study in the University for free","People from the same gender were allowed to get married","All musical events were free"],"a":[0],"e":"The position of women in the workplace was improved during the ‘Swinging Sixties’."},{"q":"Where was Emmeline Pankhurst born?","o":["Liverpool","Kent","Manchester","Chester"],"a":[2],"e":"Emmeline Pankhurst was born in Manchester in 1858."},{"q":"Which of the following is a traditional English food?","o":["French fries","Roast beef","Haggis","Ulster fry"],"a":[1],"e":"Roast beef is a traditional English food."},{"q":"What are pantomimes based on?","o":["Fairy stories","Drama stories","Traditional stories","War stories"],"a":[0],"e":"Pantomimes are based on fairy stories and are light-hearted plays with music and comedy, enjoyed by family audiences."}]},{"id":"test-9","name":"Test 9","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"What was inscribed in the first coins to be minted in Britain during the Iron Age?","o":["Celtic symbols","The names of Iron Age Kings","Drawings of animals","The names of medicine plants"],"a":[1],"e":"The people of the Iron Age made the first coins to be minted in Britain, some inscribed with the names of Iron Age kings."},{"q":"What name is given to the Church of England in Scotland and the United States?","o":["The Main Church","The King’s Church","The Episcopal Church","The Catholic Church"],"a":[2],"e":"The Church of England is called the Episcopal Church in Scotland and the United States."},{"q":"Who directed the movie ‘Four Weddings and a Funeral’ in 1994?","o":["Mike Newell","Hugh Hudson","Kevin MacDonald","Nicolas Roeg"],"a":[0],"e":"Four Weddings and a Funeral (1994) was directed by Mike Newell."},{"q":"What was the population of the UK in 1951?","o":["20 million","40 million","35 million","50 million"],"a":[3],"e":"The population of the UK in 1951 was 50 million people."},{"q":"Which TWO religions celebrate Diwali?","o":["Buddhist","Muslim","Hindu","Sikh"],"a":[2,3],"e":"Diwali is celebrated by Hindus and Sikhs. It normally falls in October or November and lasts for five days. It is also often called the Festival of Lights."},{"q":"Which of the following statements is true?","o":["Proceedings in Parliament are broadcast on television and published in official reports","Proceedings in Parliament are not broadcast on television but published in official reports","Proceedings in Parliament are broadcast on the radio and not published in official reports","Proceedings in Parliament are broadcast on the radio only"],"a":[0],"e":"Proceedings in Parliament are broadcast on television and published in official reports called Hansard."},{"q":"In Northern Ireland, a newly qualified driver must display an R plate for one year after passing the test.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is TRUE."},{"q":"When did Emmeline Pankhurst set up the women’s Franchise League, which fought to get the vote in local elections for married women?","o":["1880","1889","1890","1899"],"a":[1],"e":"Emmeline Pankhurst was born in Manchester in 1858. She set up the women’s Franchise League in 1889, which fought to get the vote in local elections for married women. In 1903 she helped found the Women’s Social and Political Union (WSPU)."},{"q":"How many ski centres are there in Scotland?","o":["4","5","6","7"],"a":[1],"e":"There are five ski centres in Scotland, as well as Europe’s longest dry ski slope near Edinburgh."},{"q":"The public can listen to debates in the Palace of Westminster from public galleries in the House of Commons but not in the House of Lords:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"The public can listen to debates in the Palace of Westminster from public galleries in both the House of Commons and the House of Lords."},{"q":"Which is the oldest tennis tournament in the world?","o":["The Australian open","The U.S. Open","Roland Garros","The Wimbledon Championships"],"a":[3],"e":"The most famous tournament hosted in Britain is The Wimbledon Championships, which takes place each year at the All England Lawn Tennis and Croquet Club. It is the oldest tennis tournament in the world and the only ‘Grand Slam’ event played on grass."},{"q":"Which of the following is a British invention of the 20th century?","o":["The Harrier jump jet","Radon","Paraffin wax","The lightbulb"],"a":[0],"e":"The Harrier jump jet, an aircraft capable of taking off vertically, is a British invention of the 20th century."},{"q":"Churchill was the son of a politician and, before becoming a Conservative MP in 1900, was a soldier and journalist.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"During the 17th century there were two main groups in Parliament, which were known as:","o":["The Whigs and the Tories","The Liberal Democrats and the Labour Party","The lefties and the right wing","The Conservative Party and the Liberal Democrats"],"a":[0],"e":"During the 17th century there were two main groups in Parliament, known as the Whigs and the Tories."},{"q":"Where is the ‘Eden Project’ located?","o":["Cornwall","Devon","Doncaster","Dublin"],"a":[0],"e":"The Eden Project is located in Cornwall, in the south west of England."},{"q":"When was the Women’s Social and Political Union (WSPU) established?","o":["1889","1901","1899","1903"],"a":[3],"e":"Emmeline Pankhurst was born in Manchester in 1858. She set up the women’s Franchise League in 1889, which fought to get the vote in local elections for married women. In 1903 she helped found the Women’s Social and Political Union (WSPU)."},{"q":"Which of the following is a traditional food from Northern Ireland?","o":["Roast beef","Haggis","Sausage casserole","Ulster fry"],"a":[3],"e":"Ulster fry – a fried meal with bacon, eggs, sausage, black pudding, tomatoes, mushrooms, soda bread and potato bread – is a traditional food from Northern Ireland."},{"q":"Who is the author of the murder mystery play ‘The Mousetrap’?","o":["Tim Rice","Andrew Lloyd Webber","Dame Agatha Christie","Gilbert and Sullivan"],"a":[2],"e":"The Mousetrap, a murder-mystery play by Dame Agatha Christie, has been running in the west end since 1952 and has had the longest initial run of any show in history."},{"q":"Under which Act was the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland created?","o":["The Act of Union","The Great Act","The United Kingdom Act","The Act of Great Britain"],"a":[0],"e":"In 1801, Ireland became unified with England, Scotland and Wales after the Act of Union of 1800. This created the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland."},{"q":"When was the voting age reduced to 18 for men and women?","o":["1965","1969","1919","1929"],"a":[1],"e":"In 1969, the voting age was reduced to 18 for men and women."},{"q":"When is April Fool’s Day?","o":["1st of April","13th of April","30th of April","5th of April"],"a":[0],"e":"April Fool’s Day is on the 1st of April."},{"q":"Where is the city of Bradford located?","o":["In England","In Scotland","In Wales","In Northern Ireland"],"a":[0],"e":"Bradford is located in England."},{"q":"Which flag comprises a red cross on a white ground?","o":["The cross of St Andrew, patron saint of Scotland","The cross of St David, patron saint of Wales","The cross of St Patrick, patron saint of Ireland","The cross of St George, patron saint of England"],"a":[3],"e":"The cross of St George, patron saint of England, is a red cross on a white ground."},{"q":"When did the first farmers arrive in Britain?","o":["6,000 years ago","7,000 years ago","8,000 years ago","10,000 years ago"],"a":[0],"e":"The first farmers arrived in Britain 6,000 years ago."}]},{"id":"test-10","name":"Test 10","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"In which English county is the monument of Stonehenge located?","o":["Bedfordshire","Cheshire","Wiltshire","Cornwall"],"a":[2],"e":"The ancestors of the first farmers probably came from south-east Europe. These people built houses, tombs and monuments on the land. One of these monuments, Stonehenge, still stands in what is now the English county of Wiltshire."},{"q":"What is the best-known work from Robert Burns?","o":["Auld Lang Syne","Hamlet","A rose by another name","George’s Marvellous Medicine"],"a":[0],"e":"Burns’ best-known work is probably the song Auld Lang Syne, which is sung by people in the UK and other countries when they are celebrating the New Year (or Hogmanay as it is called in Scotland)."},{"q":"Which of the following crosses does NOT form part of the Union Flag?","o":["The cross of St George, patron saint of England","The cross of St David, patron saint of Wales","The cross of St Patrick, patron saint of Ireland","The cross of St Andrew, patron saint of Scotland"],"a":[1],"e":"The cross of St David, patron saint of Wales does not form part of the Union Flag because, when the first Union Flag was created in 1606 from the flags of Scotland and England, the Principality of Wales was already untied with England."},{"q":"What day is Halloween celebrated?","o":["30th of November","30th of October","14th of February","31st of October"],"a":[3],"e":"Halloween is celebrated on the 31st of October."},{"q":"Who was the first British distance runner to win a gold medal in the Olympic games in the 10,000 metres?","o":["Mo Farah","Bradley Wiggins","Sir Chris Hoy","David Weir"],"a":[0],"e":"Mo Farah (1983-) is a British distance runner, born in Somalia. He won gold medals in the 2012 Olympics for the 5,000 and 10,000 metres and is the first Briton to win the Olympic gold medal in the 10,000 metres."},{"q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["For some Scottish people, Hogmanay is a bigger holiday than Christmas","For some Scottish people, Hogmanay is a bigger holiday than New Year’s Eve","For some Welsh people, Hogmanay is a bigger holiday than Christmas","For some Irish people, Hogmanay is a bigger holiday than New Year’s Eve"],"a":[0],"e":"In Scotland, the 31st December is called Hogmanay and 2 January is also a public holiday. For some Scottish people, Hogmanay is a bigger holiday than Christmas."},{"q":"Light-hearted plays with music and comedy, which are enjoyed by family audiences and based on fairy stories are known as:","o":["Children’s plays","Pantomimes","Hogmanay","Parades"],"a":[1],"e":"Many theatres produce a pantomime at Christmas time. They are based on fairy stories and are light-plays with music and comedy, enjoyed by family audiences."},{"q":"What British actor was best known for his roles in various Shakespeare plays?","o":["Sir Laurence Olivier","Joseph Turner","Andrew Lloyd Webber","Tim Rice"],"a":[0],"e":"The Laurence Olivier Awards take place annually at different venues in London. There are a variety of categories, including best director, best actor and best actress. The awards are named after the British actor Sir Laurence Olivier, late Lord Olivier, who was best known for his roles in various Shakespeare plays."},{"q":"Who built the Tower of London after becoming king in 1066?","o":["Oliver Cromwell","Henry VIII","William the Conqueror","Henry VII"],"a":[2],"e":"The Tower of London was first built by William the Conqueror after he became king in 1066."},{"q":"Who succeeded David Cameron as Prime Minister after the referendum on the UK’s membership of the European Union?","o":["Boris Johnson","Theresa May","Gordon Brown","Liz Truss"],"a":[1],"e":"David Cameron was succeeded as Prime Minister after the Brexit referendum by Theresa May on 13 July 2016."},{"q":"Who composed a suite of pieces themed around the planets and the solar system called ‘The Planets’?","o":["The Edward Elgar","George Frederick Handel","Gustav Holst","Sir William Walton"],"a":[2],"e":"Important British composers include Gustav Holst, whose work includes The Planets, a suite of pieces themed around the planets and the solar system."},{"q":"What was the population of the UK in 1998?","o":["40 million","50 million","55 million","57 million"],"a":[3],"e":"The population of the UK in 1998 was 57 million people."},{"q":"Who is the chief officer of the House of Commons?","o":["The Prime Minister","The Speaker","The Archbishop of Canterbury","The leader of the opposition"],"a":[1],"e":"Debates in the House of Commons are chaired by the Speaker. This person is the chief officer of the House of Commons."},{"q":"What characterises the British constitution?","o":["It was written by the Prime Minister","It was written by the King","It is not written down in any single document, and therefore it is described as ‘unwritten","It was written by Henry VIII"],"a":[2],"e":"The British constitution is not written down in any single document, and therefore it is described as ‘unwritten’."},{"q":"How old was Queen Victoria when she became Queen in 1837?","o":["A week old","4 years old","20 years old","18 years old"],"a":[3],"e":"In 1837, Queen Victoria became queen of the UK at the age of 18."},{"q":"When did the First World War start?","o":["1912","1914","1942","1940"],"a":[1],"e":"On 28 June 1914, Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria was assassinated. This set off a chain of events leading to the First World War (1914-18)."},{"q":"Who forms the judiciary?","o":["The judges collectively","The Magistrates together","The MPs together","The judges and Magistrates together"],"a":[0],"e":"Judges (who are together called ‘the judiciary’) are responsible for interpreting the law and ensuring that trials are conducted fairly."},{"q":"Which of the following is a musical venue located in London?","o":["The O2","Glastonbury","The Dome","The Fringe"],"a":[0],"e":"There are many large venues that host music events throughout the year, such as: The O2 in Greenwich, south-east London."},{"q":"Who directed the movie ‘The Killing Fields’ in 1984?","o":["Roland Joffé","Hugh Hudson","Kevin MacDonald","Nicolas Roeg"],"a":[0],"e":"The Killing Fields (1984) was directed by Roland Joffé."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following castles are located in Scotland?","o":["Conwy Castle","Caernarfon Castle","Crathes Castle","Inveraray Castle"],"a":[2,3],"e":"Inveraray Castle and Crathes Castle are located in Scotland."},{"q":"Which battle signified the end of the Wars of the Roses?","o":["The Battle of Agincourt","The Battle of Bosworth Field","The Battle of Bannockburn","The Battle of Marston Moor"],"a":[1],"e":"The Wars of the Roses ended with the Battle of Bosworth Field in 1485."},{"q":"Which court deals with minor criminal offences in Scotland?","o":["The Justice of the Peace Court","The Magistrates’ Court","The Sheriff Court","The Crown Court"],"a":[0],"e":"In Scotland, minor criminal offences go to a Justice of the Peace Court."},{"q":"If an MP dies or resigns, there will be a fresh election, which is known as:","o":["Hansard","First past the post","By-election","Coalition"],"a":[2],"e":"If an MP dies or resigns, there will be a fresh election, called a by-election, in his or her constituency."},{"q":"A person who has not paid enough National Insurance contributions will not be able to receive certain contributory benefits including (choose TWO options):","o":["Jobseeker’s Allowance","A full state retirement pension","Maternity Allowance","Disability Living Allowance"],"a":[0,1],"e":"Anyone who does not pay enough National Insurance Contributions will not be able to receive certain contributory benefits such as Jobseeker’s Allowance or a full state retirement pension."}]},{"id":"test-11","name":"Test 11","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"Who succeeded Theresa May as Prime Minister on the 24th July 2019?","o":["Rishi Sunak","Gordon Brown","David Cameron","Boris Johnson"],"a":[3],"e":"Theresa May was succeeded by Boris Johnson on 24 July 2019."},{"q":"What name is given to the period of rapid development of industry in Britain in the 18th and 19th centuries?","o":["The Enlightenment","The Industrial Revolution","The Victorian period","The inter-war period"],"a":[1],"e":"The Industrial Revolution was the rapid development of industry in Britain in the 18th and 19th centuries."},{"q":"During the slave trade, slaves were taken from West Africa to America and the Caribbean to work on tobacco and sugar plantations.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is TRUE."},{"q":"Who was responsible for the construction of the Great Western Railway?","o":["Isambard Kingdom Brunel","Sir Norman Foster","Sir Christopher Wren","Robert Adam"],"a":[0],"e":"Isambard Kingdom Brunel was responsible for constructing the Great Western Railway, which was the first major railway built in Britain."},{"q":"In which of the following areas has the Senedd the power to make laws (choose four answers)?","o":["Education and training","Military defence","Economic development","Housing","Immigration","Health and social services"],"a":[0,2,3,5],"e":"The Senedd has the power to make laws in 21 areas, including: education and training, health and social services, economic development and housing."},{"q":"How many members comprise the UN Security Council?","o":["5","10","15","25"],"a":[2],"e":"There are 15 members on the UN Security Council, which recommends action when there are international crises and threats to peace."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following forts form part of Hadrian’s Wall?","o":["Vindolanda","Stonehenge","Housesteads","Maiden"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Parts of Hadrian’s Wall include the forts of Housesteads and Vindolanda."},{"q":"Members of the Welsh government can speak in either English or Welsh, but all of the Senedd’s publications have to be in English:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"Members of the Welsh government can speak in either English or Welsh, and all publications of the Senedd’s publications are in both languages."},{"q":"Why did Elizabeth I keep her cousin Mary prisoner for 20 years?","o":["She did not agree with her political views","She suspected Mary of wanting to take over the English throne","She had killed her son","She was a spy of the Spanish Armada"],"a":[1],"e":"Elizabeth suspected Mary of wanting to take over the English throne, and kept her prisoner for 20 years."},{"q":"What was the name of the first cloned mammal?","o":["Molly","Dolly","Colin","Alice"],"a":[1],"e":"In 1996, two British scientists, Sir Ian Wilmot and Keith Campbell, led a team which was the first to succeed in cloning a mammal, Dolly the sheep."},{"q":"Who is responsible for the creation of the European Convention on Human Rights and Fundamental Freedoms?","o":["The UN Security Council","The Council of Europe","The United Nations","The Commonwealth"],"a":[1],"e":"The Council of Europe has no power to make laws but draws up conventions and charters, the most well-known of which is the European Convention on Human Rights and Fundamental Freedoms."},{"q":"Who became Prime Minister of the UK in May 1940?","o":["Margaret Thatcher","Winston Churchill","Clement Attlee","Harold Macmillan"],"a":[1],"e":"Churchill was the son of a politician and, before becoming a Conservative MP in 1900, was a soldier and journalist. In May 1940 he became Prime Minister."},{"q":"Members of the House of Lords may stand for election to the House of Commons and are eligible for all public offices:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"Members of the House of Lords may not stand for election to the House of Commons but are eligible for all other public offices."},{"q":"What can you find at the ‘Eden Project’?","o":["Giant greenhouses, house plants from all over the world","Animals from all over the world","Prehistoric monuments","An open market with local food"],"a":[0],"e":"The Eden Project is located in Cornwall, in the south west of England. Its biomes, which are like giant greenhouses, house plants from all over the world. The Eden Project is also a charity which runs environmental and social projects internationally."},{"q":"What TWO names are given to the people who give tours at the Tower of London?","o":["Beefeaters","Suffragettes","Chartists","Yeoman Wardens"],"a":[0,3],"e":"Tours to the Tower of London are given by the Yeoman Warders, also known as Beefeaters, who tell visitors about the building’s history."},{"q":"Which of the following is a traditional Scottish food?","o":["Roast beef","Haggis","Ulster fry","Custard pies"],"a":[1],"e":"Haggis – a sheep’s stomach stuffed with offal, suet, onions and oatmeal – is a traditional Scottish food."},{"q":"How often do the Laurence Olivier Awards take place?","o":["Every six months","Annually","Every four years","Every two years"],"a":[1],"e":"The Laurence Olivier Awards take place annually at different venues in London."},{"q":"When is Valentine’s Day?","o":["30th of October","14th of February","30th of November","27th of December"],"a":[1],"e":"Valentine’s Day is celebrated on the 14th of February."},{"q":"When is St Patrick’s Day, patron to of Northern Ireland?","o":["23rd of April","30th of November","1st of March","17th of March"],"a":[3],"e":"St Patrick’s Day, patron to of Northern Ireland is on the 17th of March."},{"q":"What does the term ‘PDSA’ stand for?","o":["People’s Defence for Sick Animals","People’s Dispensary for Sick Animals","Pilot Program for Sustainable Agriculture","Program Directive for Sustainable Agriculture"],"a":[1],"e":"‘PDSA’ stands for People’s Dispensary for Sick Animals."},{"q":"Which of the following TWO islands are ‘Crown dependencies’?","o":["Ceylon","The Isle of Man","The Channel Islands","Ireland"],"a":[1,2],"e":"There are several islands which are closely linked with the UK but are not part of it: the Channel Islands and the Isle of Man. These have their own governments and are called ‘Crown dependencies’."},{"q":"When did English settlers begin to colonise the eastern coast of America?","o":["During James I’s time","During Henry VII’s time","During Henry VIII’s time","During Elizabeth I’s time"],"a":[3],"e":"In Elizabeth I’s time, English settlers first began to colonise the eastern coast of America."},{"q":"When did the Black Death come to England?","o":["In 1248","In 1348","In 1358","In 1414"],"a":[1],"e":"In 1348, a disease, probably a form of plague, came to Britain. This was known as the Black Death."},{"q":"What is the name of the annual event that gives awards in a range of musical categories, such as best British group and best British solo artist?","o":["The Brit Awards","The MTV Awards","The BAFTA Awards","The Mercury Music Prize"],"a":[0],"e":"The Brit Awards is an annual event that gives awards in a range of categories, such as best British group and best British solo artist."}]},{"id":"test-12","name":"Test 12","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"What name is given to the elected members of the Welsh government?","o":["MSPs","SMs","MLAs","MPs"],"a":[1],"e":"In Wales the elected members are known as SMs or Senedd Members."},{"q":"How often do the ministers that form the Cabinet meet to make important decisions about government policy?","o":["Every day","Weekly","Monthly","Every six months"],"a":[1],"e":"The cabinet is a committee which usually meets weekly and makes important decisions about government policy."},{"q":"How many Welsh-language channels are there in Wales?","o":["None","One","Two","Three"],"a":[1],"e":"There is one Welsh-language channel in Wales."},{"q":"What is the nickname for the great bell of the clock at the Houses of Parliament in London?","o":["Tower of London","Buckingham clock","Big Ben","Big Clock"],"a":[2],"e":"Big Ben is the nickname for the great bell of the clock at the House of Parliament in London."},{"q":"How many countries are members of the United Nations?","o":["More than 190 countries","More than 150 countries","More than 140 countries","More than 180 countries"],"a":[0],"e":"The UK is part of the United Nations (UN), an international organization with more than 190 countries as members."},{"q":"Which of the following statements is true?","o":["The police force is a public service that helps and protects everyone","The police force is a private service that helps and protects everyone","The police force is a private service in charge of rescuing people from dangerous situations","The police force is a public service that works to avoid road accidents"],"a":[0],"e":"The police force is a public service that helps and protects everyone, no matter what their background or where they live."},{"q":"What is the system by which the income tax is automatically taken from the employee’s income by their employer and paid directly to HM Revenue & Customs (HMRC)?","o":["Self-assessment","“Pay As You Earn” (PAYE)","Tax return","Pension tax"],"a":[1],"e":"For most people, the right amount of income tax is automatically taken from their income from employment by their employer and paid directly to HM Revenue & Customs (HMRC), the government department that collects taxes. This system is called “Pay As You Earn” (PAYE)."},{"q":"How many British casualties were recorded during the First World War?","o":["More than 2 million people","More than 3 million people","More than 4 million people","More than 5 million people"],"a":[0],"e":"During the First World War millions of people were killed or wounded, with more than 2 million British casualties."},{"q":"Why did Henry VIII decide to divorce Catherine of Aragon?","o":["She was unpopular in the country","She was too old to give him another child","For political reasons","She was accused of taking lovers to the Palace"],"a":[1],"e":"When Catherine was too old to give him another child, Henry VIII decided to divorce her, hoping that another wife would give him a son to be his heir."},{"q":"How many local authorities are there in London?","o":["22","25","33","43"],"a":[2],"e":"London has 33 local authorities, with the Greater London Authority and the Mayor of London coordinating policies across the capital."},{"q":"When did British combat troops leave Iraq?","o":["In 2004","In 2005","In 2006","In 2009"],"a":[3],"e":"British combat troops left Iraq in 2009."},{"q":"Where is the Parliament of the UK?","o":["In Manchester","In Westminster","In Windsor Castle","In Buckingham Palace"],"a":[1],"e":"The UK is governed by the parliament sitting in Westminster."},{"q":"Which of the following is a criminal offence?","o":["Selling tobacco to someone under the age of 18","Owing money to someone","Discrimination in the workplace","﻿Selling faulty goods or services"],"a":[0],"e":"It is illegal and a criminal offence to sell tobacco products (for example, cigarettes, cigars, roll-up tobacco) to anyone under the age of 18."},{"q":"﻿Who used a system of land ownership known as feudalism?","o":["The Greeks","The Vikings","The Normans","The Welsh"],"a":[2],"e":"The Normans used a system of land ownership known as feudalism."},{"q":"The line ‘all the world’s a stage’ belongs to which of the following plays or poems written by William Shakespeare?","o":["Romeo and Juliet","Hamlet","As You Like It","Sonnet 18"],"a":[2],"e":"All the world’s a stage’ are lines from William Shakespeare’s play As You Like It."},{"q":"During the times of Elizabeth I, English settlers began to colonise:","o":["Indonesia","The eastern coast of America","The western coast of Australia","The Pacific Islands"],"a":[1],"e":"In Elizabeth I’s time, English settlers first began to colonise the eastern coast of America."},{"q":"In which city is the Welsh government building, also known as Senedd, located?","o":["Edinburgh","Belfast","Cardiff Bay","Swansea"],"a":[2],"e":"In Wales the elected members, known as SMs, meet at the Senedd in Cardiff Bay."},{"q":"What architectural style became popular in the 19th century?","o":["Corinthian","Barroco","Medieval Gothic","Art Deco"],"a":[2],"e":"In the 19th century, the medieval ’gothic’ style became popular again. As cities expanded, many great public buildings were built in this style. The Houses of Parliament and St Pancras Station were built at this time, as were the town halls in cities such as Manchester and Sheffield."},{"q":"Which of the following gardens is NOT located in England?","o":["Mount Stewart","Kew Gardens","Sissinghurst","Hidcote"],"a":[0],"e":"There are famous gardens to visit throughout the UK, including Kew Gardens, Sissinghurst and Hidcote in England, Crathes Castle and Inveraray Castle in Scotland, Bodnant Garden in Wales, and Mount Stewart in Northern Ireland."},{"q":"Which TWO families started a civil war to decide who should be king of England in 1455?","o":["The House of Lancaster","The House of Kent","The House of York","The House of Newcastle"],"a":[0,2],"e":"In 1455, a civil war was begun to decide who should be king of England. It was fought between the supporters of two families: the House of Lancaster and the House of York."},{"q":"Who was in charge of the British fleet at the Battle of Trafalgar?","o":["Admiral Nelson","Oliver Cromwell","The Duke of Wellington","Henry VIII"],"a":[0],"e":"Admiral Nelson was in charge of the British fleet at Trafalgar and was killed in the battle."},{"q":"Which court deals with serious criminal offences in Northern Ireland?","o":["The Sheriff Court","The Crown Court","The Magistrates’ Court","The High Court"],"a":[1],"e":"In England, Wales and Northern Ireland, serious offences are tried in front of a judge and a jury in a Crown Court. In Scotland, serious cases are heard in a Sheriff Court with either a sheriff or a sheriff with a jury."},{"q":"Which political party does the Speaker represent?","o":["The Liberal Democrats","The Conservative party","The Labour Party","They do not represent any political party"],"a":[3],"e":"The Speaker is neutral and does not represent a political party, even though he or she is an MP, represents a constituency and deals with the constituents’ problems like any other MP."},{"q":"When was the Scottish Parliament formed?","o":["In 2000","In 2002","In 1999","In 1998"],"a":[2],"e":"The Scottish Parliament was formed in 1999."}]},{"id":"test-13","name":"Test 13","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"What characterised the Bronze Age?","o":["People were farmers who lived in large villages","People lived in roundhouses and buried their dead in tombs","People were nomads and hunters","People started to use plastics"],"a":[1],"e":"During the Bronze Age people lived in roundhouses and buried their dead in tombs called round barrows."},{"q":"What name is given to the elected members of the Scottish Parliament?","o":["MSPs","SMs","MLAs","MPs"],"a":[0],"e":"Elected members of the Scottish Parliament are known as Members of the Scottish Parliament (MSPs)."},{"q":"Which game do people usually play at Halloween?","o":["Chess","Trick or treat","Hide and seek","Netball"],"a":[1],"e":"During Halloween young people will often dress up in frightening costumes to play ‘trick or treat’."},{"q":"The public can listen to debates in the Palace of Westminster from public galleries located in:","o":["The House of Commons","The House of Lords","Both, the House of Commons and House of Lords","All of the above are false"],"a":[2],"e":"The public can listen to debates in the Palace of Westminster from public galleries in both the House of Commons and the House of Lords."},{"q":"What is the most well-known gallery in Wales?","o":["Tate Britain","The National Museum","The National Gallery","Tate Modern"],"a":[1],"e":"In the UK, some of the most well-known galleries are The National Gallery, Tate Britain and Tate Modern in London, the National Museum in Cardiff, and the National Gallery of Scotland in Edinburgh."},{"q":"The Man Booker Prize for Fiction is awarded annually for the best fiction novel written by an author from:","o":["The Commonwealth, Ireland or Zimbabwe","The European Union, Zambia or Zimbabwe","The Commonwealth, the USA or Australia","The European Union, Canada or Australia"],"a":[0],"e":"The Man Booker Prize for Fiction is awarded annually for the best fiction novel written by an author from the Commonwealth, Ireland or Zimbabwe. It has been awarded since 1968."},{"q":"Which of the following statements regarding Gaelic language is TRUE?","o":["In Wales, Gaelic is spoken in some parts of the coast","In Scotland, Gaelic is spoken everywhere","In Scotland, Gaelic is spoken in some parts of the Highlands and Islands","In Wales, Gaelic is the main language"],"a":[2],"e":"In Scotland, Gaelic is spoken in some parts of the Highlands and Islands, and in Northern Ireland some people speak Irish Gaelic."},{"q":"Participation in community life is a fundamental principle of British life.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"The fundamental principles of British life include: participation in community life."},{"q":"How many British citizens left the country between 1853 and 1913?","o":["10 million","13 million","15 million","18 million"],"a":[1],"e":"Between 1853 and 1913, as many as 13 million British citizens left the country."},{"q":"Where was the main Olympic site for the 2012 Games?","o":["Stratford","Bristol","Liverpool","Watford"],"a":[0],"e":"The main Olympic site for the 2012 Games was in Stratford, East London."},{"q":"Who forms the shadow cabinet?","o":["Senior opposition MPs appointed by the leader of the opposition","Junior opposition MPs appointed by the leader of the governing party","Junior opposition MPs appointed by the leader of the opposition","Local councillors"],"a":[0],"e":"The leader of the opposition also appoints senior opposition MPs to be ‘shadow ministers’. They form the shadow cabinet and their role is to challenge the government and put forward alternative policies."},{"q":"Who directed the movie ‘Brief Encounter’ in 1945?","o":["Alfred Hitchcock","Hugh Hudson","Carol Reed","David Lean"],"a":[3],"e":"Brief Encounter (1945) was directed by David Lean."},{"q":"Who was the leader of the English Republic?","o":["King Richard III","Oliver Cromwell","Charles II","Charles I"],"a":[1],"e":"Oliver Cromwell was the leader of the English republic."},{"q":"According to the 2011 Census, what percentage of the population identified themselves as Hindu?","o":["0.5%","1%","1.5%","3%"],"a":[2],"e":"In the 2011 Census, 59% of people identified themselves as Christian. Much smaller proportions identified themselves as Muslim (4.8%), Hindu (1.5%), Sikh (0.8%), Jewish or Buddhist (both less than 0.5%)."},{"q":"People in the UK have to pay tax on any earnings from (choose THREE options)?","o":["Profits in self-employment","Income from property, savings and dividends","Valuable gifts","Taxable benefits"],"a":[0,1,3],"e":"People in the UK have to pay tax on their income, which includes: wages from paid employment, profits from self-employment, taxable benefits, pensions and income from property, savings and dividends."},{"q":"How many devolved administrations are there in the UK?","o":["2","3","4","5"],"a":[1],"e":"There are three devolved administrations in the UK, these include: the Welsh government, the Scottish Parliament and the Northern Ireland Assembly."},{"q":"Who is responsible for interpreting the law and ensuring that trials are conducted fairly?","o":["The government","The police officers","The judiciary","The Prime Minister and the MPs"],"a":[2],"e":"Judges (who are together called ‘the judiciary’ ) are responsible for interpreting the law and ensuring that trials are conducted fairly."},{"q":"How often do people who are 70 years old or over need to renew their driving licence?","o":["Every year","Every 2 years","Every 3 years","Every 5 years"],"a":[2],"e":"Drivers can use their driving licence until they are 70 years old. After that, the licence is valid for three years at a time."},{"q":"Where was Margaret Thatcher born?","o":["Buckinghamshire","Cheshire","Lincolnshire","Bedfordshire"],"a":[2],"e":"Margaret Thatcher was the daughter of a grocer from Grantham in Lincolnshire."},{"q":"Where are the Welsh government and the Senedd based?","o":["In Cardiff","In Swansea","In Conwy","In Llandudno"],"a":[0],"e":"The Welsh government and Senedd are based in Cardiff, the capital city of Wales."},{"q":"Jenson Button is a famous:","o":["British poet","Football player","Tennis player","Formula 1 driver"],"a":[3],"e":"Jenson Button is a famous Formula 1 driver."},{"q":"Who introduced the Statute of Rhuddlan by which Wales was annexed to the Crown of England in 1284?","o":["King Edward I of England","Robert the Bruce","King Henry V","William III of England"],"a":[0],"e":"In 1284 King Edward I of England introduced the Statute of Rhuddlan, which annexed Wales to the Crown of England."},{"q":"When was the Brexit referendum held?","o":["23rd June 2015","13th July 2015","13th July 2016","23rd June 2016"],"a":[3],"e":"The Brexit referendum was held on 23 June 2016."},{"q":"In the UK, alcohol cannot be sold to anyone under the age of:","o":["16","18","20","21"],"a":[1],"e":"It is a criminal offence to sell alcohol to anyone who is under 18 or to buy alcohol for people who are under the age of 18. (There is one exception: people aged 16 or over can drink alcohol with a meal in a hotel or restaurant."}]},{"id":"test-14","name":"Test 14","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"Who was known as the Iron Duke?","o":["The Duke of Edinburgh","The Duke of Normandy","The Duke of Wellington","The Duke of Lancaster"],"a":[2],"e":"In 1815, the French Wars ended with the defeat of the Emperor Napoleon by the Duke of Wellington at the Battle of Waterloo. Wellington was known as the Iron Duke and later became Prime Minister."},{"q":"Who appoints the members of the Cabinet?","o":["The King","The Prime Minister","The Prince of Wales","The Magistrates"],"a":[1],"e":"The Prime Minister (PM) is the leader of the political party in power. He or she appoints the members of the cabinet and has control over many important public appointments."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a British invention?","o":["The World Wide Web","The Turing machine","The hovercraft","The mobile phone"],"a":[3],"e":"The World Wide Web, the Turing machine and the hovercraft are British inventions. The mobile phone is not."},{"q":"When did Argentina invade the Falklands Islands?","o":["In 1980","In 1982","In 1984","In 1992"],"a":[1],"e":"In 1982, Argentina invaded the Falkland Islands, a British overseas territory in the South Atlantic."},{"q":"Which of the following statements is true?","o":["County Courts deal with a wide range of civil disputes including family matters, breaches of contract and divorce.","County Courts deal with a wide range of criminal disputes including family matters, breaches of contract and divorce."],"a":[0],"e":"County Courts deal with a wide range of civil disputes including family matters, breaches of contract and divorce."},{"q":"When is Boxing Day?","o":["24th of December","25th of December","26th of December","31st of December"],"a":[2],"e":"Boxing Day is on the 26th of December, the day after Christmas Day and is a public holiday."},{"q":"According to 2011 Census, what percentage of the population identified themselves as Sikh?","o":["0.1%","0.5%","0.8%","2%"],"a":[2],"e":"In the 2011 Census, 59% of people identified themselves as Christian. Much smaller proportions identified themselves as Muslim (4.8%), Hindu (1.5%), Sikh (0.8%), Jewish or Buddhist (both less than 0.5%)."},{"q":"Which court usually deals with serious criminal cases in Scotland?","o":["The Sheriff Court","The Crown Court","The High Court","The Magistrates’ Court"],"a":[0],"e":"In Scotland, serious criminal cases are heard in a Sheriff Court with either a sheriff or a sheriff with a jury. The most serious cases in Scotland, such as murder, are heard at a High Court with a judge and jury."},{"q":"Which of the following are British overseas territories?","o":["Ireland","The Canary Islands","St Helena","The Falkland Islands"],"a":[2,3],"e":"There are several British overseas territories in other parts of the world, such as St Helena and the Falkland Islands. They are also linked to the UK but are not part of it."},{"q":"When did Alexander Fleming win the Nobel Prize in medicine for the discovery of the penicillin?","o":["In 1935","In 1945","In 1950","In 1952"],"a":[1],"e":"Fleming won the Nobel Prize in Medicine in 1945 for the discovery of the penicillin."},{"q":"What is Sir Jackie Stewart famous for?","o":["He captained the English football team that won the World Cup in 1966","He captained the English cricket team","He won the Formula 1 world championship three times","He won gold medals in rowing in five consecutive Olympic Games"],"a":[2],"e":"Sir Jackie Stewart is a Scottish former racing driver who won the Formula 1 world championship three times."},{"q":"The decade of the 1960s was a period of significant social change, when there was growth in British fashion, cinema and popular music:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"When did Britain and France develop the world’s only supersonic commercial airliner known as ‘The Concorde’?","o":["1970s","1950s","1980s","1960s"],"a":[3],"e":"The 1960s was a time of technological progress, when Britain and France developed the world’s only supersonic commercial airliner, Concorde."},{"q":"When was the United Nations set up?","o":["Before the Second World War","After the Second World War","Before the First World War","Before the War of the Roses"],"a":[1],"e":"The UN was set up after the Second World War and aims to prevent war and promote international peace and security."},{"q":"Where can you find copies of the ‘Hansard’?","o":["In the Guardian newspaper","In large libraries and at www.parliament.uk","In bookshops","In any library"],"a":[1],"e":"Proceedings in Parliament are broadcast on television and published in official reports called Hansard. Written reports can be found in large libraries and at www.parliament.uk."},{"q":"Which court normally deals with cases of children aged 10 to 17 in England, Wales and Northern Ireland?","o":["The High Court","The Youth Court","The Magistrates’ Court","The Sheriff Court"],"a":[1],"e":"In England, Wales and Northern Ireland, if an accused person is aged 10 to 17, the case is normally heard in a Youth Court in front of up to three specially trained magistrates or a District Judge. The most serious cases will go the Crown Court."},{"q":"How old do you need to be to be able to go into betting shops or gambling clubs?","o":["16 years old","18 years old","20 years old","21 years old"],"a":[1],"e":"You have to be 18 to go into betting shops or gambling clubs."},{"q":"Which flag has a diagonal white cross on a blue ground?","o":["The cross of St Andrew, patron saint of Scotland","The cross of St Patrick, patron saint of Ireland","The cross of St George, patron saint of England","The cross of St David, patron saint of Wales"],"a":[0],"e":"The cross of St Andrew, patron saint of Scotland, is a diagonal white cross on a blue ground."},{"q":"How long do Muslims fast for during Ramadan?","o":["A week","A month","Two weeks","Three weeks"],"a":[1],"e":"Eid a-Fitr celebrates the end of Ramadan, when Muslims have fasted for a month."},{"q":"Which British actor has recently won an Oscar?","o":["Hugh Laurie","Colin Firth","Jude Law","Charles Chaplin"],"a":[1],"e":"Recent British actors to have won Oscars include Colin Firth, Sir Antony Hopkins, Dame Judi Dench, Kate Winslet and Tilda Swinton."},{"q":"Which was the first major railway built in Britain?","o":["High Speed 1","West Coast Main Line","Great Western Railway","East London Line"],"a":[2],"e":"The Great Western Railway was the first major railway built in Britain."},{"q":"In the 15th century, Henry Tudor, the leader of the House of Lancaster, became:","o":["King Henry VIII","King Richard I","King Richard III","King Henry VII"],"a":[3],"e":"In 1485, Henry Tudor, the leader of the House of Lancaster, became King Henry VII after defeating King Richard III of the House of York at the Battle of Bosworth Field."},{"q":"How many senior MPs are appointed by the Prime Minister?","o":["Less than 10","About 15","About 20","More than 25"],"a":[2],"e":"The Prime Minister appoints about 20 senior MPs to become ministers in charge of departments."},{"q":"Who won an Olympic gold medal in the pentathlon in 1972?","o":["Sir Ian Botham","Mary Peters","Dame Kelly Holmes","Jessica Ennis"],"a":[1],"e":"Mary Peters was a talented athlete who won an Olympic gold medal in the pentathlon in 1972."}]},{"id":"test-15","name":"Test 15","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"What is the capital city of Wales?","o":["Swansea","Cardiff","Cornwall","Bangor"],"a":[1],"e":"The capital city of Wales is Cardiff."},{"q":"When did the Roman army leave Britain?","o":["AD 410","AD 510","AD 600","AD 530"],"a":[0],"e":"The Roman army left Britain in AD 410 to defend other parts of the Roman Empire and never returned."},{"q":"Which of the following statements is true?","o":["You will receive a fine up to £2000 if you watch TV but do not have a TV licence.","You will receive a fine up to £1,000 if you watch TV but do not have a TV licence.","You will receive a fine up to £10,000 if you watch TV but do not have a TV licence.","You will receive a fine up to £5,000 if you watch TV but do not have a TV licence."],"a":[1],"e":"You will receive a fine up to £1,000 if you watch TV but do not have a TV licence."},{"q":"Emmeline Pankhurst set up the women’s Franchise League in 1889, which fought to get the vote in local elections for married women.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true. Emmeline Pankhurst was born in Manchester in 1858. She set up the women’s Franchise League in 1889, which fought to get the vote in local elections for married women."},{"q":"The king is the head of state in the UK:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"King Charles III is the head of state of the UK."},{"q":"How long did the First World War last for?","o":["Two years","Three years","Four years","Six years"],"a":[2],"e":"The First World War lasted four years (1914 – 1918)."},{"q":"Who was Henry VII?","o":["The leader of the House of York","The leader of the House of Lancaster","The leader of the House of Buckingham","The leader of the House of Leeds"],"a":[1],"e":"Henry VII was the leader of the House of Lancaster."},{"q":"When were the Houses of Parliament built?","o":["In the 17th century","In the 18th century","In the 19th century","In the 20th century"],"a":[2],"e":"The Houses of Parliament and St Pancras Station were built in the 19th century, as were the town halls in cities such as Manchester and Sheffield."},{"q":"How many volunteers work for the National Trust?","o":["More than 71,000","More than 81,000","More than 61,000","More than 91,000"],"a":[2],"e":"There are now more than 61,000 volunteers helping to keep the National Trust running."},{"q":"What was the population of the UK in 1851?","o":["20 million","40 million","30 million","50 million"],"a":[0],"e":"The population of the UK in 1851 was 20 million people."},{"q":"The line ‘a rose by any other name’ belongs to which of the following plays or poems written by William Shakespeare?","o":["Romeo and Juliet","Hamlet","As You Like It","Henry V"],"a":[0],"e":"‘A rose by any other name’ is a line from William Shakespeare’s play Romeo and Juliet."},{"q":"Which religious community celebrates Vaisakhi?","o":["Muslim","Hindu","Sikh","Jewish"],"a":[2],"e":"Vaisakhi (also spelled Baisakhi) is a Sikh festival which celebrates the founding of the Sikh community known as the Khalsa."},{"q":"Which book written by JRR Tolkien was voted the country’s best-loved novel in 2003?","o":["Harry Potter","The Lord of the Rings","Sense and Sensibility","Pride and Prejudice"],"a":[1],"e":"In 2003, The Lord of the Rings by JRR Tolkien was voted the country’s best-loved novel."},{"q":"The Senedd has 129 Senedd members.","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"The Senned has 60 Senedd members and the Scottish Parliament has 129 members."},{"q":"Which British scientist made important discoveries working with steam power?","o":["David Hume","Adan Smith","Alan Turing","James Watt"],"a":[3],"e":"James Watt ’s work on steam power, helped the progress of the Industrial Revolution."},{"q":"How can you visit the Northern Ireland Assembly (choose TWO options)?","o":["Contacting the Education Service","Contacting an MSP","Contacting an MLA","Arranging a tour through the visitor services"],"a":[0,2],"e":"There are two ways to arrange a visit to the Northern Ireland Assembly at Stormont. You can either contact the Education Service (details are on the Northern Ireland Assembly website at http://www.niassembly.gov.uk) or contact an MLA."},{"q":"When was the first television broadcast made?","o":["In 1922","In 1932","In 1942","In 1952"],"a":[1],"e":"In 1932 Scotsman John Logie Baird made the first television broadcast between London and Glasgow."},{"q":"For what sort of claims is the small claims procedure in Northern Ireland used for?","o":["Claims of less than £3,000","Claims of less than £4,000","Claims of less than £5,000","Claims of less than £6,000"],"a":[0],"e":"The small claims procedure is used for claims of less than £3,000 in Northern Ireland."},{"q":"How many Russian and Polish Jews came to Britain to escape prosecution between 1870 and 1914?","o":["Around 120,000","Around 150,000","Around 200","Around 400"],"a":[0],"e":"Between 1870 and 1914, around 120,000 Russian and Polish Jews came to Britain to escape persecution."},{"q":"Which Anglo-Saxon poem is about a hero’s battle against monsters and has been translated into modern English?","o":["Beowulf","Paradise Lost","Canterbury Tales","Braveheart"],"a":[0],"e":"The Anglo-Saxon poem Beowulf tells of its hero’s battles against monsters and is still translated into modern English."},{"q":"Which of the following movies was directed by Alfred Hitchcock in 1935?","o":["Don’t Look Now","Touching the Void","The Third Man","The 39 Steps"],"a":[3],"e":"The 39 Steps (1935) was directed by Alfred Hitchcock."},{"q":"When was the National Health System (NHS) established?","o":["1945","1948","1952","1934"],"a":[1],"e":"In 1948, Aneurin (Nye) Bevan, the Minister for Health, led the establishment of the National Health Service (NHS), which guaranteed a minimum standard of health care for all, free at the point of use."},{"q":"The King has reigned since her mother’s death in 2022.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"What is the note with the highest value in the UK?","o":["£20","£50","£200","£500"],"a":[1],"e":"The note with the highest value in the UK is the £50 note."}]},{"id":"test-16","name":"Test 16","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"Who was Prime Minister of Britain from 1721 to 1742?","o":["Sir Robert Walpole","Admiral Nelson","Henry Pelham","Charles Edward Stuart"],"a":[0],"e":"Sir Robert Walpole was Prime Minister from 1721 to 1742."},{"q":"What is celebrated during Diwali?","o":["Jesus Christ rising from the dead","The victory of good over evil and the gaining of knowledge","The founding of the Sikh community known as the Khalsa","The beginning of the winter season"],"a":[1],"e":"Diwalli celebrates the victory of good over evil and the gaining of knowledge."},{"q":"Who chairs the debates in the House of Commons?","o":["The shadow cabinet","The Speaker","The Prime Minister","The leader of the opposition"],"a":[1],"e":"Debates in the House of Commons are chaired by the Speaker."},{"q":"The Battle of Hastings is commemorated in a great piece of embroidery, which is known as:","o":["The Hastings tapestry","The Bayeux tapestry","The Normand tapestry","The Domesday tapestry"],"a":[1],"e":"The Battle of Hastings is commemorated in a great piece of embroidery, known as the Bayeux tapestry, which can still be seen in France today."},{"q":"A woman who is violent towards her partner cannot be prosecuted in the UK:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. Anyone who is violent towards their partner – whether they are a man or a woman, married or living together – can be prosecuted."},{"q":"According to the 2011 census, what percentage of the population identified themselves as Christian?","o":["35%","55%","70%","59%"],"a":[3],"e":"According to the 2011 Census, 59% of people identified themselves as Christians."},{"q":"Many MPs, SMs and MSPs hold regular local events where constituents can go in person to talk about issues that are of concern to them. These are known as:","o":["Hansard","Local ‘surgeries’","Local ‘meetings’","Constituents ‘reunions’"],"a":[1],"e":"Many MPs, SMs and MSPs hold regular local ‘surgeries’, where constituents can go in person to talk about issues that are of concern to them. These surgeries are often advertised in the local newspaper."},{"q":"Which golf ‘Major’ tournament is hosted by a different golf course every year?","o":["The Open Championship","The PGA Championship","The Masters Tournament","The U.S. Open"],"a":[0],"e":"The open championship is the only ‘Major’ tournament held outside United States. It is hosted by a different golf course every year."},{"q":"Which act introduced the Forced Marriage Protection Orders for England, Wales and Northern Ireland in 2008?","o":["The Forced Marriage (Civil Protection) Act 2007","The Free Marriage (Civil Protection) Act 2007","The Forced Marriage (Civil Protection) Act 2011","The Free Marriage (Civil Protection) Act 2011"],"a":[0],"e":"Forced Marriage Protection Orders were introduced in 2008 for England, Wales and Northern Ireland under the Forced Marriage (Civil Protection) Act 2007. Similar Protection Orders were introduced in Scotland in November 2011."},{"q":"Which of the following countries did NOT join the European Economic Community when it was first formed in 1957?","o":["Luxembourg","West Germany","The UK","The Netherlands"],"a":[2],"e":"West Germany, France, Belgium, Italy, Luxembourg and the Netherlands formed the European Economic Community (EEC) in 1957. At first the UK did not wish to join the EEC but it eventually did so in 1973."},{"q":"How many members does the Northern Ireland assembly have?","o":["90","80","118","108"],"a":[0],"e":"The Northern Ireland Assembly has 90 elected members, known as MLAs (members of the Legislative Assembly). They are elected with a form of proportional representation."},{"q":"Which of the following civil laws include disputes between landlords and tenants over issues such as repairs and eviction?","o":["Consumer law","Housing law","Debt law","Living law"],"a":[1],"e":"Housing law includes disputes between landlords and tenants over issues suck as repairs and eviction."},{"q":"How can you visit the Scottish Parliament?","o":["Arranging a tour through the visitor services","Contacting the Education Service","Contacting an AM","Contacting an MP"],"a":[0],"e":"To visit the Scottish Parliament, you can get information, book tickets or arrange tours through visitor services. You can also write to them at the Scottish Parliament, Edinburgh, EH99 1SP, telephone 0131 348 5200 or email sp.booking@scottish.parliament."},{"q":"Which of the following gardens is located in Wales?","o":["Bodnant Garden","Kew Gardens","Hidcote","Sissinghurst"],"a":[0],"e":"There are famous gardens to visit throughout the UK, including Kew Gardens, Sissinghurst and Hidcote in England, Crathes Castle and Inveraray Castle in Scotland, Bodnant Garden in Wales, and Mount Stewart in Northern Ireland."},{"q":"Who was reining in Britain when the British Empire became the largest empire the world has ever seen, with an estimated population of more than 400 million people?","o":["Elizabeth I","Queen Anne","Queen Victoria","Henry VIII"],"a":[2],"e":"During the Victorian period, the British Empire grew to cover all of India, Australia and large parts of Africa. It became the largest empire the world has ever seen, with an estimated population of more than 400 million people."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a duty of the police?","o":["Protect life and property","Protect the environment","Prevent disturbances","Prevent and detect crimes"],"a":[1],"e":"The job of the police in the UK is to protect life and property, prevent disturbances (also known as keeping the peace) and prevent and detect crime."},{"q":"The UK is not part of which of the following international institutions?","o":["Council of Europe","North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO)","United Nations","European Union"],"a":[3],"e":"The UK is a member of the Council of Europe, the United Nations and the North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO). The UK is no longer a member of the European Union."},{"q":"Where is the National Gallery of Scotland located?","o":["In Edinburgh","In Glasgow","In Aberdeen","In Fife"],"a":[0],"e":"The National Gallery of Scotland is in Edinburgh."},{"q":"Where are the ancestors of the first farmers to arrive in Britain believed to come from?","o":["North-east Europe","North-west Europe","South-east Europe","South-west Europe"],"a":[2],"e":"The first farmers arrived in Britain 6,000 years ago. The ancestors of these first farmers probably came from south-east Europe."},{"q":"What religion did Elizabeth I follow?","o":["Catholic","Protestant","Hindu","Buddhist"],"a":[1],"e":"Queen Elizabeth I was a Protestant."},{"q":"Who directed the movie ‘Chariots of Fire’ in 1981?","o":["Mike Newell","Hugh Hudson","Kevin MacDonald","Nicolas Roeg"],"a":[1],"e":"Chariots of Fire (1981) was directed by Hugh Hudson."},{"q":"What do new citizens have to swear or affirm as part of the citizenship ceremony?","o":["They will remain in the UK","Loyalty to the King","Loyalty to the Pope","They will go to church at least once a week"],"a":[1],"e":"New citizens swear or affirm loyalty to the King as part of the citizenship ceremony."},{"q":"In which TWO cases may a person who has been summoned to do jury service be exempted from doing it:","o":["If they have a criminal conviction","If they have children","If they provide a good reason to be excused, such as ill health","If they have to go to work"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Everyone who is summoned to do jury service must do it unless they are not eligible (for example, because they have a criminal conviction) or they provide a good reason to be excused, such as ill health."},{"q":"What is the role of the shadow cabinet?","o":["To keep order during political debates to make sure the rules are followed","To challenge the government and put forward alternative policies","To point out what they see as the government’s failures and weaknesses","To control important public appointments"],"a":[1],"e":"Incorrect The role of the shadow cabinet is to challenge the government and put forward alternative policies."}]},{"id":"test-17","name":"Test 17","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"Which hill fort from the Iron Age can be seen in the county of Dorset?","o":["Conwy Castle","Caernarfon Castle","Edinburgh Castle","Maiden Castle"],"a":[3],"e":"A very impressive hill fort from the Iron Age can still be seen today at Maiden Castle, in the English county of Dorset."},{"q":"On which of the following issues the Northern Ireland Assembly CANNOT make decisions?","o":["Health","Education","The Environment","Planning"],"a":[3],"e":"The Northern Ireland Assembly can make decisions on issues such as education, agriculture, the environment, health and social services."},{"q":"Who was the first person to sail single-handed around the world in 1966/67?","o":["Sir Robin Knox-Johnston","Sir Francis Chichester","Sir Francis Drake","Dame Ellen MacArthur"],"a":[1],"e":"A British sailor, Sir Francis Chichester, was the first person to sail singlehanded around the world, in 1966/67."},{"q":"In 1837, Queen Victoria became queen of the UK at the age of 20.","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"In 1837, Queen Victoria became queen of the UK at the age of 18."},{"q":"Who led the group of Catholics who tried to kill the Protestant king with a bomb in the Houses of the Parliament in 1605?","o":["Oliver Cromwell","Alexander II","Guy Fawkes","Adrian IV"],"a":[2],"e":"In 1605, a group of Catholics led by Guy Fawkes failed in their plan to kill the Protestant king with a bomb in the Houses of Parliament. This is the origin of the Bonfire Night celebration on the 5th November, when people in Great Britain set off fireworks at home or in special displays."},{"q":"What was the biggest source of employment in Britain before the 18th century?","o":["The ship industry","Agriculture","Teaching","Manufacturing jobs"],"a":[1],"e":"Before the 18th century, agriculture was the biggest source of employment in Britain."},{"q":"In which city is the Scottish Parliament building, also known as Holyrood, located?","o":["Edinburgh","Belfast","Aberdeen","Glasgow"],"a":[0],"e":"In Scotland the elected members, called MSPs, meet in the Scottish Parliament building at Holyrood in Edinburgh."},{"q":"The UK continues to be a world leader in the development and manufacture of motor-sport technology:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"There is a long history of motor sport in the UK, for both cars and motor cycles. Motor-car racing in the UK started in 1902. The UK continues to be a world leader in the development and manufacture of motor-sport technology."},{"q":"Why was the ‘Habeas Corpus Act’ of 1679 an important piece of legislation?","o":["It allowed people over 21 years old to vote","It guaranteed that no one could be held prisoner unlawfully","It abolished slavery throughout the British Empire","It abolished a number of taxes on imported goods in the UK"],"a":[1],"e":"The Habeas Corpus Act became law in 1679. This was a very important piece of legislation which remains relevant today. The Act guaranteed that no one could be held prisoner unlawfully. Every prisoner has a right to a court hearing."},{"q":"What British artist was awarded the Turner Prize?","o":["David Allan","Damien Hirst","Henry Moore","John Petts"],"a":[1],"e":"Damien Hirst."},{"q":"How many members has the Council of Europe?","o":["15","47","190","5"],"a":[1],"e":"The Council of Europe is separated from the EU. It has 47 member countries, including the UK, and is responsible for the protection and promotion of human rights in those countries."},{"q":"In the UK, Members of the Parliament (MPs) are elected on the basis of:","o":["Personal achievements","Instant run-off","First past the post system (the candidate who gets the most votes)","Proportional representation"],"a":[2],"e":"MPs are elected through a system called ‘first past the post’. In constituency, the candidate who gets the most votes is elected."},{"q":"Which of the following statements regarding television in Northern Ireland is TRUE?","o":["Some programmes are broadcasted in Irish Gaelic","There is one Irish-language channel","All programmes are broadcasted in English","There are no specific programmes to Northern Ireland"],"a":[0],"e":"There are programmes specific to Northern Ireland and some programmes broadcast in Irish Gaelic."},{"q":"In the second half of the 19th century there was an important group of artists who painted detailed pictures on religious or literary themes in bright colours. These were known as:","o":["The Pre-Raphaelites","Suffragettes","Chartists","Elizabethans"],"a":[0],"e":"The Pre-Raphaelites were an important group of artists in the second half of the 19th century. They painted detailed pictures on religious or literary themes in bright colours. The group included Holman Hunt, Dante Gabriel Rossetti and Sir John Millais."},{"q":"The Muslim festival known as Eid ul Adha reminds Muslims of their own commitment to God:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"Eid ul Adha reminds Muslims of their own commitment to God."},{"q":"What was the population of the UK in 1901?","o":["20 million","40 million","30 million","50 million"],"a":[1],"e":"The population of the UK in 1901 was 40 million people."},{"q":"People in the UK do NOT have to pay tax on:","o":["Income from expensive gifts","Income from savings","Income from property","Pensions"],"a":[0],"e":"People in the UK have to pay tax on their income, which includes: wages from paid employment, profits from self-employment, taxable benefits, pensions and income from property, savings and dividends."},{"q":"Which British scientist co-invented the MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) scanner?","o":["Sir Peter Mansfield","Sir Robert Edwards","Patrick Steptoe","Sir Tim Berners-Lee"],"a":[0],"e":"Sir Peter Mansfield (1933-), a British scientist, is the co-inventor of the MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) scanner. This enables doctors and researchers to obtain exact and non-invasive images of human internal organs and has revolutionised diagnostic medicine."},{"q":"Which political party called a referendum on the UK’s membership of the European Union?","o":["The Conservative party","The Labour party","The Liberal Democrats party","The Scottish National party"],"a":[0],"e":"The Conservative government called a referendum on the UK’s membership of the European Union."},{"q":"Which of the following is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and a popular area for walkers?","o":["The Giants Causeway","Hadrian’s Wall","The house of the parliament","Buckingham Palace"],"a":[1],"e":"Parts of Hadrian’s Wall, including the forts of Housesteads and Vindolanda is a popular area for walkers and is a UNESCO (United Nations Educational Scientific and Cultural Organization) World Heritage Site."},{"q":"When did hereditary peers lose the automatic right to attend the House of Lords?","o":["1979","1989","1999","2001"],"a":[2],"e":"Since 1999, hereditary peers have lost the automatic right to attend the House of Lords. They now elect a few of their number to represent them in the House of Lords."},{"q":"Which charity works for the preservation of buildings in England?","o":["Friends of the Earth","Oxfam","The National Trust","The RSPB"],"a":[2],"e":"In the UK, many parts of the countryside and places of interest are kept open by the National Trust in England, Wales and Northern Ireland and the National Trust for Scotland. Both are charities that work to preserve important buildings, coastline and countryside in the UK."},{"q":"What name is given to the new social classes that appeared after the Black Death plague and who owned large areas of land?","o":["Gentry","Bayeux","Canute","Grimsby"],"a":[0],"e":"Following the Black Death, the smaller population meant there was less need to grow cereal crops. There were labour shortages and peasants began to demand higher wages. New social classes appeared, including owners of large areas of land (later called the gentry ), and people left the countryside to live in the towns. In the towns, growing wealth led to the development of a strong middle class."},{"q":"Which of the following is a traditional Welsh food?","o":["Roast beef","Welsh cakes","Haggis","Ulster fry"],"a":[1],"e":"Welsh cakes are a traditional Welsh snack made from flour, dried fruits and spices, and served either hot or cold."}]},{"id":"test-18","name":"Test 18","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"Which of the following was one of the most famous battles of the Hundred Years War?","o":["The Battle of Agincourt","The Battle of Bannockburn","The Battle of Culloden","The Battle of Waterloo"],"a":[0],"e":"One of the most famous battles of the Hundred Years War was the Battle of Agincourt in 1415, where King Henry V’s vastly outnumbered English army defeated the French. The English left France in the 1450s."},{"q":"During the Middle Ages, England was an important trading nation and people came to England from abroad to trade and also to work. Where did the engineers come from?","o":["France","Holland","Germany","Italy"],"a":[2],"e":"During the Middle Ages, people came to England from abroad to trade and also to work. Many had special skills, such as weavers from France, engineers from Germany, glass manufacturers from Italy and canal builders from Holland."},{"q":"Who was the captain of the English football team that won the World Cup in 1966?","o":["David Beckham","Bobby Moore","Sir Ian Botham","Sir Roger Bannister"],"a":[1],"e":"Bobby Moore (1941-93) captained the English football team that won the World Cup in 1966."},{"q":"Membership to the Commonwealth is compulsory for its members:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"Membership to the Commonwealth is voluntary."},{"q":"How many verdicts are possible in trials in Scotland?","o":["Two: ‘guilty’ or ‘not guilty’","Three: ‘guilty’, ‘not guilty’ or ‘not proven’","Three: ‘guilty’, ‘not guilty’ or ‘on hold’","Two: ‘guilty’ or ‘not proven’"],"a":[1],"e":"The jury has to listen to the evidence presented at the trial and then decide a verdict of ‘guilty’ or ‘not guilty’ based of what they have heard. In Scotland, a third verdict of ‘not proven’ is also possible."},{"q":"How many wives did Henry VIII have?","o":["Five","Six","Seven","Eight"],"a":[1],"e":"Henry VIII had six wives."},{"q":"Which of the following charities works with sick animals?","o":["PDSA","NSPCC","Crisis","Age UK"],"a":[0],"e":"The PDSA is the People’s Dispensary for Sick Animals."},{"q":"Which of the following is the responsibility of the Home Secretary?","o":["Crime, policing and immigration","Managing relationships with foreign countries","Education and health","National and international defence"],"a":[0],"e":"The Home Secretary is responsible for crime, policing and immigration."},{"q":"Which of the following sports can be traced as far back as the Roman times?","o":["Tennis","Football","Horse racing","Pool"],"a":[2],"e":"There is a very long history of horse racing in Britain, with evidence of events taking place as far back as Roman times."},{"q":"When did the English become the preferred language of the royal court and Parliament?","o":["In 1300","In 1400","In 1345","In 1567"],"a":[1],"e":"By 1400, in England, official documents were being written in English, and English had become the preferred language of the royal court and Parliament."},{"q":"When is St Andrews Day, patron saint of Scotland?","o":["23rd of April","1st of March","17th of March","30th of November"],"a":[3],"e":"St Andrews Day, patron saint of Scotland, is celebrated on the 30th of November."},{"q":"Which organisation has been organising ‘The Proms’ since 1927?","o":["The MTV","Channel 4","The BBC","CNTV"],"a":[2],"e":"The Proms is an eight-week summer season of orchestral classical music that takes place in various venues, including the Royal Albert Hall in London. It has been in various venues, including the Royal Albert Hall in London. It has been organised by the British Broadcasting Corporation (BBC) since 1927."},{"q":"Which of the following organisations has no power to make laws but draws up conventions and charters, including the European Convention on Human Rights and Fundamental Freedoms?","o":["The UN Security Council","The Council of Europe","The United Nations","The European Union"],"a":[1],"e":"The Council of Europe is responsible for the protection and promotion of human rights in those countries. It has no power to make laws but draws up conventions and charters, the most well-known of which is the European Convention on Human Rights and Fundamental Freedoms, usually called the European Convention on Human Rights."},{"q":"Where is Snowdonia National Park located?","o":["North Wales","Northern Ireland","South Wales","England"],"a":[0],"e":"Snowdonia is a national park in North Wales."},{"q":"What actions did Henry VII take after his victory in the Wars of the Roses?","o":["Reduced the power of the nobles","Broke away with the Church of Rome","Converted Britain to the Christianism","Increased the power of the nobles"],"a":[0],"e":"After his victory in the Wars of the Roses, Henry VII wanted to make sure that England remained peaceful and that his position as king was secure. He deliberately strengthened the central administration of England and reduced the power of the nobles."},{"q":"Which Court deals with the most serious civil cases in Scotland?","o":["The High Court","The Sheriff Court","The Court of Session","The Crown Court"],"a":[2],"e":"More serious civil cases – for example, when a large amount of compensation is being claimed – are dealt with in the High Court of England, Wales and Northern Ireland. In Scotland, they are dealt with in the Court of Session in Edinburgh."},{"q":"The leader of the opposition leads his or her party in pointing out what they see as the government’s (choose TWO options):","o":["Failures","Achievements","Strengths","Weaknesses"],"a":[0,3],"e":"The leader of the opposition leads his or her party in pointing out what they see as the government’s failures and weaknesses."},{"q":"Who became Prime Minister of the UK in May 2010?","o":["David Cameron","Gordon Brown","Tony Blair","Ed Miliband"],"a":[0],"e":"In May 2010, and for the first time in the UK since February 1974, no political party won an overall majority in the General Election. The Conservative and Liberal Democrat parties formed a coalition and the leader of the Conservative Party, David Cameron, became Prime Minister."},{"q":"The Reform Act of 1832 abolished the old pocket and rotten boroughs.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"What is the minimum age requirement to drive a moped in the UK?","o":["16","18","20","21"],"a":[0],"e":"You need to be at least 16 years old to ride a moped, and there are other age requirements and special tests for driving large vehicles."},{"q":"Which of the following statements is true?","o":["Great Britain refers only to England, Wales, Northern Ireland, not to Scotland.","Great Britain refers only to England, Northern Ireland and Scotland, not to Wales.","Great Britain refers to England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland.","Great Britain refers only to England, Scotland and Wales, not to Northern Ireland."],"a":[3],"e":"Great Britain refers only to England, Scotland and Wales, not to Northern Ireland."},{"q":"How many countries formed the European Economic Community (EEC) when it was first created in 1957?","o":["Five","Six","Seven","Nine"],"a":[1],"e":"The European Economic Community (EEC), was set up by six western European countries (Belgium, France, Germany, Italy, Luxembourg and the Netherlands) who signed the Treaty of Rome on 25 March 1957."},{"q":"Northern Ireland and Wales have their own banknotes, which are valid everywhere in the UK. However, shop and businesses do not have to accept them:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"Northern Ireland and Scotland have their own banknotes, which are valid everywhere in the UK. However, shops and businesses do not have to accept them."},{"q":"What is donated blood used for?","o":["To help people with a wide range of injuries and illnesses","Blood cannot be donated in the UK","To carry out research with your DNA","To be stored in the hospital in case you need it in the future"],"a":[0],"e":"Donated blood is used by hospitals to help people with a wide range of injuries and illnesses."}]},{"id":"test-19","name":"Test 19","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"How often are the general elections held in the UK?","o":["Every year","Every 2 years","Every 4 years","Every 5 years"],"a":[3],"e":"MPs are elected at a General Election, which is held at least every five years."},{"q":"Where is the Grand National horse racing event celebrated?","o":["In London","In Aintree","In Bedford","In Slough"],"a":[1],"e":"The Grand National horse racing event is celebrated in Aintree near Liverpool."},{"q":"What was Charles Chaplin famous for?","o":["Special effects movies","Horror movies","Silent movies","Murder-mystery movies"],"a":[2],"e":"Sir Charles (Charlie) Chaplin became famous in silent movies for his tramp character and was one of many British actors to make a career in Hollywood."},{"q":"What was Boudicca, the queen of the Iceni in what is now eastern England, known for?","o":["Her rebellion against the Vikings","Her rebellion against the Romans","Her rebellion against the Normans","Her rebellion against the Anglo-Saxons"],"a":[1],"e":"One of the tribal leaders who fought against the Romans was Boudicca, the queen of the Iceni in what is now eastern England."},{"q":"What percentage of the British population has a parent or grandparent born outside of the UK as a result of post-war immigration?","o":["Nearly 5% of the population","Nearly 10% of the population","Nearly 15% of the population","Nearly 25% of the population"],"a":[1],"e":"Post-war immigration means that nearly 10% of the population has a parent or grandparent born outside the UK."},{"q":"When was the hovercraft invented?","o":["In the 1920s","In the 1930s","In the 1940s","In the 1950s"],"a":[3],"e":"Sir Christopher Cockerell (1910-99), a British inventor, invented the hovercraft in the 1950s."},{"q":"Who is the author of the famous play ‘Macbeth’?","o":["Alexander Thomas","William Shakespeare","Harold Pinter","Evelyn Waugh"],"a":[1],"e":"Macbeth is a famous play by William Shakespeare."},{"q":"What animal was cloned by scientists Sir Ian Wilmot and Keith Campbell?","o":["A cow","A rabbit","A cat","A sheep"],"a":[3],"e":"In 1996, two British scientists, Sir Ian Wilmot (1944-) and Keith Campbell (1954-2012), led a team which was the first to succeed in cloning a mammal, Dolly the sheep."},{"q":"What is the name of the building where the Northern Ireland Assembly meet?","o":["Westminster","Holyrood","Senedd","Stormont"],"a":[3],"e":"The Northern Ireland Assembly building is known as Stormont."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a function of the House of Lords?","o":["Suggest amendments to existing laws","Check laws that have been passed by the House of Commons","Propose new laws","Cancel existing laws"],"a":[3],"e":"The House of Lords can suggest amendments or propose new laws, which are then discussed by MPs. The House of Lords checks laws that have been passed by the House of Commons to ensure they are fit for purpose. It also holds the government to account to make sure that it is working in the best interests of the people."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a World Heritage Site?","o":["The London Eye","Stonehenge","The forts of Housesteads and Vindolanda","The Houses of Parliament"],"a":[0],"e":"The London Eye is not a World Heritage Site."},{"q":"Which famous murder-mystery play has been running in the west end since 1952 and has had the longest initial run of any show in history?","o":["The Phantom of the Opera","The Mousetrap","Evita","Jesus Christ Superstar"],"a":[1],"e":"The Mousetrap, a murder-mystery play by Dame Agatha Christie, has been running in the west end since 1952 and has had the longest initial run of any show in history."},{"q":"When was the Giant’s Causeway formed?","o":["20 million years ago","40 million years ago","50 million years ago","15 million years ago"],"a":[2],"e":"The Giant’s Causeway was formed about 50 million years ago."},{"q":"Who was the first person to sail single-handed around the world without stopping?","o":["Sir Francis Drake","Dame Ellen MacArthur","Sir Robin Knox-Johnston","Sir Francis Chichester"],"a":[2],"e":"A British sailor, Sir Francis Chichester, was the first person to sail singlehanded around the world, in 1966/67. Two years later, Sir Robin Knox- Johnston became the first person to do this without stopping."},{"q":"What British writer created the fictional detective Sherlock Holmes?","o":["Sir Kingsley Amis","Robert Louis Stevenson","Sir Arthur Conan Doyle","Jane Austen"],"a":[2],"e":"Sir Arthur Conan Doyle was a Scottish doctor and writer. He was best known for his stories about Sherlock Holmes, who was one of the first fictional detectives."},{"q":"How can you visit the Senedd?","o":["Contacting the Education Service","Contacting the Senedd Booking Service","Contacting an MP","Contacting an MLA"],"a":[1],"e":"To book guided tours or seats in the public galleries for the Senedd you can contact the Senedd Booking Service on 0300 200 6565, email contact@senedd.wales or visit www.senedd.wales for an online booking form."},{"q":"How were Elizabeth I and ‘Mary, Queen of Scots’ related?","o":["They were cousins","They were sisters","They were mother and daughter","They were not related"],"a":[0],"e":"‘Mary, Queen of Scots’ was Elizabeth I’s cousin."},{"q":"Choose the correct answer. The Middle Ages were:","o":["A period of peace in Britain","A period where liberal ideas were introduced in Britain","A period of constant war","A period where new ideas about politics and philosophy were introduced in Britain"],"a":[2],"e":"The Middle Ages (or the medieval period) was a time of almost constant war."},{"q":"What does the NATO stand for?","o":["The North American Transparent Organisation","The North Atlantic Trust Organisation","The North Atlantic Treaty Organisation","The North American Treaty Organisation"],"a":[2],"e":"NATO stands for The North Atlantic Treaty Organisation."},{"q":"What characterised the people from the Bronze Age?","o":["They were accomplished metalworkers","They were farmers who lived in large villages","They were nomads and hunters","They were divided into clans"],"a":[0],"e":"The people of the Bronze Age were accomplished metalworkers who made many beautiful objects in bronze and gold, including tools, ornaments and weapons."},{"q":"Where is the National Horseracing Museum located?","o":["In Newmarket, Suffolk","In St Andrews, Scotland","In Richmond, London","In Aintree, near Liverpool"],"a":[0],"e":"The National Horseracing Museum is located in Newmarket, Suffolk."},{"q":"In which of the following matters the Scottish Parliament CANNOT legislate?","o":["Health","Education","Immigration","Planning"],"a":[2],"e":"The matters on which the Scottish Parliament can legislate include: civil and criminal law, health, education, planning and additional tax-raising powers."},{"q":"Which of the following is a popular showcase of mainly theatre and comedy performances and the biggest and most well-known festival that takes place during the Edinburgh Festival?","o":["The Proms","The Fringe","The Festival of Lights","The Aldeburgh Festival"],"a":[1],"e":"The Fringe is a showcase of mainly theatre and comedy performances. It often shows experimental work.The biggest and most well-known being the Edinburgh Festival Fringe."},{"q":"Does Britain have a constitutional monarchy?","o":["Yes","No"],"a":[0],"e":"The UK has a constitutional monarchy."}]},{"id":"test-20","name":"Test 20","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"Who defeated the French at the battle of Agincourt in 1415?","o":["King Edward I of England","William III of England","Henry VIII","King Henry V"],"a":[3],"e":"One of the most famous battles of the Hundred Years War was the Battle of Agincourt in 1415, where King Henry V ’s vastly outnumbered English army defeated the French."},{"q":"Which is the most famous tennis tournament hosted in Britain and which takes place each year at the All England Lawn Tennis and Croquet Club?","o":["Roland Garros","The Wimbledon Championships","The British open","The Queen’s open"],"a":[1],"e":"The most famous tournament hosted in Britain is The Wimbledon Championships, which takes place each year at the All England Lawn Tennis and Croquet Club."},{"q":"What is the minimum age required for a citizen to stand for public office?","o":["16","18","21","25"],"a":[1],"e":"Most citizens of the UK, the Irish republic or the Commonwealth aged 18 or over can stand for public office."},{"q":"Who makes sure that the opposition has a guaranteed amount of time to debate issues which it chooses during political debates?","o":["The shadow cabinet","The Speaker","The King","Nobody"],"a":[1],"e":"The Speaker keeps order during political debates to make sure the rules are followed. This includes making sure the opposition has a guaranteed amount of time to debate issues which it chooses."},{"q":"Citizens of Liechtenstein can drive in the UK for as long as their driving license is valid:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"If your driving licence if from a country in the European Union (EU), Iceland, Liechtenstein or Norway, you can drive in the UK for as long as your licence is valid."},{"q":"Which British Prime Minister nationalized major industries including coal and steel?","o":["Harold Macmillan","Clement Attlee","John Major","Sir Alec Douglas-Home"],"a":[1],"e":"Clement Attlee ’s government undertook the nationalisation of major industries (like coal and steel), created the National Health Service and implemented many of Beveridge’s plans for a stronger welfare state. Attlee also introduced measures to improve the conditions of workers."},{"q":"Who was the first Archbishop of Canterbury?","o":["St Augustine","St Columba","St Andrew","Kenneth MacAlpin"],"a":[0],"e":"St Augustine became the first Archbishop of Canterbury."},{"q":"What is the money raised from the Income Tax used for (choose THREE options)?","o":["Education","The National Grid system","Police and the Armed Forces","Roads"],"a":[0,2,3],"e":"Money raised from income tax pays for government services such as roads, education, police and the armed forces."},{"q":"What name is given to the clock tower at the Houses of Parliament in honour of Queen Elizabeth II’s Diamond Jubilee in 2012?","o":["Big Ben","Big Clock","Elizabeth Tower","Clock Tower"],"a":[2],"e":"The clock tower at the Houses of Parliament in London is named ‘Elizabeth Tower’ in honour of Queen Elizabeth II’s Diamond Jubilee in 2012."},{"q":"When were the Forced Marriage Protection Orders introduced for England, Wales and Northern Ireland, which aimed at protecting a person from being forced into a marriage, or to protect a person in a forced marriage?","o":["2007","2008","2010","2011"],"a":[1],"e":"Forced Marriage Protection Orders were introduced in 2008 for England, Wales and Northern Ireland under the Forced Marriage (Civil Protection) Act 2007."},{"q":"Whose details are NOT listed in the Phone Book?","o":["The King","MPs, SMs and MSPs","MEPs","All of the above"],"a":[0],"e":"MPs, Senedd members (SMs) and members of the Scottish Parliament (MSPs) are also listed in The Phone Book, published by BT. The King is not."},{"q":"Which of the following words comes from the Norman French?","o":["Apple","Beauty","Cow","Summer"],"a":[1],"e":"Some words in modern English – for example, ‘park’ and ‘beauty’ – are based on Norman French words."},{"q":"How are the official reports where the Parliament’s proceedings are published called?","o":["Domesday Book","Hansard","Parliamentary reports","Carta Magna"],"a":[1],"e":"Proceedings in Parliament are broadcast on television and published in official reports called Hansard."},{"q":"How many Senedd members does the Welsh government have?","o":["40","50","60","75"],"a":[2],"e":"The Welsh government has 60 Senedd members (SMs) and elections are held every four years using a form of proportional representation."},{"q":"Catherine Parr, the sixth wife of Henry VIII, was a cousin of Anne Boleyn:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"Catherine Howard was a cousin of Anne Boleyn."},{"q":"Who deals with cases involving personal injury, family matters, breaches of contract and divorce in Scotland?","o":["County Courts","The Sheriff Court","The Crown Court","The High Court"],"a":[1],"e":"County Courts deal with a wide range of civil disputes. These include people trying to get back money that is owed to them, cases involving personal injury, family matters, breaches of contract, and divorce. In Scotland, most of these matters are dealt with in the Sheriff Court."},{"q":"What was the symbol of York during the Wars of the Roses?","o":["A Red Rose","A White Rose","A Black Rose","A Purple Rose"],"a":[1],"e":"The symbol of York during the Wars of the Roses was a white rose."},{"q":"According to the 2011 Census, what percentage of the population identified themselves as Jewish?","o":["Less than 0.5%","Less than 1%","Less than 1.5%","Less than 2%"],"a":[0],"e":"According to the 2011 Census, less than 0.5% of people identify themselves as Jewish."},{"q":"During which movement the Parliament passed laws giving women the right to equal pay and made it illegal for employees to discriminate against women because of their gender:","o":["The Punk movement","The Enlightenment","The Swinging Sixties","The Suffrage"],"a":[2],"e":"The Swinging Sixties was a time when the Parliament passed new laws giving women the right to equal pay and made it illegal for employers to discriminate against women because of their gender."},{"q":"What name is given to the members of the House of Lords?","o":["Cabinet","Cavaliers","Peers","Housesteads"],"a":[2],"e":"Members of the House of Lords, known as peers, are not elected by the people and do not represent a constituency."},{"q":"Where can you find a statue of Boudicca, the queen of the Iceni and one of the tribal leaders who fought against the Romans?","o":["At London Bridge","At Westminster Bridge","At the Tower of London","At St James’s Park"],"a":[1],"e":"There is a statue of Boudicca, the queen of the Iceni, on Westminster Bridge in London, near the Houses of Parliament."},{"q":"Which of the following castles is located in Scotland?","o":["Conwy Castle","Caernarfon Castle","Crathes Castle","Windsor Castle"],"a":[2],"e":"Crathes Castle and Inveraray Castle are located in Scotland."},{"q":"For how long was Britain a republic under the leadership of Oliver Cromwell?","o":["9 years","10 years","11 years","12 years"],"a":[2],"e":"Britain was a republic under the leadership of Oliver Cromwell for 11 years."},{"q":"Magistrates and Justices of the Peace (JPs) are members of:","o":["The House of Commons","The Cabinet","The local community","The Parliament"],"a":[2],"e":"Magistrates and Justices of the Peace (JPs) are members of the local community."}]},{"id":"test-21","name":"Test 21","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"Which British explorer mapped the coast of Australia?","o":["Sake Dean Mahomet","Richard Arkwright","Captain James Cook","Admiral Nelson"],"a":[2],"e":"Captain James Cook mapped the coast of Australia and a few colonies were established there."},{"q":"How many members does a jury have in Scotland?","o":["15","10","14","16"],"a":[0],"e":"In England, Wales and Northern Ireland a jury has 12 members, and in Scotland a jury has 15 members."},{"q":"Who can get a 50% discount on their TV licences?","o":["Blind people","People over 65","People over 75","People on benefits"],"a":[0],"e":"People over 75 can apply for a free TV licence and blind people can get a 50% discount."},{"q":"Which British city is famous for its celebration of Diwali?","o":["London","Newcastle upon Tyne","Leicester","Manchester"],"a":[2],"e":"There is a famous celebration of Diwali in Leicester."},{"q":"Which of the following British actresses has NOT won an Oscar?","o":["Tilda Swinton","Dame Judi Dench","Emily Watson","Kate Winslet"],"a":[2],"e":"Recent British actors to have won Oscars include Colin Firth, Sir Antony Hopkins, Dame Judi Dench, Kate Winslet and Tilda Swinton. Emily Watson has not won an oscar."},{"q":"Which of the following sports has a long association with royalty?","o":["Rugby","Horse racing","Formula 1","Sailing"],"a":[1],"e":"Horse racing has a long association with royalty."},{"q":"Hanukkah is a Jewish festival celebrated to remember the Jews’ struggle for religious freedom.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"Hanukkah is in November or December and is celebrated for eight days. It is to remember the Jews’ struggle for religious freedom."},{"q":"How can you visit the Northern Ireland Assembly (choose TWO options)?","o":["Contacting the Education Service","Contacting an MSP","Contacting an MLA","Arranging a tour through the visitor services"],"a":[0,2],"e":"There are two ways to arrange a visit to the Northern Ireland Assembly at Stormont. You can either contact the Education Service (details are on the Northern Ireland Assembly website at http://www.niassembly.gov.uk) or contact an MLA."},{"q":"Most citizens of the UK, the Irish republic or the Commonwealth aged 18 or over can stand for public office, except for (choose THREE options):","o":["People found guilty of certain criminal offences","People without a university degree","Civil servants","Members of the Armed Forces"],"a":[0,2,3],"e":"Members of the Commonwealth aged 18 or over can stand for public office. There are some exceptions, including: members of the armed forces, civil servants and people found guilty of certain criminal offences."},{"q":"What were the main occupations of the people during the Iron Age?","o":["Fishermen and butchers","Farmers, craft workers or warriors","Knights, doctors or musicians","Engineers, teachers or lawyers"],"a":[1],"e":"During the Iron Age most people were farmers, craft workers or warriors."},{"q":"To look after yourself and your family is one of the responsibilities that you will have as a permanent resident or citizen of the UK.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is TRUE."},{"q":"When is the electoral register updated?","o":["April or May","May or June","September or October","October or November"],"a":[2],"e":""},{"q":"Which of the following is the official church of the state?","o":["The Church of Great Britain","The Church of England","The Church of the United Kingdom","The British Church"],"a":[1],"e":"The official Church of the state is the Church of England (called the Anglican Church in other countries and the Episcopal Church in Scotland and the United States)."},{"q":"Where does the Scottish Parliament sit?","o":["In Glasgow","In St Andrews","In Edinburgh","In Aberdeen"],"a":[2],"e":"In Scotland the elected members, called MSPs, meet in the Scottish Parliament building at Holyrood in Edinburgh."},{"q":"Who became one of the most popular monarchs in English history, particularly after 1588, when the English defeated the Spanish Armada?","o":["Henry VIII","Elizabeth I","James I","Mary I"],"a":[1],"e":"Elizabeth I became one of the most popular monarchs in English history, particularly after 1588, when the English defeated the Spanish Armada (a large fleet of ships), which had been sent by Spain to conquer England and restore Catholicism."},{"q":"What was the population of the UK in 1700?","o":["2 million","4 million","5 million","8 million"],"a":[2],"e":"The population of the UK in 1700 was 5 million people."},{"q":"When did the ‘Concorde’ aircraft retired from service?","o":["In 1976","In 1999","In 2003","In 2000"],"a":[2],"e":""},{"q":"When did the Allies defeat Germany marking the end of the Second World War?","o":["In June 1943","In May 1945","In August 1945","In August 1946"],"a":[1],"e":"Following victory on the beaches of Normandy, the allied forces pressed on through France and eventually into Germany. The Allies comprehensively defeated Germany in May 1945."},{"q":"How many square miles does Loch Lomond and the Trossachs National Park cover?","o":["720","740","820","840"],"a":[0],"e":"Loch Lomond and the Trossachs National Park covers 720 square miles (1,865 square kilometres) in the west of Scotland."},{"q":"Which British athlete won 16 medals, including 11 gold medals, in races over five Paralympic Games?","o":["Dame Kelly Holmes","Sir Ian Botham","Baroness Tanni Grey-Thompson","Dame Elle MacArthur"],"a":[2],"e":"Baroness Tanni Grey-Thompson (1969-) is an athlete who uses a wheelchair and won 16 Paralympic medals, including 11 gold medals, in races over five Paralympic Games. She won the London Marathon six times and broke a total of 30 world records."},{"q":"When is New Year’s Eve celebrated?","o":["25th of December","30th of December","31st of December","1st of January"],"a":[2],"e":"New Year’s Eve is celebrated on the 31st of December."},{"q":"What is the capital city of Scotland?","o":["Dundee","Aberdeen","Edinburgh","Glasgow"],"a":[2],"e":"The capital city of Scotland is Edinburgh."},{"q":"What British sportsmen holds a number of English Test cricket records?","o":["Bobby Moore","Sir Jackie Steward","Sir Ian Botham","Sir Roger Bannister"],"a":[2],"e":"Sir Ian Botham (1955-) captained the English cricket team and holds a number of English Test cricket records, both for batting and for bowling."},{"q":"During the Middle Ages, England was an important trading nation and people came to England from abroad to trade and also to work. Where did the weavers come from?","o":["Germany","Italy","France","Holland"],"a":[2],"e":"During the Middle Ages, people came to England from abroad to trade and also to work. Many had special skills, such as weavers from France, engineers from Germany, glass manufacturers from Italy and canal builders from Holland."}]},{"id":"test-22","name":"Test 22","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"What was the name of Sir Francis Drake’s ship and which was one of the first to sail around the world?","o":["The Golden Eye","The Golden Hind","Elizabeth","The Sharp"],"a":[1],"e":"Sir Francis Drake, one of the commanders in the defeat of the Spanish Armada, was one of the founders of England’s naval tradition. His ship, the Golden Hind, was one of the first to sail right around (‘circumnavigate’) the world."},{"q":"To whom do new citizens have to swear or affirm loyalty as part of the citizenship ceremony?","o":["The Pope","The Prime Minister","The King","The Church of England"],"a":[2],"e":"New citizens swear or affirm loyalty to the King as part of the citizenship ceremony."},{"q":"Why did Henry VIII marry Anne of Cleves?","o":["He was deeply in love","For political reasons","For her fortune","She was expecting a child"],"a":[1],"e":"Henry married Anne of Cleves for political reasons but divorced her soon after."},{"q":"How many Formula 1 Grand Prix events are held in the UK every year?","o":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"a":[0],"e":"A Formula 1 Grand Prix event is held in the UK each year and a number of British Grand Prix drivers have won the Formula 1 World Championship."},{"q":"How can you visit the UK Parliament (choose TWO options)?","o":["Arranging a tour through the visitor services","Writing to your local MP to ask for tickets","Queueing on the day at the public entrance","Contacting an MSP"],"a":[1,2],"e":"To visit the UK Parliament, you can write to your local MP in advance to ask for tickets or you can Queue on the day at the public entrance."},{"q":"Forced marriage is where one or both parties do not or cannot give their consent to enter into the partnership:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"Forced marriage is where one or both parties do not or cannot give their consent to enter into the partnership."},{"q":"Northern Ireland and Scotland have their own banknotes, which are valid everywhere in the UK and shops and businesses are obliged to accept them:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"Northern Ireland and Scotland have their own banknotes, which are valid everywhere in the UK. However, shops and businesses do not have to accept them."},{"q":"How many American colonies declared their independence in 1776, stating that people had a right to establish their own governments?","o":["11","12","13","14"],"a":[2],"e":"In 1776, 13 American colonies declared their independence, stating that people had a right to establish their own governments."},{"q":"Which TWO words come from the Viking language?","o":["Grimsby","Scunthorpe","Thunderstorm","Butterfly"],"a":[0,1],"e":"Many of the Viking invaders stayed in Britain – especially in the east and north of England in an area known as the Danelaw (many place names there, such as Grimsby and Scunthorpe come from the Viking languages)."},{"q":"Which of the following drugs is NOT illegal in the UK?","o":["Cannabis","Cocaine","Ecstasy","All of the above are illegal"],"a":[3],"e":"Drugs such as heroin, cocaine, ecstasy and cannabis are illegal in the UK."},{"q":"How is the Speaker elected?","o":["By other MPs in a secret ballot","By the members of the House of Lords by proportional representation","By the members of the House of Commons in a public ballot","By the Prime Minister"],"a":[0],"e":"The Speaker is chosen by other MPs in a secret ballot."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following are traditional British foods?","o":["Pasta carbonara","Green curry","Roast Beef","Fish and Chips"],"a":[2,3],"e":"Roast beef and fish and chips are traditional British foods."},{"q":"When was the Northern Ireland Parliament established for the first time?","o":["In 1922","In 1932","In 1925","In 1928"],"a":[0],"e":"A Northern Ireland Parliament was established in 1922, when Ireland was divided, but it was abolished in 1972, shortly after the Troubles broke out in 1969."},{"q":"Throughout the 1990s, Britain played a leading role in coalition forces involved in the liberation of Kuwait, following the Iraqi invasion in 1990, and the conflict in the former Republic of Yugoslavia:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"The ‘swinging sixties’ was a period of significant social change and it was known for its growth in:","o":["British fashion, cinema and popular music","The Punk movement","English literature and comedy","The ownership of cars"],"a":[0],"e":"The decade of the 1960s was a period of significant social change. It was known as the ‘swinging sixties’. There was growth in British fashion, cinema and popular music."},{"q":"What is the name given to the system used to deal with children and young people who have committed an offence in Scotland?","o":["The Children’s Hearings System","The Youth Court System","The Sheriff Court for Young Children","The Young’s System"],"a":[0],"e":"In Scotland a system called the Children’s Hearings System is used to deal with children and young people who have committed an offence."},{"q":"Which prehistoric village located in northern Europe has helped archaeologists to understand more about how people live near the end of the Stone Age?","o":["Stonehenge","Scunthorpe","Danelaw","Skara Brae"],"a":[3],"e":"Skara Brae on Orkney, off the north coast of Scotland, is the best preserved prehistoric village in northern Europe, and has helped archaeologists to understand more about how people lived near the end of the Stone Age."},{"q":"During the Middle Ages, a Parliament was developed in Scotland, which had three Houses called Estates. These were:","o":["The lords, the commons and the clergy","The lords, the commons and the farmers","The farmers, the blacksmiths and the clergy","The farmers, the blacksmiths and the teachers"],"a":[0],"e":"The Parliament developed in Scotland in the Middle Ages had three Houses, called Estates: the lords, the commons and the clergy."},{"q":"Which landscape architect designed grounds around country houses so that the landscape appeared to be natural, with grass, trees and lakes?","o":["Clarice Cliff","Lancelot ‘Capability’ Brown","Alexander McQueen","Vivienne Westwood"],"a":[1],"e":"In the 18th century, Lancelot ‘Capability’ Brown designed the grounds around country houses so that the landscape appeared to be natural, with grass, trees and lakes. He often worked with Edwin Lutyens to design colourful gardens around the houses he designed."},{"q":"Which of the following scientific discoveries helped the progress of the Industrial Revolution?","o":["Penicillin","The aeroplane","Steam power","The lightbulb"],"a":[2],"e":"Scientific discoveries, such as James Watt’s work on steam power, helped the progress of the Industrial Revolution."},{"q":"What is the function of the UN Security Council?","o":["Recommend action when there are international crises and threats to peace","Recommend humanitarian action when there are natural disasters","Protect and promote human rights","Promote international trade relationships"],"a":[0],"e":"The UN Security Council, which recommends action when there are international crises and threats to peace."},{"q":"Who were the parents of Elizabeth I?","o":["Henry VIII and Anne Boleyn","Henry VII and Anne of Cleves","Henry VIII and Catherine of Aragon","Henry VII and Catherine Howard"],"a":[0],"e":"Henry VIII and Anne Boleyn were the parents of Elizabeth I."},{"q":"The Isle of Man is a Crown dependency.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"Which of the following charities works for the prevention of cruelty to children?","o":["PDSA","RSPB","NSPCC","Crisis"],"a":[2],"e":"The NSPCC is the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children."}]},{"id":"test-23","name":"Test 23","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"How old do you need to be to buy a lottery ticket or scratch card?","o":["16","18","21","25"],"a":[1],"e":"People under 18 are not allowed to participate in the National Lottery."},{"q":"Where was Catherine of Aragon (the first wife of Henry VIII) from?","o":["Spain","Germany","England","French"],"a":[0],"e":"Catherine of Aragon was a Spanish princess."},{"q":"When did motor-car racing start in the UK?","o":["In 1889","In 1901","In 1902","In 1922"],"a":[2],"e":"Motor-car racing in the UK started in 1902."},{"q":"The Commonwealth is based on the core values of democracy, good government and the rule of law:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"The Commonwealth is based on the core values of democracy, good government and the rule of law."},{"q":"How long does it take to donate blood?","o":["About half an hour","About an hour","About two hours","About 15 minutes"],"a":[1],"e":"Giving blood only takes about an hour to do."},{"q":"When did the post-war economic boom come to an end?","o":["In the early 1970s","In the late 1970s","In the early 1960s","In the late 1960s"],"a":[1],"e":"In the late 1970s, the post-war economic boom came to an end."},{"q":"During its government, Margaret Thatcher made structural changes to the economy through the privatisation of nationalised industries and imposed legal controls on trade union powers:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"Margaret Thatcher made structural changes to the economy through the privatisation of nationalised industries and imposed legal controls on trade union powers."},{"q":"Who sat at the House of Commons during the Middle Ages?","o":["Knights and wealthy people from towns and cities","Farmers and blacksmiths","Great landowners and bishops","Everyone could sit at the House of Commons"],"a":[0],"e":"In the Middle Ages, Knights, who were usually smaller landowners, and wealthy people from towns and cities were elected to sit in the House of Commons."},{"q":"Which of the following words come from the Anglo-Saxon language?","o":["Apple and summer","Music and dance","Grimsby and scunthorpe","Canute and boat"],"a":[0],"e":"The words ‘Apple’ and ‘summer’ are based on Anglo-Saxon words."},{"q":"Where do local authorities get funding from (choose TWO answers)?","o":["The King","The central government","Donations made by local residents","Local taxes"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Local authorities provide a range of services in their areas. They are funded by money from central government and by local taxes."},{"q":"If you are self-employed:","o":["You need to pay your own tax","Tax is automatically taken from your earnings by a specialised agency","You don’t need to pay tax","You only needed to pay tax on 50% of your earnings"],"a":[0],"e":"If you are self-employed, you need to pay your own tax through a system called ‘self-assessment’, which includes completing a tax return."},{"q":"What name is given to the elected members of the Northern Ireland Assembly?","o":["MSPs","MLAs","MPs","SMs"],"a":[1],"e":"Members of the Northern Ireland Assembly are known as MLAs (members of the Legislative Assembly)."},{"q":"How long does the Hanukkah celebration last for?","o":["Eight days","40 days","Five days","A fortnight"],"a":[0],"e":"Hanukkah is in November or December and is celebrated for eight days."},{"q":"Which of the following areas did the British Empire NOT cover during the Victorian period?","o":["Mexico","Australia","India","Large parts of Africa"],"a":[0],"e":"During the Victorian period, the British Empire grew to cover all of India, Australia and large parts of Africa."},{"q":"What percentage of the total British population is located in Wales?","o":["30%","5%","8%","3%"],"a":[1],"e":"The total British population located in Wales is around 5%."},{"q":"Which senior MP is responsible for issues relating to crime, policing and immigration?","o":["The Home Secretary","The Foreign Secretary","The Chancellor of the Exchequer","The Prime Minister"],"a":[0],"e":"The Home Secretary is responsible for crime, policing and immigration."},{"q":"Which British scientist led the development of the atomic bomb?","o":["Howard Florey","Ernst Chain","Ernest Rutherford","Keith Campbell"],"a":[2],"e":"Scientists led by Ernest Rutherford, working at Manchester and then Cambridge University, were the first to ‘split the atom’ and took part in the Manhattan Project in the United States, which developed the atomic bomb."},{"q":"Which of the following movies played an important role in boosting morale during the Second World War?","o":["The Ladykillers","Carry on","Passport to Pimlico","In Which We Serve"],"a":[3],"e":"During the Second World War, British movies (for example, In Which We Serve ) played an important part in boosting morale."},{"q":"After the Black Death, the growing wealth in the towns led to the development of a strong middle class:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"After the Black Death, new social classes appeared, including owners of large areas of land (later called the gentry), and people left the countryside to live in the towns. In the towns, growing wealth led to the development of a strong middle class."},{"q":"What was the ability of the Harrier jump jet aircraft?","o":["To carry 1,000 passengers","To take off vertically","To float on water","To reach supersonic speeds"],"a":[1],"e":"The Harrier jump jet, an aircraft capable of taking off vertically, was also designed and developed in the UK."},{"q":"Who won the 2012 Olympic gold medal in heptathlon?","o":["Ellie Simmonds","Jessica Ennis","Dame Kelly Holmes","Jayne Torvill"],"a":[1],"e":"Jessica Ennis (1986-) won the 2012 Olympic gold medal in the heptathlon, which includes seven different track and field events. She also holds a number of British athletics records."},{"q":"What is the name of the annual cultural Welsh festival which includes music, dance, art and original performances largely in Welsh?","o":["The Arts Festival of Wales","The National Eisteddfod of Wales","The Music Festival of Wales","The National Performance Festival of Wales"],"a":[1],"e":"The National Eisteddfod of Wales is an annual cultural festival which includes music, dance, art and original performances largely in Welsh. It includes a number of important competitions for Welsh poetry."},{"q":"What is the most well-known rugby league (club) competition?","o":["The Grand National","The All England championship","The Six Nations championship","The Super League"],"a":[3],"e":"The Super League is the most well-known rugby league (club) competition."},{"q":"Where do the Laurence Olivier Awards take place?","o":["In London","In Manchester","In Liverpool","In Edinburgh"],"a":[0],"e":"The Laurence Olivier Awards take place annually at different venues in London."}]},{"id":"test-24","name":"Test 24","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"How old was the Queen of Scotland, Mary Stuart, when she became queen?","o":["Two years old","A month old","A week old","5 years old"],"a":[2],"e":"The queen of Scotland, Mary Stuart (often now called ‘Mary, Queen of Scots’) was a Catholic. She was only a week old when her father died and she became queen."},{"q":"Where was the first tennis club founded?","o":["Brixton","Leamington Spa","Haywards Heath","St Andrews"],"a":[1],"e":"The first tennis club was founded in Leamington Spa in 1872."},{"q":"Which British playwright invented many common English words and had a great influence on the English language?","o":["William Shakespeare","Graham Greene","Dylan Thomas","Harold Pinter"],"a":[0],"e":"William Shakespeare had a great influence on the English language and invented many words that are still common today."},{"q":"In which city is the Northern Ireland Assembly building, also known as Stormont, located?","o":["London","Belfast","Cardiff","Dublin"],"a":[1],"e":"The Northern Ireland Assembly building is known as Stormont and it is located in Belfast."},{"q":"What proportion of the population died as a result of the Black Death in England?","o":["Half of the population","One third of the population","55% of the population","One quarter of the population"],"a":[1],"e":"As a result of the Black Death, one third of the population of England died and a similar proportion in Scotland and Wales."},{"q":"Which famous poem tells the story of the knights at the court of King Arthur?","o":["Beowulf","Paradise Lost","Sir Gawain and the Green Knight","King Arthur and his knights"],"a":[2],"e":"Poems which survive from the Middle Ages include Chaucer’s Canterbury Tales and a poem called Sir Gawain and the Green Knight, about one of the knights at the court of King Arthur."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a fundamental principle of British life?","o":["Tolerance of those with different faiths and beliefs","The rule of law","Autocracy","Democracy"],"a":[2],"e":"The fundamental principles of British life include: tolerance of those with different faiths and beliefs, the rule of law, democracy, individual liberty and participation in community life."},{"q":"Which flower is associated with Northern Ireland?","o":["Daffodil","Rose","Thistle","Shamrock"],"a":[3],"e":"The shamrock is the flower associated with Northern Ireland."},{"q":"The longest distance on the mainland in the UK is from John O’Groats on the north coast of Scotland to a place in the south-west corner of England known as:","o":["Land’s Point","Earth Point","Eastbourne","Land’s End"],"a":[3],"e":"The longest distance on the mainland is from John O’Groats on the north coast of Scotland to Land’s End in the south-west corner of England. It is about 870 miles (approximately 1,400 kilometres)."},{"q":"Who wrote a famous series of poems about a group of people going to Canterbury on a pilgrimage known as ‘The Canterbury Tales’?","o":["William Shakespeare","Geoffrey Chaucer","Jane Austen","Charles Dickens"],"a":[1],"e":"In the years leading up to 1400, Geoffrey Chaucer wrote a series of poems in English about a group of people going to Canterbury on a pilgrimage. The people decided to tell each other stories on the journey, and the poems describe the travellers and some of the stories they told. This collection of poems is called The Canterbury Tales."},{"q":"Which of the following categories is recognised at the Laurence Olivier Awards?","o":["Music","Theatre","Sport","Literature"],"a":[1],"e":"The Laurence Olivier Awards recognise achievements in the area of theatre."},{"q":"In the UK, there is a National Lottery for which draws are made every day.","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"In the UK, there is a National Lottery for which draws are made every week."},{"q":"Where is Europe’s longest dry ski slope located?","o":["Near Swansea, in Wales","Near Edinburgh, in Scotland","Near Glasgow, in Scotland","Near Belfast, in Northern Ireland"],"a":[1],"e":"There are five ski centres in Scotland, as well as Europe’s longest dry ski slope near Edinburgh."},{"q":"Which TWO of Henry VIII’s wives were accused of taking lovers and executed?","o":["Anne of Cleves","Catherine Howard","Catherine Parr","Anne Boleyn"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Anne Boleyn and Catherine Howard were accused of taking lovers and executed."},{"q":"Who is the head of the state in the UK?","o":["The Prime Minister","King Charles III","Prince William of Wales","Prince Philip, the Duke of Edinburgh"],"a":[1],"e":"King Charles III is the head of state in the UK."},{"q":"What was the population of the UK in 1600?","o":["Just over 3 million","Just over 4 million","Just under 3 million","Just over 5 million"],"a":[1],"e":"The population of the UK in 1600 was just over 4 million people."},{"q":"What name is given to the 31st of December in Scotland?","o":["Hogmanay","Bonfire Night","Vaisakhi","Diwali"],"a":[0],"e":"In Scotland, 31 December is called Hogmanay."},{"q":"In which of the following languages is the life in the UK test NOT available?","o":["English","Scottish Gaelic","French","Welsh"],"a":[2],"e":"The Life in the UK test is usually taken is English, although special arrangements can be made if you wish to take it in Welsh or Scottish Gaelic."},{"q":"Which two scientists were successful at cloning the first mammal, Dolly the sheep?","o":["Sir Frank Whittle and Sir Christopher Cockrell","Sir Robert Edwards and Patrick Steptoe","Sir Ian Wilmot and Keith Campbell","Francis Creek and John Logie Baird"],"a":[2],"e":"In 1996, two British scientists, Sir Ian Wilmot and Keith Campbell, led a team which was the first to succeed in cloning a mammal, Dolly the sheep."},{"q":"When is the Sikh Vaisakhi festival celebrated?","o":["14th of February","14th of April","30th of March","30th of April"],"a":[1],"e":"Vaisakhi (also spelled Baisakhi) is a Sikh festival which celebrates the founding of the Sikh community known as the Khalsa. It is celebrated on the 14th of April each year with parades, dancing and singing."},{"q":"Who were the Pre-Raphaelites?","o":["An important group of musicians from the 16th century","An important group who demanded the vote for the working classes","An important group of artists in the second half of the 19th century","A group of explorers who arrived in the south-east coast of Australia"],"a":[2],"e":"The Pre-Raphaelites were an important group of artists in the second half of the 19th century. They painted detailed pictures on religious or literary themes in bright colours. The group included Holman Hunt, Dante Gabriel Rossetti and Sir John Millais."},{"q":"When was St Pancras Station built?","o":["In the 18th century","In the 19th century","In the 20th century","In the 21st century"],"a":[1],"e":"St Pancras Station was built in the 19th century."},{"q":"Members of the Scottish Parliament (MSPs) are elected on the basis of:","o":["Proportional representation","Personal achievements","Instant run-off","First past the post system (the candidate who gets the most votes)"],"a":[0],"e":"Members of the Scottish Parliament (MSPs) are elected by a form of proportional representation."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following soap operas are popular in the UK?","o":["Braveheart","Coronation Street","EastEnders","Heroes"],"a":[1,2],"e":"Popular programmes in the UK include regular soap operas such as Coronation Street and EastEnders."}]},{"id":"test-25","name":"Test 25","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"What does the term ‘Hansard’ relate to?","o":["Official reports where proceedings in Parliament are published","A popular British soap opera","A festival which celebrates the founding of the Sikh community","A public holiday"],"a":[0],"e":"Proceedings in Parliament are broadcast on television and published in official reports called Hansard."},{"q":"Who was the first king of the House of Tudor?","o":["King Richard III","Henry Tudor","Henry V","James I"],"a":[1],"e":"Henry VII (Henry Tudor) was the first king of the House of Tudor."},{"q":"What do Lewis Hamilton, Jenson Button and Damon Hill have in common?","o":["They are football players","They have won the Formula 1 World Championship","They won gold Olympic medals","They have won the Six Nations Championship"],"a":[1],"e":"Lewis Hamilton, Jenson Button and Damon Hill have won the Formula 1 World Championship."},{"q":"According to the 2011 Census, what percentage of people said that they had no religion?","o":["10%","11%","20%","25%"],"a":[3],"e":"According to the 2011 census, 25% of people said they had no religion."},{"q":"During which period were the first coins produced in Britain?","o":["The Stone Age","The Golden Age","The Silver Age","The Iron Age"],"a":[3],"e":"The people of the Iron Age had a sophisticated culture and economy. They made the first coins to be minted in Britain, some inscribed with the names of Iron Age kings."},{"q":"What other name is given to Diwali, the religious celebration that celebrates the victory of good over evil and the gaining of knowledge?","o":["The Festival of Lights","Hanukkah","The Festival of the Lanterns","The Festival of the Candles"],"a":[0],"e":"Diwali normally falls in October or November and lasts for five days. It is often called the Festival of Lights."},{"q":"In Wales, the jury has to listen to the evidence presented at the trial and then decide a verdict of ‘guilty’, ‘not guilty’ or ‘not proven’ based of what they have heard:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"The jury has to listen to the evidence presented at the trial and then decide a verdict of ‘guilty’ or ‘not guilty’ based of what they have heard. In Scotland, a third verdict of ‘not proven’ is also possible."},{"q":"The Prime Minister has a country house outside London called:","o":["Sparkle","Downing","Windsor","Chequers"],"a":[3],"e":"The Prime Minister has a country house outside London called Chequers."},{"q":"In which English county is Maiden Castle located?","o":["Dorset","Devon","Somerset","Cornwall"],"a":[0],"e":"A very impressive hill fort can be seen today at Maiden Castle, in the English county of Dorset."},{"q":"A National Insurance number does on its own prove to an employer that you have the right to work in the UK:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"A National Insurance number does not on its own prove to an employer that you have the right to work in the UK."},{"q":"Which of the following is a ceremonial role to be undertaken by the King?","o":["Opening the new parliamentary session every year","Visiting the major cities in the UK once every six months","Choosing the Prime Minister","Giving a speech on New Year’s Eve"],"a":[0],"e":"The King has important ceremonial roles, such as the opening of the new parliamentary session each year. On this occasion the King makes a speech which summarises the government’s policies for the year ahead."},{"q":"How long can you use your driving licence in the UK if you have a licence from another country?","o":["For up to 12 months","You need a UK driving licence","For as long as it is valid","For up to 2 years"],"a":[0],"e":"If you have a licence from another country, you may use it in the UK for up to 12 months."},{"q":"What British discovery was influential in the development of computer science and the modern-day computer? The Radar","o":["The Radar","The Turing machine","The MRI scanner","The aeroplane"],"a":[1],"e":"The Turing machine is a theoretical mathematical device invented by Alan Turing (1912-54), a British mathematician, in the 1930s. The theory was influential in the development of computer science and the modern-day computer."},{"q":"Which Court deals with the serious criminal offences in England, Wales and Northern Ireland?","o":["The County Court","The Crown Court","The High Court","The Sheriff Court"],"a":[1],"e":"In England, Wales and Northern Ireland, serious criminal offences are tried in front of a judge and a jury in a Crown Court."},{"q":"Which famous philosopher developed ideas about human nature during the 18th century and that have continued to influence philosophers over the years?","o":["Aristotle","Sigmund Freud","Platon","David Hume"],"a":[3],"e":"During the 18th century, David Hume ’s ideas about human nature continue to influence philosophers."},{"q":"If you are arrested and taken to a police station, a police officer should:","o":["Make a copy of your ID and retain you until your lawyer arrives","Tell you the reason for your arrest and that you will be able to seek legal advice","Offer you a cup of tea","Explain the law to you and wait for your family to arrive"],"a":[1],"e":"If you are arrested and taken to a police station, a police officer will tell you the reason for your arrest and you will be able to seek legal advice."},{"q":"Which British actor has recently won an Oscar?","o":["Hugh Grant","Sir Anthony Hopkins","Jude Law","Richard Burton"],"a":[1],"e":"Recent British actors to have won Oscars include Colin Firth, Sir Antony Hopkins, Dame Judi Dench, Kate Winslet and Tilda Swinton."},{"q":"What TWO names are given to the day before Lent?","o":["Pancake day","Domesday","Shrove Tuesday","Ash Wednesday"],"a":[0,2],"e":"The day before Lent starts is called Shrove Tuesday, or Pancake Day."},{"q":"When did the ‘Concorde’ aircraft carry passengers for the first time?","o":["In 1969","In 1970","In 1972","In 1976"],"a":[3],"e":"The ‘Concorde’ began carrying passengers in 1976."},{"q":"The UK is one of five permanent members of the Security Council:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"The UK is one of five permanent members of the Security Council."},{"q":"Which of the following landmarks is the largest expanse of fresh water in mainland Britain?","o":["The River Thames","Loch Lomond","Loch Ness","Lake District"],"a":[1],"e":"Loch Lomond is the largest expanse of fresh water in mainland Britain and probably the best-known part of the park."},{"q":"Who wrote the music for the popular show Jesus Christ Superstar?","o":["Andrew Lloyd Webber","Gilbert and Sullivan","Hans Holbein and Sir Anthony Van Dyck","Sir Laurence Olivier"],"a":[0],"e":"Andrew Lloyd Webber has written the music for shows which have been popular throughout the world, including, in collaboration with Tim Rice, Jesus Christ Superstar and Evita, and also Cats and The Phantom of the Opera."},{"q":"Which one of the following four changes did the Chartists did NOT campaign for?","o":["For any man to be able to stand as MP","Secret ballots","Elections every six years","For MPs to be paid"],"a":[2],"e":"The Chartists campaigned for every man to have the vote, elections every year, for all regions to be equal in the electoral system, secret ballots, for any man to be able to stand as an MP and for MPs to be paid."},{"q":"Pantomimes are a British tradition that many theatres produce during:","o":["Easter","Christmas","Halloween","Pancake Day"],"a":[1],"e":"Many theatres produce a pantomime at Christmas time."}]},{"id":"test-26","name":"Test 26","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"How many weeks does the orchestral classical music event known as ‘the Proms’ last for?","o":["Three weeks","Four weeks","Six weeks","Eight weeks"],"a":[3],"e":"The Proms is an eight-week summer season of orchestral classical music that takes place in various venues, including the Royal Albert Hall in London."},{"q":"When did King Charles III start reining?","o":["In 2020","In 2021","In 2022","In 2023"],"a":[2],"e":"The King has reigned since his mother’s death in September 2022."},{"q":"What did the Chartists campaign for?","o":["The right to vote for women over 30 years old","The right to vote for the working classes and other people without property","The right to vote for women and men over 30 years old","The right to vote for every citizen"],"a":[1],"e":"The Chartists, demanded the vote for the working classes and other people without property."},{"q":"What is the name of the land formation consisting of columns made of volcanic lava located on the north-east coast of Northern Ireland?","o":["Eden Project","The Giant’s Causeway","Loch Lomond","Snowdonia"],"a":[1],"e":"Located on the north-east coast of Northern Ireland, the Giant’s Causeway is a land formation of columns made from volcanic lava."},{"q":"Members of the public are not allowed in Youth Courts, and the name or photographs of the accused young person cannot be published in newspapers or used by the media:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"A set of principles by which a country is governed and which includes all of the institutions that are responsible for running the country and how their power is kept in check is the definition for:","o":["Revolution","Constitution","Monarchy","Democracy"],"a":[1],"e":"A constitution is a set of principles by which a country is governed. It includes all of the institutions that are responsible for running the country and how their power is kept in check."},{"q":"When did Emmeline Pankhurst die?","o":["1928","1935","1936","1930"],"a":[0],"e":"Emmeline Pankhurst died in 1928. Shortly before Emmeline’s death, women were given the right to vote at the age of 21, the same as men."},{"q":"What TWO houses were confronted during the Wars of the Roses?","o":["The House of Lancaster","The House of Leicester","The House of Canterbury","The House of York"],"a":[0,3],"e":"The Wars of the Roses were fought between the supporters of two families: the House of Lancaster and the House of York."},{"q":"What is the National Anthem of the UK?","o":["God save the King","God save the Queen","United kingdoms","God save the land"],"a":[0],"e":"The National Anthem of the UK is ‘God Save the King’."},{"q":"Who was the first woman to become Prime Minister in the UK?","o":["J K Rowling","Margaret Thatcher","Jane Austen","Hillary Clinton"],"a":[1],"e":"Margaret Thatcher, Britain’s first woman Prime Minister, led the Conservative government from 1979 to 1990."},{"q":"When did the Brexit referendum take place?","o":["23rd June 2016","23rd July 2016","23rd June 2015","23rd July 2015"],"a":[0],"e":"The referendum on the UK’s membership of the European Union or Brexit referendum was held on 23 June 2016."},{"q":"Which one of the following gardens is located in Northern Ireland?","o":["Bodnant Garden","Mount Stewart","Sissinghurst","Hidcote"],"a":[1],"e":"Mount Stewart is located in Northern Ireland, Bodnant Garden in Wales and Sissinghurst and Hidcote in England."},{"q":"When is the Mercury Music Prize awarded?","o":["July","August","September","October"],"a":[2],"e":"The Mercury Music Prize is awarded each September for the best album from the UK and Ireland."},{"q":"Which of the following statements is true?","o":["To apply to become a permanent resident or citizen of the UK, you will need to have a university degree","To apply to become a permanent resident or citizen of the UK, you will need to have a good understanding of life in the UK","To apply to become a permanent resident or citizen of the UK, you will need to have British relatives","To apply to become a permanent resident or citizen of the UK, you will need to have knowledge of the British laws"],"a":[1],"e":"To apply to become a permanent resident or citizen of the UK, you will need to have a good understanding of life in the UK and be able to speak and read English."},{"q":"Who invented the cash-dispensing ATM (automatic teller machine) or ‘cashpoint’?","o":["Sir Peter Mansfield","James Goodfellow","Patrick Steptoe","Sir Tim Berners-Lee"],"a":[1],"e":"In the 1960s, James Goodfellow (1937-) invented the cash-dispensing ATM (automatic teller machine) or ‘cashpoint’. The first of these was put into use by Barclays Bank in Enfield, north London in 1967."},{"q":"What is the name of the only ‘Grand Slam’ tennis event played on grass?","o":["The Australian open","The Wimbledon Championships","The U.S. open","The Queen’s open"],"a":[1],"e":"The Wimbledon Championships is the oldest tennis tournament in the world and the only ‘Grand Slam’ event played on grass."},{"q":"What is the meaning of the term ‘Habeas corpus’?","o":["You must present the person in court","You must obey the law","You should go to court","You should not be judged"],"a":[0],"e":"Habeas corpus is Latin for ‘you must present the person in court’. The Habeas Corpus Act guaranteed that no one could be held prisoner unlawfully. Every prisoner has a right to a court hearing."},{"q":"What name is given to the candelabrum lit during the eight-day holiday of Hanukkah?","o":["Menorah","Diwali","Ramadan","Baisakhi"],"a":[0],"e":"Hanukkah is in November or December and is celebrated for eight days. On each day of the festival a candle is lit on a stand of eight candles (called a menorah ) to remember the story of the festival, where oil that should have lasted only a day did so for eight."},{"q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["It is against the law to walk your dog on the beach","It is against the law to treat a pet cruelly or to neglect it","It is against the law to take pets into public places","It is against the law to feed your pet in public places"],"a":[1],"e":"It is against the law to treat a pet cruelly or to neglect it."},{"q":"Which German princess got married with Henry VIII?","o":["Jane Seymour","Anne of Cleves","Catherine Parr","Anne Boleyn"],"a":[1],"e":"Anne of Cleves was a German princess who married Henry VIII for political reasons."},{"q":"In which of the following matters can the Scottish Parliament legislate (choose FIVE options)?","o":["Additional tax-raising powers","Economic development","Health","Education","Civil and criminal law","Immigration","Planning"],"a":[0,2,3,4,6],"e":"The Scottish Parliament can legislate on: civil and criminal law, health, education, planning and additional tax-raising powers."},{"q":"Drinking in public can be a criminal offence and you can be fined or arrested for it:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"Drinking in public: some places have alcohol-free zones where you cannot drink in public. The police can also confiscate alcohol or move young people on from public places. You can be fined or arrested."},{"q":"What was the most spoken language during the Iron Age?","o":["Anglo-Saxon","French","Celtic","Irish"],"a":[2],"e":"The language spoken during the Iron Age was part of the Celtic language family."},{"q":"What charity works for the preservation of important buildings, coastline and countryside in the UK?","o":["The National Trust","The RSPB","Shelter","Oxfam"],"a":[0],"e":"The National Trust works for the preservation of important buildings, coastline and countryside in the UK."}]},{"id":"test-27","name":"Test 27","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"When did Henry VIII die?","o":["In January 1547","In January 1557","In February 1547","In February 1557"],"a":[0],"e":"Henry VIII died on the 28th of January 1547."},{"q":"Which of the following lines from Shakespeare’s plays and poems is often still quoted?","o":["The Darling buds of May","I have nothing to offer but blood, toil, tears and sweat","Long live our noble King","God save the King"],"a":[0],"e":"‘The darling buds of May’ is a line from William Shakespeare’s play Sonnet 18 – Shall I Compare Thee To A Summer’s Day."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following are fundamental principles of British life?","o":["Tolerance of those with different faiths and beliefs","Buying food from local business","The rule of law","Doing the military service"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Tolerance of those with different faiths and beliefs and the rule of law are fundamental principles of British life."},{"q":"What is the capital city of the UK?","o":["Manchester","Liverpool","London","Cambridge"],"a":[2],"e":"The capital city of the UK is London."},{"q":"Which of the following statements about Mary, Queen of Scotland, is NOT true?","o":["She spent most of her childhood in France","She was a Protestant","She gave her throne to her Protestant son, James VI of Scotland.","She was kept her prisoner for 20 years"],"a":[1],"e":"The queen of Scotland, Mary Stuart (often now called ‘Mary, Queen of Scots’) was a Catholic."},{"q":"When did Ireland become unified with England, Wales and Scotland?","o":["In 1801","In 1811","In 1901","In 1925"],"a":[0],"e":"In 1801, Ireland became unified with England, Scotland and Wales after the Act of Union of 1800. This created the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland."},{"q":"What is Hogmanay?","o":["The equivalent of Christmas Day in Scotland","The equivalent of New Year’s Eve in Scotland","The equivalent of Christmas Day in Wales","The equivalent of New Year’s Eve in Wales"],"a":[1],"e":"In Scotland, the 31st of December is called Hogmanay."},{"q":"The devolved administrations of Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland each have their own civil service:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"The devolved administrations each have their own civil service."},{"q":"On Remembrance Day, there is a two-minute silence at 11 AM to commemorate those who died fighting during the First World War.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"On Remembrance Day at 11.00 am there is a two-minute silence and wreaths are laid at the Cenotaph in Whitehall, London."},{"q":"What is the distance between the north coast of Scotland and the south-west corner of England?","o":["470 miles","490 miles","840 miles","870 miles"],"a":[3],"e":"In the UK, the longest distance on the mainland is from John O’Groats on the north coast of Scotland to Land’s End in the south-west corner of England. It is about 870 miles (approximately 1,400 kilometres)."},{"q":"Who was Henry Purcell?","o":["A German-born composer","An organist at Westminster Abbey","An opera composer","A jazz musician"],"a":[1],"e":"Henry Purcell (1659-95) was the organist at Westminster Abbey. He wrote church music, operas and other pieces, and developed a British style distinct from that elsewhere in Europe."},{"q":"How are life peers appointed?","o":["By the monarch on the advice of the Prime Minister","By the Prime Minister on the advice of the King","By the shadow cabinet","By the MPs"],"a":[0],"e":"Life peers are appointed by the monarch on the advice of the Prime Minister."},{"q":"Where is the Giant’s Causeway located?","o":["Northern Ireland","Scotland","South-east coast of Wales","South-west coast of England"],"a":[0],"e":"The Giant’s Causeway is located on the north-east coast of Northern Ireland."},{"q":"Who proposed that enemy aircrafts could be detected by radio waves and is responsible for the development of the radar?","o":["Sir Robert Watson-Watt","John McLeod","John Logie Baird","Alan Turing"],"a":[0],"e":"The radar was developed by Scotsman Sir Robert Watson-Watt (1892-1973), who proposed that enemy aircraft could be detected by radio waves. The first successful radar test took place in 1935."},{"q":"During Halloween people carve lanterns out of ______ and put a candle inside of them.","o":["Pumpkins","Aubergines","Clay","Potatoes"],"a":[0],"e":"During Halloween people carve lanterns out of pumpkins and put a candle inside of them."},{"q":"In England, Wales and Northern Ireland, if an accused person is aged 18, the case will be heard in a Youth Court:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"In England, Wales and Northern Ireland, if an accused person is aged 10 to 17, the case is normally heard in a Youth Court in front of up to three specially trained magistrates or a District Judge."},{"q":"The Prime Minister appoints about 40 senior MPs to become ministers in charge of departments.","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"The Prime Minister appoints about 20 senior MPs to become ministers in charge of departments."},{"q":"Royal Ascot is a four-day horse race meeting in Berkshire attended by members of the Royal Family:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"Royal Ascot, a five-day race meeting in Berkshire attended by members of the Royal Family."},{"q":"Who sat at the House of Lords during the Middle Ages?","o":["Great landowners and bishops","Knights, who were usually smaller landowners","Wealthy people from towns and cities","Farmers and blacksmiths"],"a":[0],"e":"During the Middle Ages, great landowners and bishops sat in the House of Lords."},{"q":"Which of the following statements regarding Scottish television is TRUE?","o":["There is a channel with programmes in the Gaelic language","There are three channels in the Gaelic language","All programmes are broadcasted in English","There are no specific programmes to Scotland"],"a":[0],"e":"In Scotland, some Scotland-specific programmes are shown and there is also a channel with programmes in the Gaelic language."},{"q":"Where does the biggest and most well-known Fringe Festival take place?","o":["Cambridge","Oxford","Edinburgh","Cardiff"],"a":[2],"e":"The biggest and most well-known Fringe Festival is the Edinburgh Festival Fringe."},{"q":"The UN was set up after the First World War and aims to prevent war and promote international peace and security:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"The UN was set up after the Second World War and aims to prevent war and promote international peace and security."},{"q":"Which of the following countries was NOT part of the Central Powers during the First World War?","o":["Germany","Serbia","Austria","Hungary"],"a":[1],"e":"During the First World War Serbia formed part of the Allied powers."},{"q":"What is played at important national occasions and at events attended by the King or the Royal Family?","o":["Folk music","The Beatles","The National Anthem","Opera music"],"a":[2],"e":"The National Anthem of the UK is ‘God Save the King’ is played at important national occasions and at events attended by the King or the Royal Family."}]},{"id":"test-28","name":"Test 28","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"For which purpose was the Statute of Rhuddlan introduced?","o":["To annex Scotland to the Crown of England","To annex Northern Ireland to the Crown of England","To annex Ireland to the Crown of England","To annex Wales to the Crown of England"],"a":[3],"e":"In 1284 King Edward I of England introduced the Statute of Rhuddlan, which annexed Wales to the Crown of England."},{"q":"How can a person be protected from being forced into a marriage?","o":["By giving them a place to stay away from their family","Court orders can be obtained to protect a person from being forced into a marriage","By speaking to the person’s parents","There are no laws in the UK to avoid this"],"a":[1],"e":"Court orders can be obtained to protect a person from being forced into a marriage, or to protect a person in a forced marriage."},{"q":"When was the first tennis club founded in the UK?","o":["1772","1872","1862","1762"],"a":[1],"e":"The first tennis club was founded in Leamington Spa in 1872."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT classified as a criminal offence in the UK?","o":["Smoking in public places","Selling or buying drugs","Carrying a weapon","Unfair dismissal or discrimination in the workplace"],"a":[3],"e":"Unfair dismissal or discrimination in the workplace is classified as a civil offence."},{"q":"When did Henry VIII start reining?","o":["In April 1509","In June 1497","In March 1515","In August 1495"],"a":[0],"e":"Henry VIII was king of England from 21 April 1509 until his death on 28 January 1547."},{"q":"What is the currency in the UK?","o":["Euro","Dollar","Pound","Dinar"],"a":[2],"e":"The currency in the UK is the pound sterling (symbol £)."},{"q":"In May 2010, the Conservative and Liberal Democrat parties formed a coalition and the leader of the Liberal Democrat Party, David Cameron, became Prime Minister:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"In May 2010, and for the first time in the UK since February 1974, no political party won an overall majority in the General Election. The Conservative and Liberal Democrat parties formed a coalition and the leader of the Conservative Party, David Cameron, became Prime Minister."},{"q":"When is Diwali normally celebrated?","o":["June or July","October or November","November or December","March or April"],"a":[1],"e":"Diwali normally falls in October or November and lasts for five days."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT considered to be a main part of the British government?","o":["The monarchy","The European Council","The Prime Minister","The local government"],"a":[1],"e":"The European Council is not part of the British government."},{"q":"Which TWO universities compete with each other on a rowing race along the River Thames every year?","o":["University College of London","Imperial College","Cambridge University","Oxford University"],"a":[2,3],"e":"There is a popular yearly rowing race on the Thames between Oxford and Cambridge Universities."},{"q":"What is the name of Admiral Nelson’s ship?","o":["HMS Victory","Golden Hind","Canute","Grimsby"],"a":[0],"e":"Admiral Nelson’s ship was known as the HMS Victory."},{"q":"When driving in the UK, where do you need to display the tax disc which shows that the annual road tax has been paid?","o":["Anywhere inside the car","The windowscreen of the car","The wheel of the car","The boot of the car"],"a":[1],"e":"When driving in the UK, you must pay an annual road tax and display the tax disc, which shows that the tax has been paid, on the windowscreen."},{"q":"What is the capital of the UK?","o":["Edinburgh","London","Belfast","Cardiff"],"a":[1],"e":"The capital city of the UK is London."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a British film?","o":["Lawrence of Arabia","The 39 Steps","The Lord of the Rings","Women in Love"],"a":[2],"e":"The Lord of the Rings is not the British film."},{"q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["A jury is made up of members of the public who have applied to be part of it","A jury is made up of members of the public chosen at random from the local electoral register"],"a":[1],"e":"A jury is made up of members of the public chosen at random from the local electoral register."},{"q":"When is St David’s Day, patron of Wales?","o":["1st of March","17th of March","23rd of April","30th of November"],"a":[0],"e":"St David’s Day, patron of Wales is celebrated on the 1st of March."},{"q":"What name is given to the red flowers found on the battlefields of the First World War and which people wear during Remembrance Day?","o":["Blitzs","Poppies","Daffodils","Carnations"],"a":[1],"e":"Remembrance Day people wear poppies (the red flower found on the battlefields of the First World War)."},{"q":"It is a civil offence to cause harassment, alarm or distress to someone because of their religion or ethnic origin:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"It is a criminal offence to cause harassment, alarm or distress to someone because of their religion or ethnic origin."},{"q":"Where can you get contact details for all your representatives and their parties (choose TWO options)?","o":["At bookshops","Your local supermarket","Your local library","www.parliament.uk"],"a":[2,3],"e":"You can get contact details for all your representatives and their parties from your local library and from http://www.parliament.uk."},{"q":"What is the Old Bailey?","o":["A Famous criminal Court","A Scottish drink","The name of a traditional Welsh cake","A British national park"],"a":[0],"e":"The Old Bailey is probably the most famous criminal court in the world."},{"q":"What is the name of the oldest continuously working film studio facility in the world?","o":["Ealing Studios","Pinewood Studios Group","Pixar Animation Studios","Time Warner"],"a":[0],"e":"Ealing Studios has a claim to being the oldest continuously working film studio facility in the world."},{"q":"What are the British scientists Sir Ian Wilmot and Keith Campbell famous for?","o":["The first ‘test-tube baby’","The cloning of the first mammal, Dolly the sheep","The invention of the MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) scanner","The invention of the World Wide Web"],"a":[1],"e":"Sir Ian Wilmot and Keith Campbell led a team which was the first to succeed in cloning a mammal, Dolly the sheep."},{"q":"By which name is London’s west end known for?","o":["Theatreland","Operaland","Home of rugby","Home of golf"],"a":[0],"e":"London’s west end is also known as ‘Theatreland’."},{"q":"Which of the following was one of the commanders in the defeat of the Spanish Armada and one of the founders of England’s naval tradition?","o":["Alan Turing","Sir Francis Drake","James VI of Scotland","Oliver Cromwell"],"a":[1],"e":"Sir Francis Drake was one of the commanders in the defeat of the Spanish Armada and one of the founders of England’s naval tradition."}]},{"id":"test-29","name":"Test 29","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"Where was William Shakespeare born?","o":["London","Liverpool","Stratford upon Avon","Newcastle upon Tyne"],"a":[2],"e":"William Shakespeare was born in Stratford-upon-Avon."},{"q":"Which TWO islands are closely linked to the UK but are not part of it?","o":["The Isle of Man","Malta","The Channel Islands","Fiji"],"a":[0,2],"e":"The Channel Islands and the Isle of Man are closely linked to the UK but are not part of it."},{"q":"Who gave Henry VIII the son he wanted, Edward?","o":["Jane Seymour","Catherine of Aragon","Anne of Cleves","Catherine Parr"],"a":[0],"e":"Jane Seymour gave Henry VIII the son he wanted, Edward."},{"q":"The Middle Ages or medieval times relate to the time that goes from the period after the Norman Conquest until the year:","o":["1385","1485","1495","1595"],"a":[1],"e":"The period after the Norman Conquest up until about 1485 is called the Middle Ages (or the medieval period)."},{"q":"Who is the heir to the throne of the UK?","o":["Prince William","Prince Harry","Prince James","Prince Philip"],"a":[0],"e":"The King’s eldest son, Prince William (the Prince of Wales), is the heir to the throne."},{"q":"Which flower is associated with Wales?","o":["Daffodil","Rose","Shamrock","Thistle"],"a":[0],"e":"The daffodil is the flower associated with Wales."},{"q":"What was the nickname given to the Scottish poet Robert Burns?","o":["The Great","The Bard","The Lover","The Scottish"],"a":[1],"e":"Robert Burns, a Scottish poet, was Known in Scotland as ‘The Bard’."},{"q":"When was Ireland divided into two countries?","o":["In 1920","In 1922","In 1925","In 1928"],"a":[1],"e":"In 1921 a peace treaty was signed and in 1922 Ireland became two countries."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a city of the UK?","o":["Belfast","Newport","Dublin","Dundee"],"a":[2],"e":"Dublin is not a city of the UK."},{"q":"When is Bonfire Night celebrated?","o":["5th of September","5th of November","15th of September","15th of November"],"a":[1],"e":"Bonfire Night is celebrated on the 5th of November."},{"q":"What is the name of the event that refers to the 6 June 1944 when Allied Forces landed in Normandy aiming to attack Hitler’s forces in Western Europe during World War II?","o":["D-Day","Domesday","A-Day","Allies Day"],"a":[0],"e":"‘D-Day’ refers to the 6th of June 1944, when allied forces landed in Normandy aiming to attack Hitler’s forces in Western Europe during World War II."},{"q":"In the UK, many people continue to visit the countryside for holidays and for leisure activities such as walking, camping and fishing:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"Many people continue to visit the countryside for holidays and for leisure activities such as walking, camping and fishing."},{"q":"What is the name of the building where the Northern Ireland Assembly meet?","o":["Westminster","Holyrood","Senedd","Stormont"],"a":[3],"e":"In Northern Ireland elected members, known as MLAs, meet in the Northern Ireland Assembly at Stormont, in Belfast."},{"q":"Which British Prime Minister led the Conservative government from 1979 to 1990?","o":["John Major","Harold Macmillan","Margaret Thatcher","Sir Alec Douglas-Home"],"a":[2],"e":"Margaret Thatcher, Britain’s first woman Prime Minister, led the Conservative government from 1979 to 1990."},{"q":"Which of the following civilisations is known for having built roads and public buildings, creating a structure of law and having introduced new plants and animals in Britain?","o":["The Romans","The Vikings","The Normans","The Germans"],"a":[0],"e":"The Romans remained in Britain for 400 years. They built roads and public buildings, created a structure of law, and introduced new plants and animals."},{"q":"How often does the Edinburgh Festival take place?","o":["Every six months","Every summer","Every spring","Every month"],"a":[1],"e":"The Edinburgh Festival takes place in Edinburgh, Scotland, every summer."},{"q":"Which of the following countries is NOT a member of the Commonwealth?","o":["Uganda","Ghana","Malawi","Zimbabwe"],"a":[3],"e":"Zimbabwe is not a member of the Commonwealth."},{"q":"What marked the beginning of what is called ‘constitutional monarchy’?","o":["The laws passed after the Glorious Revolution","A speech given by the King","The emergence of new ideas about politics, philosophy and science","The development of the Bessemer process"],"a":[0],"e":"The laws passed after the Glorious Revolution are the beginning of what is called ‘constitutional monarchy’."},{"q":"What name is given to the day that marks the end of the Ramadan, when Muslims have fasted for a month?","o":["Vaisakhi","Eid a-Fitr","Eid ul Adha","Hanukkah"],"a":[1],"e":"Eid a-Fitr celebrates the end of Ramadan, when Muslims have fasted for a month."},{"q":"Who is the fastest person to have sailed around the world single-handed?","o":["Dame Kelly Holmes","Dame Ellen MacArthur","Jayne Torvill","Bradley Wiggins"],"a":[1],"e":"Dame Ellen MacArthur is a yachtswoman and in 2004 became the fastest person to sail around the world single-handed."},{"q":"Who makes the decisions on government policies in the UK?","o":["The monarch","Only the Prime Minister can make the decisions on government policies","The Prime Minister and the Cabinet","The monarch and the Prime Minister"],"a":[2],"e":"Decisions on government policies are made by the Prime Minister and cabinet."},{"q":"Where does the most famous sailing event in the UK take place?","o":["Holyhead, North Wales","Cowes, The Isle of Wight","Douglas, The Isle of Man","Plymouth, England"],"a":[1],"e":"The most famous sailing event in the UK takes place at Cowes on the Isle of Wight."},{"q":"How often is the electoral register updated?","o":["Every year","Every six months","Every two years","Every three years"],"a":[0],"e":"The electoral register is updated every year in September or October."},{"q":"What do you need to drive a car in the UK?","o":["A driving licence, a valid motor insurance, a MOT certificate and the road tax","A driving licence and a valid motor insurance only","A driving licence and a MOT certificate only","A driving licence only"],"a":[0],"e":"To drive a car in the UK you need a driving licence, a valid motor insurance, a MOT certificate (for cars over three years old) and the road tax."}]},{"id":"test-30","name":"Test 30","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"The action of handing out leaflets in the street or knocking on people’s doors to ask for their political support is known as:","o":["Shadowing","Canvassing","Marketing","Persuasion"],"a":[1],"e":"The action of handing out leaflets in the street or knocking on people’s doors to ask for their political support is known as canvassing."},{"q":"Who defeated the Vikings?","o":["King Alfred the Great","William I","Boudicca","James II"],"a":[0],"e":"King Alfred the Great defeated the Vikings."},{"q":"When was Queen Elizabeth II’s Diamond Jubilee celebrated?","o":["2005","2010","2011","2012"],"a":[3],"e":"Queen Elizabeth II’s Diamond Jubilee was celebrated in 2012."},{"q":"During the Middle Ages, England was an important trading nation and people came to England from abroad to trade and also to work. Where did the canal builders come from?","o":["Germany","Holland","France","Italy"],"a":[1],"e":"During the Middle Ages, England was an important trading nation and people came to England from abroad to trade and also to work. Canal builders came from Holland."},{"q":"The Elizabethan period in England was a time of growing patriotism: a feeling of pride in being English, English explorers sought new trade routes and tried to expand British trade into the Spanish colonies in the Americas:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is TRUE."},{"q":"Which of the following are ‘Crown dependencies’?","o":["The Maldives and Anglesey","Ireland and Malta","The Isle of Man and the Channel Islands","The Isle of Wight and Northern Ireland"],"a":[2],"e":"The Isle of Man and the Channel Islands are ‘Crown dependencies’."},{"q":"Which of the following is a charity that works with old people?","o":["Shelter","Age UK","Crisis","Cancer Research UK"],"a":[1],"e":"Age UK is a charity that works with old people."},{"q":"Which popular British sport can be traced back to the 15th century?","o":["Football","Golf","Rugby","Tennis"],"a":[1],"e":"The modern game of golf can be traced back to 15th century Scotland."},{"q":"How many crosses form the Union Flag?","o":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"a":[1],"e":"The Union Flag has three crosses."},{"q":"Who were the suffragettes?","o":["A group who demanded the vote for the working classes","A group who used civil disobedience to gain the vote for women","A group who campaigned for the vote of men over 21 years of age","A group who introduced new ideas about politics"],"a":[1],"e":"The suffragettes were a group who used civil disobedience to gain the vote for women."},{"q":"What does the UK offer to its permanent residents or citizens?","o":["Free undergraduate tuition fees","A right to a fair trial","VIP tickets for local events","Free parking across the country"],"a":[1],"e":"A right to a fair trial is the correct answer."},{"q":"Which of the following meals is made of a fried meal with bacon, eggs, sausage,black pudding, tomatoes, mushrooms, soda bread and potato bread?","o":["English breakfast","Haggis","Ulster fry","Sunday roast"],"a":[2],"e":"Ulster fry is a fried meal from Northern Ireland with bacon, eggs, sausage, black pudding, tomatoes, mushrooms, soda bread and potato bread."},{"q":"What can living people donate that may help other people with injuries or illnesses (choose TWO options)?","o":["Blood","Saliva","A kidney","Their liver"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Living people can donate blood and a kidney."},{"q":"Where is the office of the MPs located?","o":["10 Downing Street","The House of Commons","The House of Lords","Buckingham Palace"],"a":[1],"e":"The MP’s office is located at The House of Commons, Westminster, London, SW1A OAA."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following statements is TRUE with regards to Magistrates and Justices of the Peace (JPs) in England, Wales and Scotland?","o":["They usually work unpaid","They do not need legal qualifications","They are legally qualified","They are paid"],"a":[0,1],"e":"In England, Wales and Scotland Magistrates and Justices of the Peace (JPs) usually work unpaid and do not need legal qualifications."},{"q":"Which of the following is a showcase of mainly theatre and comedy performances?","o":["The Fringe","Diwali","Halloween","Lent"],"a":[0],"e":"The Fringe is a showcase of mainly theatre and comedy performances."},{"q":"Which of the following days are public holidays in Scotland (choose TWO options)?","o":["1st of January","24th of December","3rd of January","2nd of January"],"a":[0,3],"e":"In Scotland, the 1st of January and the 2nd of January are bank holidays."},{"q":"The House of Commons is normally more independent of the government than the House of Lords:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"The House of Lords is normally more independent of the government than the House of Commons."},{"q":"When was the Turing machine invented?","o":["In the 1920s","In the 1930s","In the 1940s","In the 1950s"],"a":[1],"e":"A Turing machine is a theoretical mathematical device invented by Alan Turing (1912-54), a British mathematician, in the 1930s."},{"q":"During which period were the House of Lords and House of Commons established?","o":["Middle Ages","Iron Age","The Roman Invasion","The Anglo-Saxon Invasion"],"a":[0],"e":"In the Middle Ages, the numbers attending Parliament increased and two separate parts, known as Houses were established. These were the House of the Commons and the House of the Lords."},{"q":"What name is given to the period of time when Christians take time to reflect and prepare for Easter?","o":["Halloween","Lent","Diwali","Hanukkah"],"a":[1],"e":"Lent is a time when Christians take time to reflect and prepare for Easter."},{"q":"In the new Church of England created by Henry VIII, who had the power to appoint bishops and order how people should worship?","o":["The Pope","The King","The Prime Minister","The Archbishop of Canterbury"],"a":[1],"e":"In the new Church of England, the king, not the Pope, would have the power to appoint bishops and order how people should worship."},{"q":"In Wales, many people speak Gaelic – a completely different language from English – and it is taught in schools and universities:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"In Wales, many people speak Welsh – a completely different language from English – and it is taught in schools and universities."},{"q":"How many years did Queen Victoria reign for?","o":["Only two years","Almost 64 years","Less than two years","Almost 50 years"],"a":[1],"e":"In 1837, Queen Victoria became queen of the UK at the age of 18. She reigned until 1901, almost 64 years."}]},{"id":"test-31","name":"Test 31","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"The UK population is ethnically diverse and changing rapidly, especially in large cities such as London:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"The UK population is ethnically diverse and changing rapidly, especially in large cities such as London."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a role of the King:","o":["To inaugurate important business in the UK","To receive foreign ambassadors and high commissioners","To entertain visiting heads of state","To make state visits overseas"],"a":[0],"e":"The King receives foreign ambassadors and high commissioners, entertains visiting heads of state, and makes state visits overseas in support of diplomatic and relationships with other counties."},{"q":"The Speaker is a neutral MP and does not represent a political party:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"The Speaker is neutral and does not represent a political party, even though he or she is an MP, represents a constituency and deals with the constituents’ problems like any other MP."},{"q":"What is the height of the London Eye?","o":["125 metres","135 metres","140 metres","152 metres"],"a":[1],"e":"The London Eye is situated on the southern bank of the River Thames and is a Ferris wheel that is 43 feet (135 metres) tall."},{"q":"How can a person facing domestic violence find a safe place to stay (choose TWO options)?","o":["Going to a hotel away from their home","Contacting the National Domestic Violence Freephone Helpline","Contacting the police","Contacting the hospital"],"a":[1,2],"e":"If you are facing domestic violence you can get help from the 24-hour National Domestic Violence Freephone Helpline on 0808 2000 247 at any time, or the police can help you find a safe place to stay."},{"q":"What name is given to the celebration when people in Great Britain set off fireworks at home or in special displays on the 5th of November?","o":["Bonfire night","Fireworks night","Bright night","The candles night"],"a":[0],"e":"Bonfire Night, 5 November, is an occasion when people in Great Britain set off fireworks at home or in special displays."},{"q":"When did the Vikings first visit Britain to raid coastal towns and take away goods and slaves?","o":["AD 600","AD 789","AD 790","AD 804"],"a":[1],"e":"The Vikings came from Denmark and Norway. They first visited Britain in AD 789 to raid coastal towns and take away goods and slaves."},{"q":"Who has control over policy and laws governing defence, foreign affairs, immigration, taxation and social security in Northern Ireland?","o":["The Irish government","The central UK government","The Northern Ireland Assembly","The monarch"],"a":[1],"e":"Policy and laws governing defence, foreign affairs, immigration, taxation and social security all remain under central UK government control. However, many other public services, such as education, are controlled by the devolved administrations."},{"q":"How many National Parks are there in England, Wales and Scotland?","o":["10","12","15","25"],"a":[2],"e":"There are 15 national parks in England, Wales and Scotland. They are areas of protected countryside that everyone can visit, and where people live, work and look after the landscape."},{"q":"Which services can be obtained from veterinary surgeons?","o":["They are places where pets can be left when you go on holidays","They are places where vaccinations and medical treatment for animals is available","They are places specifically prepare to groom pets","Amusement places for pets"],"a":[1],"e":"Vaccinations and medical treatment for animals are available from veterinary surgeons (vets)."},{"q":"The devolved governments in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland cannot control policies and laws governing taxation and social security:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"Policy and laws governing defence, foreign affairs, immigration, taxation and social security all remain under central UK government control. However, many other public services, such as education, are controlled by the devolved administrations."},{"q":"The longer life expectancy in the UK has an effect on (choose TWO options):","o":["The number of homeless people","Food scarcity","The cost of pensions","Health care"],"a":[2,3],"e":"People in the UK are living longer than ever before. This is due to improved living standards and better health care. There are now a record number of people aged 85 and over. This has an impact on the cost of pensions and health care."},{"q":"What percentage of the total British population is located in Scotland?","o":["Over 30%","Over 5%","Over 8%","Over 3%"],"a":[2],"e":"The population is very unequally distributed over the four parts of the UK. England more or less consistently makes up 84% of the total population, Wales around 5%, Scotland just over 8%, and Northern Ireland less than 3%."},{"q":"When Cromwell died, his son, Richard, became Lord Protector in his place but was not able to control the army or the government:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This is true."},{"q":"Which of the following academy awards are the equivalent of the Oscars in the UK?","o":["The Brit Awards","The BAFTA Awards","The Mercury Music Prize","The MTV Awards"],"a":[1],"e":"The annual British Academy Film Awards, hosted by the British Academy of Film and Television Arts (BAFTA), are the British equivalent of the Oscars."},{"q":"Which political party was elected after the Second World War?","o":["The Liberal Democrats","The Green Party","The Conservative Party","The Labour Party"],"a":[3],"e":"The Labour Party won the 1945 election. Clement Attlee was Prime Minister from 1945 to 1951 and led the Labour Party for 20 years."},{"q":"Which British sportsman was captain of the English cricket team?","o":["Bobby Moore","Sir Ian Botham","Sir Steve Redgrave","Sir Jackie Steward"],"a":[1],"e":"Sir Ian Botham captained the English cricket team and holds number of English Test cricket records, both for batting and for bowling."},{"q":"In which Japanese cities did the United States drop atomic bombs in August 1945?","o":["Osaka and Nagasaki","Hiroshima and Osaka","Osaka and Kyoto","Hiroshima and Nagasaki"],"a":[3],"e":"The war against Japan ended in August 1945 when the United States dropped its newly developed atom bombs on the Japanese cities of Hiroshima and Nagasaki."},{"q":"What is a bank holiday (choose TWO answers)?","o":["A festive pagan day","A private holiday","A day when banks are closed","A public holiday"],"a":[2,3],"e":"In the UK, there are public holidays each year called bank holidays, when banks and many other businesses are closed for the day."},{"q":"By 1200, the English ruled an area of Scotland known as the Pale, around Edinburgh:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"By 1200, the English ruled an area of Ireland known as the Pale, around Dublin."},{"q":"Which of the following countries does not take part in the Six Nations Championship?","o":["Wales","Italy","Germany","France"],"a":[2],"e":"The most famous rugby union competition is the Six Nations Championship between England, Ireland, Scotland, Wales, France and Italy."},{"q":"Who became Queen after Edward VI?","o":["Elizabeth I","Queen Mary","Anne Boleyn","Catherine of Aragon"],"a":[1],"e":"Edward VI died at the age of 15 after ruling for just over six years, and his half-sister Mary became queen."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT classified as a criminal offence in the UK?","o":["Selling alcohol to anyone under the age of 18","Selling or buying drugs","Carrying a knife","Unfair dismissal or discrimination in the workplace"],"a":[3],"e":"Cases of unfair dismissal or discrimination in the workplace are considered civil offences."},{"q":"The right to life and the right to liberty and security are some of the principles included in the European Convention of Human Rights:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This is true."}]},{"id":"test-32","name":"Test 32","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"British values and principles are based on history and traditions and are protected by law, customs and expectations.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"When did the first Christian communities begin to appear in Britain?","o":["During the 1st and 2nd centuries AD","During the 2nd and 3rd centuries AD","During the 3rd and 4th centuries AD","During the 4th and 5th centuries AD"],"a":[2],"e":"It was during the 3rd and 4th centuries AD that the first Christian communities began to appear in Britain."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a devolved administration?","o":["The Scottish Parliament","The Welsh government","The Irish Parliament","The Northern Ireland Assembly"],"a":[2],"e":"There are three devolved administrations in the UK, this include: the Welsh government, the Scottish Parliament and the Northern Ireland Assembly"},{"q":"Who built a wall in the north of England to keep out the Picts (ancestors of the Scottish people)?","o":["Emperor Claudius","Napoleon","Emperor Hadrian","Julius Caesar"],"a":[2],"e":"Areas of what is now Scotland were never conquered by the Romans, and the Emperor Hadrian built a wall in the north of England to keep out the Picts (ancestors of the Scottish people)."},{"q":"Who looks after Edinburgh Castle?","o":["Historic Environment Scotland","SEPA","The Scottish Parliament","The National Trust"],"a":[0],"e":"Edinburgh castle is a dominant feature of the skyline in Edinburgh, Scotland. It has a long history, dating back to the early Middle Ages. It is looked after by Historic Environment Scotland."},{"q":"Each MP represents a parliamentary constituency, which is a small area of the country:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"Who were the pioneers of the IVF (in-vitro fertilisation) therapy?","o":["Sir Frank Whittle and Sir Christopher Cockerell","Sir Robert Edwards and Patrick Steptoe","Francis Creek and John Logie Baird","Sir Ian Wilmot and Keith Campbell"],"a":[1],"e":"IVF (in-vitro fertilisation) therapy for the treatment of infertility was pioneered in Britain by physiologist Sir Robert Edwards (1925-) and gynaecologist Patrick Steptoe (1913-88). The world’s first ‘test-tube baby’ was born in Oldham, Lancashire in 1978."},{"q":"Who led the establishment of the National Health System (NHS) in 1948?","o":["Aneurin (Nye) Bevan","Harold Macmillan","Clement Attlee","William Beveridge"],"a":[0],"e":"In 1948, Aneurin (Nye) Bevan, the Minister for Health, led the establishment of the National Health Service (NHS), which guaranteed a minimum standard of health care for all, free at the point of use."},{"q":"Which of the following countries were granted their independence in 1947 (choose THREE answers)?","o":["India","Bangladesh","Pakistan","Ceylon (Sri Lanka)"],"a":[0,2,3],"e":"In 1947, independence was granted to nine countries, including India, Pakistan and Ceylon (now Sri Lanka)."},{"q":"In the UK, bank holidays of no religious significance are celebrated at the beginning of May, in late May or early June, and in August:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"Where can Admiral Nelson’s ship known as HMS Victory be visited?","o":["Plymouth","Bournemouth","Portsmouth","Hastings"],"a":[2],"e":"Admiral Nelson’s ship, HMS Victory, can be visited in Portsmouth."},{"q":"Who were the Puritans?","o":["A group of Catholics who advocated strict and simple religious doctrine and worship","A group of Protestants who advocated strict and simple religious doctrine and worship","A group of landowners from the 18th century","A group of local leaders that surged in Wales with strict religious views"],"a":[1],"e":"The Puritans were a group of Protestants who advocated strict and simple religious doctrine and worship. They did not agree with the king’s religious views and disliked his reforms of the Church of England."},{"q":"What TWO different types of rugby are practised in the UK?","o":["Union","League","Rugby open","Free style"],"a":[0,1],"e":"There are two different types of rugby, which have different rules: union and league."},{"q":"What do William Blake, John Keats and Lord Byron have in common?","o":["They were British poets of the 19th century","They were British painters of the Swinging Sixties","They were musicians of the middle ages","They were British scientist"],"a":[0],"e":"Poetry was very popular in the 19th century, with poets such as William Blake, John Keats, Lord Byron, Percy Shelley, Alfred Lord Tennyson, and Robert and Elizabeth Browning."},{"q":"The Laurence Olivier Awards take place annually at different venues in Liverpool:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"The Laurence Olivier Awards take place annually at different venues in London."},{"q":"An additional rented piece of land where people can grow fruit and vegetables is known as:","o":["Parcel","Garden","Terrain","Allotment"],"a":[3],"e":"In the UK, some people rent additional land called ‘an allotment’, where they grow fruit and vegetables."},{"q":"What is the name of the battle where the Scottish led by Robert the Bruce defeated the English in 1314?","o":["The Battle of Agincourt","The Battle of Bannockburn","The Battle of Trafalgar","The Battle of Waterloo"],"a":[1],"e":"In 1314 the Scottish, led by Robert the Bruce, defeated the English at the Battle of Bannockburn."},{"q":"Who took the English throne after the death of Henry VIII?","o":["Elizabeth I","Edward VI","Queen Mary","James I"],"a":[1],"e":"Henry VIII was succeeded by his son Edward VI."},{"q":"Carrying a weapon is a criminal offence, unless it is for self-defence:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"It is a criminal offence to carry a weapon of any kind, even if it is for self-defence. This includes a gun, a knife or anything that is made or adapted to cause injury."},{"q":"Prohibition of thought, conscience and religion is one of the principles included in the European Convention of Human Rights:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"Freedom of thought, conscience and religion is one of the principles included in the European Convention of Human Rights."},{"q":"Which TWO pop music groups became popular in the 1960s?","o":["Queen","The Rolling Stones","The Beatles","The Carpenters"],"a":[1,2],"e":"Two well-known pop music groups in the 1960s were The Beatles and The Rolling Stones."},{"q":"Which of the following words comes from the Viking language?","o":["Table","Scunthorpe","Pencil","Rabbit"],"a":[1],"e":"Words such as Grimsby and Scunthorpe come from the Viking languages."},{"q":"Eid ul Adha is the day when Muslims thank Allah for giving them the strength to complete the fast:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"The day when Muslims thank Allah for giving them the strength to complete the fast is called Eid a-Fitr."},{"q":"Will a non-UK national living in the UK and looking for work, starting work or setting up as self-employed need a National Insurance number?","o":["Yes","No"],"a":[0],"e":"A non-UK national living in the UK and looking for work, starting work or setting up as self-employed will need a National Insurance number. However, you can start work without one."}]},{"id":"test-33","name":"Test 33","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"What is the name of the highest mountain in Wales?","o":["Lake District","Snowdon","South Downs","Giant’s Causeway"],"a":[1],"e":"Snowdon is the highest mountain in Wales."},{"q":"Where did the Vikings come from?","o":["France and Germany","Sweden and Finland","Denmark and Norway","Sweden and Germany"],"a":[2],"e":"The Vikings came from Denmark and Norway."},{"q":"Who led a Scottish army against Oliver Cromwell just after England had been declared a Republic?","o":["Charles I","Charles II","Henry V","James I"],"a":[1],"e":"Charles II was crowned king of Scotland and led a Scottish army into England. Cromwell defeated this army in the Battles of Dunbar and Worcester."},{"q":"How many pence are there in a pound?","o":["100 pence","50 pence","60 pence","10 pence"],"a":[0],"e":"There are 100 pence in a pound."},{"q":"When are local elections for councillors held in most local authorities?","o":["In May","In March","In February","In June"],"a":[0],"e":"For most local authorities, local elections for councillors are held in May every year."},{"q":"What sort of stories were collected by the Canterbury Tales written by Geoffrey Chaucer?","o":["Love stories","Children’s stories","Travellers’ stories","War stories"],"a":[2],"e":"In the years leading up to 1400, Geoffrey Chaucer wrote a series of poems in English about a group of people going to Canterbury on a pilgrimage. The people decided to tell each other stories on the journey, and the poems describe the travellers and some of the stories they told. This collection poems is called The Canterbury Tales."},{"q":"When did rugby originate?","o":["In the 15th century","In the 16th century","In the 18th century","In the 19th century"],"a":[3],"e":"Rugby originated in England in the early 19th century and is very popular in the UK today."},{"q":"Which of the following lines does NOT form part of the citizenship pledge?","o":["I will give my loyalty to the United Kingdom","I will uphold its democratic values","Long live our noble King!","I will observe its laws faithfully"],"a":[2],"e":"Long live our noble King! is the second sentence of the National Anthem of the UK."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT protected by the laws of discrimination?","o":["Traditional customs","Disability","Pregnancy and Maternity","Religion or Belief"],"a":[0],"e":"UK laws ensure that people are not treated unfairly in any area of life or work because of their age, disability, sex, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion or belief, sexuality or marital status."},{"q":"Which Act abolished the old pocket and rotten boroughs and allowed more parliamentary seats to the towns and cities?","o":["The Reform Act of 1832","The Reform Act of 1842","The Emancipation Act 1833","The Emancipation Act 1835"],"a":[0],"e":"The Reform Act of 1832 had greatly increased the number of people with the right to vote. The act also abolished the old pocket and rotten boroughs and more parliamentary seats were given to the towns and cities."},{"q":"In the UK, if the jury finds a defendant guilty, the judge decides the penalty:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"Which of the following countries is a member of the Commonwealth?","o":["Greece","Austria","Mexico","Bangladesh"],"a":[3],"e":"Bangladesh is a member of the Commonwealth."},{"q":"During the Crusades, European Christians fought for the control of:","o":["The holy land","Ireland","Central Europe","England"],"a":[0],"e":"European Christians fought for control of the Holy Land."},{"q":"The line ‘to be or not to be’ belongs to which of the following plays or poems written by William Shakespeare?","o":["Romeo and Juliet","Hamlet","As You Like It","Henry V"],"a":[1],"e":"‘To be or not to be’ is a line from William Shakespeare’s play Hamlet."},{"q":"Charles I wanted the worship of the Church of England to include more ceremony and introduced a revised Prayer Book.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a principle included in the European Convention of Human Rights?","o":["Prohibition of torture","Prohibition of immoral thoughts","Freedom of expression (speech)","Prohibition of slavery and forced labour"],"a":[1],"e":"Prohibition of immoral thoughts is not one of the principles included in the European Convention of Human Rights."},{"q":"What was the name of the project through which the atomic bomb was developed?","o":["The Manhattan project","The Michigan project","The Cambridge project","The Manchester project"],"a":[0],"e":"Scientists led by Ernest Rutherford, working at Manchester and then Cambridge University, were the first to ‘split the atom’ and took part in the Manhattan Project in the United States, which developed the atomic bomb."},{"q":"Who supported the Parliament during the Civil War that began in 1642?","o":["The Squareheads","The Cavaliers","The Suffragettes","The Roundheads"],"a":[3],"e":"A civil war between the king and Parliament began in 1642. The country split into those who supported the king (the Cavaliers) and those who supported Parliament (the Roundheads)."},{"q":"Which of the following poets wrote poems inspired by Scotland and the traditional stories and songs from the area on the borders of Scotland and England?","o":["Geoffrey Chaucer","William Blake","Sir Walter Scott","William Wordsworth"],"a":[2],"e":"Sir Walter Scot t wrote poems inspired by Scotland and the traditional stories and songs from the area on the borders of Scotland and England"},{"q":"A system of government where the whole adult population gets a say is known as:","o":["Monarchy","Republic","Communism","Democracy"],"a":[3],"e":"Democracy is a system of government where the whole adult population gets a say. This might be by direct voting or by choosing representatives to make decisions on their behalf."},{"q":"Which of the following statements is correct?","o":["Arranged marriages refer to marriages where both parties agree to the marriage","Arranged marriages refer to marriages where one or both parties do not give their consent to enter into the partnership"],"a":[0],"e":"Arranged marriages refer to marriages where both parties agree to the marriage."},{"q":"The devolved governments in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland have the power to legislate on any issues that affect them:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"Devolved governments in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland have the power to legislate on certain issues."},{"q":"What name is given to the members of the House of Commons?","o":["MPs","AMs","Peers","MEPs"],"a":[0],"e":"All of the elected MPs form the House of Commons."},{"q":"Which golf ‘Major’ tournament is held outside the United States?","o":["The Masters Tournament","The U.S. Open","The Open Championship","The PGA Championship"],"a":[2],"e":"The open championship is the only ‘Major’ tournament held outside the United States."}]},{"id":"test-34","name":"Test 34","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"As part of the citizenship ceremony, new citizens pledge their loyalty to the United Kingdom and to respect its rights and freedoms.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"Which of the following countries is NOT a member of the Commonwealth?","o":["Ghana","Cyprus","Greece","Sierra Leone"],"a":[2],"e":"Greece is not a member of the Commonwealth."},{"q":"The Welsh government has 30 Senedd members.","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"The Welsh government has 60 Senedd members."},{"q":"Who was the first person to print books using a printing press in England?","o":["William Caxton","Geoffrey Chaucer","John Barbour","Jane Austen"],"a":[0],"e":"William Caxton was the first person in England to print books using a printing press."},{"q":"In England, Wales and Scotland, most minor criminal cases are dealt with in a Magistrates’ Court:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"In England, Wales and Northern Ireland, most minor criminal cases are dealt with in a Magistrates’ Court. In Scotland, minor criminal offences go to a Justice of the Peace Court."},{"q":"When did the UK sign the European Convention of Human Rights?","o":["1940","1945","1950","1955"],"a":[2],"e":"The UK was one of the first countries to sign the Convention in 1950."},{"q":"Which is the most famous rugby union competition?","o":["The Grand National","The Six Nations Championship","The All England Championship","The Super League"],"a":[1],"e":"The most famous rugby union competition is the Six Nations Championship between England, Ireland, Scotland, Wales, France and Italy."},{"q":"After Oliver Cromwell defeated the Scottish army in the Battles of Dunbar and Worcester, Charles II escaped from Worcester, famously hiding in an oak tree on one occasion, and eventually fled to Europe:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"Which of the following is an ancient festival and has roots in the pagan festival to mark the beginning of winter?","o":["Lent","Easter","Halloween","Hogmanay"],"a":[2],"e":"Halloween, 31 October, is and ancient festival and has roots in the pagan festival to mark the beginning of winter."},{"q":"Which TWO British kings believed in the ‘Divine Right of Kings’?","o":["Richard I","James I","Charles I","Henry V"],"a":[1,2],"e":"James I and his son Charles I both believed in the ‘Divine Right of Kings’: the idea that the king was directly appointed by God to rule. They thought that the king should be able to act without having to seek approval from Parliament."},{"q":"What was the name of the European Union when it was first established?","o":["European Political Union","Union of European Countries","European Economic Community","Europe United"],"a":[2],"e":"West Germany, France, Belgium, Italy, Luxembourg and the Netherlands formed the European Economic Community (EEC) in 1957. The EEC became a part of the European Union when it was formed in 1993."},{"q":"By 1200, the English ruled an area of Scotland known as the Pale, around Edinburgh:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"By 1200, the English ruled an area of Ireland known as the Pale, around Dublin."},{"q":"Which of the following British poets was inspired by nature?","o":["Geoffrey Chaucer","William Blake","Sir Walter Scott","William Wordsworth"],"a":[3],"e":"William Wordsworth was inspired by nature."},{"q":"Which Act incorporated the European Convention of Human Rights into UK law?","o":["The Discrimination Act 1995","The Equality Act 2010","The Freedom Act 2012","The Human Rights Act 1998"],"a":[3],"e":"The Human Rights Act 1998 incorporated the European Convention of Human Rights into UK law."},{"q":"After the Second World War, the UK was exhausted economically.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"Although the UK had won the war, the country was exhausted economically after WWII."},{"q":"What percentage of the total British population is located in Northern Ireland?","o":["30%","5%","8%","Less than 3%"],"a":[3],"e":"The population is very unequally distributed over the four parts of the UK. England more or less consistently makes up 84% of the total population, Wales around 5%, Scotland just over 8%, and Northern Ireland less than 3%."},{"q":"Bonfire Night remembers the day when a group of Catholics led by Guy Fawkes killed the Protestant king with a bomb in the Houses of Parliament:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"Bonfire Night remembers the day when a group of Catholics led by Guy Fawkes failed in their plan to kill the Protestant king with a bomb in the Houses of Parliament."},{"q":"Who was Florence Nightingale?","o":["A nurse who worked in military hospitals, treating soldiers who were fighting in the Crimean War","One of the tribal leaders who fought against the Romans","A scientist who proposed that enemy aircraft could be detected by radio waves","A mathematician who was influential in the development of computer science"],"a":[0],"e":"Florence Nightingale was a nurse who worked in military hospitals, treating soldiers who were fighting in the Crimean War."},{"q":"Where is the Scottish Grand National horse racing event celebrated?","o":["St Andrews","Ayr","Fife","Glasgow"],"a":[1],"e":"The Scottish Grand National horse racing event is celebrated at Ayr."},{"q":"Arranged marriages are not acceptable in the UK:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"Arranged marriages, where both parties agree to the marriage, are acceptable in the UK."},{"q":"Who was the Prime Minister of the UK from 1945 to 1951?","o":["Margaret Thatcher","John Major","Clement Attlee","Oliver Cromwell"],"a":[2],"e":"Clement Attlee was Prime Minister from 1945 to 1951 and led the Labour Party for 20 years."},{"q":"Who represents the UK to the rest of the world?","o":["The Prime Minister","Prince Harry","Prince William","The King"],"a":[3],"e":"The King represents the UK to the rest of the world."},{"q":"Great Britain refers only to England, Scotland and Wales, not to Northern Ireland.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"Queen Elizabeth I was a Catholic:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"Queen Elizabeth I was a Protestant."}]},{"id":"test-35","name":"Test 35","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"What is the meaning of the term the ‘Divine Right of Kings’?","o":["The idea that the king was appointed by landowners","The Parliament and the King shared responsibilities","The King could rule the country but with the support of the Parliament","The idea that the king was directly appointed by God to rule"],"a":[3],"e":"The ‘Divine Right of Kings’ refers to the idea that the king was directly appointed by God to rule and that the king should be able to act without having to seek approval from Parliament."},{"q":"Who was the first person to lead a Roman invasion in Britain in 55 BC?","o":["Emperor Claudius","Napoleon","Julius Caesar","William Caxton"],"a":[2],"e":"Julius Caesar led a Roman invasion of Britain in 55 BC. This was unsuccessful and for nearly 100 years Britain remained separate from the Roman Empire."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT the responsibility of the MPs?","o":["Scrutinise and comment on what the government is doing","Represent everyone in the constituency","Protect life and property","Debate important national issues"],"a":[2],"e":"MPs have a number of different responsibilities. They represent everyone in their constituency, help to create new laws, scrutinize and comment on what the government is doing and debate important national issues."},{"q":"When Henry VIII took the title ‘King of Ireland’, English laws were introduced and everyone except for local leaders were expected to follow the instructions of the Lord Lieutenants in Dublin:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"When Henry VIII took the title ‘King of Ireland’, English laws were introduced and local leaders were expected to follow the instructions of the Lord Lieutenants in Dublin."},{"q":"When is the anniversary of the Battle of the Boyne celebrated in Northern Ireland?","o":["March","May","June","July"],"a":[3],"e":"In Northern Ireland, the anniversary of the Battle of the Boyne is celebrated in July and is also a public holiday."},{"q":"The clock tower is named ‘Elizabeth Tower’ in honour of ___________.","o":["Queen Elizabeth II’s Diamond Jubilee in 2012","Queen Elizabeth I’s Diamond Jubilee in 2012","Queen Elizabeth II’s Golden Jubilee in 2002","Queen Elizabeth I’s Golden Jubilee in 2002"],"a":[0],"e":"The clock tower is named ‘Elizabeth Tower’ in honour of Queen Elizabeth II’s Diamond Jubilee in 2012."},{"q":"When was Lincoln Cathedral built?","o":["In the 19th century","In medieval times","After the Roman invasion","In the 18th century"],"a":[1],"e":"Lincoln Cathedral was built in the middle ages or medieval times."},{"q":"Which of the following territories forms part of the UK?","o":["Scotland","The Isle of Man","Fiji","St Helena"],"a":[0],"e":"The UK is formed by England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland."},{"q":"How old was Edward VI when he died?","o":["15 years old","1 year old","12 years old","17 years old"],"a":[0],"e":"Edward VI died at the age of 15 after ruling for just over six years, and his half-sister Mary became queen."},{"q":"What name was given to those who supported the king during the Civil War that began in 1642?","o":["The Cavaliers","The Suffragettes","The Suffragettes","The Squareheads"],"a":[0],"e":"During the Civil War that started in 1642 those who supported the king (the Cavaliers) and those who supported Parliament (the Roundheads)."},{"q":"The right to a fair trial is not included amongst the principles of the European Convention of Human Rights:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"The right to a fair trial is one of the principles of the European Convention of Human Rights."},{"q":"Why was Queen Mary known as ‘Bloody Mary’?","o":["She used to drink a lot","Because of all the blood from the wars she caused","She was a devout Catholic and persecuted Protestants","She used to punish people"],"a":[2],"e":"Queen Mary was a devout Catholic and persecuted Protestants (for this reason, she became known as ‘Bloody Mary’)."},{"q":"Which of the following sentences is true?","o":["The UK is governed by the parliament sitting in Edinburgh.","Scotland and Wales have parliaments or assemblies of their own, but not Northern Ireland.","Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland also have parliaments or assemblies of their own, with devolved powers in defined areas.","The UK is governed by the parliament sitting in Glasgow."],"a":[2],"e":"Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland also have parliaments or assemblies of their own, with devolved powers in defined areas."},{"q":"Which of the following lines from Shakespeare’s plays and poems is often still quoted?","o":["A daffodil by another name","All the world’s a stage","We shall never surrender","You shall be with me"],"a":[1],"e":"‘All the world’s a stage’ is a line from William Shakespeare’s play As You Like It."},{"q":"How many volunteers did the National Trust have when it first was formed in 1895?","o":["Two","Three","200","300"],"a":[1],"e":"The National Trust was founded in 1895 by three volunteers."},{"q":"In the UK, brutality and violence in the home is not a crime:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"In the UK, brutality and violence in the home is a serious crime."},{"q":"When did modern tennis evolve in England?","o":["In the early 18th century","In the late 18th century","In the early 19th century","In the late 19th century"],"a":[3],"e":"Modern tennis evolved in England in the late 19th century."},{"q":"UK laws ensure that people are not treated unfairly in any area of life or work because of their age, disability, sex, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion or belief, sexuality or marital status:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"The population is very equally distributed over the four parts of the UK:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"The population is very unequally distributed over the four parts of the UK. England more or less consistently makes up 84% of the total population, Wales around 5%, Scotland just over 8%, and Northern Ireland less than 3%."},{"q":"Where was Anne Boleyn executed?","o":["In Conwy Castle","At the Tower of London","At the Houses of the Parliament","In France"],"a":[1],"e":"Anne Boleyn was executed at the tower of London."},{"q":"At the beginning of the Middle Ages, Ireland was not an independent country:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"At the beginning of the Middle Ages, Ireland was an independent country."},{"q":"Since 1997, some powers have been devolved from the central government to give people in Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland more control over matters that directly affect them:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"Where did Mary Stuart, the Queen of Scotland, spend most of her childhood?","o":["Spain","Germany","Holland","France"],"a":[3],"e":"Mary Stuart, the Queen of Scotland, spent most of her childhood in France."},{"q":"During the Middle Ages, several cathedrals had windows of stained glass, telling stories about:","o":["The journey of pilgrims to Canterbury","Noah’s Ark","The Bible and Christian saints","Medieval priests"],"a":[2],"e":"During the middle ages, several cathedrals had windows of stained glass, telling stories about the Bible and Christian saints. The glass in York Minster is a famous example."}]},{"id":"test-36","name":"Test 36","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"Where does the UK Parliament sit?","o":["Westminster","Stormont","Holyrood","Senedd"],"a":[0],"e":"The UK is governed by the parliament sitting in Westminster."},{"q":"When did King Edward I of England introduced the Statute of Rhuddlan by which Wales annexed to the Crown of England?","o":["In 1284","In 1235","In 1304","In 1340"],"a":[0],"e":"In 1284 King Edward I of England introduced the Statute of Rhuddlan, which annexed Wales to the Crown of England."},{"q":"Which of the following public services can be controlled by the devolved administrations?","o":["Defence","Immigration","Foreign Affairs","Education"],"a":[3],"e":"Some public services, such as education, are controlled by the devolved administrations."},{"q":"The Senedd has been suspended on a few occasions:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"The Northern Ireland Assembly has been suspended on a few occasions."},{"q":"The settlements of Scottish and English Protestants in Ulster (the northern province of Ireland) during the reigns of Elizabeth I and James I, who took over the land from Catholic landholders is known as:","o":["Plantations","Cavaliers","Puritans","Pale"],"a":[0],"e":"These settlements were known as plantations."},{"q":"Which of the following jobs is usually unpaid in England, Wales and Scotland?","o":["Magistrates and Justices of the Peace","Civil Lawyers","Wardens","Policeman"],"a":[0],"e":"In England, Wales and Scotland, Magistrates and Justices of the Peace (JPs) are members of the local community and they usually work unpaid and do not need legal qualifications."},{"q":"Which of the following is a traditional character of the pantomimes?","o":["The gentleman","The Dame","A unicorn","A mermaid"],"a":[1],"e":"One of the traditional characters of the pantomimes is the Dame, a woman played by a man."},{"q":"In the UK, most people live in towns and cities and very small parts of Britain are countryside:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. Most people live in towns and cities but much of Britain is still countryside."},{"q":"What was the symbol of Lancaster during the Wars of the Roses?","o":["A Red Rose","A White Rose","A Green Rose","A Purple Rose"],"a":[0],"e":"During the Wars of the Roses, the symbol of Lancaster was a red rose."},{"q":"After King Alfred the Great defeated the Vikings, many of the Viking invaders stayed in Britain, especially in the east and north of England in an area called:","o":["Bayeux","Stonehenge","Danelaw","Canute"],"a":[2],"e":"Many of the Viking invaders stayed in Britain – especially in the east and north of England in an area known as the Danelaw."},{"q":"The Reform Act of 1832 increased the number of people with the right to vote:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"True. The Reform Act of 1832 had greatly increased the number of people with the right to vote."},{"q":"Which of the following is a famous example of stained glass?","o":["York Minster","Bayeux","Vindolanda","Housesteads"],"a":[0],"e":"York Minster is a famous example of stained glass used on the windows of some cathedrals during the middle ages."},{"q":"Who is the head of the Church of England?","o":["The Pope","The monarch","The Archbishop of Canterbury","The Prime Minister"],"a":[1],"e":"The monarch is the head of the Church of England."},{"q":"Who is responsible for managing relationships with foreign countries?","o":["The Chancellor of the Exchequer","The Foreign Secretary","The Home Secretary","The King"],"a":[1],"e":"The Foreign Secretary is responsible for managing relationships with foreign countries."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following poems are from the Middle Ages?","o":["The Canterbury Tales","Paradise Lost","Sir Gawain and the Green Knight","MacBeth"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Poems which survive from the Middle Ages include Chaucer’s Canterbury Tales and a poem called Sir Gawain and the Green Knight, about one of the knights at the court of King Arthur."},{"q":"If a husband forces his wife to have sex, he can be charged with rape:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"True. Any man who forces a woman to have sex, including a woman’s husband, can be charged with rape."},{"q":"Who defeated the English at the Battle of Bannockburn in 1314?","o":["Robert the Bruce","King Edward I","Admiral Nelson","The Normans"],"a":[0],"e":"In 1314 the Scottish, led by Robert the Bruce, defeated the English at the Battle of Bannockburn."},{"q":"In the UK, owners are responsible for keeping their dog under control and for cleaning up after the animal in public places:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"How long did the Hundred Years War fought between England and France last for?","o":["100 years","101 years","115 years","116 years"],"a":[3],"e":"English kings fought a long war with France, called the Hundred Years War (even though it actually lasted 116 years )."},{"q":"When did Gordon Brown take over as Prime Minister from his predecessor Tony Blair?","o":["In 2007","In 2008","In 2009","In 2010"],"a":[0],"e":"Gordon Brown took over as Prime Minister from Tony Blair in 2007."},{"q":"The Life in the UK test is usually taken is English, although special arrangements can be made if you wish to take it in French.","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. The Life in the UK test is usually taken is English, although special arrangements can be made if you wish to take it in Welsh or Scottish Gaelic."},{"q":"Where in England was one of the Anglo-Saxon kings buried with treasure and armour?","o":["Maiden Castle, Dorset","Sutton Hoo, Suffolk","Conwy Castle, Wales","The Tower of London"],"a":[1],"e":"The burial place of one of the Anglo-Saxon kings was at Sutton Hoo in modern Suffolk."},{"q":"Which British writer wrote satirical novels including Brideshead Revisited?","o":["Evelyn Waugh","Sir Arthur Conan Doyle","Sir Kingsley Amis","Thomas Hardy"],"a":[0],"e":"Evelyn Waugh wrote satirical novels, including Decline and Fall and Scoop. He is perhaps best known for Brideshead Revisited."},{"q":"Where is the best preserved prehistoric village in northern Europe located?","o":["Cardiff, Wales","Edinburgh, the capital of the Scotland","Orkney, off the north coast of Scotland","The Isle of Wight"],"a":[2],"e":"Skara Brae on Orkney, off the north coast of Scotland, is the best preserved prehistoric village in northern Europe."}]},{"id":"test-37","name":"Test 37","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"Which of the following sports began in the UK?","o":["Cricket","Football","Rugby","All of the above"],"a":[3],"e":"Many famous sports, including cricket, football, lawn tennis, golf and rugby, began in Britain."},{"q":"Which famous architect designed Dumfries House?","o":["Sir Christopher Wren","Robert Adam","Sir Norman Foster","Dame Zaha Hadid"],"a":[1],"e":"The Scottish architect Robert Adam designed the inside decoration as well as the building itself in great houses such as Dumfries House in Scotland."},{"q":"What did the Education Act of 1944 introduce?","o":["Free primary education in England and Wales","Free secondary education in England and Wales","Free high school education in England and Wales","Free university education in England and Wales"],"a":[1],"e":"The Education Act 1944 (often called ‘The Butler Act’), introduced free secondary education in England and Wales."},{"q":"Which of the following tribes invaded Britain?","o":["The Jutes","The Angles","The Saxons","All of the above"],"a":[3],"e":"The Roman army left Britain in AD 410 to defend other parts of the Roman Empire and never returned. Britain was again invaded by tribes from northern Europe: the Jutes, the Angles and the Saxons."},{"q":"Many schools organise events such as book sales, toy sales or bringing food to sell to raise money for extra equipment or out-of-school activities.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is TRUE."},{"q":"Do you need a TV licence for watching TV on a mobile phone?","o":["Yes","No"],"a":[0],"e":"Everyone in the UK with a TV, computer or other medium which can be used for watching TV must have a television licence."},{"q":"What do you need to apply to become a British citizen?","o":["Be single and without children","Be over 25 years old","Have lived in the UK for 3 years","Have a good understanding of life in the UK"],"a":[3],"e":"To apply to become a permanent resident or citizen of the UK, you will need to have a good understanding of life in the UK."},{"q":"Which of the following is a British overseas territory located in the South Atlantic?","o":["Guernsey","Northern Ireland","The Falkland Islands","Jersey"],"a":[2],"e":"The Falkland Islands, a British overseas territory is located in the South Atlantic."},{"q":"Who wrote the novel Charlie and the Chocolate Factory?","o":["Rudyard Kipling","Sir William Golding","Charles Dickens","Roald Dahl"],"a":[3],"e":"Roald Dahl is the author of Charlie and the Chocolate Factory."},{"q":"The Normans used a system of land ownership known as __________.","o":["Clergy","Chartism","Feudalism","Imperialism"],"a":[2],"e":"The Normans used a system of land ownership known as feudalism."},{"q":"Who were the Huguenots?","o":["Protestant refugees from France","Catholic refugees from Spain","Catholic refugees from Italy","Protestant refugees from Germany"],"a":[0],"e":"Between 1680 and 1720 many refugees called Huguenots came from France. They were Protestants and had been persecuted for their religion. Many were educated and skilled and worked as scientists, in banking, or in weaving or other crafts."},{"q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["The official name of the country is the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Southern Ireland.","The official name of the country is Great Britain and Southern Ireland.","The official name of the country is the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland.","The official name of the country is the United Kingdom and Great Ireland."],"a":[2],"e":"The official name of the country is the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland."},{"q":"When was slavery abolished throughout the British Empire?","o":["1833","1843","1844","1861"],"a":[0],"e":"In 1833 the Emancipation Act abolished slavery throughout the British Empire."},{"q":"Magistrates and Justices of the Peace (JPs) are members of the _________.","o":["Government","Local community","British Society","NHS"],"a":[1],"e":"Magistrates and Justices of the Peace (JPs) are members of the local community."},{"q":"Where can you find details about the small claims procedure?","o":["From the local community","At the police station","At your local County Court or Sheriff Court","At any local shop"],"a":[2],"e":"You can get details about the small claims procedure from your local County Court or Sheriff Court."},{"q":"When is Mothering Sunday?","o":["The Sunday three weeks before Easter.","The Sunday three weeks after Easter.","The Sunday three weeks before Christmas.","The Sunday three weeks after Christmas."],"a":[0],"e":"Mothering Sunday (or Mother’s day) is the Sunday three weeks before Easter. Children send cards or buy gifts for their mothers."},{"q":"What should you do if your vehicle is over three years old?","o":["Take it to the MOT every year.","Take it to the MOT 2 years.","Take it to the MOT 3 years.","Take it to the MOT six months."],"a":[0],"e":"If you vehicle is over three years old, you must take it to the Ministry of Transport (MOT) test every year."},{"q":"Which countries fought alongside Britain against Russia during the Crimean War?","o":["France and Cyprus","Germany and Cyprus","Germany and Turkey","France and Turkey"],"a":[3],"e":"From 1853 to 1856, Britain fought with Turkey and France against Russia in the Crimean War."},{"q":"On average, boys leave school with better qualifications than girls.","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. On average, girls leave school with better qualifications than boys."},{"q":"When did William of Orange invade England to proclaim himself king?","o":["1689","1690","1688","1687"],"a":[2],"e":"In 1688, important Protestants in England asked William of Orange to invade England and proclaim himself king. When William reached England, there was no resistance. James fled to France and William took over the throne, becoming William III in England, Wales and Ireland, and William II of Scotland."},{"q":"Which British painter is considered to be the artist who raised the profile of landscape painting?","o":["Joseph Turner","David Allan","John Constable","Sir John Lavery"],"a":[0],"e":"Joseph Turner (1775-1851) was an influential landscape painter in a modern style. He is considered the artist who raised the profile of landscape painting."},{"q":"The development of the Bessemer process during the Industrial Revolution led to the development of which two industries:","o":["Shipbuilding industry","Chemical industry","Railways industry","Car industry"],"a":[0,2],"e":"The development of the Bessemer process for the mass production of steel led to the development of the shipbuilding industry and the railways."},{"q":"What is the length of the Bayeux tapestry?","o":["130 feet","230 feet","330 feet","320 feet"],"a":[1],"e":"The Bayeux Tapestry is nearly 70 metres (230 feet) long and is embroidered with coloured wool."},{"q":"Which of the following countries is not a member of the Commonwealth?","o":["Swaziland","Trinidad and Tobago","Dominica","Costa Rica"],"a":[3],"e":"Costa Rica is not a member of the Commonwealth."}]},{"id":"test-38","name":"Test 38","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"In which of the following years did England win the World Cup?","o":["1974","1986","1982","1966"],"a":[3],"e":"England won the World Cup in 1966 when it was captained by Bobby Moore."},{"q":"During the Battle of Trafalgar Britain fought against:","o":["France and Belgium","France and Spain","France and Italy","Italy and Spain"],"a":[1],"e":"Britain’s navy fought against combined French and Spanish fleets, winning the Battle of Trafalgar in 1805."},{"q":"What is the name given to the first formal anti-slavery groups set up in the late 1700s?","o":["Beefeaters","Suffragettes","Quakers","Canutes"],"a":[2],"e":"The first formal anti-slavery groups were set up by the Quakers in the late 1700s, and they petitioned Parliament to ban the practice."},{"q":"More women than men study at university.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is TRUE."},{"q":"Why did Ireland suffer a famine in the middle of the 19th century?","o":["There was a heat wave","The whole country was flooded","The potato crop failed","There were not enough farmers"],"a":[2],"e":"In the middle of the century the potato crop failed, and Ireland suffered a famine. A million people died from disease and starvation."},{"q":"What sort of battle was ‘the Battle of Britain’?","o":["A naval battle against the Spanish.","An aerial battle against the French.","An aerial battle against the Germans.","A naval battle against the French."],"a":[2],"e":"Hitler wanted to invade Britain, but before sending in troops, Germany needed to control the air campaign against Britain, but the British resisted with their fighter planes and eventually won the crucial aerial battle against the Germans, called ‘the Battle of Britain’, in the summer of 1940."},{"q":"Who is in charge of appointing the local Chief Constable?","o":["A judge","Police and Crime Commissioners","The mayor","The local community members"],"a":[1],"e":"PCCs (Police and Crime Commissioners) set local police priorities and the local policing budget. They also appoint the local Chief Constable."},{"q":"What does the money from the National Insurance Contributions pay for (CHOOSE TWO)?","o":["Police force salaries","State retirement pensions","National Health Service","University fees"],"a":[1,2],"e":"The money raised from National Insurance Contributions is used to pay for state benefits and services such as the state retirement pension and the National Health Service (NHS)."},{"q":"How many British died on the first day alone of the Battle of Somme?","o":["60,000","65,000","40,000","55,000"],"a":[0],"e":"The British attack of the Somme in July 1916, resulted in about 60,000 British casualties on the first day alone."},{"q":"When was the first Union flag created?","o":["1515","1616","1505","1606"],"a":[3],"e":"The first Union Flag was created in 1606 from the flags of Scotland and England, the Principality of Wales was already untied with England."},{"q":"Which of the following territories is not part of the UK?","o":["Northern Ireland","The Isle of Man","Wales","Scotland"],"a":[1],"e":"The UK comprises England, Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland. There are several islands which are closely linked with the UK but are not part of it: the Channel Islands and the Isle of Man."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a fundamental principle of British life?","o":["Individual liberty","Democracy","Communism","Participation in community life"],"a":[2],"e":"The fundamental principles of British life include: democracy, the rule of law, individual liberty, tolerance of those with different faiths and beliefs and participation in community life."},{"q":"What do Sir Terence Conran, Clarice Cliff and Thomas Chippendale have in common?","o":["They were British designers","They developed the atomic bomb","They were British explorers","They were British poets"],"a":[0],"e":"Britain has produced many great designers, from Thomas Chippendale (who designed furniture in the 18th century) to Clarice Cliff (who designed Art Deco ceramics) to Sir Terence Conran (a 20th-century interior designer)."},{"q":"Which of the following cities is not in Wales?","o":["Cardiff","Swansea","Bradford","Newport"],"a":[2],"e":"Bradford is located in England."},{"q":"People on the electoral register with ages between 18 and 70 can be asked to serve on a jury.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"True. As well as getting the right to vote, people on the electoral register are randomly selected to serve on a jury. Anyone who is on the electoral register and is aged 18 to 70 can be asked to do this."},{"q":"Which Saxon king of England was killed at the Battle of Hastings in 1066?","o":["Kenneth MacAlpin","Harold","William the Conqueror","Henry V"],"a":[1],"e":"In 1066, an invasion led by William, the Duke of Normandy (in what is now northern France), defeated Harold, the Saxon king of England, at the Battle of Hastings. Harold was killed in the battle."},{"q":"During the ‘swinging sixties’ Britain experienced a growth in:","o":["Sports such as rowing and cycling","Fashion, cinema and popular music","Holistic therapies","People going to university"],"a":[1],"e":"The decade of the 1960s was a period of significant social change. It was known as the ‘swinging sixties’. There was growth in British fashion, cinema and popular music."},{"q":"As a permanent resident or citizen of the UK you should:","o":["Treat other with fairness","Look after yourself and your family","Look after the area in which you live and the environment","All the answers are correct"],"a":[3],"e":"If you wish to be a permanent resident or citizen of the UK, you should: respect and obey the law, respect the rights of others, including their right to their own opinions, treat others with fairness, look after yourself and your family and look after the area in which you live and the environment."},{"q":"When did the BBC start the world’s first regular television service?","o":["1922","1932","1936","1944"],"a":[2],"e":"The BBC started radio broadcasts in 1922 and began the world’s first regular television service in 1936."},{"q":"Women in Britain today make up about one third of the workforce.","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"Women in Britain today make up about half of the workforce."},{"q":"The evacuation of British and French soldiers from France in a huge naval operation during WWII gave rise to which phrase:","o":["The Harbour spirit","The Blitz operation","The Dunkirk spirit","The D-Day operation"],"a":[2],"e":"During WWII as France fell, the British decided to evacuate British and French soldiers from France in a huge naval operation. Many civilian volunteers in small pleasure and fishing boats from Britain helped the Navy to rescue more than 300,000 men from the beaches around Dunkirk. The evacuation gave rise to the phrase ‘the Dunkirk spirit’."},{"q":"Which programme helps 16 and 17 year-olds develop their skills and are part in community projects?","o":["The Youth Working Programme","The National Development Programme","The National Citizen Service Programme","The Skills Development Programme"],"a":[2],"e":"The National Citizen Service programme gives 16- and 17-year-olds the opportunity to enjoy outdoor activities, develop their skills and take part in a community project."},{"q":"Queen Victoria became queen in:","o":["1837","1847","1835","1836"],"a":[0],"e":"In 1837, Queen Victoria became queen of the UK at the age of 18."},{"q":"Who wrote the books ‘Pride and Prejudice’ and ‘Sense and Sensibility’?","o":["Charles Dickens","Robert Louis Stevenson","Evelyn Waugh","Jane Austen"],"a":[3],"e":"Jane Austen (1775-1817) was an English novelist. Her books include Pride and Prejudice and Sense and Sensibility."}]},{"id":"test-39","name":"Test 39","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"Which political party did Margaret Thatcher belong to?","o":["Green Party","Conservative Party","Liberal Democrats Party","Democratic Unionist Party"],"a":[1],"e":"Margaret Thatcher led the Conservative government from 1979 to 1990."},{"q":"In which city is the Millennium Stadium located?","o":["Birmingham","Nottingham","Liverpool","Cardiff"],"a":[3],"e":"The Millennium Stadium is located in Cardiff."},{"q":"Who lost a lot of power and influence after the Battle of Culloden in 1746?","o":["The church","Politicians","The clans","The King"],"a":[2],"e":"The clans lost a lot of their power and influence after Culloden. Chieftains became landlords if they had the favour of the English king, and clansmen became tenants who had to pay for the land they used."},{"q":"What UK landmark was voted as Britain’s favourite view in 2007?","o":["Wastwater in the Lake District","Snowdon in Snowdonia","The Crown Jewels at the Tower of London","Loch Lomond in Scotland"],"a":[0],"e":"In 2007, television viewers voted Wastwater as Britain’s favourite view."},{"q":"When did the development of a free press take place?","o":["1795","1685","1695","1785"],"a":[2],"e":"From 1695, newspapers were allowed to operate without a government licence. Increasing numbers of newspapers began to be published."},{"q":"Which was the last successful foreign invasion of England?","o":["The Roman invasion","The Anglo-Saxon invasion","The Norman Conquest","The Viking invasion"],"a":[2],"e":"The Norman Conquest was the last successful foreign invasion of England and led to many changes in government and social structures in England."},{"q":"Who was Richard Austen Butler?","o":["A British economist","A Welsh poet","A Labour MP","A Conservative MP"],"a":[3],"e":"Richard Austen Butler (later Lord Butler) became a Conservative MP in 1923 and held several positions before becoming responsible for education in 1941."},{"q":"What kind of sport is played at the Wimbledon Championships?","o":["Football","Tennis","Rugby","Cricket"],"a":[1],"e":"The most famous tennis tournament hosted in Britain is The Wimbledon Championships, which takes place each year at the All England Lawn Tennis and Croquet Club."},{"q":"Where was Isaac Newton from?","o":["Lincolnshire","Stratford-upon-Avon","Cardiff","Edinburgh"],"a":[0],"e":"Isaac Newton was born in Lincolnshire, eastern England."},{"q":"Under which Act was The Kingdom of Great Britain created?","o":["The Reform Act","The Act of Union","The Emancipation Act","The Butler Act"],"a":[1],"e":"The Act of Union, known as the Treaty of Union in Scotland, was therefore agreed in 1707, creating the Kingdom of Great Britain."},{"q":"The Council of Europe has the power to make new laws.","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. The Council of Europe has NO power to make laws but draws up conventions and charters, the most well-known of which is the European Convention on Human Rights and Fundamental Freedoms, usually called the European Convention on Human Rights."},{"q":"Which TWO British poets wrote about their experiences in the WWI?","o":["Lord Byron and Percy Shelley","John Keats and Alfred Lord Tennyson","Robert and Elizabeth Browning","Wilfred Owen and Siegfried Sassoon"],"a":[3],"e":"Wilfred Owen and Siegfried Sassoon were inspired to write about their experiences in the First World War."},{"q":"If you are found driving while exceeding the alcohol limit you will:","o":["Not be allowed to drive again","Be taken to the hospital","Be arrested","Be taken home by the police"],"a":[2],"e":"If you are found driving while exceeding the alcohol limit you will be arrested."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following are core values of a civil servant?","o":["Objectivity","Competitiveness","Talent","Honesty"],"a":[0,3],"e":"The core values of civil servants include: integrity, honesty, objectivity and impartiality (including being politically neutral)."},{"q":"Which of the following countries belongs to the Commonwealth?","o":["Norway","Zimbabwe","Nauru","Algeria"],"a":[2],"e":"Nauru is a member of the Commonwealth."},{"q":"What is the name of the first Danish king to rule in England?","o":["Canute","Kenneth MacAlpin","Henry V","Edward I"],"a":[0],"e":"Anglo-Saxon kings continued to rule what is now England, except for a short period when there were Danish kings. The first of these was Cnut, also named Canute."},{"q":"What name was given to the members of the Women’s Social and Political Union founded in 1903?","o":["Gentry","Boers","Rebels","Suffragettes"],"a":[3],"e":"In 1903 Emmeline Pankhurst helped found the Women’s Social and Political Union (WSPU). This was the first group whose members were called ‘suffragettes’."},{"q":"It is a civil offence to owe money to someone and you might be taken to court.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"True. Owing money to someone is a civil offence."},{"q":"In which country of the British Empire did the Boer War take place?","o":["South Africa","India","Bangladesh","Mauritius"],"a":[0],"e":"The Boer War of 1899 to 1902 made the discussions about the future of the Empire more urgent. The British went to war in South Africa with settlers from the Netherlands called the Boers."},{"q":"Robert Louis Stevenson, Graham Greene and Sir Kingsley Amis were British writers.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is True."},{"q":"Which new industries developed in the UK during the Great Depression (choose 2 answers)?","o":["The metallurgic industry","The automobile industry","The shipbuilding industry","The aviation industry"],"a":[1,3],"e":"In 1929, the world entered the ‘Great Depression’ and some parts of the UK suffered mass unemployment. The effects of the depression of the 1930s were felt differently in different parts of the UK. The traditional heavy industries such as shipbuilding were badly affected but new industries – including the automobile and aviation industries – developed."},{"q":"Which of the following is a traditional pub game?","o":["Darts","Hide and seek","Bingo","Checkers"],"a":[0],"e":"Pool and darts are traditional pub games."},{"q":"When was the Battle of Agincourt fought?","o":["1348","1415","1315","1448"],"a":[1],"e":"One of the most famous battles of the Hundred Years War was the Battle of Agincourt in 1415, where King Henry V’s vastly outnumbered English army defeated the French. The English left France in the 1450s."},{"q":"What is the Bessemer process?","o":["An industrial process for the mass-production of iron","An industrial process for the mass-production of copper","An industrial process for the mass-production of titanium","An industrial process for the mass-production of steel"],"a":[3],"e":"The development of the Bessemer process for the mass production of steel led to the development of the shipbuilding industry and the railways."}]},{"id":"test-40","name":"Test 40","group":"Full tests","questions":[{"q":"Which country invented rugby?","o":["India","Argentina","England","France"],"a":[2],"e":"Rugby originated in England in the early 19th century and is very popular in the UK today."},{"q":"Who were the Huguenots?","o":["Refugees from France","Refugees from Italy","Refugees from Pakistan","Refugees from Cyprus"],"a":[0],"e":"Between 1680 and 1720 many refugees called Huguenots came from France. They were Protestants and had been persecuted for their religion."},{"q":"Which British scientist showed that gravity applied to the whole universe?","o":["Alan Turing","Alexander Fleming","Isaac Newton","Ernest Rutherford"],"a":[2],"e":"Isaac Newton most famous published work was Philosophiae Naturalis Principia Mathematica (‘Mathematical Principle of Natural Philosophy’), which showed how gravity applied to the whole universe."},{"q":"Which composer wrote music for King George I and for his son, George II?","o":["George Frederick Handel","Gustav Holst","Ralph Vaughan Williams","Henry Purcell"],"a":[0],"e":"George Frederick Handel wrote the Water Music for King George I and Music for the Royal Fireworks for his son, George II."},{"q":"When did the English civil war start?","o":["1632","1635","1642","1652"],"a":[2],"e":"Civil war between the king and Parliament could not now be avoided and began in 1642."},{"q":"During the 19th century, the UK was the world’s major producer of which THREE materials?","o":["Coal","Iron","Silk","Cotton cloth"],"a":[0,1,3],"e":"British industry led the world in the 19th century. The UK produced more that half of the world’s iron, coal and cotton cloth."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following British athletes have won gold medals in the Olympic Games?","o":["Dame Kelly Holmes","Jenson Button","Tim henman","Jessica Enis"],"a":[0,3],"e":"Dame Kelly Holmes and Jessica Enis won gold medals in the Olympic Games."},{"q":"The Romans remained in Britain for 500 years.","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"The Romans remained in Britain for 400 years."},{"q":"Which country invaded the Falkland Islands in 1982?","o":["Germany","The Netherlands","Argentina","France"],"a":[2],"e":"In 1982, Argentina invaded the Falkland Islands, a British overseas territory in the South Atlantic."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following are examples of criminal offences:","o":["Cases of unfair dismissal or discrimination in the workplace","Selling tobacco to anyone under the age of 18","A dispute about faulty goods or services","Smoking in public places"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Selling tobacco to anyone under the age of 18 and drinking in public are examples of criminal offences."},{"q":"Which British landmark was built as part of the UK’s celebration of the new millenium?","o":["The O2","Tower Bridge","Westminster Abbey","The London Eye"],"a":[3],"e":"The London Eye was originally built as part of the UK’s celebration of the new millennium and continues to be an important part of New Year celebrations."},{"q":"Where did slaves mainly come from during the slave trade?","o":["South America","West Africa","Southeast Asia","North Africa"],"a":[1],"e":"Slaves came primarily from West Africa."},{"q":"Who introduced ‘shampooing’ in Britain?","o":["Emmeline Pankhurst","James Cook","Sake Dean Mahomet","Florence Nightingale"],"a":[2],"e":"Sake Dean Mahomet and his wife also introduced ‘shampooing’, the Indian art of head massage, to Britain."},{"q":"To be able to vote in England, you have to register at your local council electoral registration office.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"Where can the Bayeux tapestry be seen?","o":["France","Wales","Northern Ireland","England"],"a":[0],"e":"The Battle of Hastings is commemorated in a great piece of embroidery, known as the Bayeux tapestry, which can still be seen in France today."},{"q":"It is an offence not to have an MOT certificate if your vehicle is more than two years old or without car insurance.","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"It is an offence not to have an MOT certificate if your vehicle is more than THREE years old or without car insurance."},{"q":"What was the Spanish Armada?","o":["A large fleet of planes","A group of radicals","A group of politicians","A large fleet of ships"],"a":[3],"e":"The Spanish Armada was a large fleet of ships, which had been sent by Spain to conquer England and restore Catholicism."},{"q":"England was split into two groups during the English Civil War of 1642, which were known as:","o":["The House of Lords and the House of Commons","The Puritans and the Chartists","The Cavaliers and the Roundheads","The Beefeaters and the Yeoman Warders"],"a":[2],"e":"The English Civil war began in 1642. The country split into those who supported the king (the Cavaliers) and those who supported Parliament (the Roundheads)."},{"q":"What are the key roles of the governors and school boards? select THREE answers","o":["Providing funding for all students","Setting the strategic direction of the school","Ensuring accountability","Monitoring and evaluating school performance"],"a":[1,2,3],"e":"Governors and school boards have an important part to play in raising school standards. They have three key roles: setting the strategic direction of the school, ensuring accountability and monitoring and evaluating school performance."},{"q":"A popular way for people to stay in touch with friends, organise social events, and share photos, videos and opinions is through social networking websites such as:","o":["Dropbox and itunes","Email and Fax","Ebay and Amazon","Facebook and Twitter"],"a":[3],"e":"Social networking websites such as Facebook and Twitter are a popular way for people to stay in touch with friends, organise social events, and share photos, videos and opinions."},{"q":"Why is it important to recycle (select two answers)?","o":["Using recycled materials to make new products uses less energy than using raw materials","It is a criminal offence and you could be arrested if you do not do it","To reduce the expenses of the local council","Less rubbish is created, so the amount being put into landfill is reduced"],"a":[0,3],"e":"It is important to recycle as much of your waste as you can. Using recycled materials to make new products uses less energy and means that we do not need to extract more raw materials from the earth. It also means that less rubbish is created, so the amount being put into landfill is reduced."},{"q":"In Northern Ireland, a newly qualified driver must display an ____ plate for one year after passing the test.","o":["S","N","R","L"],"a":[2],"e":"In Northern Ireland, a newly qualified driver must display an ‘R’ plate (for restricted driver) for one year after passing the test."},{"q":"When is the birth of Jesus Christ celebrated?","o":["24th December","25th December","26th December","31st December"],"a":[1],"e":"Christmas Day, 25 December, celebrates the birth of Jesus Christ."},{"q":"Prince William is also known as:","o":["The Duke of Wellington","The Prince of Wales","The Duke of Edinburgh","The Prince of Northern Ireland"],"a":[1],"e":"Prince William is also known as the Prince of Wales."}]},{"id":"test-1-2","name":"Test 1.2","group":"Chapters 1–2","questions":[{"q":"What does the UK offer to its permanent residents or citizens?","o":["A right to judge others","A right to join in the election of a government","Free access to all national museums","A discount in taxes"],"a":[1],"e":"A right to join in the election of a government is the correct answer."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a fundamental principle of British life?","o":["Tolerance of those with different faiths and beliefs","The rule of law","Autocracy","Democracy"],"a":[2],"e":"The fundamental principles of British life include: tolerance of those with different faiths and beliefs, the rule of law, democracy, individual liberty and participation in community life."},{"q":"What do you need to apply to become a permanent resident or citizen of the UK (choose TWO options)?","o":["A British driving licence","To be able to speak and read English","A British university degree","A good understanding of life in the UK"],"a":[1,3],"e":"The UK offers to its residents or citizens: freedom of belief and religion, freedom of speech, freedom from unfair discrimination, a right to a fair trial and a right to join in the election of a government."},{"q":"What does the UK offer to its residents or citizens (choose FIVE answers)?","o":["Freedom of belief and religion","Freedom of speech","Freedom from unfair discrimination","A right to a fair trial","Free university tuition fees","A right to join in the election of a government"],"a":[0,1,2,3,5],"e":"To apply to become a permanent resident or citizen of the UK, you will need to speak and read English and have a good understanding of life in the UK."},{"q":"Which of the following is a fundamental principle of life in the UK?","o":["Intolerance of those with different beliefs","Individual liberty","Rule of men","Lack of social cohesion"],"a":[1],"e":"The fundamental principles of British life include: democracy, the rule of law, individual liberty, tolerance of those with different faiths and beliefs, and participation in community life."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following responsibilities will you have as a permanent resident or citizen of the UK?","o":["To respect and obey the law","To go to church on Sundays","To look after the area in which you live and the environment","To do the military service"],"a":[0,2],"e":"If you wish to be a permanent resident or citizen of the UK, you should respect and obey the law, respect the rights of others, including their right to their own opinions, treat others with fairness, look after yourself and your family and look after the area in which you live and the environment."},{"q":"Participation in community life is a fundamental principle of British life.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"The fundamental principles of British life include: participation in community life."},{"q":"To look after yourself and your family is one of the responsibilities that you will have as a permanent resident or citizen of the UK.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is TRUE."},{"q":"In which of the following languages is the life in the UK test NOT available?","o":["English","Scottish Gaelic","French","Welsh"],"a":[2],"e":"The Life in the UK test is usually taken is English, although special arrangements can be made if you wish to take it in Welsh or Scottish Gaelic."},{"q":"Which of the following statements is true?","o":["To apply to become a permanent resident or citizen of the UK, you will need to have a university degree","To apply to become a permanent resident or citizen of the UK, you will need to have a good understanding of life in the UK","To apply to become a permanent resident or citizen of the UK, you will need to have British relatives","To apply to become a permanent resident or citizen of the UK, you will need to have knowledge of the British laws"],"a":[1],"e":"To apply to become a permanent resident or citizen of the UK, you will need to have a good understanding of life in the UK and be able to speak and read English."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following are fundamental principles of British life?","o":["Tolerance of those with different faiths and beliefs","Buying food from local business","The rule of law","Doing the military service"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Tolerance of those with different faiths and beliefs and the rule of law are fundamental principles of British life."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following islands are known as ‘Crown dependencies’?","o":["Ceylon","The Isle of Man","The Channel Islands","Ireland"],"a":[1,2],"e":"There are also several islands which are closely linked with the UK but are not part of it: the Channel Islands and the Isle of Man. These have their own governments and are called ‘Crown dependencies’."},{"q":"What does the UK offer in return to its residents or citizens?","o":["Free undergraduate tuition fees","A right to a fair trial","VIP tickets for local events","Free parking across the country"],"a":[1],"e":"A right to a fair trial is the correct answer."},{"q":"Which of the following territories does not belong to Great Britain?","o":["Scotland","Wales","Northern Ireland","England"],"a":[2],"e":"‘Great Britain’ refers only to England, Scotland and Wales, not to Northern Ireland."},{"q":"Which of the following are British overseas territories?","o":["Ireland","The Canary Islands","St Helena","The Falkland Islands"],"a":[2,3],"e":"There are several British overseas territories in other parts of the world, such as St Helena and the Falkland Islands. They are also linked to the UK but are not part of it."},{"q":"Where is the Parliament of the UK located?","o":["In Manchester","In Westminster","In Windsor Castle","In Buckingham Palace"],"a":[1],"e":"The UK is governed by the parliament sitting in Westminster."},{"q":"St Helena is a British overseas territory.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"British overseas territories in other parts of the world include St Helena and the Falkland Islands. They are also linked to the UK but are not part of it."},{"q":"The Isle of Man is a Crown dependency.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"There are several islands which are closely linked with the UK but are not part of it: the Channel Islands and the Isle of Man. These have their own governments and are called ‘Crown dependencies’."},{"q":"British values and principles are based on history and traditions and are protected by law, customs and expectations.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"The Channel Islands and the Isle of Man are closely linked to the UK but are not part of it."},{"q":"Which of the following lines does NOT form part of the citizenship pledge?","o":["I will give my loyalty to the United Kingdom","I will uphold its democratic values","Long live our noble King!","I will observe its laws faithfully"],"a":[2],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"As part of the citizenship ceremony, new citizens pledge their loyalty to the United Kingdom and to respect its rights and freedoms.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"Long live our noble King! is the second sentence of the National Anthem of the UK."},{"q":"Which of the following are ’Crown dependencies’?","o":["Ireland and Malta","The Maldives and Anglesey","The Isle of Man and the Channel Islands","The Isle of Wight and Northern Ireland"],"a":[2],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"Which TWO islands are closely linked to the UK but are not part of it?","o":["The Isle of Man","Malta","The Channel Islands","Fiji"],"a":[0,2],"e":"The Isle of Man and the Channel Islands are ’Crown dependencies’."},{"q":"Which of the following sentences is true?","o":["The UK is governed by the parliament sitting in Edinburgh.","Scotland and Wales have parliaments or assemblies of their own, but not Northern Ireland.","Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland also have parliaments or assemblies of their own, with devolved powers in defined areas.","The UK is governed by the parliament sitting in Glasgow."],"a":[2],"e":"Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland also have parliaments or assemblies of their own, with devolved powers in defined areas."}]},{"id":"test-3-1","name":"Test 3.1","group":"Chapter 3","questions":[{"q":"Who was the first person to lead a Roman invasion in Britain in 55 BC?","o":["Emperor Claudius","Julius Caesar","Napoleon","William Caxton"],"a":[1],"e":"Julius Caesar led a Roman invasion of Britain in 55 BC."},{"q":"Which name is given to the tombs where people buried their dead during the Bronze Age?","o":["Skara Brae","Hill forts","Round barrows","Canute"],"a":[2],"e":"During the Bronze Age people lived in roundhouses and buried their dead in tombs called round barrows."},{"q":"What did the ‘Bill of Rights‘ confirm in 1689?","o":["Women‘s right to vote","The rights of Parliament and the limits of the king‘s power","The supreme power of the king","The freedom of speech"],"a":[1],"e":"The Bill of Rights, 1689, confirmed the rights of Parliament and the limits of the king’s power. Parliament took control of who could be monarch and declared that the king or queen must be a Protestant."},{"q":"Who designed the Clifton Suspension Bridge located over the Avon George?","o":["Sir Norman Foster","Isambard Kingdom Brunel","Richard Arkwright","Robert Adam"],"a":[1],"e":"The Clifton Suspension Bridge was designed by Isambard Kingdom Brunel and is spanning the Avon Gorge."},{"q":"During the Middle Ages, England was an important trading nation and people came to England from abroad to trade and also to work. Where did the glass manufacturers come from?","o":["Germany","Italy","Holland","France"],"a":[1],"e":""},{"q":"Which American President worked closely with Margaret Thatcher?","o":["Hillary Clinton","Ronald Reagan","Jimmy Carter","George Bush"],"a":[1],"e":"Margaret Thatcher worked closely with the United States President, Ronald Reagan, and was one of the first Western leaders to recognise and welcome the changes in the leadership of the Soviet Union which eventually led to the end of the Cold War."},{"q":"Who was the first Archbishop of Canterbury?","o":["St Patrick","St Columba","St Augustine","St Peter"],"a":[2],"e":"St Augustine was the first Archbishop of Canterbury."},{"q":"Who was the first wife of Henry VIII?","o":["Anne Boleyn","Catherine of Aragon","Anne of Cleves","Catherine Howard"],"a":[1],"e":"Catherine of Aragon was the first wife of Henry VIII."},{"q":"Which of the following flags is not represented in the union flag?","o":["Welsh","Scottish","English","Irish"],"a":[0],"e":"The Welsh dragon does not appear on the Union Flag because, when the first Union Flag was created in 1606 from the flags of Scotland and England, the Principality of Wales was already united with England."},{"q":"When did ‘the Concorde’ aircraft fly for the first time?","o":["In 1969","In 1970","In 1975","In 1976"],"a":[0],"e":"The Concorde first flew in 1969 and began carrying passengers in 1976."},{"q":"By what other name is the Conservative Party also known as?","o":["The Labour Party","The Tudors","The Green Party","The Tories"],"a":[3],"e":"The modern Conservative Party is still sometimes referred to as the Tories."},{"q":"When did Adolf Hitler come to power in Germany?","o":["In 1922","In 1931","In 1933","In 1942"],"a":[2],"e":"Adolf Hitler came to power in Germany in 1933."},{"q":"What was inscribed in the first coins to be minted in Britain during the Iron Age?","o":["Celtic symbols","The names of Iron Age Kings","Drawings of animals","The names of medicine plants"],"a":[1],"e":"They made the first coins to be minted in Britain, some inscribed with the names of Iron Age kings."},{"q":"When did Emmeline Pankhurst set up the Women’s Franchise League, which fought to get the vote in local elections for married women?","o":["1880","1889","1890","1899"],"a":[1],"e":"Emmeline Pankhurst was born in Manchester in 1858. She set up the women‘s Franchise League in 1889, which fought to get the vote in local elections for married women. In 1903 she helped found the Women‘s Social and Political Union (WSPU)."},{"q":"In which English county is the monument of Stonehenge located?","o":["Bedfordshire","Cheshire","Wiltshire","Cornwall"],"a":[2],"e":"The ancestors of these first farmers probably came from south-east Europe. These people built houses, tombs and monuments on the land. One of these monuments,Stonehenge, still stands in what is now the English county of Wiltshire."},{"q":"What is the best-known work from Robert Burns?","o":["Auld Lang Syne","Hamlet","A rose by another name","George\\'s Marvellous Medicine"],"a":[0],"e":"Burns‘ best-known work is probably the song Auld Lang Syne, which is sung by people in the UK and other countries when they are celebrating the New Year (or Hogmanay as it is called in Scotland)."},{"q":"Which of the following crosses does NOT form part of the Union Flag?","o":["The cross of St George, patron saint of England","The cross of St David, patron saint of Wales","The cross of St Patrick, patron saint of Ireland","The cross of St Andrew, patron saint of Scotland"],"a":[1],"e":"The cross of St David, patron saint of Wales does not form part of the Union Flag because, when the first Union Flag was created in 1606 from the flags of Scotland and England, the Principality of Wales was already untied with England."},{"q":"What British actor was best known for his roles in various Shakespeare plays?","o":["Sir Laurence Olivier","Joseph Turner","Andrew Lloyd Webber","Tim Rice"],"a":[0],"e":"The Laurence Olivier Awards take place annually at different venues in London. There are a variety of categories, including best director, best actor and best actress. The awards are named after the British actor Sir Laurence Olivier, late Lord Olivier, who was best known for his roles in various Shakespeare plays."},{"q":"What name is given to the period of rapid development of industry in Britain in the 18th and 19th centuries?","o":["The Enlightenment","The Industrial Revolution","The Victorian period","The inter-war period"],"a":[1],"e":"The Industrial Revolution was the rapid development of industry in Britain in the 18th and 19th centuries."},{"q":"Who was responsible for the construction of the Great Western Railway?","o":["Isambard Kingdom Brunel","Sir Norman Foster","Sir Christopher Wren","Robert Adam"],"a":[0],"e":"Isambard Kingdom Brunel was responsible for constructing the Great Western Railway, which was the first major railway built in Britain."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following forts form part of Hadrian’s Wall?","o":["Vindolanda","Stonehenge","Housesteads","Maiden"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Parts of Hadrian‘s Wall include the forts of Housesteads and Vindolanda."},{"q":"Why did Elizabeth I keep her cousin Mary prisoner for 20 years?","o":["She did not agree with her political views","She suspected Mary of wanting to take over the English throne","She had killed her son","She was a spy of the Spanish Armada"],"a":[1],"e":"Elizabeth suspected Mary of wanting to take over the English throne, and kept her prisoner for 20 years."},{"q":"What was the name of the first cloned mammal?","o":["Molly","Dolly","Colin","Alice"],"a":[1],"e":"In 1996, two British scientists, Sir Ian Wilmot (1944-) and Keith Campbell (1954-2012), led a team which was the first to succeed in cloning a mammal, Dolly the sheep."},{"q":"How many British casualties were recorded during the First World War?","o":["More than 2 million people","More than 3 million people","More than 4 million people","More than 5 million people"],"a":[0],"e":"During the First World War millions of people were killed or wounded, with more than 2 million British casualties."}]},{"id":"test-3-2","name":"Test 3.2","group":"Chapter 3","questions":[{"q":"Why did Henry VIII decide to divorce Catherine of Aragon?","o":["She was unpopular in the country","She was too old to give him another child","For political reasons","She was accused of taking lovers to the Palace"],"a":[1],"e":"When Catherine was too old to give him another child, Henry decided to divorce her, hoping that another wife would give him a son to be his heir."},{"q":"What characterised the Bronze Age?","o":["People were farmers who lived in large villages","People lived in roundhouses and buried their dead in tombs","People were nomads and hunters","People were divided into clans"],"a":[1],"e":"During the Bronze Age people lived in roundhouses and buried their dead in tombs called round barrows."},{"q":"How many British citizens left the country between 1853 and 1913?","o":["10 million","13 million","15 million","18 million"],"a":[1],"e":"Between 1853 and 1913, as many as 13 million British citizens left the country."},{"q":"Who was known as the Iron Duke?","o":["The Duke of Edinburgh","The Duke of Normandy","The Duke of Wellington","The Duke of Lancaster"],"a":[2],"e":"In 1815, the French Wars ended with the defeat of the Emperor Napoleon by the Duke of Wellington at the Battle of Waterloo. Wellington was known as the Iron Duke and later became Prime Minister."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a British invention?","o":["The World Wide Web","The Turing machine","The hovercraft","The mobile phone"],"a":[3],"e":"The World Wide Web, the Turing machine and the hovercraft are British inventions. The mobile phone is not."},{"q":"When did Argentina invade the Falklands Islands?","o":["In 1980","In 1982","In 1984","In 1992"],"a":[1],"e":"In 1982, Argentina invaded the Falkland Islands, a British overseas territory in the South Atlantic."},{"q":"When did Alexander Fleming win the Nobel Prize in medicine for the discovery of penicillin?","o":["In 1935","In 1945","In 1950","In 1952"],"a":[1],"e":"Fleming won the Nobel Prize in Medicine in 1945 for the discovery of the penicillin."},{"q":"When did the Roman army leave Britain?","o":["AD 410","AD 510","AD 600","AD 530"],"a":[0],"e":"The Roman army left Britain in AD 410 to defend other parts of the Roman Empire and never returned."},{"q":"When was Emmeline Pankhurst born?","o":["1858","1868","1845","1901"],"a":[0],"e":"Emmeline Pankhurst was born in Manchester in 1858. She set up the women‘s Franchise League in 1889, which fought to get the vote in local elections for married women."},{"q":"How long did the First World War last for?","o":["Two years","Three years","Four years","Six years"],"a":[2],"e":"The First World War lasted four years (1914 – 1918)."},{"q":"Who was Henry VII?","o":["The leader of the House of York","The leader of the House of Lancaster","The leader of the House of Buckingham","The leader of the House of Leeds"],"a":[1],"e":"Henry VII was the leader of the House of Lancaster."},{"q":"Which of the following areas did the British Empire NOT cover during the Victorian period?","o":["Mexico","Australia","India","Large parts of Africa"],"a":[0],"e":"During the Victorian period, the British Empire grew to cover all of India, Australia and large parts of Africa."},{"q":"Which British scientist led the development of the atomic bomb?","o":["Howard Florey","Ernst Chain","Ernest Rutherford","Keith Campbell"],"a":[2],"e":"Scientists led by Ernest Rutherford, working at Manchester and then Cambridge University, were the first to ‘split the atom‘ and took part in the Manhattan Project in the United States, which developed the atomic bomb."},{"q":"After the Black Death, the growing wealth in the towns led to the development of a strong middle class:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"True. After the Black Death, new social classes appeared, including owners of large areas of land (later called the gentry), and people left the countryside to live in the towns. In the towns, growing wealth led to the development of a strong middle class."},{"q":"What was the ability of the Harrier jump jet aircraft?","o":["To carry 1,000 passengers","To take off vertically","To float on water","To reach supersonic speeds"],"a":[1],"e":"The Harrier jump jet, an aircraft capable of taking off vertically, was also designed and developed in the UK."},{"q":"Which TWO of Henry VIII’s wives were accused of taking lovers and executed?","o":["Anne of Cleves","Catherine Howard","Catherine Parr","Anne Boleyn"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Anne Boleyn and Catherine Howard were accused of taking lovers and executed."},{"q":"Which two scientists led a team which was the first to succeed in cloning a mammal, Dolly the sheep?","o":["Sir Frank Whittle and Sir Christopher Cockrell","Sir Robert Edwards and Patrick Steptoe","Sir Ian Wilmot and Keith Campbell","Francis Creek and John Logie Baird"],"a":[2],"e":"In 1996, two British scientists, Sir Ian Wilmot (1944-) and Keith Campbell (1954-2012), led a team which was the first to succeed in cloning a mammal, Dolly the sheep."},{"q":"What British discovery was influential in the development of computer science and the modern-day computer?","o":["The Radar","The Turing machine","The MRI scanner","The aeroplane"],"a":[1],"e":"The Turing machine is a theoretical mathematical device invented by Alan Turing (1912-54), a British mathematician, in the 1930s. The theory was influential in the development of computer science and the modern-day computer."},{"q":"Which famous philosopher developed ideas about human nature during the 18th century and that have continued to influence philosophers over the years?","o":["Aristotle","Sigmund Freud","Platon","David Hume"],"a":[3],"e":"During the 18th century, David Hume ‘s ideas about human nature continue to influence philosophers."},{"q":"When did the ‘Concorde‘ aircraft carry passengers for the first time?","o":["In 1969","In 1970","In 1972","In 1976"],"a":[3],"e":"The ‘Concorde‘ began carrying passengers in 1976."},{"q":"Which prehistoric village located in northern Europe has helped archaeologists to understand more about how people lived near the end of the Stone Age?","o":["Stonehenge","Scunthorpe","Danelaw","Skara Brae"],"a":[3],"e":"Skara Brae on Orkney, off the north coast of Scotland, is the best preserved prehistoric village in northern Europe, and has helped archaeologists to understand more about how people lived near the end of the Stone Age."},{"q":"During the Middle Ages, a Parliament was developed in Scotland, which had three Houses called Estates. These were:","o":["The lords, the commons and the clergy","The lords, the commons and the farmers","The farmers, the blacksmiths and the clergy","The farmers, the blacksmiths and the teachers"],"a":[0],"e":"The Parliament developed in Scotland in the Middle Ages had three Houses, called Estates: the lords, the commons and the clergy."},{"q":"Which of the following scientific discoveries helped the progress of the Industrial Revolution?","o":["Penicilin","The aeroplane","Steam power","The lightbulb"],"a":[2],"e":"Scientific discoveries, such as James Watt‘s work on steam power, helped the progress of the Industrial Revolution."},{"q":"Where in England was one of the Anglo-Saxon kings buried with treasure and armour?","o":["Maiden Castle, Dorset","Sutton Hoo, Suffolk","Conwy Castle, Wales","The Tower of London"],"a":[1],"e":"The burial place of one of the Anglo-Saxon kings was at Sutton Hoo in modern Suffolk."}]},{"id":"test-3-3","name":"Test 3.3","group":"Chapter 3","questions":[{"q":"Which of the following words comes from the Norman French (choose ONE option)?","o":["Apple","Beauty","Cow","Summer"],"a":[1],"e":"Some words in modern English – for example, ‘park‘ and ‘beauty‘ – are based on Norman French words."},{"q":"Catherine Parr, the sixth wife of Henry VIII, was Anne Boleyn’s cousin:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. Catherine Howard was a cousin of Anne Boleyn."},{"q":"What was the symbol of York during the Wars of the Roses?","o":["A Red Rose","A White Rose","A Black Rose","A Purple Rose"],"a":[1],"e":"The symbol of York during the Wars of the Roses was a white rose."},{"q":"During which movement the Parliament passed laws giving women the right to equal pay and made it illegal for employees to discriminate against women because of their gender:","o":["The Punk movement","The Enlightenment","The Swinging Sixties","The Suffrage"],"a":[2],"e":"The Swinging Sixties was a time when the Parliament passed new laws giving women the right to equal pay and made it illegal for employers to discriminate against women because of their gender."},{"q":"Where can you find a statue of Boudicca, the queen of the Iceni and one of the tribal leaders who fought against the Romans?","o":["At London Bridge","At Westminster Bridge","At the Tower of London","At St James‘s Park"],"a":[1],"e":"There is a statue of Boudicca, the queen of the Iceni, on Westminster Bridge in London, near the Houses of Parliament."},{"q":"For how long was Britain a republic under the leadership of Oliver Cromwell?","o":["9 years","10 years","11 years","12 years"],"a":[2],"e":"Britain was a republic under the leadership of Oliver Cromwell for 11 years."},{"q":"Who became one of the most popular monarchs in English history, particularly after 1588, when the English defeated the Spanish Armada?","o":["Henry VIII","Elizabeth I","James I","Mary I"],"a":[1],"e":"Elizabeth I became one of the most popular monarchs in English history, particularly after 1588, when the English defeated the Spanish Armada (a large fleet of ships), which had been sent by Spain to conquer England and restore Catholicism."},{"q":"When did the ‘Concorde‘ aircraft retire from service?","o":["In 1976","In 1999","In 2003","In 2000"],"a":[2],"e":"The ‘Concorde‘ was retired from service in 2003."},{"q":"When did the Allies defeat Germany marking the end of the Second World War?","o":["In June 1943","In May 1945","In August 1945","In August 1946"],"a":[1],"e":"Following victory on the beaches of Normandy, the allied forces pressed on through France and eventually into Germany. The Allies comprehensively defeated Germany in May 1945."},{"q":"During the Middle Ages, England was an important trading nation and people came to England from abroad to trade and also to work. Where did the weavers come from?","o":["Germany","Italy","France","Holland"],"a":[2],"e":"During the Middle Ages, people came to England from abroad to trade and also to work. Many had special skills, such as weavers from France, engineers from Germany, glass manufacturers from Italy and canal builders from Holland."},{"q":"Throughout the 1990s, Britain played a leading role in coalition forces involved in the liberation of Kuwait, following the Iraqi invasion in 1990, and the conflict in the former Republic of Yugoslavia:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"True. Throughout the 1990s, Britain played a leading role in coalition forces involved in the liberation of Kuwait, following the Iraqi invasion in 1990, and the conflict in the former Republic of Yugoslavia."},{"q":"The ‘swinging sixties’ was a period of significant social change and it was known for its growth in:","o":["British fashion, cinema and popular music","The Punk movement","English literature and comedy","The ownership of cars I"],"a":[0],"e":"The decade of the 1960s was a period of significant social change. It was known as the ‘swinging sixties’. There was growth in British fashion, cinema and popular music."},{"q":"Which prehistoric village located in northern Europe has helped archaeologists to understand more about how people lived near the end of the Stone Age?","o":["Stonehenge","Scunthorpe","Danelaw","Skara Brae"],"a":[3],"e":"Skara Brae on Orkney, off the north coast of Scotland, is the best preserved prehistoric village in northern Europe, and has helped archaeologists to understand more about how people lived near the end of the Stone Age."},{"q":"During the Middle Ages, a Parliament was developed in Scotland, which had three Houses called Estates. These were:","o":["The lords, the commons and the clergy","The lords, the commons and the farmers","The farmers, the blacksmiths and the clergy","The farmers, the blacksmiths and the teachers"],"a":[0],"e":"The Parliament developed in Scotland in the Middle Ages had three Houses, called Estates: the lords, the commons and the clergy."},{"q":"Which of the following scientific discoveries helped the progress of the Industrial Revolution?","o":["Penicilin","The aeroplane","Steam power","The lightbulb"],"a":[2],"e":"Scientific discoveries, such as James Watt’s work on steam power, helped the progress of the Industrial Revolution."},{"q":"Who where the parents of Elizabeth I?","o":["Henry VIII and Anne Boleyn","Henry VII and Anne of Cleves","Henry VIII and Catherine of Aragon","Henry VII and Catherine Howard"],"a":[0],"e":"Henry VIII and Anne Boleyn were the parents of Elizabeth I."},{"q":"Which of the following areas did the British Empire NOT occupy during the Victorian period?","o":["France","Australia","India","Large parts of Africa"],"a":[0],"e":"During the Victorian period, the British Empire grew to cover all of India, Australia and large parts of Africa."},{"q":"Who were the first people to live in Britain in what we call the Stone Age?","o":["Farmers","Hunter-gatherers","Warriors","Pirates"],"a":[1],"e":"The first people to live in Britain were hunter-gatherers, in what we call the Stone Age."},{"q":"Which of the following statements regarding the Black Death is NOT true?","o":["One third of the population of England died and a similar proportion in Scotland and Wales","It was one of the worst disasters ever to strike Britain","It affected children and old people only","Following the Black Death, there were labour shortages"],"a":[2],"e":"In 1348, a disease, probably a form of plague, came to Britain. This was known as the Black Death. One third of the population of England died and a similar proportion in Scotland and Wales. This was one of the worst disasters ever to strike Britain. Following the Black Death, the smaller population meant there was less need to grow cereal crops. There were labour shortages and peasants began to demand higher wages."},{"q":"Who was reigning in Britain when Wales became formally united with England by the Act for the Government of Wales?","o":["Henry VIII","Henry VII","Elizabeth I","James I"],"a":[0],"e":"During the reign of Henry VIII, Wales became formally united with England by the Act for the Government of Wales."},{"q":"The Elizabethan period is known for the richness of its poetry and drama, especially for the plays and poems of which playwright?","o":["Alexander Dumas","William Shakespeare","Charles Dickens","Thomas Hardy"],"a":[1],"e":"The Elizabethan period is also remembered for the richness of its poetry and drama, especially the plays and poems of William Shakespeare."},{"q":"Which flag has a diagonal red cross on a white ground?","o":["The cross of St George, patron saint of England","The cross of St Patrick, patron saint of Ireland","The cross of St David, patron saint of Wales","The cross of St Andrew, patron saint of Scotland"],"a":[1],"e":"The cross of St Patrick, patron saint of Ireland, is a diagonal red cross on a white ground."},{"q":"What is the name of the best preserved prehistoric village in northern Europe and which origin traces back to the Stone Age?","o":["Maiden","Skara Brae","Stonehenge","Bayeux"],"a":[1],"e":"Skara Brae on Orkney, off the north coast of Scotland, is the best preserved prehistoric village in northern Europe, and has helped archaeologists to understand more about how people lived near the end of the Stone Age."},{"q":"After the Black Death, new social classes appeared in England, including owners of large areas of land known as:","o":["Judiciary","Gentry","Clans","Nobility"],"a":[1],"e":"Following the Black Death, the smaller population meant there was less need to grow cereal crops. There were labour shortages and peasants began to demand higher wages. New social classes appeared, including owners of large areas of land (later called the gentry ), and people left the countryside to live in the towns. In the towns, growing wealth led to the development of a strong middle class."}]},{"id":"test-3-4","name":"Test 3.4","group":"Chapter 3","questions":[{"q":"Elizabeth I was the younger daughter of which King?","o":["James I","William the Great","Henry VII","Henry VIII"],"a":[3],"e":"Elizabeth I was the younger daughter of Henry VIII."},{"q":"When did the First World War end?","o":["9th of November 1918","11th of November 1918","9th of December 1918","11th of December 1918"],"a":[1],"e":"The First World War ended at 11.00 am on 11th November 1918 with victory for Britain and its allies."},{"q":"Which British scientist was awarded a Nobel Prize for discovering the structure of the DNA molecule?","o":["Alan Turing","Sir Frank Whittle","Sir Christopher Cockrell","Francis Crick"],"a":[3],"e":"The structure of the DNA molecule was discovered in 1953 through work at British universities in London and Cambridge. This discovery contributed to many scientific advances, particularly in medicine and fighting crime. Francis Crick (1916-2004), one of those awarded the Nobel Prize for this discovery, was British."},{"q":"When did people learn how to make bronze?","o":["Around 3,000 years ago","Around 4,000 years ago","Around 5,000 years ago","Around 2,000 years ago"],"a":[1],"e":"Around 4,000 years ago, people learned to make bronze. We call this period the Bronze Age."},{"q":"Who was Boudicca?","o":["One of the tribal leaders who fought against the Romans","One of the architects who built Hadrian’s Wall","A Viking king","One of the tribal leaders who fought against the Vikings"],"a":[0],"e":"One of the tribal leaders who fought against the Romans was Boudicca, the queen of the Iceni in what is now eastern England."},{"q":"What actions did Henry VII take after his victory in the Wars of the Roses?","o":["Reduced the power of the nobles","Broke away with the Church of Rome","Converted Britain to the Christianism","Increased the power of the nobles"],"a":[0],"e":"After his victory in the Wars of the Roses, Henry VII wanted to make sure that England remained peaceful and that his position as king was secure. He deliberately strengthened the central administration of England and reduced the power of the nobles."},{"q":"Who became Prime Minister of the UK in May 2010?","o":["David Cameron","Gordon Brown","Tony Blair","Ed Miliband"],"a":[0],"e":"In May 2010, and for the first time in the UK since February 1974, no political party won an overall majority in the General Election. The Conservative and Liberal Democrat parties formed a coalition and the leader of the Conservative Party, David Cameron, became Prime Minister."},{"q":"How were Elizabeth I and ‘Mary, Queen of Scots’ related?","o":["They were cousins","They were sisters","They were mother and daughter","They were not related"],"a":[0],"e":"Mary, Queen of Scots’ was Elizabeth I’s cousin."},{"q":"Choose the correct answer, the Middle Ages was:","o":["A period of peace in Britain","A period where liberal ideas were introduced in Britain","A period of constant war","A period where new ideas about politics and philosophy were introduced in Britain"],"a":[2],"e":"The Middle Ages (or the medieval period) was a time of almost constant war."},{"q":"What does the acronym NATO stand for?","o":["The North American Transparent Organisation","The North Atlantic Trust Organisation","The North Atlantic Treaty Organisation","The North American Treaty Organisation"],"a":[2],"e":"NATO stands for The North Atlantic Treaty Organisation."},{"q":"What characterised the people from the Bronze Age?","o":["They were accomplished metalworkers","They were farmers who lived in large villages","They were nomads and hunters","They were divided into clans"],"a":[0],"e":"The people of the Bronze Age were accomplished metalworkers who made many beautiful objects in bronze and gold, including tools, ornaments and weapons."},{"q":"What is the name of the event that refers to the 6 June 1944 when Allied Forces landed in Normandy aiming to attack Hitler’s forces in Western Europe during World War II?","o":["D-Day","Domesday","A-Day","Allies Day"],"a":[0],"e":"‘D-Day’ refers to the 6th of June 1944, when allied forces landed in Normandy aiming to attack Hitler’s forces in Western Europe during World War II."},{"q":"Which British Prime Minister led the Conservative government from 1979 to 1990?","o":["John Major","Harold Macmillan","Margaret Thatcher","Sir Alec Douglas-Home"],"a":[2],"e":"Margaret Thatcher, Britain’s first woman Prime Minister, led the Conservative government from 1979 to 1990."},{"q":"Which of the following civilisations is known for having built roads and public buildings, creating a structure of law and having introduced new plants and animals in Britain?","o":["The Romans","The Vikings","The Normans","The Germans"],"a":[0],"e":"The Romans remained in Britain for 400 years. They built roads and public buildings, created a structure of law, and introduced new plants and animals."},{"q":"What marked the beginning of what is called ‘constitutional monarchy’?","o":["The laws passed after the Glorious Revolution","A speech given by the King","The emergence of new ideas about politics, philosophy and science","The development of the Bessemer process"],"a":[0],"e":"The laws passed after the Glorious Revolution are the beginning of what is called ‘constitutional monarchy’."},{"q":"When was the Turing machine discovered?","o":["In the 1920s","In the 1930s","In the 1940s","In the 1950s"],"a":[1],"e":"A Turing machine is a theoretical mathematical device invented by Alan Turing (1912-54), a British mathematician, in the 1930s."},{"q":"During which period were the House of Lords and House of Commons established?","o":["Middle Ages","Iron Age","The Roman Invasion","The Anglo-Saxon Invasion"],"a":[0],"e":"In the Middle Ages, the numbers attending Parliament increased and two separate parts, known as Houses were established. These were the House of the Commons on the House of the Lords."},{"q":"How many years did Queen Victoria reign for?","o":["Only two years","Almost 64 years","Less than two years","Almost 50 years"],"a":[1],"e":"In 1837, Queen Victoria became queen of the UK at the age of 18. She reigned until 1901, almost 64 years."},{"q":"What is the name of Admiral Nelson’s ship?","o":["HMS Victory","Golden Hind","Canute","Grimsby"],"a":[0],"e":"Admiral Nelson’s ship was known as the HMS Victory."},{"q":"What are the British scientists Sir Ian Wilmot and Keith Campbell famous for?","o":["The first ‘test-tube baby’","The cloning of the first mammal, Dolly the sheep","The invention of the MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) scanner","The invention of the World Wide Web"],"a":[1],"e":"Sir Ian Wilmot and Keith Campbell led a team which was the first to succeed in cloning a mammal, Dolly the sheep."},{"q":"Which of the following was one of the commanders in the defeat of the Spanish Armada and one of the founders of England’s naval tradition?","o":["Alan Turing","Sir Francis Drake","James VI of Scotland","Oliver Cromwell"],"a":[1],"e":"Sir Francis Drake was one of the commanders in the defeat of the Spanish Armada and one of the founders of England’s naval tradition."},{"q":"Who was the inventor of the World Wide Web?","o":["Sir Tim Berners-Lee","Sir Peter Mansfield","Sir Ian Wilmot","Sir Bernard Lovell"],"a":[0],"e":"The inventor of the World Wide Web, Sir Tim Berners-Lee (1955-), is British. Information was successfully transferred via the web for the first time on 25 December 1990."},{"q":"When did the Wars of the Roses start?","o":["1388","1455","1462","1478"],"a":[1],"e":"In 1455, a civil war was begun to decide who should be king of England. It was fought between the supporters of two families: the House of Lancaster and the House of York. This war was called the Wars of the Roses, because the symbol of Lancaster was a red rose and the symbol of York was a white rose."},{"q":"When did the UK join the European Economic Community (EEC)?","o":["In 1957","In 1963","In 1973","In 1977"],"a":[2],"e":"At first the UK did not wish to join the EEC but it eventually did so in 1973."}]},{"id":"test-3-5","name":"Test 3.5","group":"Chapter 3","questions":[{"q":"Who defeated the French at the battle of Agincourt in 1415?","o":["King Edward I of England","William III of England","Henry VIII","King Henry V"],"a":[3],"e":"One of the most famous battles of the Hundred Years War was the Battle of Agincourt in 1415, where King Henry V ’s vastly outnumbered English army defeated the French."},{"q":"Which British Prime Minister nationalised major industries including coal and steel?","o":["Harold Macmillan","Clement Attlee","John Major","Sir Alec Douglas-Home"],"a":[1],"e":"Clement Attlee ’s government undertook the nationalisation of major industries (like coal and steel), created the National Health Service and implemented many of Beveridge’s plans for a stronger welfare state. Attlee also introduced measures to improve the conditions of workers."},{"q":"Who was the first Archbishop of Canterbury?","o":["St Augustine","St Columba","St Andrew","Kenneth MacAlpin"],"a":[0],"e":"St Augustine became the first Archbishop of Canterbury."},{"q":"Which British explorer mapped the coast of Australia?","o":["Sake Dean Mahomet","Richard Arkwright","Captain James Cook","Admiral Nelson"],"a":[2],"e":"Captain James Cook mapped the coast of Australia and a few colonies were established there."},{"q":"What were the main occupations of the people during the Iron Age?","o":["Fishermen and butchers","Farmers, craft workers or warriors","Knights, doctors or musicians","Engineers, teachers or lawyers"],"a":[1],"e":"During the Iron Age most people were farmers, craft workers or warriors."},{"q":"What was the name of Sir Francis Drake’s ship and which was one of the first to sail around the world?","o":["The Golden Eye","The Golden Hind","Elizabeth","The Sharp"],"a":[1],"e":"Sir Francis Drake, one of the commanders in the defeat of the Spanish Armada, was one of the founders of England’s naval tradition. His ship, the Golden Hind, was one of the first to sail right around (‘circumnavigate’) the world."},{"q":"Why did Henry VIII marry Anne of Cleves?","o":["He was deeply in love","For political reasons","For her fortune","She was expecting a child"],"a":[1],"e":"Henry married Anne of Cleves for political reasons but divorced her soon after."},{"q":"How many American colonies declared their independence in 1776, stating that people had a right to establish their own governments?","o":["11","12","13","14"],"a":[2],"e":"In 1776, 13 American colonies declared their independence, stating that people had a right to establish their own governments."},{"q":"Where was Catherine of Aragon (the first wife of Henry VIII) from?","o":["Spain","Germany","England","French"],"a":[0],"e":"Catherine of Aragon was a Spanish princess."},{"q":"When did the post-war economic boom come to an end?","o":["In the early 1970s","In the late 1970s","In the early 1960s","In the late 1960s"],"a":[1],"e":"In the late 1970s, the post-war economic boom came to an end."},{"q":"During her government, Margaret Thatcher made structural changes to the economy through the privatisation of nationalised industries and imposed legal controls on trade union powers:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"True. Margaret Thatcher made structural changes to the economy through the privatisation of nationalised industries and imposed legal controls on trade union powers."},{"q":"Who sat at the House of Commons during the Middle Ages?","o":["Knights and wealthy people from towns and cities","Farmers and blacksmiths","Great landowners and bishops","Everyone could sit at the House of Commons"],"a":[0],"e":"In the Middle Ages, Knights, who were usually smaller landowners, and wealthy people from towns and cities were elected to sit in the House of Commons."},{"q":"Which of the following words come from the Anglo-Saxon language?","o":["Apple and summer","Music and dance","Grimsby and scunthorpe","Canute and boat"],"a":[0],"e":"The words ‘Apple’ and ‘summer’ are based on Anglo-Saxon words."},{"q":"How old was the Queen of Scotland, Mary Stuart, when she became queen?","o":["Two years old","A month old","A week old","5 years old"],"a":[2],"e":"The queen of Scotland, Mary Stuart (often now called ‘Mary, Queen of Scots’) was a Catholic. She was only a week old when her father died and she became queen."},{"q":"Which British playwright had a great influence on the English language and invented many words that are still common today?","o":["William Shakespeare","Graham Greene","Dylan Thomas","Harold Pinter"],"a":[0],"e":"William Shakespeare had a great influence on the English language and invented many words that are still common today."},{"q":"What proportion of the population died as a result of the Black Death in England?","o":["Half of the population","One third of the population","55% of the population","One quarter of the population"],"a":[1],"e":"As a result of the Black Death, one third of the population of England died and a similar proportion in Scotland and Wales."},{"q":"Who wrote a famous series of poems in English about a group of people going to Canterbury on a pilgrimage and known as ‘The Canterbury Tales’?","o":["William Shakespeare","Geoffrey Chaucer","Jane Austen","Charles Dickens"],"a":[1],"e":"In the years leading up to 1400, Geoffrey Chaucer wrote a series of poems in English about a group of people going to Canterbury on a pilgrimage. The people decided to tell each other stories on the journey, and the poems describe the travellers and some of the stories they told."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a British invention of the 20th century?","o":["The radar","The Turing machine","The World Wide Web","Radioactivity"],"a":[3],"e":"Radioactivity is not a British invention."},{"q":"Which British Prime Minister was famous for the speech ‘I have nothing to offer but blood, toil, tears and sweat’?","o":["Clement Attlee","Anthony Eden","Winston Churchill","Harold Wilson"],"a":[2],"e":"During the Second World War, Winston Churchill made many famous speeches including lines which you may still hear: ‘I have nothing to offer but blood, toil, tears and sweat’. This was Churchill’s first speech to the House of Commons after he became Prime Minister, 1940."},{"q":"Which of the following countries did NOT belong to the Allies during World War II?","o":["France","Bulgaria","The UK","Australia"],"a":[1],"e":"Bulgaria was not part of the Allies during the Second World War."},{"q":"Which famous architect designed St Paul’s Cathedral after it was destroyed by a fire in 1666?","o":["Sir Christopher Wren","Isaac Newton","Robert Burns","Isambard Kingdom Brunel"],"a":[0],"e":"In 1666, a great fire destroyed much of the city of London, including many churches and St Paul’s Cathedral. London was rebuilt with a new St Paul’s, which was designed by a famous architect, Sir Christopher Wren."},{"q":"Who proposed that enemy aircrafts could be detected by radio waves and is responsible for the development of the radar?","o":["Sir Robert Watson-Watt","John McLeod","John Logie Baird","Alan Turing"],"a":[0],"e":"Radar was developed by Scotsman Sir Robert Watson-Watt (1892-1973), who proposed that enemy aircraft could be detected by radio waves. The first successful radar test took place in 1935."},{"q":"Who sat at the House of Lords during the Middle Ages?","o":["Great landowners and bishops","Knights, who were usually smaller landowners","Wealthy people from towns and cities","Farmers and blacksmiths"],"a":[0],"e":"During the Middle Ages, great landowners and bishops sat in the House of Lords."},{"q":"Which of the following countries was NOT part of the Central Powers during the First World War?","o":["Germany","Serbia","Austria","Hungary"],"a":[1],"e":"During the First World War Serbia formed part of the Allied powers."}]},{"id":"test-3-6","name":"Test 3.6","group":"Chapter 3","questions":[{"q":"Who was the first English Prime Minister?","o":["Henry Pelham","Sir Robert Walpole","Admiral Nelson","Oliver Cromwell"],"a":[1],"e":"The first man to be called this was Sir Robert Walpole, who was Prime Minister from 1721 to 1742."},{"q":"Who was Robert Burns?","o":["A British musician","A Scottish poet","A Welsh scientist","An Irish engineer"],"a":[1],"e":"Known in Scotland as ‘The Bard’, Robert Burns was a Scottish poet."},{"q":"Who established the Church of England?","o":["The King","The Archbishop of Canterbury","Henry VIII","Elizabeth I"],"a":[2],"e":"To divorce his first wife, Henry VIII needed the approval of the Pope. When the Pope refused, Henry established the Church of England. In this new church, the king, not the Pope, would have the power to appoint bishops and order how people should worship."},{"q":"What name is given to the period in the 18th century when new ideas about politics, philosophy and science were developed?","o":["The Enlightenment","The Glorious Revolution","The Industrial Revolution","Elizabethan"],"a":[0],"e":"During the 18th century, new ideas about politics, philosophy and science were developed. This is often called ‘the Enlightenment’."},{"q":"What is the meaning of ‘Magna Carta’?","o":["The Big Charter","The Great Charter","The King’s Charter","The Small Charter"],"a":[1],"e":"The Magna Carta (which means the Great Charter ). The Magna Carta established the idea that even the king was subject to the law. It protected the rights of the nobility and restricted the king’s power to collect taxes or to make and change laws."},{"q":"When did the Wars of the Roses end?","o":["1455","1462","1478","1485"],"a":[3],"e":"The Wars of the Roses ended with the Battle of Bosworth Field in 1485."},{"q":"The UK is not a full member of the European Union because it does not use the Euro currency:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. The UK is a full member of the European Union but does not use the Euro currency."},{"q":"Who was the British Minister after Margaret Thatcher?","o":["Tony Blair","Harold Macmillan","Gordon Brown","John Major"],"a":[3],"e":"John Major was Prime Minister after Mrs Thatcher, and helped establish the Northern Ireland peace process."},{"q":"Who defeated Emperor Napoleon at the Battle of Waterloo in 1815?","o":["The Duke of Wellington","Admiral Nelson","Richard Arkwright","The Duke of Edinburgh"],"a":[0],"e":"In 1815, the French Wars ended with the defeat of the Emperor Napoleon by the Duke of Wellington at the Battle of Waterloo."},{"q":"What was the symbol of the House of Tudor?","o":["A red rose with a white rose inside it","A red rose","A white rose","A white rose with a red rose inside it"],"a":[0],"e":"The symbol of the House of Tudor was a red rose with a white rose inside it as a sign that the Houses of York and Lancaster were now allies."},{"q":"What animal is represented in the Welsh flag?","o":["A lion","A dragon","A tiger","A cat"],"a":[1],"e":"There is also an official Welsh flag, which shows a Welsh dragon. The Welsh dragon does not appear on the Union Flag because, when the first Union Flag was created in 1606 from the flags of Scotland and England, the Principality of Wales was already untied with England."},{"q":"Which famous economist developed ideas about economics during the 18th century and which are still referred to today?","o":["Robert Dudley Baxter","David Hume","Adam Smith","Henry George"],"a":[2],"e":"During the 18th century, new ideas about politics, philosophy and science were developed. This is often called ‘the Enlightenment’. Many of the great thinkers of the Enlightenment were Scottish. Adam Smith developed ideas about economics which are still referred to today."},{"q":"Who invented the cash-dispensing ATM (automatic teller machine) or ‘cashpoint’?","o":["Sir Peter Mansfield","James Goodfellow","Patrick Steptoe","Sir Tim Berners-Lee"],"a":[1],"e":"In the 1960s, James Goodfellow (1937-) invented the cash-dispensing ATM (automatic teller machine) or ‘cashpoint’. The first of these was put into use by Barclays Bank in Enfield, north London in 1967."},{"q":"What is the meaning of the term ‘Habeas corpus’?","o":["You must present the person in court","You must obey the law","You should go to court","You should not be judged"],"a":[0],"e":"Habeas corpus is Latin for ‘you must present the person in court’. The Habeas Corpus Act guaranteed that no one could be held prisoner unlawfully. Every prisoner has a right to a court hearing."},{"q":"Which German princess got married with Henry VIII?","o":["Jane Seymour","Anne of Cleves","Catherine Parr","Anne Boleyn"],"a":[1],"e":"Anne of Cleves was a German princess who married Henry VIII for political reasons."},{"q":"What was the most spoken language during the Iron Age?","o":["Anglo-Saxon","French","Celtic","Irish"],"a":[2],"e":"The language spoken during the Iron Age was part of the Celtic language family."},{"q":"Which of the following was one of the most famous battles of the Hundred Years War?","o":["The Battle of Agincourt","The Battle of Bannockburn","The Battle of Culloden","The Battle of Waterloo"],"a":[0],"e":"One of the most famous battles of the Hundred Years War was the Battle of Agincourt in 1415, where King Henry V’s vastly outnumbered English army defeated the French. The English left France in the 1450s."},{"q":"During the Middle Ages, England was an important trading nation and people came to England from abroad to trade and also to work. Where did the engineers come from?","o":["France","Holland","Germany","Italy"],"a":[2],"e":"During the Middle Ages, people came to England from abroad to trade and also to work. Many had special skills, such as weavers from France, engineers from Germany, glass manufacturers from Italy and canal builders from Holland."},{"q":"How many wives did Henry VIII have?","o":["Five","Six","Seven","Eight"],"a":[1],"e":"Henry VIII had six wives."},{"q":"When did the English become the preferred language of the royal court and Parliament?","o":["In 1300","In 1400","In 1345","In 1567"],"a":[1],"e":"By 1400, in England, official documents were being written in English, and English had become the preferred language of the royal court and Parliament."},{"q":"What was Boudicca, the queen of the Iceni in what is now eastern England, known for?","o":["Her rebellion against the Vikings","Her rebellion against the Romans","Her rebellion against the Normans","Her rebellion against the Anglo-Saxons"],"a":[1],"e":"One of the tribal leaders who fought against the Romans was Boudicca, the queen of the Iceni in what is now eastern England."},{"q":"When was the hovercraft invented?","o":["In the 1920s","In the 1930s","In the 1940s","In the 1950s"],"a":[3],"e":"Sir Christopher Cockerell (1910-99), a British inventor, invented the hovercraft in the 1950s."},{"q":"Who is the author of the famous play ‘Macbeth’?","o":["Alexander Thomas","William Shakespeare","Harold Pinter","Evelyn Waugh"],"a":[1],"e":"Macbeth is a famous play by William Shakespeare."},{"q":"What animal was cloned by scientists Sir Ian Wilmot and Keith Campbell?","o":["A cow","A rabbit","A cat","A sheep"],"a":[3],"e":"In 1996, two British scientists, Sir Ian Wilmot (1944-) and Keith Campbell (1954-2012), led a team which was the first to succeed in cloning a mammal, Dolly the sheep."}]},{"id":"test-3-7","name":"Test 3.7","group":"Chapter 3","questions":[{"q":"What did the Chartists campaign for?","o":["The right to vote for women over 30 years old","The right to vote for the working classes and other people without property","The right to vote for women and men over 30 years old","The right to vote for every citizen"],"a":[1],"e":"The Chartists, demanded the vote for the working classes and other people without property."},{"q":"When did Emmeline Pankhurst die?","o":["1928","1935","1936","1930"],"a":[0],"e":"Emmeline Pankhurst died in 1928. Shortly before Emmeline’s death, women were given the right to vote at the age of 21, the same as men."},{"q":"What TWO houses were confronted during the Wars of the Roses?","o":["The House of Lancaster","The House of Leicester","The House of Canterbury","The House of York"],"a":[0,3],"e":"The Wars of the Roses were fought between the supporters of two families: the House of Lancaster and the House of York."},{"q":"Who was the first woman to become Prime Minister in the UK?","o":["J K Rowling","Margaret Thatcher","Jane Austen","Hillary Clinton"],"a":[1],"e":"Margaret Thatcher, Britain’s first woman Prime Minister, led the Conservative government from 1979 to 1990."},{"q":"How old was Queen Victoria when she became Queen in 1837?","o":["A week old","4 years old","20 years old","18 years old"],"a":[3],"e":"In 1837, Queen Victoria became queen of the UK at the age of 18."},{"q":"When did the First World War start?","o":["1912","1914","1942","1940"],"a":[1],"e":"On 28 June 1914, Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria was assassinated. This set off a chain of events leading to the First World War (1914-18)."},{"q":"Which battle signified the end of the Wars of the Roses?","o":["The Battle of Agincourt","The Battle of Bosworth Field","The Battle of Bannockburn","The Battle of Marston Moor"],"a":[1],"e":"The Wars of the Roses ended with the Battle of Bosworth Field in 1485."},{"q":"Who became Prime Minister in the UK in May 1940?","o":["Margaret Thatcher","Winston Churchill","Clement Attlee","Harold Macmillan"],"a":[1],"e":"Winston Churchill was the son of a politician and, before becoming a Conservative MP in 1900, was a soldier and journalist. In May 1940 he became Prime Minister."},{"q":"When did English settlers begin to colonise the eastern coast of America?","o":["In James I’s time","In Henry VII’s time","In Henry VIII’s time","In Elizabeth I’s time"],"a":[3],"e":"In Elizabeth I’s time, English settlers first began to colonise the eastern coast of America."},{"q":"When did the Black Death come to England?","o":["In 1248","In 1348","In 1358","In 1414"],"a":[1],"e":"In 1348, a disease, probably a form of plague, came to Britain. This was known as the Black Death."},{"q":"When did British combat troops leave Iraq?","o":["In 2004","In 2005","In 2006","In 2009"],"a":[3],"e":"British combat troops left Iraq in 2009."},{"q":"The line ‘all the world’s a stage’ belongs to which of the following plays or poems written by William Shakespeare?","o":["Romeo and Juliet","Hamlet","As You Like It","Sonnet 18"],"a":[2],"e":"All the world’s a stage’ are lines from William Shakespeare’s play As You Like It."},{"q":"During the times of Elizabeth I, English settlers began to colonise:","o":["Indonesia","The eastern coast of America","The western coast of Australia","The Pacific Islands"],"a":[1],"e":"In Elizabeth I’s time, English settlers first began to colonise the eastern coast of America."},{"q":"Which TWO families started a civil war to decide who should be king of England in 1455?","o":["The House of Lancaster","The House of Kent","The House of York","The House of Newcastle"],"a":[0,2],"e":"In 1455, a civil war was begun to decide who should be king of England. It was fought between the supporters of two families: the House of Lancaster and the House of York."},{"q":"Who was in charge of the British fleet at the Battle of Trafalgar?","o":["Admiral Nelson","Oliver Cromwell","The Duke of Wellington","Henry VIII"],"a":[0],"e":"Admiral Nelson was in charge of the British fleet at Trafalgar and was killed in the battle."},{"q":"Who was the leader of the English Republic?","o":["King Richard III","Oliver Cromwell","Charles II","Charles I"],"a":[1],"e":"Oliver Cromwell was the leader of the English republic."},{"q":"Where was Margaret Thatcher born?","o":["Buckinghamshire","Cheshire","Lincolnshire","Bedfordshire"],"a":[2],"e":"Margaret Thatcher was the daughter of a grocer from Grantham in Lincolnshire."},{"q":"Who introduced the Statute of Rhuddlan by which Wales annexed to the Crown of England in 1284?","o":["King Edward I of England","Robert the Bruce","King Henry V","William III of England"],"a":[0],"e":"In 1284 King Edward I of England introduced the Statute of Rhuddlan, which annexed Wales to the Crown of England."},{"q":"The decade of the 1960s was a period of significant social change, when there was growth in British fashion, cinema and popular music:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"True. The decade of the 1960s was a period of significant social change. It was known as the ‘swinging sixties’. There was growth in British fashion, cinema and popular music."},{"q":"When did Britain and France develop the world’s only supersonic commercial airliner known as ‘The Concorde’?","o":["1970s","1950s","1980s","1960s"],"a":[3],"e":"The 1960s was also a time of technological progress. Britain and France developed the world’s only supersonic commercial airliner, Concorde."},{"q":"Which flag has a diagonal white cross on a blue ground?","o":["The cross of St Andrew, patron saint of Scotland","The cross of St Patrick, patron saint of Ireland","The cross of St George, patron saint of England","The cross of St David, patron saint of Wales"],"a":[0],"e":"The cross of St Andrew, patron saint of Scotland, is a diagonal white cross on a blue ground."},{"q":"Which was the first major railway built in Britain?","o":["High Speed 1","West Coast Main Line","Great Western Railway","East London Line"],"a":[2],"e":"The Great Western Railway, which was the first major railway built in Britain."},{"q":"In the 15th century, Henry Tudor, the leader of the House of Lancaster, became:","o":["King Henry VIII","King Richard I","King Richard III","King Henry VII"],"a":[3],"e":"In 1485, Henry Tudor, the leader of the House of Lancaster, became King Henry VII after defeating King Richard III of the House of York at the Battle of Bosworth Field."},{"q":"Who defeated the Vikings?","o":["King Alfred the Great","William I","Boudicca","James II"],"a":[0],"e":"King Alfred the Great defeated the Vikings."}]},{"id":"test-3-8","name":"Test 3.8","group":"Chapter 3","questions":[{"q":"During the Middle Ages, England was an important trading nation and people came to England from abroad to trade and also to work. Where did the canal builders come from?","o":["Germany","Holland","France","Italy"],"a":[1],"e":"During this Middle Ages, England was an important trading nation and people came to England from abroad to trade and also to work. Canal builders came from Holland."},{"q":"The Elizabethan period in England was a time of growing patriotism: a feeling of pride in being English, English explorers sought new trade routes and tried to expand British trade into the Spanish colonies in the Americas:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is TRUE."},{"q":"How many crosses form the Union Flag?","o":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"a":[1],"e":"The Union Flag has three crosses."},{"q":"Who were the suffragettes?","o":["A group who demanded the vote for the working classes","A group who used civil disobedience to gain the vote for women","A group who campaigned for the vote of men over 21 years of age","A group who introduced new ideas about politics"],"a":[1],"e":"The suffragettes were a group who used civil disobedience to gain the vote for women."},{"q":"When did Henry VIII die?","o":["In January 1547","In January 1557","In February 1547","In February 1557"],"a":[0],"e":"Henry VIII died on the 28th of January 1547."},{"q":"Which of the following lines from Shakespeare’s plays and poems is often still quoted?","o":["The Darling buds of May","I have nothing to offer but blood, toil, tears and sweat","Long live our noble King","God save the King"],"a":[0],"e":"The darling buds of May ’ is a line from William Shakespeare’s play Sonnet 18 – Shall I Compare Thee To A Summer’s Day."},{"q":"Which of the following statements about Mary, Queen of the Scotland, is NOT true?","o":["She spent most of her childhood in France","She was a Protestant","She gave her throne to her Protestant son, James VI of Scotland.","She was kept her prisoner for 20 years"],"a":[1],"e":"The queen of Scotland, Mary Stuart (often now called ‘Mary, Queen of Scots’) was a Catholic."},{"q":"When did Ireland become unified with England, Wales and Scotland?","o":["In 1801","In 1811","In 1901","In 1925"],"a":[0],"e":"In 1801, Ireland became unified with England, Scotland and Wales after the Act of Union of 1800. This created the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland."},{"q":"What was the estimated population of the British Empire during the Victorian period?","o":["More than 300 million people","More than 400 million people","More than 450 million people","﻿More than 500 million people"],"a":[1],"e":"﻿During the Victorian period, the British Empire became the largest empire the world has ever seen, with an estimated population of more than 400 million people."},{"q":"Which of the following countries was NOT part of the Allied Powers during the First World War?","o":["﻿France","Japan","Serbia","Bulgaria"],"a":[3],"e":"Bulgaria was part of the Central Powers."},{"q":"When did the First World War finish?","o":["At 12 am on 11th November 1916","At 11 am on 11th November 1918","At 11 pm on 13th November 1916","At 12 am on 13th February 1918"],"a":[1],"e":"The First World War ended at 11.00 am on 11th November 1918 with victory for Britain and its allies."},{"q":"Florence Nightingale is known for her work on:","o":["Human rights","The development of nursing in the UK","The development of computer science","The development and manufacture of motor-sport technology"],"a":[1],"e":"Florence Nightingale is often regarded as the founder of modern nursing."},{"q":"Which Act abolished slavery throughout the British Empire in 1833?","o":["The Freedom Act","The Habeas Corpus Act","The Emancipation Act","The Slavery Act"],"a":[2],"e":"In 1833 the Emancipation Act abolished slavery throughout the British Empire."},{"q":"Who became King of England when Queen Anne died in 1714?","o":["James II","George II","George I","Charles I"],"a":[2],"e":"When Queen Anne died in 1714, Parliament chose a German, George I, to be the next king, because he was Anne’s nearest Protestant relative."},{"q":"Who discovered the penicillin in 1928?","o":["Sir Robert Watson-Watt","Alexander Fleming","John McLeod","Howard Florey"],"a":[1],"e":"Alexander Fleming was researching influenza (the ‘flu’) in 1928 when he discovered penicillin."},{"q":"How many British casualties were recorded during the first day of the British attack of the Somme in July 1916 during the First World War?","o":["60,000","70,000","30,000","50,000"],"a":[0],"e":"During the First World War, one battle, the British attack of the Somme in July 1916, resulted in about 60,000 British casualties on the first day alone."},{"q":"The Welsh dragon on the Welsh flag does not appear on the Union Flag because, when the first Union Flag was created in 1606 from the flags of Scotland and England, the Principality of Wales was already untied with England:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is True."},{"q":"Which two countries developed the ‘Concorde’ aircraft?","o":["Germany and Italy","USA and Canada","Germany and Switzerland","France and Britain"],"a":[3],"e":"Britain and France developed the world’s only supersonic commercial airliner, Concorde."},{"q":"Which Scottish physician and researcher co-discovered the insulin used to treat diabetes?","o":["Francis Crick","Sir Robert Watson-Watt","John MacLeod","John Logie Baird"],"a":[2],"e":"John Macleod (1876-1935) was the co-discoverer of insulin, used to treat diabetes."},{"q":"Who was voted the greatest Briton of all time in 2002?","o":["Clement Attlee","Winston Churchill","Anthony Eden","Harold Wilson"],"a":[1],"e":"Winston Churchill was voted the greatest Briton of all time by the public in 2002."},{"q":"Which of the following social laws were liberalised during the 1960s?","o":["The law of abortion","The law of divorce","The law of immigration","The law of adoption"],"a":[0,1],"e":"The 1960s was a time when social laws were liberalised, for example in relation to divorce and to abortion in England, Wales and Scotland."},{"q":"Who was the mother of Elizabeth I?","o":["Jane Seymour","Anne of Cleves","Catherine of Aragon","Anne Boleyn"],"a":[3],"e":"Anne Boleyn and Henry VIII had one daughter, Elizabeth I."},{"q":"When was the National Health System (NHS) established?","o":["1945","1948","1952","1934"],"a":[1],"e":"In 1948, Aneurin (Nye) Bevan, the Minister for Health, led the establishment of the National Health Service (NHS), which guaranteed a minimum standard of health care for all, free at the point of use."},{"q":"How many Russian and Polish Jews came to Britain to escape prosecution between 1870 and 1914?","o":["Around 120,000","Around 150,000","Around 200","Around 400"],"a":[0],"e":"Between 1870 and 1914, around 120,000 Russian and Polish Jews came to Britain to escape persecution."}]},{"id":"test-3-9","name":"Test 3.9","group":"Chapter 3","questions":[{"q":"The line ‘a rose by any other name’ belongs to which of the following plays or poems written by William Shakespeare?","o":["Romeo and Juliet","Hamlet","As You Like It","Henry V"],"a":[0],"e":"A rose by any other name’ is a line from William Shakespeare’s play Romeo and Juliet."},{"q":"Which British scientist made important discoveries working with steam power?","o":["David Hume","Adan Smith","Alan Turing","James Watt"],"a":[3],"e":"Scientific discoveries, such as James Watt ’s work on steam power, helped the progress of the Industrial Revolution."},{"q":"When was the first television broadcast made?","o":["In 1922","In 1932","In 1942","In 1952"],"a":[1],"e":"In 1932 Scotsman John Logie Baird made the first television broadcast between London and Glasgow."},{"q":"Who invented the hovercraft?","o":["Sir Frank Whittle","Sir Christopher Cockerell","John Logie Baird","Sir Robert Edwards"],"a":[1],"e":"﻿Sir Christopher Cockerell invented the hovercraft."},{"q":"Which of the following events relates to the German bombing of London and other cities at night time during the World War II?","o":["The Bombing","The German raid","The Blitz","The Axis"],"a":[2],"e":"The Blitz﻿ relates to the German air force bombing London and other British cities at night-time during ‘the Battle of Britain’, in the summer of 1940."},{"q":"Which of the following social changes took place during the ‘Swinging Sixties’?","o":["The position of women in the workplace was improved","Everyone could study in the University for free","People from the same gender were allowed to get married","All musical events were free"],"a":[0],"e":"﻿During the ‘Swinging Sixties’ the position of women in the workplace improved."},{"q":"Where was Emmeline Pankhurst born?","o":["Liverpool","Kent","Manchester","Chester"],"a":[2],"e":"Emmeline Pankhurst was born in Manchester in 1858."},{"q":"Which of the following is a British invention from the 20th century?","o":["The Harrier jump jet","Radon","Paraffin wax","The lightbulb"],"a":[0],"e":"The Harrier jump jet, an aircraft capable of taking off vertically, is a British invention of the 20th century."},{"q":"When did Winston Churchill die?","o":["In 1964","In 1960","In 1965","In 1955"],"a":[2],"e":"Winston Churchill died in 1965."},{"q":"During the 17th century there were two main groups in Parliament, which were known as:","o":["The Whigs and the Tories","The Liberal Democrats and the Labour Party","The lefties and the right wing","The Conservative Party and the Liberal Democrats"],"a":[0],"e":"During the 17th century there were two main groups in Parliament, known as the Whigs and the Tories."},{"q":"When was the Women’s Social and Political Union (WSPU) established?","o":["1889","1901","1899","1903"],"a":[3],"e":"﻿In 1903 Emmeline Pankhurst helped found the Women’s Social and Political Union (WSPU)."},{"q":"Under which Act was the United Kingdom of Great Britain created?","o":["The Act of Union","The Great Act","The United Kingdom Act","The Act of Great Britain"],"a":[0],"e":"﻿The Act of Union, known as the Treaty of Union in Scotland, was agreed in 1707, creating the Kingdom of Great Britain."},{"q":"When did the first farmers arrive in Britain?","o":["6,000 years ago","7,000 years ago","8,000 years ago","10,000 years ago"],"a":[0],"e":"The first farmers arrived in Britain 6,000 years ago."},{"q":"For which purpose was the Statute of Rhuddlan introduced?","o":["To annex Scotland to the Crown of England","To annex Northern Ireland to the Crown of England","To annex Ireland to the Crown of England","To annex Wales to the Crown of England"],"a":[3],"e":"In 1284 King Edward I of England introduced the Statute of Rhuddlan, which annexed Wales to the Crown of England."},{"q":"When did Henry VIII start reining?","o":["In April 1509","In June 1497","In March 1515","In August 1495"],"a":[0],"e":"Henry VIII was king of England from 21 April 1509 until his death on 28 January 1547."},{"q":"In May 2010, the Conservative and Liberal Democrat parties formed a coalition and the leader of the Liberal Democrat Party, David Cameron, became Prime Minister:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. In May 2010, and for the first time in the UK since February 1974, no political party won an overall majority in the General Election. The Conservative and Liberal Democrat parties formed a coalition and the leader of the Conservative Party, David Cameron, became Prime Minister."},{"q":"Which hill fort from the Iron Age can be seen in the county of Dorset?","o":["Conwy Castle","Caernarfon Castle","Edinburgh Castle","Maiden Castle"],"a":[3],"e":"A very impressive hill fort from the Iron Age can still be seen today at Maiden Castle, in the English county of Dorset."},{"q":"What was the biggest source of employment in Britain before the 18th century?","o":["The ship industry","Agriculture","Teaching","Manufacturing jobs"],"a":[1],"e":"Before the 18th century, agriculture was the biggest source of employment in Britain."},{"q":"Why was the ‘Habeas Corpus Act’ of 1679 an important piece of legislation?","o":["It allowed people over 21 years old to vote","It guaranteed that no one could be held prisoner unlawfully","It abolished slavery throughout the British Empire","It abolished a number of taxes on imported goods in the UK"],"a":[1],"e":"The Habeas Corpus Act became law in 1679. This was a very important piece of legislation which remains relevant today. The Act guaranteed that no one could be held prisoner unlawfully. Every prisoner has a right to a court hearing."},{"q":"Who was Prime Minister in England from 1721 to 1742?","o":["Sir Robert Walpole","Admiral Nelson","Henry Pelham","Charles Edward Stuart"],"a":[0],"e":"The first man to be called this was Sir Robert Walpole, who was Prime Minister from 1721 to 1742."},{"q":"The Battle of Hastings is commemorated in a great piece of embroidery, which is known as:","o":["The Hastings tapestry","The Bayeux tapestry","The Normand tapestry","The Domesday tapestry"],"a":[1],"e":"The Battle of Hastings is commemorated in a great piece of embroidery, known as the Bayeux tapestry, which can still be seen in France today."},{"q":"Which of the following countries did NOT join the European Economic Community when it was first formed in 1957 (choose ONE option)?","o":["Luxembourg","West Germany","The UK","The Netherlands"],"a":[2],"e":"West Germany, France, Belgium, Italy, Luxembourg and the Netherlands formed the European Economic Community (EEC) in 1957. At first the UK did not wish to join the EEC but it eventually did so in 1973."},{"q":"When was Ireland divided into two countries?","o":["In 1920","In 1922","In 1925","In 1928"],"a":[1],"e":"In 1921 a peace treaty was signed and in 1922 Ireland became two countries."},{"q":"The Middle Ages or medieval times relate to the time that goes from the period after the Norman Conquest until the year:","o":["1385","1485","1495","1595"],"a":[1],"e":"The period after the Norman Conquest up until about 1485 is called the Middle Ages (or the medieval period)."}]},{"id":"test-3-10","name":"Test 3.10","group":"Chapter 3","questions":[{"q":"When did the Vikings first visit Britain to raid coastal towns and take away goods and slaves?","o":["AD 600","AD 789","AD 790","AD 804"],"a":[1],"e":"The Vikings came from Denmark and Norway. They first visited Britain in AD 789 to raid coastal towns and take away goods and slaves."},{"q":"Where can Admiral Nelson’s ship known as HMS Victory be visited?","o":["Bournemouth","Plymouth","Hastings","Portsmouth"],"a":[3],"e":"Admiral Nelson’s ship, HMS Victory, can be visited in Portsmouth."},{"q":"Who were the Puritans?","o":["A group of local leaders that surged in Wales with strict religious views","A group of Protestants who advocated strict and simple religious doctrine and worship","A group of Catholics who advocated strict and simple religious doctrine and worship","A group of landowners from the 18th century"],"a":[1],"e":"The Puritans were a group of Protestants who advocated strict and simple religious doctrine and worship. They did not agree with the king’s religious views and disliked his reforms of the Church of England."},{"q":"What is the name of the battle where the Scottish led by Robert the Bruce defeated the English in 1314?","o":["The Battle of Trafalgar","The Battle of Waterloo","The Battle of Agincourt","The Battle of Bannockburn"],"a":[3],"e":"In 1314 the Scottish, led by Robert the Bruce, defeated the English at the Battle of Bannockburn."},{"q":"Who took the British throne after the death of Henry VIII?","o":["Elizabeth I","Edward VI","Queen Mary","James I"],"a":[1],"e":"Henry VIII was succeeded by his son Edward VI."},{"q":"Which of the following words comes from the Viking language?","o":["Pencil","Table","Scunthorpe","Rabbit"],"a":[2],"e":"Words such as Grimsby and Scunthorpe come from the Viking languages."},{"q":"During the Crusades, European Christians fought for the control of:","o":["The Holy Land","Central Europe","Ireland","England"],"a":[0],"e":"During the Middle Ages many knights took part in the Crusades, in which European Christians fought for control of the Holy Land."},{"q":"The line ‘to be or not to be’ belongs to which of the following plays or poems written by William Shakespeare?","o":["As You Like It","Hamlet","Henry V","Romeo and Juliet"],"a":[1],"e":"To be or not to be’ is a line from William Shakespeare’s play Hamlet."},{"q":"Charles I wanted the worship of the Church of England to include more ceremony and introduced a revised Prayer Book:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"What was the name of the project through which the atomic bomb was developed?","o":["The Manhattan project","The Cambridge project","The Michigan project","The Manchester project"],"a":[0],"e":"Scientists led by Ernest Rutherford, working at Manchester and then Cambridge University, were the first to ‘split the atom’ and took part in the Manhattan Project in the United States, which developed the atomic bomb."},{"q":"Who supported the Parliament during the Civil War that began in 1642?","o":["The Roundheads","The Suffragettes","The Cavaliers","The Squareheads"],"a":[0],"e":"A civil war between the king and Parliament began in 1642. The country split into those who supported the king (the Cavaliers) and those who supported Parliament (the Roundheads)."},{"q":"What is the meaning of the term the ‘Divine Right of Kings’?","o":["The idea that the king was directly appointed by God to rule","The idea that the king was appointed by landowners","The King could rule the country but with the support of the Parliament","The Parliament and the King shared responsibilities"],"a":[0],"e":"The ‘Divine Right of Kings’ refers to the idea that the king was directly appointed by God to rule and that the king should be able to act without having to seek approval from Parliament."},{"q":"Who was the first person to lead a Roman invasion in Britain in 55 BC?","o":["William Caxton","Emperor Claudius","Julius Caesar","Napoleon"],"a":[2],"e":"Julius Caesar led a Roman invasion of Britain in 55 BC. This was unsuccessful and for nearly 100 years Britain remained separate from the Roman Empire."},{"q":"When Henry VIII took the title ‘King of Ireland’. English laws were introduced and everyone except for local leaders were expected to follow the instructions of the Lord Lieutenants in Dublin:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. When Henry VIII took the title ‘King of Ireland’. English laws were introduced and local leaders were expected to follow the instructions of the Lord Lieutenants in Dublin."},{"q":"When was Lincoln Cathedral built?","o":["In the 18th century","In medieval times","After the Roman invasion","In the 19th century"],"a":[1],"e":"Lincoln Cathedral was built in the middle ages or medieval times."},{"q":"How old was Edward VI when he died?","o":["1 year old","12 years old","15 years old","17 years old"],"a":[2],"e":"Edward VI died at the age of 15 after ruling for just over six years, and his half-sister Mary became queen."},{"q":"What name was given to those who supported the king during the Civil War that began in 1642?","o":["The Suffragettes","The Squareheads","The Cavaliers","The Roundheads"],"a":[2],"e":"During the Civil War that started in 1642 those who supported the king (the Cavaliers) and those who supported Parliament (the Roundheads)."},{"q":"By 1200, the English ruled an area of Scotland known as the Pale, around Edinburgh:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"By 1200, the English ruled an area of Ireland known as the Pale, around Dublin."},{"q":"After the Second World War, England was exhausted economically.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"True. Although the UK had won the war, the country was exhausted economically after WWII."},{"q":"Who was Florence Nightingale?","o":["A scientist who proposed that enemy aircraft could be detected by radio waves","One of the tribal leaders who fought against the Romans","A nurse who worked in military hospitals, treating soldiers who were fighting in the Crimean War","A mathematician who was influential in the development of computer science"],"a":[2],"e":"Florence Nightingale was a nurse who worked in military hospitals, treating soldiers who were fighting in the Crimean War."},{"q":"Who was the Prime Minister of the UK from 1945 to 1951?","o":["Oliver Cromwell","Clement Attlee","John Major","Margaret Thatcher"],"a":[1],"e":"Clement Attlee was Prime Minister from 1945 to 1951 and led the Labour Party for 20 years."},{"q":"Queen Elizabeth I was a Catholic:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. Queen Elizabeth I was a Protestant."},{"q":"When did King Edward I of England introduce the Statute of Rhuddlan by which Wales annexed to the Crown of England?","o":["In 1284","In 1304","In 1340","In 1235"],"a":[0],"e":"In 1284 King Edward I of England introduced the Statute of Rhuddlan, which annexed Wales to the Crown of England."},{"q":"The settlements of Scottish and English Protestants in Ulster (the northern province of Ireland) during the reigns of Elizabeth I and James I, who took over the land from Catholic landholders is known as:","o":["Pale","Puritans","Cavaliers","Plantations"],"a":[3],"e":"These settlements were known as plantations."}]},{"id":"test-4-1","name":"Test 4.1","group":"Chapter 4","questions":[{"q":"Who was the first Briton to win the ‘Tour de France’?","o":["Sir Chris Hoy","Bradley Wiggins","Andy Murray","Sir Steve Redgrave"],"a":[1],"e":"Sir Bradley Wiggins (1980-) was the first Briton to win the Tour de France."},{"q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["The English language has no dialects","The English language has many accents and dialects","Gaelic is spoken in North Wales","In parts of Northern Ireland people speak French"],"a":[1],"e":""},{"q":"How old do you need to be to go into betting shops or gambling clubs?","o":["16 years old","18 years old","20 years old","21 years old"],"a":[1],"e":"You have to be 18 to go into betting shops or gambling clubs."},{"q":"What do Ian McEwan, Hilary Mantel and Julian Barnes have in common?","o":["They are British sports players","They were awarded with the Man Booker Prize for Fiction","They won a Nobel Prize in Literature","They were awarded with the Mercury Music Prize"],"a":[1],"e":"The Man Booker Prize for Fiction is awarded annually for the best fiction novel written by an author from the Commonwealth, Ireland or Zimbabwe. It has been awarded since 1968. Past winners include Ian McEwan, Hilary Mantel and Julian Barnes."},{"q":"Who was Henry Purcell?","o":["A German-born composer","An organist at Westminster Abbey","An opera composer","A jazz musician"],"a":[1],"e":"Henry Purcell (1659-95) was the organist at Westminster Abbey. He wrote church music, operas and other pieces, and developed a British style distinct from that elsewhere in Europe."},{"q":"Where is the Giant’s Causeway located?","o":["Northern Ireland","Scotland","South-east coast of Wales","South-west coast of England"],"a":[0],"e":"The Giant’s Causeway is located on the north-east coast of Northern Ireland."},{"q":"During Halloween people carve lanterns out of ______ and put a candle inside of them.","o":["Pumpkins","Aubergines","Clay","Potatoes"],"a":[0],"e":"During Halloween people carve lanterns out of pumpkins and put a candle inside of them."},{"q":"Royal Ascot is a four-day horse race meeting in Berkshire attended by members of the Royal Family:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"Royal Ascot, a five-day race meeting in Berkshire attended by members of the Royal Family."},{"q":"Which of the following statements regarding Scottish television is TRUE?","o":["There is a channel with programmes in the Gaelic language","There are three channels in the Gaelic language","All programmes are broadcasted in English","There are no specific programmes to Scotland"],"a":[0],"e":"In Scotland, some Scotland-specific programmes are shown and there is also a channel with programmes in the Gaelic language."},{"q":"Where does the biggest and most well-known Fringe Festival take place?","o":["Cambridge","Oxford","Edinburgh","Cardiff"],"a":[2],"e":"The biggest and most well-known Fringe Festival is the Edinburgh Festival Fringe."},{"q":"Who composed a suite of pieces themed around the planets and the solar system called ‘The Planets’?","o":["The Edward Elgar","George Frederick Handel","Gustav Holst","Sir William Walton"],"a":[2],"e":"More recently, important composers include Gustav Holst (1874-1934), whose work includes The Planets, a suite of pieces themed around the planets and the solar system."},{"q":"What was the population of the UK in 1998?","o":["40 million","50 million","55 million","57 million"],"a":[3],"e":"The population of the UK in 1998 was 57 million people."},{"q":"Which of the following is a musical venue located in London?","o":["The O2","Glastonbury","The Dome","The Fringe"],"a":[0],"e":"There are many large venues that host music events throughout the year, such as: Wembley Stadium; The O2 in Greenwich, south-east London."},{"q":"Who directed the movie ‘The Killing Fields’ in 1984?","o":["Roland Joffé","Hugh Hudson","Kevin MacDonald","Nicolas Roeg"],"a":[0],"e":"The Killing Fields (1984) was directed by Roland Joffé."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following castles are located in Scotland?","o":["Conwy Castle","Caernarfon Castle","Crathes Castle","Inveraray Castle"],"a":[2,3],"e":"Inveraray Castle and Crathes Castle are located in Scotland."},{"q":"Where is the Grand National horse racing event celebrated?","o":["In London","In Aintree","In Bedford","In Slough"],"a":[1],"e":"The Grand National horse racing event is celebrated in Aintree near Liverpool."},{"q":"What was Charles Chaplin famous for?","o":["Special effects movies","Horror movies","Silent movies","Murder-mystery movies"],"a":[2],"e":"Sir Charles (Charlie) Chaplin became famous in silent movies for his tramp character and was one of many British actors to make a career in Hollywood."},{"q":"What percentage of the British population has a parent or grandparent born outside of the UK as a result of post-war immigration?","o":["Nearly 5% of the population","Nearly 10% of the population","Nearly 15% of the population","Nearly 25% of the population"],"a":[1],"e":"Post-war immigration means that nearly 10% of the population has a parent or grandparent born outside the UK."},{"q":"Which of the following categories is recognised at the Laurence Olivier Awards?","o":["Music","Theatre","Event","Literature"],"a":[1],"e":"The Laurence Olivier Awards recognise achievements in the area of theatre."},{"q":"In the UK, there is a National Lottery for which draws are made every month:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. There is a National Lottery for which draws are made every week."},{"q":"Where is Europe’s longest dry ski slope located?","o":["Near Swansea, in Wales","Near Edinburgh, in Scotland","Near Glasgow, in Scotland","Near Belfast, in Northern Ireland"],"a":[1],"e":"There are five ski centres in Scotland, as well as Europe’s longest dry ski slope near Edinburgh."},{"q":"What was the population of the UK in 1600?","o":["Just over 3 million","Just over 4 million","Just under 3 million","Just over 5 million"],"a":[1],"e":"The population of the UK in 1600 was just over 4 million people."},{"q":"What name is given to the 31st December in Scotland?","o":["Hogmanay","Bonfire Night","Vaisakhi","Diwali"],"a":[0],"e":"In Scotland, the 31st of December is called Hogmanay."},{"q":"When is the Sikh festival which celebrates the founding of the Sikh community (Khalsa) and known as Vaisakhi celebrated?","o":["14th of February","14th of April","30th of March","30th of April"],"a":[1],"e":"Vaisakhi (also spelled Baisakhi) is a Sikh festival which celebrates the founding of the Sikh community known as the Khalsa. It is celebrated on 14 April each year with parades, dancing and singing."}]},{"id":"test-4-2","name":"Test 4.2","group":"Chapter 4","questions":[{"q":"When does the Edinburgh Festival take place?","o":["Every summer","Every spring","Every winter","Every autumn"],"a":[0],"e":"﻿The Edinburgh Festival takes place in Edinburgh, Scotland, every summer. It is a series of different arts and cultural festivals, with the biggest and most well-known being the Edinburgh Festival Fringe (‘the Fringe’)."},{"q":"Who directed the movie ‘Brief Encounter’ in 1945?","o":["Alfred Hitchcock","Hugh Hudson","Carol Reed","David Lean"],"a":[3],"e":"Brief Encounter (1945) was directed by David Lean."},{"q":"According to the 2011 Census, what percentage of the population identified themselves as Hindu?","o":["0.5%","1%","1.5%","3%"],"a":[2],"e":"According to the 2011 Census, 1.5% of the people identify themselves as Hindu."},{"q":"Which landscape architect designed grounds around country houses so that the landscape appeared to be natural, with grass, trees and lakes?","o":["Clarice Cliff","Lancelot ‘Capability’ Brown","Alexander McQueen","Vivienne Westwood"],"a":[1],"e":"In the 18th century, Lancelot ‘Capability’ Brown designed the grounds around country houses so that the landscape appeared to be natural, with grass, trees and lakes. He often worked with Edwin Lutyens to design colourful gardens around the houses he designed."},{"q":"Which flower is associated with Scotland?","o":["Rose","Thistle","Daffodil","Shamrock"],"a":[1],"e":"The thistle is the flower associated with Scotland."},{"q":"What is the money from TV licences used for?","o":["To pay actors and actresses","To pay for the British Broadcasting Corporation (BBC)","To pay for publicity","To pay for private channels"],"a":[1],"e":"The money from TV licences is used to pay for the British Broadcasting Corporation (BBC). This is a public service broadcaster providing television and radio programmes. The BBC is the largest broadcaster in the world. It is the only wholly state-funded media organisation that is independent of government."},{"q":"Where is the centrepiece to the Remembrance Day service known as Cenotaph located?","o":["Cardiff, Wales","Whitehall, London","Hounslow Heath, London","Piccadilly Circus, London"],"a":[1],"e":"The Cenotaph, the Centrepiece to the Remembrance Day service, is located in Whitehall, London."},{"q":"Where was the first tennis club founded in the UK located?","o":["Brixton","Leamington Spa","Haywards Heath","St Andrews"],"a":[1],"e":"The first tennis club was founded in Leamington Spa in 1872."},{"q":"Which famous poem tells the story of the knights at the court of King Arthur?","o":["Beowulf","Paradise Lost","Sir Gawain and the Green Knight","King Arthur and his knights"],"a":[2],"e":"Poems which survive from the Middle Ages include Chaucer’s Canterbury Tales and a poem called Sir Gawain and the Green Knight, about one of the knights at the court of King Arthur."},{"q":"Which of the following is a responsibility or freedom shared by all those living in the UK and which people wishing to be a permanent resident or citizen of the UK should respect?","o":["Go to church","Treat others with fairness","Eat traditional British food","Look after your neighbours"],"a":[1],"e":"Treat others with fairness is one of the responsibilities shared by all those living in the UK and which people wishing to be a permanent resident or citizen of the UK should respect."},{"q":"Which flower is associated with Northern Ireland?","o":["Daffodil","Rose","Thistle","Shamrock"],"a":[3],"e":"The shamrock is the flower associated with Northern Ireland."},{"q":"The longest distance on the mainland in the UK is from John O’Groats on the north coast of Scotland to a location in the south-west corner of England known as:","o":["Land’s Point","Earth Point","Eastbourne","Land’s End"],"a":[3],"e":"The longest distance on the mainland is from John O’Groats on the north coast of Scotland to Land’s End in the south-west corner of England. It is about 870 miles (approximately 1,400 kilometres)."},{"q":"When is Christmas Eve celebrated?","o":["24th of December","26th of December","25th of December","27th of December"],"a":[0],"e":"Christmas Eve is celebrated on the 24th of December."},{"q":"Which British sportsman won five consecutive gold medals at the Olympic Games in the rowing category?","o":["Sir Chris Hoy","Christopher Dean","Bradley Wiggins","Sir Steve Redgrave"],"a":[3],"e":"Sir Steve Redgrave (1962-) won gold medals in rowing in five consecutive Olympic Games and is one of Britain’s greatest Olympians."},{"q":"What is the name of the centrepiece to the Remembrance Day service located in Whitehall (London)?","o":["Cenotaph","Stonehenge","Grimsby","Scunthorpe"],"a":[0],"e":"The Cenotaph in Whitehall is the site of the annual Remembrance Day service attended by the King, politicians and foreign ambassadors."},{"q":"What is the minimum age requirement in the UK for people to be able to drink wine or beer with a meal in a hotel or restaurant as long as they are with someone over 18?","o":["14 years old","15 years old","16 years old","17 years old"],"a":[2],"e":"When they are 16, people can drink wine or beer with a meal in a hotel or restaurant (including eating areas in pubs) as long as they are with someone over 18."},{"q":"Who is the spiritual leader of the Church of England?","o":["The Pope","The Archbishop of Canterbury","St Augustine","St Columba"],"a":[1],"e":"The spiritual leader of the Church of England is the Archbishop of Canterbury."},{"q":"What day is Halloween celebrated?","o":["30th of November","30th of October","14th of February","31st of October"],"a":[3],"e":"Halloween is celebrated on the 31st of October."},{"q":"Who was the first British distance runner to win a gold medal in the Olympic games in the 10,000 metres?","o":["Mo Farah","Bradley Wiggins","Sir Chris Hoy","David Weir"],"a":[0],"e":"Mo Farah (1983-) is a British distance runner, born in Somalia. He won gold medals in the 2012 Olympics for the 5,000 and 10,000 metres and is the first Briton to win the Olympic gold medal in the 10,000 metres."},{"q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["For some Scottish people, Hogmanay is a bigger holiday than Christmas","For some Scottish people, Hogmanay is a bigger holiday than New Year’s Eve","For some Welsh people, Hogmanay is a bigger holiday than Christmas","For some Irish people, Hogmanay is a bigger holiday than New Year’s Eve"],"a":[0],"e":"In Scotland, 31 December is called Hogmanay and 2 January is also a public holiday. For some Scottish people, Hogmanay is a bigger holiday than Christmas."},{"q":"Light-hearted plays with music and comedy, which are enjoyed by family audiences and based on fairy stories are known as:","o":["Children’s plays","Pantomimes","Hogmanay","Parades"],"a":[1],"e":"Many theatres produce a pantomime at Christmas time. They are based on fairy stories and are light-plays with music and comedy, enjoyed by family audiences."},{"q":"Who built the Tower of London after becoming king in 1066?","o":["Oliver Cromwell","Henry VIII","William the Conqueror","Henry VII"],"a":[2],"e":"The Tower of London was first built by William the Conqueror after he became king in 1066."},{"q":"Who was the captain of the English football team that won the World Cup in 1966?","o":["David Beckham","Bobby Moore","Sir Ian Botham","Sir Roger Bannister"],"a":[1],"e":"Bobby Moore (1941-93) captained the English football team that won the World Cup in 1966."},{"q":"Which of the following sports can be traced as far back as the Roman times?","o":["Tennis","Football","Horse racing","Pool"],"a":[2],"e":"There is a very long history of horse racing in Britain, with evidence of events taking place as far back as Roman times."}]},{"id":"test-4-3","name":"Test 4.3","group":"Chapter 4","questions":[{"q":"Which of the following venues is located in Glasgow?","o":["The O2","Wembley Stadium","The SEC Centre","Buckingham Palace"],"a":[2],"e":"The Scottish Exhibition and Conference Centre (SEC Centre) is located in Glasgow."},{"q":"What was the population of the UK in 1801?","o":["5 million","8 million","10 million","12 million"],"a":[1],"e":"The population of the UK in 1801 was 8 million people."},{"q":"Where is the UK geographically located?","o":["In the north east of Europe","In the south east of Europe","In the north west of Europe","In the south west of Europe"],"a":[2],"e":"﻿The UK is located in the north west of Europe."},{"q":"When did Sir Alfred Hitchcock die?","o":["1970","1975","1978","1980"],"a":[3],"e":"﻿Alfred Hitchcock died in 1980."},{"q":"When is St George’s Day, patron of England, celebrated?","o":["23rd of April","1st of March","17th of March","30th of November"],"a":[0],"e":"﻿St George’s Day, patron of England, is celebrated on the 23rd of April."},{"q":"How many Welsh-language channels are there in Wales?","o":["None","One","Two","Three"],"a":[1],"e":"There is a Welsh-language channel in Wales."},{"q":"What is the nickname given to the great bell of the clock at the House of Parliament in London?","o":["Tower of London","Buckingham clock","Big Ben","Big Clock"],"a":[2],"e":"Big Ben is the nickname for the great bell of the clock at the House of Parliament in London."},{"q":"According to the 2011 Census, what percentage of the population identified themselves as Jewish?","o":["Less than 0.5%","Less than 1%","Less than 1.5%","Less than 2%"],"a":[0],"e":"According to the 2011 census, less than 0.5% of people identify themselves as Jewish."},{"q":"Which of the following castles is located in Scotland?","o":["Conwy Castle","Caernarfon Castle","Crathes Castle","Windsor Castle"],"a":[2],"e":"Crathes Castle and Inveraray Castle are located in Scotland."},{"q":"Who were the Pre-Raphaelites?","o":["An important group of musicians from the 16th century","An important group who demanded the vote for the working classes","An important group of artists in the second half of the 19th century","A group of explorers who arrived in the south-east coast of Australia"],"a":[2],"e":"The Pre-Raphaelites were an important group of artists in the second half of the 19th century. They painted detailed pictures on religious or literary themes in bright colours. The group included Holman Hunt, Dante Gabriel Rossetti and Sir John Millais."},{"q":"When was St Pancras Station built?","o":["In the 18th century","In the 19th century","In the 20th century","In the 21st century"],"a":[1],"e":"St Pancras Station was built in the 19th century."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following soap operas are popular in the UK?","o":["Braveheart","Coronation Street","EastEnders","Heroes"],"a":[1,2],"e":"Popular programmes in the UK include regular soap operas such as Coronation Street and EastEnders."},{"q":"Which of the following gardens is located in Wales?","o":["Bodnant Garden","Kew Gardens","Hidcote","Sissinghurst"],"a":[0],"e":"There are famous gardens to visit throughout the UK, including Kew Gardens, Sissinghurst and Hidcote in England, Crathes Castle and Inveraray Castle in Scotland, Bodnant Garden in Wales, and Mount Stewart in Northern Ireland."},{"q":"Where is the National Gallery of Scotland located?","o":["In Edinburgh","In Glasgow","In Aberdeen","In Fife"],"a":[0],"e":"The National Gallery of Scotland is in Edinburgh."},{"q":"Who directed the movie ‘Chariots of Fire’ in 1981?","o":["Mike Newell","Hugh Hudson","Kevin MacDonald","Nicolas Roeg"],"a":[1],"e":"Chariots of Fire (1981) was directed by Hugh Hudson."},{"q":"When was the first tennis club founded in the UK?","o":["1772","1872","1862","1762"],"a":[1],"e":"The first tennis club was founded in Leamington Spa in 1872."},{"q":"What is the currency in the UK?","o":["Euro","Dollar","Pound","Dinar"],"a":[2],"e":"The currency in the UK is the pound sterling (symbol £)."},{"q":"When is Diwali normally celebrated?","o":["June or July","October or November","November or December","March or April"],"a":[1],"e":"Diwali normally falls in October or November and lasts for five days."},{"q":"Which TWO universities compete with each other on a rowing race along the River Thames every year?","o":["University College of London","Imperial College","Cambridge University","Oxford University"],"a":[2,3],"e":"There is a popular yearly rowing race on the Thames between Oxford and Cambridge Universities."},{"q":"Where is the city of Swansea located?","o":["In England","In Scotland","In Wales","In the Isle of Man"],"a":[2],"e":"Swansea is located in Wales."},{"q":"What time do pubs usually open during the week?","o":["9 am","10 am","11 am","12 pm"],"a":[2],"e":"Pubs are usually open during the day from 11.00 am (12 noon on Sundays)."},{"q":"In which TWO movie categories does Britain continue to be particularly strong at?","o":["Special effects movies","Action movies","Thrillers","Animation movies"],"a":[0,3],"e":"Britain continues to be particularly strong in special effects and animation."},{"q":"Which notable British artist was a portrait painter who often painted people in country or garden scenery?","o":["Thomas Gainsborough","David Allan","Joseph Turner","John Constable"],"a":[0],"e":"Thomas Gainsborough (1727-88) was a portrait painter who often painted people in country or garden scenery."},{"q":"During which religious celebration do Muslims sacrifice an animal to eat?","o":["Eid ul Adha","Eid a-Fitr","Ramadan","Vaisakhi"],"a":[0],"e":"Many Muslims sacrifice an animal to eat during Eid ul Adha."}]},{"id":"test-4-4","name":"Test 4.4","group":"Chapter 4","questions":[{"q":"Which of the following is the official church of the state?","o":["The Church of Great Britain","The Church of England","The Church of the United Kingdom","The British Church"],"a":[1],"e":"The official Church of the state is the Church of England (called the Anglican Church in other countries and the Episcopal Church in Scotland and the United States)."},{"q":"What was the population of the UK in 1700?","o":["2 million","4 million","5 million","8 million"],"a":[2],"e":"The population of the UK in 1700 was 5 million people."},{"q":"How many square miles does Loch Lomond and the Trossachs National Park cover?","o":["720","740","820","840"],"a":[0],"e":"Loch Lomond and the Trossachs National Park covers 720 square miles (1,865 square kilometres) in the west of Scotland."},{"q":"Which British athlete won 16 medals, including 11 gold medals, in races over five Paralympic Games?","o":["Dame Kelly Holmes","Sir Ian Botham","Baroness Tanni Grey-Thompson","Dame Elle MacArthur"],"a":[2],"e":"Baroness Tanni Grey-Thompson (1969-) is an athlete who uses a wheelchair and won 16 Paralympic medals, including 11 gold medals, in races over five Paralympic Games. She won the London Marathon six times and broke a total of 30 world records."},{"q":"When is New Year’s Eve celebrated?","o":["25th of December","30th of December","31st of December","1st of January"],"a":[2],"e":"New Year’s Eve is celebrated on the 31st of December."},{"q":"What is the capital city of Scotland?","o":["Dundee","Aberdeen","Edinburgh","Glasgow"],"a":[2],"e":"The capital city of Scotland is Edinburgh."},{"q":"What British sportsmen holds a number of English Test cricket records?","o":["Bobby Moore","Sir Jackie Steward","Sir Ian Botham","Sir Roger Bannister"],"a":[2],"e":"Sir Ian Botham (1955-) captained the English cricket team and holds a number of English Test cricket records, both for batting and for bowling."},{"q":"What can you find at the ‘Eden Project’?","o":["Giant greenhouses, house plants from all over the world","Animals from all over the world","Prehistoric monuments","An open market with local food"],"a":[0],"e":"The Eden Project is located in Cornwall, in the south west of England. Its biomes, which are like giant greenhouses, house plants from all over the world. The Eden Project is also a charity which runs environmental and social projects internationally."},{"q":"What TWO names are given to the people who give tours at the Tower of London?","o":["Beefeaters","Suffragettes","Chartists","Yeoman Wardens"],"a":[0,3],"e":"Tours to the Tower of London are given by the Yeoman Warders, also known as Beefeaters, who tell visitors about the building’s history."},{"q":"Which of the following is a traditional Scottish food?","o":["Roast beef","Haggis","Ulster fry","Custard pies"],"a":[1],"e":"Haggis – a sheep’s stomach stuffed with offal. Suet, onions and oatmeal – is a traditional Scottish food."},{"q":"How often do the Laurence Olivier Awards take place?","o":["Every six months","Annually","Every four years","Every two years"],"a":[1],"e":"The Laurence Olivier Awards take place annually at different venues in London."},{"q":"When is Valentine’s Day celebrated?","o":["30th of October","14th of February","30th of November","27th of December"],"a":[1],"e":"Valentine’s Day is celebrated on the 14th of February."},{"q":"When is St Patrick’s Day, patron to of Northern Ireland, celebrated?","o":["23rd of April","30th of November","1st of March","17th of March"],"a":[3],"e":"St Patrick’s Day, patron to of Northern Ireland is on the 17th of March."},{"q":"What is the name of the annual event that gives awards in a range of musical categories, such as best British group and best British solo artist?","o":["The Brit Awards","The MTV Awards","The BAFTA Awards","The Mercury Music Prize"],"a":[0],"e":"The Brit Awards is an annual event that gives awards in a range of categories, such as best British group and best British solo artist."},{"q":"How old do you need to be to be able to play the National Lottery?","o":["16 years old","18 years old","20 years old","21 years old"],"a":[1],"e":"People under 18 are not allowed to participate in the National Lottery."},{"q":"Where is the five-day race meeting attended by members of the Royal Family and known as Royal Ascot celebrated?","o":["In Luton","In Berkshire","In Salisbury","In Kent"],"a":[1],"e":"Royal Ascot, the five-day race meeting attended by members of the Royal Family, takes place in Berkshire."},{"q":"Who wrote ‘The Lord of the Rings’?","o":["JK Rowling","JRR Tolkien","Sir William Golding","Jane Austen"],"a":[1],"e":"The Lord of the Rings was written by JRR Tolkien."},{"q":"According to the 2011 Census, what percentage of the population identified themselves as Buddhist?","o":["Less than 0.5%","Less than 1%","Less than 2%","Less than 3%"],"a":[0],"e":"In the 2011 Census, 59% of people identified themselves as Christian. Much smaller proportions identified themselves as Muslim (4.8%), Hindu (1.5%), Sikh (0.8%), Jewish or Buddhist (both less than 0.5% )."},{"q":"In which category did Jayne Torvill and Christopher Dean win gold medals at the Olympic Games in 1984?","o":["Rowing","Marathon","Swimming","Ice dancing"],"a":[3],"e":"Jayne Torvill and Christopher Dean won gold medals for ice dancing at the Olympic Games in 1984 and is one of Britain’s greatest Olympians."},{"q":"What name is given to the Church of England in Scotland and the United States?","o":["The Main Church","The King’s Church","The Episcopal Church","The Catholic Church"],"a":[2],"e":"The official Church of the state is the Church of England (called the Anglican Church in other countries and the Episcopal Church in Scotland and the United States )."},{"q":"Who directed the movie ‘Four Weddings and a Funeral’ in 1994?","o":["Mike Newell","Hugh Hudson","Kevin MacDonald","Nicolas Roeg"],"a":[0],"e":"Four Weddings and a Funeral (1994) was directed by Mike Newell."},{"q":"What was the population of the UK in 1951?","o":["20 million","40 million","35 million","50 million"],"a":[3],"e":"The population of the UK in 1951 was 50 million people."},{"q":"Which TWO religions celebrate Diwali?","o":["Buddhist","Muslim","Hindu","Sikh"],"a":[2,3],"e":"Diwali normally falls in October or November and lasts for five days. It is often called the Festival of Lights. It is celebrated by Hindus and Sikhs."},{"q":"How many ski centres are there in Scotland?","o":["4","5","6","7"],"a":[1],"e":"There are five ski centres in Scotland, as well as Europe’s longest dry ski slope near Edinburgh."}]},{"id":"test-4-5","name":"Test 4.5","group":"Chapter 4","questions":[{"q":"Which Scottish city is known as the home of golf?","o":["Dundee","Edinburgh","Aberdeen","St Andrews"],"a":[3],"e":"St. Andrews in Scotland is known as the home of golf."},{"q":"Which of the following operas was written by Gilbert and Sullivan?","o":["The Phantom of the Opera","The Mikado","The Mousetrap","Cats"],"a":[1],"e":"In the 19th century, Gilbert and Sullivan wrote comic operas, often making fun of popular culture and politics. These operas include HMS Pinafore, The Pirates of Penzance and The Mikado."},{"q":"Where is Loch Lomond and the Trossachs National Park located?","o":["East of Wales","West of Wales","West of Scotland","East of Scotland"],"a":[2],"e":"Loch Lomond and the Trossachs National Park is located in the west of Scotland."},{"q":"By what other TWO names is the Church of England known for?","o":["The Anglican Church","The Union Church","The Greatest Church","The Episcopal Church"],"a":[0,3],"e":"The official Church of the state is the Church of England (called the Anglican Church in other countries and the Episcopal Church in Scotland and the United States )."},{"q":"Who used a system of land ownership known as feudalism?","o":["The Greek","The Vikings","The Normans","The Welsh"],"a":[2],"e":"The Normans used a system of land ownership known as feudalism."},{"q":"In which city is the Welsh Assembly building, also known as Senedd, located?","o":["Edinburgh","Belfast","Cardiff Bay","Swansea"],"a":[2],"e":"In Wales the elected members, known as AMs, meet in the Welsh Assembly in the Senedd in Cardiff Bay."},{"q":"What architectural style became popular in the 19th century?","o":["Corinthian","Barroco","Medieval Gothic","Art Deco"],"a":[2],"e":"In the 19th century, the medieval ’gothic’ style became popular again. As cities expanded, many great public buildings were built in this style. The Houses of Parliament and St Pancras Station were built at this time, as were the town halls in cities such as Manchester and Sheffield."},{"q":"Which of the following gardens is NOT located in England?","o":["Mount Stewart","Kew Gardens","Sissinghurst","Hidcote"],"a":[0],"e":"There are famous gardens to visit throughout the UK, including Kew Gardens, Sissinghurst and Hidcote in England, Crathes Castle and Inveraray Castle in Scotland, Bodnant Garden in Wales, and Mount Stewart in Northern Ireland."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a World Heritage Site?","o":["The London Eye","Stonehenge","The forts of Housesteads and Vindolanda","The Houses of Parliament"],"a":[0],"e":"The London Eye is not a World Heritage Site."},{"q":"Which famous murder-mystery play has been running in the west end since 1952 and has had the longest initial run of any show in history?","o":["The Phantom of the Opera","The Mousetrap","Evita","Jesus Christ Superstar"],"a":[1],"e":"The Mousetrap, a murder-mystery play by Dame Agatha Christie, has been running in the west end since 1952 and has had the longest initial run of any show in history."},{"q":"When was the Giant’s Causeway formed?","o":["20 million years ago","40 million years ago","50 million years ago","15 million years ago"],"a":[2],"e":"The Giant’s Causeway was formed about 50 million years ago."},{"q":"Who was the first person to sail single-handed around the world without stopping?","o":["Sir Francis Drake","Dame Ellen MacArthur","Sir Robin Knox-Johnston","Sir Francis Chichester"],"a":[2],"e":"A British sailor, Sir Francis Chichester, was the first person to sail singlehanded around the world, in 1966/67. Two years later, Sir Robin Knox-Johnston became the first person to do this without stopping."},{"q":"Where is the National Horseracing Museum located?","o":["In Newmarket, Suffolk","In St Andrews, Scotland","In Richmond, London","In Aintree, near Liverpool"],"a":[0],"e":"The National Horseracing Museum is located in Newmarket, Suffolk."},{"q":"Which of the following is a popular showcase of mainly theatre and comedy performances and the biggest and most well-known festival that takes place during the Edinburgh Festival?","o":["The Proms","The Fringe","The Festival of Lights","The Aldeburgh Festival"],"a":[1],"e":"The Fringe is a showcase of mainly theatre and comedy performances. It often shows experimental work.The biggest and most well-known being the Edinburgh Festival Fringe."},{"q":"How old do you need to be to buy a lottery ticket or scratch card?","o":["16","18","21","25"],"a":[1],"e":"People under 18 are not allowed to participate in the National Lottery."},{"q":"When did motor-car racing start in the UK?","o":["In 1889","In 1901","In 1902","In 1922"],"a":[2],"e":"Motor-car racing in the UK started in 1902."},{"q":"Which one of the following gardens is located in Northern Ireland?","o":["Bodnant Garden","Mount Stewart","Sissinghurst","Hidcote"],"a":[1],"e":"Mount Stewart is located in Northern Ireland, Bodnant Garden in Wales and Sissinghurst and Hidcote in England."},{"q":"When is the Mercury Music Prize awarded?","o":["July","August","September","October"],"a":[2],"e":"The Mercury Music Prize is awarded each September for the best album from the UK and Ireland."},{"q":"What is the name of the only ‘Grand Slam’ tennis event played on grass?","o":["The Australian open","The Wimbledon Championships","The U.S. open","The Queen’s open"],"a":[1],"e":"The Wimbledon Championships is the oldest tennis tournament in the world and the only ‘Grand Slam’ event played on grass."},{"q":"What name is given to the candelabrum lit during the eight-day holiday of Hanukkah?","o":["Menorah","Diwali","Ramadan","Baisakhi"],"a":[0],"e":"Hanukkah is in November or December and is celebrated for eight days. On each day of the festival a candle is lit on a stand of eight candles (called a menorah ) to remember the story of the festival, where oil that should have lasted only a day did so for eight."},{"q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["It is against the law to walk your dog on the beach","It is against the law to treat a pet cruelly or to neglect it","It is against the law to take pets into public places","It is against the law to feed your pet in public places"],"a":[1],"e":"It is against the law to treat a pet cruelly or to neglect it."},{"q":"What charity works for the preservation of important buildings, the coastline and countryside in the UK?","o":["The National Trust","The RSPB","Shelter","Oxfam"],"a":[0],"e":"The National Trust works for the preservation of important buildings, coastline and countryside in the UK."},{"q":"When was the Queen’s Diamond Jubilee celebrated?","o":["2005","2010","2011","2012"],"a":[3],"e":"The Queen’s Diamond Jubilee celebrated in 2012."},{"q":"Which popular British sport can be traced back to the 15th century?","o":["Football","Golf","Rugby","Tennis"],"a":[1],"e":"The modern game of golf can be traced back to 15th century Scotland."}]},{"id":"test-4-6","name":"Test 4.6","group":"Chapter 4","questions":[{"q":"When does Lent take place?","o":["40 days before Christmas","40 days after Christmas","40 days before Easter","40 days after Easter"],"a":[2],"e":"The 40 days before Easter are known as Lent. It is a time when Christians take time to reflect and prepare for Easter."},{"q":"Which of the following national galleries is NOT located in London?","o":["Tate Britain","The National Gallery","Tate Modern","The National Museum"],"a":[3],"e":"The National Museum is located in Cardiff, Wales."},{"q":"How much can you be fined if you watch TV but do not have a TV licence?","o":["Up to £500","Up to £1000","Up to £2000","Up to £3000"],"a":[1],"e":"You will receive a fine up to £1,000 if you watch TV but do not have a TV licence."},{"q":"How old is the clock at the house of the parliament in London, also known as ‘Big Ben’?","o":["100 years old","150 years old","200 years old","250 years old"],"a":[1],"e":"Big Ben is the nickname for the great bell of the clock at the House of Parliament in London. The clock is over 150 years old and is a popular tourist attraction."},{"q":"Which is the oldest tennis tournament in the world?","o":["The Australian open","The U.S. Open","Roland Garros","The Wimbledon Championships"],"a":[3],"e":"The most famous tournament hosted in Britain is The Wimbledon Championships, which takes place each year at the All England Lawn Tennis and Croquet Club. It is the oldest tennis tournament in the world and the only ‘Grand Slam’ event played on grass."},{"q":"Where is the ‘Eden Project’ located?","o":["Cornwall","Devon","Doncaster","Dublin"],"a":[0],"e":"The Eden Project is located in Cornwall, in the south west of England."},{"q":"Which of the following is a traditional food from Northern Ireland?","o":["Roast beef","Haggis","Sausage casserole","Ulster fry"],"a":[3],"e":"Ulster fry – a fried meal with bacon, eggs, sausage, black pudding, tomatoes, mushrooms, soda bread and potato bread – is a traditional Northern Ireland."},{"q":"Who is the author of the murder mystery play ‘The Mousetrap’?","o":["Tim Rice","Andrew Lloyd Webber","Dame Agatha Christie","Gilbert and Sullivan"],"a":[2],"e":"The Mousetrap, a murder-mystery play by Dame Agatha Christie, has been running in the west end since 1952 and has had the longest initial run of any show in history."},{"q":"When is April Fool’s Day?","o":["1st of April","13th of April","30th of April","5th of April"],"a":[0],"e":"April Fool’s Day is on the 1st of April."},{"q":"Where is the city of Bradford located?","o":["In England","In Scotland","In Wales","In Northern Ireland"],"a":[0],"e":"Bradford is located in England."},{"q":"Which flag comprises a red cross on a white ground?","o":["The cross of St Andrew, patron saint of Scotland","The cross of St David, patron saint of Wales","The cross of St Patrick, patron saint of Ireland","The cross of St George, patron saint of England"],"a":[3],"e":"The cross of St George, patron saint of England, is a red cross on a white ground."},{"q":"What is celebrated during Diwali?","o":["Jesus Christ rising from the dead","The victory of good over evil and the gaining of knowledge","The founding of the Sikh community known as the Khalsa","The beginning of the winter season"],"a":[1],"e":"Diwalli celebrates the victory of good over evil and the gaining of knowledge."},{"q":"According to the 2011 Census, what percentage of the population identified themselves as Christian?","o":["35%","55%","70%","59%"],"a":[3],"e":"In the 2011 census, 59% of people identified themselves as Christians."},{"q":"Which golf ‘Major’ tournament is hosted by a different golf course every year?","o":["The Open Championship","The PGA Championship","The Masters Tournament","The U.S. Open"],"a":[0],"e":"The Open Championship is the only ‘Major’ tournament held outside United States. It is hosted by a different golf course every year."},{"q":"Which British actor has recently won an Oscar?","o":["Hugh Grant","Sir Anthony Hopkins","Jude Law","Richard Burton"],"a":[1],"e":"Recent British actors to have won Oscars include Colin Firth, Sir Antony Hopkins, Dame Judi Dench, Kate Winslet and Tilda Swinton."},{"q":"What TWO names are given to the day before Lent?","o":["Pancake day","Domesday","Shrove Tuesday","Ash Wednesday"],"a":[0,2],"e":"The day before Lent starts is called Shrove Tuesday, or Pancake Day."},{"q":"Which of the following landmarks is the largest expanse of fresh water in mainland Britain?","o":["The River Thames","Loch Lomond","Loch Ness","Lake District"],"a":[1],"e":"Loch Lomond is the largest expanse of fresh water in mainland Britain and probably the best-known part of the park."},{"q":"Who wrote the music for the popular show Jesus Christ Superstar?","o":["Andrew Lloyd Webber and Tim Rice","Gilbert and Sullivan","Hans Holbein and Sir Anthony Van Dyck","Sir Laurence Olivier"],"a":[0],"e":"Andrew Lloyd Webber has written the music for shows which have been popular throughout the world, including, in collaboration with Tim Rice, Jesus Christ Superstar and Evita, and also Cats and The Phantom of the Opera."},{"q":"Pantomimes are a British tradition that many theatres produce during:","o":["Easter","Christmas","Halloween","Pancake Day"],"a":[1],"e":"Many theatres produce a pantomime at Christmas time."},{"q":"Which of the following meals is made of bacon, eggs, sausage, black pudding, tomatoes, mushrooms, soda bread and potato bread?","o":["English breakfast","Haggis","Ulster fry","Sunday roast"],"a":[2],"e":"Ulster fry is a fried meal from Northern Ireland with bacon, eggs, sausage, black pudding, tomatoes, mushrooms, soda bread and potato bread."},{"q":"Which of the following is a showcase of mainly theatre and comedy performances?","o":["The Fringe","Diwali","Halloween","Lent"],"a":[0],"e":"The Fringe is a showcase of mainly theatre and comedy performances."},{"q":"Which of the following are public holidays in Scotland (choose TWO options)?","o":["24th of December","31st of December","1st of January","2nd of January"],"a":[2,3],"e":"In Scotland, the 1st of January and the 2nd of January are bank holidays."},{"q":"What name is given to the period of time when Christians take time to reflect and prepare for Easter?","o":["Halloween","Lent","Diwali","Hanukkah"],"a":[1],"e":"Lent is a time when Christians take time to reflect and prepare for Easter."},{"q":"In Wales, many people speak Gaelic – a completely different language from English – and it is taught in schools and universities:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False.﻿In Wales, many people speak Welsh – a completely different language from English – and it is taught in schools and universities."}]},{"id":"test-4-7","name":"Test 4.7","group":"Chapter 4","questions":[{"q":"What TWO films were directed by David Lean?","o":["Brief Encounter","The 39 Steps","Lawrence of Arabia","Chariots of Fire"],"a":[0,2],"e":"﻿David Lean directed Brief Encounter in 1945 and Lawrence of Arabia in 1962."},{"q":"Which religious community celebrates Vaisakhi?","o":["Muslim","Hindu","Sikh","Jewish"],"a":[2],"e":"Vaisakhi (also spelled Baisakhi) is a Sikh festival which celebrates the founding of the Sikh community known as the Khalsa."},{"q":"Which book written by JRR Tolkien was voted the country’s best-loved novel in 2003?","o":["Harry Potter","The Lord of the Rings","Sense and Sensibility","Pride and Prejudice"],"a":[1],"e":"In 2003, The Lord of the Rings by JRR Tolkien was voted the country’s best-loved novel."},{"q":"Which Anglo-Saxon poem is about a hero’s battle against monsters and has been translated into modern English?","o":["Beowulf","Paradise Lost","Canterbury Tales","Braveheart"],"a":[0],"e":"The Anglo-Saxon poem Beowulf tells of its hero’s battles against monsters and is still translated into modern English."},{"q":"Which of the following movies was directed by Alfred Hitchcock in 1935?","o":["Don’t Look Now","Touching the Void","The Third Man","The 39 Steps"],"a":[3],"e":"The 39 Steps (1935) was directed by Alfred Hitchcock."},{"q":"What is the note with the highest value in the UK?","o":["£20","£50","£100","£500"],"a":[1],"e":"The note with the highest value in the UK is the £50 note."},{"q":"Which is the most famous tennis tournament hosted in Britain and which takes place each year at the All England Lawn Tennis and Croquet Club?","o":["Roland Garros","The Wimbledon Championships","The British open","The Queen’s open"],"a":[1],"e":"The most famous tournament hosted in Britain is The Wimbledon Championships, which takes place each year at the All England Lawn Tennis and Croquet Club."},{"q":"What name is given to the clock tower at the Houses of Parliament in honour of Queen Elizabeth II’s Diamond Jubilee in 2012?","o":["Big Ben","Big Clock","Elizabeth Tower","Clock Tower"],"a":[2],"e":"The clock tower at the Houses of Parliament in London is named ‘Elizabeth Tower’ in honour of Queen Elizabeth II’s Diamond Jubilee in 2012."},{"q":"Which British actor has recently won an Oscar?","o":["Daniel Day Lewis","Sir Anthony Hopkins","Jude Law","Richard Burton"],"a":[1],"e":"Recent British actors to have won Oscars include Colin Firth, Sir Antony Hopkins, Dame Judi Dench, Kate Winslet and Tilda Swinton."},{"q":"What TWO names are given to the day before Lent?","o":["Pancake day","Domesday","Shrove Tuesday","Ash Wednesday"],"a":[0,2],"e":"The day before Lent starts is called Shrove Tuesday, or Pancake Day."},{"q":"Which of the following landmarks is the largest expanse of fresh water in mainland Britain?","o":["The River Thames","Loch Lomond","Loch Ness","Lake District"],"a":[1],"e":"Loch Lomond is the largest expanse of fresh water in mainland Britain and probably the best-known part of the park."},{"q":"Who wrote the music for the popular show Jesus Christ Superstar?","o":["Andrew Lloyd Webber","Gilbert and Sullivan","Hans Holbein and Sir Anthony Van Dyck","Sir Laurence Olivier"],"a":[0],"e":"Andrew Lloyd Webber has written the music for shows which have been popular throughout the world, including, in collaboration with Tim Rice, Jesus Christ Superstar and Evita, and also Cats and The Phantom of the Opera."},{"q":"Pantomimes are a British tradition that many theatres produce during:","o":["Easter","Christmas","Halloween","Pancake Day"],"a":[1],"e":"Many theatres produce a pantomime at Christmas time."},{"q":"What is the minimum age requirement to buy alcohol in a pub or night club in the UK?","o":["16 years old","18 years old","20 years old","21 years old"],"a":[1],"e":"﻿To buy alcohol in a pub or night club you must be 18 or over, but people under that age may be allowed in some pubs with an adult. When they are 16, people can drink wine or beer with a meal in a hotel or restaurant (including eating areas in pubs) as long as they are with someone over 18."},{"q":"What is the coin with the highest value in the UK?","o":["50p","£2","£5","£10"],"a":[1],"e":"﻿The coin with the highest value in the UK is worth £2."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following are famous horse racing events in the UK?","o":["The Major Race","The Grand National","Royal Ascot","Six Nations Championship"],"a":[1,2],"e":"﻿ The Grand National and Royal Ascot are famous horse racing events that take place in the UK."},{"q":"Who wrote an oratorio called ‘Messiah’, which is regularly sung by choirs at Easter time?","o":["George Frederick Handel","Sir Edward Elgar","Gustav Holst","Henry Purcell"],"a":[0],"e":"﻿George Frederick Handel wrote an oratorio, Messiah, which is sung regularly by choirs, often at Easter time."},{"q":"Which of the following is England’s largest national park?","o":["The Lake District","Snowdonia","South Downs","Loch Lomond and the Trossachs"],"a":[0],"e":"﻿The Lake District is England’s largest national park, covering 912 square miles (2,362 square kilometres)."},{"q":"Which of the following is a traditional English food?","o":["French fries","Roast beef","Haggis","Ulster fry"],"a":[1],"e":"﻿ Roast beef is a traditional English food, which is served with potatoes, vegetables, Yorkshire puddings (batter that is baked in the oven) and other accompaniments."},{"q":"What are pantomimes based on?","o":["Fairy stories","Drama stories","Traditional stories","War stories"],"a":[0],"e":"Pantomimes are based on fairy stories and are light-hearted plays with music and comedy, enjoyed by family audiences."},{"q":"Who was the first person to sail single-handed around the world in 1966/67?","o":["Sir Robin Knox-Johnston","Sir Francis Chichester","Sir Francis Drake","Dame Ellen MacArthur"],"a":[1],"e":"A British sailor, Sir Francis Chichester, was the first person to sail singlehanded around the world, in 1966/67."},{"q":"Who led the group of Catholics who tried to kill the Protestant king with a bomb in the Houses of the Parliament in 1605?","o":["Oliver Cromwell","Alexander II","Guy Fawkes","Adrian IV"],"a":[2],"e":"In 1605, a group of Catholics led by Guy Fawkes failed in their plan to kill the Protestant king with a bomb in the Houses of Parliament. This is the origin of the Bonfire Night celebration on the 5th November, when people in Great Britain set off fireworks at home or in special displays."},{"q":"The UK continues to be a world leader in the development and manufacture of motor-sport technology:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"True. There is a long history of motor sport in the UK, for both cars and motor cycles. Motor-car racing in the UK started in 1902. The UK continues to be a world leader in the development and manufacture of motor-sport technology."},{"q":"Where is the Scottish Exhibition and Conference Centre (SEC Centre) located?","o":["Aberdeen","Glasgow","Edinburgh","Dundee"],"a":[1],"e":"The Scottish Exhibition and Conference Centre (SEC Centre) is in Glasgow."}]},{"id":"test-4-8","name":"Test 4.8","group":"Chapter 4","questions":[{"q":"Which is the capital city of Northern Ireland?","o":["Belfast","Dublin","Cork","Edinburgh"],"a":[0],"e":"The capital city of Northern Ireland is Belfast."},{"q":"What name is given to the day when people play jokes on each other until midday?","o":["Remembrance Day","April’s Fool Day","Halloween","Valentine’s Day"],"a":[1],"e":"April Fool’s Day, 1 April, is a day when people play jokes on each other until midday. The television and newspapers often have stories that are April Fool jokes."},{"q":"What is Dame Ellen MacArthur known for?","o":["She was the fastest person to have sailed around the world single-handed","She won the London Marathon six times","She won gold medals for ice dancing at the Olympic Games in 1984","She won gold medals in rowing in five consecutive Olympic Games"],"a":[0],"e":"Dame Ellen MacArthur (1976-) is a yachtswoman and in 2004 became the fastest person to sail around the world singlehanded."},{"q":"What are Welsh cakes made of?","o":["Suet, onions and oatmeal","Flour, dried fruits and spices","Potatoes, vegetables, Yorkshire puddings","Bacon, eggs, sausage, black pudding and potato bread"],"a":[1],"e":"Welsh cakes are a traditional Welsh snack made from flour, dried fruits and spices, and served either hot or cold."},{"q":"Where is the London Eye situated?","o":["On the northern bank of the River Thames","On the southern bank of the River Thames","On the northern bank of the River Lea","On the southern bank of the River Lea"],"a":[1],"e":"The London Eye is situated on the southern bank of the River Thames and is a Ferris wheel that is 443 feet (135 metres) tall."},{"q":"In the UK, many people continue to visit the countryside for holidays and for leisure activities such as walking, camping and fishing:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"True. Many people continue to visit the countryside for holidays and for leisure activities such as walking, camping and fishing."},{"q":"How often does the Edinburgh Festival take place?","o":["Every six months","Every summer","Every spring","Every month"],"a":[1],"e":"The Edinburgh Festival takes place in Edinburgh, Scotland, every summer."},{"q":"What name is given to the day that marks the end of the Ramadan, when Muslims have fasted for a month?","o":["Vaisakhi","Eid a-Fitr","Eid ul Adha","Hanukkah"],"a":[1],"e":"Eid a-Fitr celebrates the end of Ramadan, when Muslims have fasted for a month."},{"q":"Who is the fastest person to have sailed around the world single-handed?","o":["Dame Kelly Holmes","Dame Ellen MacArthur","Jayne Torvill","Bradley Wiggins"],"a":[1],"e":"Dame Ellen MacArthur is a yachtswoman and in 2004 became the fastest person to sail around the world single-handed."},{"q":"Where does the most famous sailing event in the UK take place?","o":["Holyhead, in North Wales","Cowes, in the Isle of Wight","Douglas, in the Isle of Man","Plymouth, in the South West of England"],"a":[1],"e":"The most famous sailing event in the UK takes place at Cowes on the Isle of Wight."},{"q":"What is Sir Jackie Stewart famous for?","o":["He captained the English football team that won the World Cup in 1966","He captained the English cricket team","He won the Formula 1 world championship three times","He won gold medals in rowing in five consecutive Olympic Games"],"a":[2],"e":"Sir Jackie Stewart (1939-) is a Scottish former racing driver who won the Formula 1 world championship three times."},{"q":"How old do you need to be to be able to go into betting shops or gambling clubs?","o":["16 years old","18 years old","20 years old","21 years old"],"a":[1],"e":"You have to be 18 to go into betting shops or gambling clubs."},{"q":"How long do Muslims fast during Ramadan?","o":["A week","A month","Two weeks","Three weeks"],"a":[1],"e":"Eid a-Fitr celebrates the end of Ramadan, when Muslims have fasted for a month."},{"q":"Which British actor has recently won an Oscar?","o":["Daniel Day Lewis","Colin Firth","Jude Law","Charles Chaplin"],"a":[1],"e":"Recent British actors to have won Oscars include Colin Firth, Sir Antony Hopkins, Dame Judi Dench, Kate Winslet and Tilda Swinton."},{"q":"When is St Andrews Day, patron of the Scotland, celebrated?","o":["23rd of April","1st of March","17th of March","30th of November"],"a":[3],"e":"﻿St Andrews Day, patron of the Scotland, is celebrated on the 30th of November."},{"q":"Which organisation has been organising ‘The Proms’ since 1927?","o":["The MTV","Channel 4","The BBC","CNTV"],"a":[2],"e":"The Proms is an eight-week summer season of orchestral classical music that takes place in various venues, including the Royal Albert Hall in London. It has been in various venues, including the Royal Albert Hall in London. It has been organised by the British Broadcasting Corporation (BBC) since 1927."},{"q":"Where is Snowdonia National Park located?","o":["North Wales","Northern Ireland","South Wales","England"],"a":[0],"e":"Snowdonia is a national park in North Wales."},{"q":"Northern Ireland and Wales have their own banknotes, which are valid everywhere in the UK. However, shop and businesses do not have to accept them:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. Northern Ireland and Scotland have their own banknotes, which are valid everywhere in the UK. However, shops and businesses do not have to accept them."},{"q":"How many Formula 1 Grand Prix events are held in the UK every year?","o":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"a":[0],"e":"A Formula 1 Grand Prix event is held in the UK each year and a number of British Grand Prix drivers have won the Formula 1 World Championship."},{"q":"Which TWO British areas have their own banknotes?","o":["Northern Ireland","Scotland","Wales","The Isle of Man"],"a":[0,1],"e":"Northern Ireland and Scotland have their own banknotes, which are valid everywhere in the UK. However, shops and businesses do not have to accept them."},{"q":"How many weeks does the orchestral classical music event known as ‘the Proms’ last for?","o":["Three weeks","Four weeks","Six weeks","Eight weeks"],"a":[3],"e":"The Proms is an eight-week summer season of orchestral classical music that takes place in various venues, including the Royal Albert Hall in London."},{"q":"What is the name of the land formation consisting of columns made of volcanic lava located on the north-east coast of Northern Ireland?","o":["Eden Project","The Giant’s Causeway","Loch Lomond","Snowdonia"],"a":[1],"e":"Located on the north-east coast of Northern Ireland, the Giant’s Causeway is a land formation of columns made from volcanic lava."},{"q":"What is the capital city of Wales?","o":["Swansea","Cardiff","Cornwall","Bangor"],"a":[1],"e":"The capital city of Wales is Cardiff."},{"q":"When were the Houses of the Parliament built?","o":["In the 17th century","In the 18th century","In the 19th century","In the 20th century"],"a":[2],"e":"The Houses of Parliament and St Pancras Station were built in the 19th century, as were the town halls in cities such as Manchester and Sheffield."}]},{"id":"test-4-9","name":"Test 4.9","group":"Chapter 4","questions":[{"q":"How many times have the Olympic Games been hosted in the UK?","o":["Two","Three","Four","Six"],"a":[1],"e":"The UK has hosted the Olympic games on three occasions: 1908, 1948 and 2012."},{"q":"When were films first shown publicly in the UK?","o":["In 1887","In 1896","In 1897","In 1886"],"a":[1],"e":"﻿Films were first shown publicly in the UK in 1896 and film screenings very quickly became popular. From the beginning, British film makers became famous for clever special effects and this continues to be an area of British expertise."},{"q":"How long does Diwali last for?","o":["A weekend","40 days","Five days","A week"],"a":[2],"e":"﻿Diwali normally falls in October or November and lasts for five days."},{"q":"What was the population of the UK in 2005?","o":["Just under 50 million","Just under 60 million","Just over 50 million","Just under 62 million"],"a":[1],"e":"The population of the UK in 2005 was just under 60 million."},{"q":"What game do usually people play at Halloween?","o":["Chess","Trick or treat","Hide and seek","Netball"],"a":[1],"e":"During Halloween young people will often dress up in frightening costumes to play ‘trick or treat’."},{"q":"What is the most well-known gallery in Wales?","o":["Tate Britain","The National Museum","The National Gallery","Tate Modern"],"a":[1],"e":"In the UK, some of the most well-known galleries are The National Gallery, Tate Britain and Tate Modern in London, the National Museum in Cardiff, and the National Gallery of Scotland in Edinburgh."},{"q":"The Man Booker Prize for Fiction is awarded annually for the best fiction novel written by an author from:","o":["The Commonwealth, Ireland or Zimbabwe","The European Union, Zambia or Zimbabwe","The Commonwealth, the USA or Australia","The European Union, Canada or Australia"],"a":[0],"e":"The Man Booker Prize for Fiction is awarded annually for the best fiction novel written by an author from the Commonwealth, Ireland or Zimbabwe. It has been awarded since 1968."},{"q":"Which of the following statements regarding Gaelic language is TRUE?","o":["In Wales, Gaelic is spoken in some parts of the coast","In Scotland, Gaelic is spoken everywhere","In Scotland, Gaelic is spoken in some parts of the Highlands and Islands","In Wales, Gaelic is the main language"],"a":[2],"e":"In Scotland, Gaelic (again, a different language) is spoken in some parts of the Highlands and Islands, and in Northern Ireland some people speak Irish Gaelic."},{"q":"Where was the main Olympic site for the 2012 Games?","o":["Stratford","Bristol","Wimbledon","Watford"],"a":[0],"e":"The main Olympic site for the 2012 Games was in Stratford, East London."},{"q":"Who can get a 50% discount on their TV licences?","o":["Blind people","People over 65","People over 75","People on benefits"],"a":[0],"e":"People over 75 can apply for a free TV licence and blind people can get a 50% discount."},{"q":"Which British city is famous for its celebration of Diwali?","o":["London","Newcastle upon Tyne","Leicester","Manchester"],"a":[2],"e":"There is a famous celebration of Diwali in Leicester."},{"q":"Which of the following British actresses has NOT won an Oscar?","o":["Tilda Swinton","Dame Judi Dench","Emily Watson","Kate Winslet"],"a":[2],"e":"Recent British actors to have won Oscars include Colin Firth, Sir Antony Hopkins, Dame Judi Dench, Kate Winslet and Tilda Swinton. Emily Watson has not won an oscar."},{"q":"Which of the following sports has a long association with royalty?","o":["Rugby","Horse racing","Formula 1","Sailing"],"a":[1],"e":"Horse racing has a long association with royalty."},{"q":"Hanukkah is a Jewish festival created to remember the Jews’ struggle for religious freedom.","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"True. Hanukkah is in November or December and is celebrated for eight days. It is to remember the Jews’ struggle for religious freedom."},{"q":"What is the capital city of the UK?","o":["Manchester","Liverpool","London","Cambridge"],"a":[2],"e":"The capital city of the UK is London."},{"q":"What is Hogmanay?","o":["The equivalent of Christmas Day in Scotland","The equivalent of New Year’s Eve in Scotland","The equivalent of Christmas Day in Wales","The equivalent of New Year’s Eve in Wales"],"a":[1],"e":"In Scotland, 31 December is called Hogmanay."},{"q":"On Remembrance Day, there is a two-minute silence at 11 AM to commemorate those who died fighting during the First World War?","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"True. On Remembrance Day at 11.00 am there is a two-minute silence and wreaths are laid at the Cenotaph in Whitehall, London."},{"q":"What is the distance between the north coast of Scotland and the south-west corner of England?","o":["470 miles","490 miles","840 miles","870 miles"],"a":[3],"e":"In the UK, the longest distance on the mainland is from John O’Groats on the north coast of Scotland to Land’s End in the south-west corner of England. It is about 870 miles (approximately 1,400 kilometres)."},{"q":"How many volunteers work for the National Trust?","o":["More than 71,000","More than 81,000","More than 61,000","More than 91,000"],"a":[2],"e":"There are now more than 61,000 volunteers helping to keep the National Trust running."},{"q":"What was the population of the UK in 1851?","o":["20 million","40 million","30 million","50 million"],"a":[0],"e":"The population of the UK in 1851 was 20 million people."},{"q":"Which British playwright had a great influence on the English language and invented many words that are still common today?","o":["William Shakespeare","Graham Greene","Dylan Thomas","Harold Pinter"],"a":[0],"e":"William Shakespeare had a great influence on the English language and invented many words that are still common today."},{"q":"What do Lewis Hamilton, Jenson Button and Damon Hill have in common?","o":["They are football players","They have won the Formula 1 World Championship","They won gold Olympic medals","They have won the Six Nations Championship"],"a":[1],"e":"Lewis Hamilton, Jenson Button and Damon Hill have won the Formula 1 World Championship."},{"q":"According to the 2011 census, what percentage of people said that they had no religion?","o":["10%","11%","20%","25%"],"a":[3],"e":"In the 2011 census, 25% of people said they had no religion."},{"q":"What other name is given to Diwali, the religious celebration that celebrates the victory of good over evil and the gaining of knowledge?","o":["The Festival of Lights","Hanukkah","The Festival of the Lanterns","The Festival of the Candles"],"a":[0],"e":"Diwali normally falls in October or November and lasts for five days. It is often called the Festival of Lights."}]},{"id":"test-4-10","name":"Test 4.10","group":"Chapter 4","questions":[{"q":"What is the name of the highest mountain in Wales?","o":["Lake District","Snowdon","South Downs","Giant’s Causeway"],"a":[1],"e":"Snowdon is the highest mountain in Wales."},{"q":"How many pence are there in a pound?","o":["100 pence","50 pence","60 pence","10 pence"],"a":[0],"e":"There are 100 pence in a pound."},{"q":"When did rugby originate?","o":["In the 15th century","In the 16th century","In the 18th century","In the 19th century"],"a":[3],"e":"Rugby originated in England in the early 19th century and is very popular in the UK today."},{"q":"How many volunteers did the National Trust have when it was first formed in 1895?","o":["Two","Three","200","300"],"a":[1],"e":"The National Trust was founded in 1895 by three volunteers."},{"q":"When did modern tennis evolve in England?","o":["In the early 18th century","In the late 18th century","In the early 19th century","In the late 19th century"],"a":[3],"e":"Modern tennis evolved in England in the late 19th century."},{"q":"The population is very equally distributed over the four parts of the UK:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. The population is very unequally distributed over the four parts of the UK. England more or less consistently makes up 84% of the total population, Wales around 5%, Scotland just over 8%, and Northern Ireland less than 3%."},{"q":"The UK population is ethnically diverse and changing rapidly, especially in large cities such as London:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"True. The UK population is ethnically diverse and changing rapidly, especially in large cities such as London."},{"q":"What name is given to the celebration when people in Great Britain set off fireworks at home or in special displays on the 5th of November?","o":["Bonfire night","Fireworks night","Bright night","The candles night"],"a":[0],"e":"Bonfire Night, 5 November, is an occasion when people in Great Britain set off fireworks at home or in special displays."},{"q":"How many National Parks are there in England, Wales and Scotland?","o":["10","12","15","25"],"a":[2],"e":"There are 15 national parks in England, Wales and Scotland. They are areas of protected countryside that everyone can visit, and where people live, work and look after the landscape."},{"q":"What services can be obtained from veterinary surgeons?","o":["They are places where pets can be left when you go on holidays","They are places where vaccinations and medical treatment for animals is available","They are places specifically prepare to groom pets","Amusement places for pets"],"a":[1],"e":"Vaccinations and medical treatment for animals are available from veterinary surgeons (vets)."},{"q":"What is the height of the London Eye?","o":["125 metres","135 metres","140 metres","152 metres"],"a":[1],"e":"The London Eye is situated on the southern bank of the River Thames and is a Ferris wheel that is 443 feet (135 metres) tall."},{"q":"Which of the following British poets was inspired by nature?","o":["Geoffrey Chaucer","William Blake","Sir Walter Scott","William Wordsworth"],"a":[3],"e":"William Wordsworth was inspired by nature."},{"q":"What percentage of the total British population is located in Northern Ireland?","o":["30%","Less than 5%","8%","Less than 3%"],"a":[3],"e":"The population is very unequally distributed over the four parts of the UK. England more or less consistently makes up 84% of the total population, Wales around 5%, Scotland just over 8%, and Northern Ireland less than 3%."},{"q":"Bonfire Night remembers the day when a group of Catholics led by Guy Fawkes killed the Protestant king with a bomb in the Houses of Parliament:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. Bonfire Night remembers the day when a group of Catholics led by Guy Fawkes failed in their plan to kill the Protestant king with a bomb in the Houses of Parliament."},{"q":"Where is the Scottish Grand National horse racing event celebrated?","o":["St Andrews","Ayr","Fife","Glasgow"],"a":[1],"e":"The Scottish Grand National horse racing event is celebrated at Ayr."},{"q":"How long does the Hanukkah celebration last for?","o":["Eight days","40 days","Five days","A fortnight"],"a":[0],"e":"Hanukkah is in November or December and is celebrated for eight days."},{"q":"What percentage of the total British population is located in Wales?","o":["30%","5%","8%","3%"],"a":[1],"e":"The total British population located in Wales is around 5%."},{"q":"Which of the following movies played an important role in boosting morale during the Second World War?","o":["The Ladykillers","Carry on","Passport to Pimlico","In Which We Serve"],"a":[3],"e":"During the Second World War, British movies (for example, In Which We Serve ) played an important part in boosting morale."},{"q":"Who won the 2012 Olympic gold medal in the heptathlon?","o":["Ellie Simmonds","Jessica Ennis","Dame Kelly Holmes","Jayne Torvill"],"a":[1],"e":"Jessica Ennis (1986-) won the 2012 Olympic gold medal in the heptathlon, which includes seven different track and field events. She also holds a number of British athletics records."},{"q":"What is the name of the annual cultural Welsh festival which includes music, dance, art and original performances largely in Welsh?","o":["The Arts Festival of Wales","The National Eisteddfod of Wales","The Music Festival of Wales","The National Performance Festival of Wales"],"a":[1],"e":"The National Eisteddfod of Wales is an annual cultural festival which includes music, dance, art and original performances largely in Welsh. It includes a number of important competitions for Welsh poetry."},{"q":"What is the most well-known rugby league (club) competition?","o":["The Grand National","The All England championship","The Six Nations championship","The Super League"],"a":[3],"e":"The Super League is the most well-known rugby league (club) competition."},{"q":"Where do the Laurence Olivier Awards take place?","o":["In London","In Manchester","In Liverpool","In Edinburgh"],"a":[0],"e":"The Laurence Olivier Awards take place annually at different venues in London."},{"q":"Which of the following is a traditional character of the pantomimes?","o":["The gentleman","The Dame","A unicorn","A mermaid"],"a":[1],"e":"One of the traditional characters of the pantomimes is the Dame, a woman played by a man."},{"q":"In the UK, most people live in towns and cities and very small parts of Britain are countryside:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. Most people live in towns and cities but much of Britain is still countryside."}]},{"id":"test-4-11","name":"Test 4.11","group":"Chapter 4","questions":[{"q":"What percentage of the total British population is located in Scotland?","o":["Over 30%","Over 5%","Over 8%","Over 3%"],"a":[2],"e":"The population is very unequally distributed over the four parts of the UK. England more or less consistently makes up 84% of the total population, Wales around 5%, Scotland just over 8%, and Northern Ireland less than 3%."},{"q":"Which of the following academy awards is the equivalent of the Oscars in the UK?","o":["The Brit Awards","The BAFTA Awards","The Mercury Music Prize","The MTV Awards"],"a":[1],"e":"The annual British Academy Film Awards, hosted by the British Academy of Film and Television Arts (BAFTA), are the British equivalent of the Oscars."},{"q":"What is a bank holiday (choose TWO answers)?","o":["A festive pagan day","A private holiday","A day when banks are closed","A public holiday"],"a":[2,3],"e":"In the UK, there are public holidays each year called bank holidays, when banks and many other businesses are closed for the day."},{"q":"Which of the following countries does not take part in the Six Nations Championship?","o":["Wales","Italy","Germany","France"],"a":[2],"e":"The most famous rugby union competition is the Six Nations Championship between England, Ireland, Scotland, Wales, France and Italy."},{"q":"Who looks after Edinburgh Castle?","o":["Historic Environment Scotland","SEPA","The Scottish Parliament","The National Trust"],"a":[0],"e":"Edinburgh Castle is looked after by Historic Environment Scotland, a Scottish government agency."},{"q":"Who were the pioneers of the IVF (in-vitro fertilisation) therapy?","o":["Sir Frank Whittle and Sir Christopher Cockrell","Sir Robert Edwards and Patrick Steptoe","Francis Creek and John Logie Baird","Sir Ian Wilmot and Keith Campbell"],"a":[1],"e":"IVF (in-vitro fertilisation) therapy for the treatment of infertility was pioneered in Britain by physiologist Sir Robert Edwards (1925-) and gynaecologist Patrick Steptoe (1913-88). The world’s first ‘test-tube baby’ was born in Oldham, Lancashire in 1978."},{"q":"In the UK, bank holidays of no religious significance are celebrated at the beginning of May, in late May or early June, and in August:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"What TWO different types of rugby are practised in the UK?","o":["Union","League","Rugby open","Free style"],"a":[0,1],"e":"There are two different types of rugby, which have different rules: union and league."},{"q":"What do William Blake, John Keats and Lord Byron have in common?","o":["They were British poets of the 19th century","They were British painters of the Swinging Sixties","They were musicians of the middle ages","They were British scientist"],"a":[0],"e":"Poetry was very popular in the 19th century, with poets such as William Blake, John Keats, Lord Byron, Percy Shelley, Alfred Lord Tennyson, and Robert and Elizabeth Browning."},{"q":"The Laurence Olivier Awards take place annually at different venues in Liverpool:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. The Laurence Olivier Awards take place annually at different venues in London."},{"q":"An additional rented piece of land where people can grow fruit and vegetables is known as:","o":["Parcel","Garden","Terrain","Allotment"],"a":[3],"e":"In the UK, some people rent additional land called ‘an allotment’, where they grow fruit and vegetables."},{"q":"Which TWO pop music groups became popular in the 1960s?","o":["Queen","The Rolling Stones","The Beatles","The Carpenters"],"a":[1,2],"e":"Two well-known pop music groups in the 1960s were The Beatles and The Rolling Stones."},{"q":"Eid ul Adha is the day when Muslims thank Allah for giving them the strength to complete the fast:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. The day when Muslims thank Allah for giving them the strength to complete the fast is called Eid a-Fitr."},{"q":"Which of the following poets wrote poems inspired by Scotland and the traditional stories and songs from the area on the borders of Scotland and England?","o":["Geoffrey Chaucer","William Blake","Sir Walter Scott","William Wordsworth"],"a":[2],"e":"Sir Walter Scott wrote poems inspired by Scotland and the traditional stories and songs from the area on the borders of Scotland and England."},{"q":"Which golf ‘Major’ tournament is held outside the United States?","o":["The Masters Tournament","The U.S. Open","The Open Championship","The PGA Championship"],"a":[2],"e":"The open championship is the only ‘Major’ tournament held outside the United States."},{"q":"Which is the most famous rugby union competition?","o":["The Grand National","The Six Nations Championship","The All England Championship","The Super League"],"a":[1],"e":"The most famous rugby union competition is the Six Nations Championship between England, Ireland, Scotland, Wales, France and Italy."},{"q":"Which of the following is an ancient festival and has roots in the pagan festival to mark the beginning of winter?","o":["Lent","Easter","Halloween","Hogmanay"],"a":[2],"e":"Halloween, 31 October, is and ancient festival and has roots in the pagan festival to mark the beginning of winter."},{"q":"When is the anniversary of the Battle of the Boyne celebrated in Northern Ireland?","o":["March","May","June","July"],"a":[3],"e":"In Northern Ireland, the anniversary of the Battle of the Boyne is celebrated in July and is also a public holiday."},{"q":"Who is the head of the Church of England?","o":["The Pope","The monarch","The Archbishop of Canterbury","The Prime Minister"],"a":[1],"e":"The monarch is the head of the Church of England."},{"q":"In the UK, owners are responsible for keeping their dog under control and for cleaning up after the animal in public places:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"Which British writer wrote satirical novels including ‘Brideshead Revisited’?","o":["Evelyn Waugh","Sir Arthur Conan Doyle","Sir Kingsley Amis","Thomas Hardy"],"a":[0],"e":"Evelyn Waugh wrote satirical novels, including Decline and Fall and Scoop. He is perhaps best known for Brideshead Revisited."},{"q":"What was the population of the UK in 1851?","o":["20 million","40 million","30 million","50 million"],"a":[0],"e":"The population of the UK in 1851 was 20 million people."},{"q":"Which book written by JRR Tolkien was voted the country’s best-loved novel in 2003?","o":["Harry Potter","The Lord of the Rings","Sense and Sensibility","Pride and Prejudice"],"a":[1],"e":"In 2003, The Lord of the Rings by JRR Tolkien was voted the country’s best-loved novel."},{"q":"What is the note with the highest value in the UK?","o":["£20","£50","£100","£500"],"a":[1],"e":"The note with the highest value in the UK is the £50 note."}]},{"id":"test-4-12","name":"Test 4.12","group":"Chapter 4","questions":[{"q":"What day does Lent start?","o":["On Shrove Tuesday","On Ash Wednesday","On Easter Monday","On Easter Sunday"],"a":[1],"e":"Lent begins on Ash Wednesday."},{"q":"How old do you need to be to be able to apply for a free TV licence?","o":["Over 65","Over 60","Over 70","Over 75"],"a":[3],"e":"People over 75 can apply for a free TV licence and blind people can get a 50% discount."},{"q":"What time do usually pubs open on Sundays in the UK?","o":["10 am","11 am","12 pm","1 pm"],"a":[2],"e":"Pubs are usually open during the day from 11.00 am ( 12 noon on Sundays )."},{"q":"Which British sportswoman won two gold medals for running in the 2004 Olympic Games?","o":["Dame Ellen MacArthur","Jayne Torvill","Dame Kelly Holmes","Jessica Ennis-Hill"],"a":[2],"e":"Dame Kelly Holmes won two gold medals for running in the 2004 Olympic Games. She has held a number of British and European records."},{"q":"What percentage of the total British population is located in England alone?","o":["84%","60%","58%","79%"],"a":[0],"e":"The population is very unequally distributed over the four parts of the UK. England more or less consistently makes up 84% of the total population, Wales around 5%, Scotland just over 8%, and Northern Ireland less than 3%."},{"q":"What was the population of the UK in 2010?","o":["Just over 50 million","Just over 62 million","Just under 50 million","Just under 60 million"],"a":[1],"e":"The population of the UK in 2010 was just over 62 million people."},{"q":"Which flower is associated with England?","o":["Daffodil","Shamrock","Rose","Thistle"],"a":[2],"e":"The rose is the flower associated with England."},{"q":"In which period did British Film studios flourish?","o":["1920s","1930s","1940s","1950s"],"a":[1],"e":"British film studios flourished in the 1930s."},{"q":"According to the 2011 Census, what percentage of the population identified themselves as Sikh?","o":["0.1%","0.5%","0.8%","2%"],"a":[2],"e":"According to the 2011 Census, 0.8% of the population identified themselves as Sikh."},{"q":"Which flower is associated with Wales?","o":["Daffodil","Rose","Shamrock","Thistle"],"a":[0],"e":"The daffodil is the flower associated with Wales."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a city of the UK?","o":["Belfast","Newport","Dublin","Dundee"],"a":[2],"e":"Dublin is not a city of the UK."},{"q":"When is Bonfire Night celebrated?","o":["5th of September","5th of November","15th of September","15th of November"],"a":[1],"e":"Bonfire Night is celebrated on the 5th of November."},{"q":"Which of the following statements regarding television in Northern Ireland is TRUE?","o":["Some programmes are broadcasted in Irish Gaelic","There is one Irish-language channel","All programmes are broadcasted in English","There are no specific programmes to Northern Ireland"],"a":[0],"e":"There are programmes specific to Northern Ireland and some programmes broadcast in Irish Gaelic."},{"q":"In the second half of the 19th century there was an important group of artists who painted detailed pictures on religious or literary themes in bright colours. These were known as:","o":["The Pre-Raphaelites","Suffragettes","Chartists","Elizabethans"],"a":[0],"e":"The Pre-Raphaelites were an important group of artists in the second half of the 19th century. They painted detailed pictures on religious or literary themes in bright colours. The group included Holman Hunt, Dante Gabriel Rossetti and Sir John Millais."},{"q":"The Muslim festival known as Eid ul Adha reminds Muslims of their own commitment to God:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"True. Eid ul Adha reminds Muslims of their own commitment to God."},{"q":"What was the population of the UK in 1901?","o":["20 million","40 million","30 million","50 million"],"a":[1],"e":"The population of the UK in 1901 was 40 million people."},{"q":"When is Boxing Day celebrated?","o":["26th December","25th December","30th December","24th December"],"a":[0],"e":"Boxing Day is the day after Christmas Day and is celebrated on the 26th December."},{"q":"Which charity works for the preservation of buildings in England?","o":["Friends of the Earth","Oxfam","The National Trust","The RSPB"],"a":[2],"e":"In the UK, many parts of the countryside and places of interest are kept open by the National Trust in England, Wales and Northern Ireland and the National Trust for Scotland. Both are charities that work to preserve important buildings, coastline and countryside in the UK."},{"q":"Which of the following is a traditional Welsh food?","o":["Roast beef","Welsh cakes","Haggis","Ulster fry"],"a":[1],"e":"Welsh cakes are a traditional Welsh snack made from flour, dried fruits and spices, and served either hot or cold."},{"q":"When is the Jewish celebration known as Hanukkah normally celebrated?","o":["June or July","October or November","November or December","March or April"],"a":[2],"e":"Hannukah is in November or December and is celebrated for eight days."},{"q":"What musical award is given during the Mercury Music Prize?","o":["Best album from the UK","Best album from the UK and Ireland","Best solo artist from the UK","Best solo artist the UK and Ireland"],"a":[1],"e":"The Mercury Music Prize is awarded each September for the best album from the UK and Ireland."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following water sports are popular in the UK?","o":["Waterpolo","Rowing","Sailing","Surfing"],"a":[1,2],"e":"Sailing continues to be popular in the UK, reflecting our maritime heritage. Rowing is also popular, both as a leisure activity and as a competitive sport."},{"q":"In the UK, what should dogs wear in public places?","o":["A collar with the name and address of the owner","A collar with the name of the dog","A tracking device in case they get lost","Clothes to avoid the cold in the winter"],"a":[0],"e":"All dogs in public places must wear a collar showing the name and address of the owner."},{"q":"According to the Citizenship Survey carried out in the UK in 2009, what percentage of the population identified themselves as Muslim?","o":["1%","2%","3%","4%"],"a":[3],"e":"In the 2009 Citizenship Survey, 70% of people identified themselves as Christian. Much smaller proportions identified themselves as Muslim (4%), Hindu (2%), Sikh (1%), Jewish or Buddhist (both less than 0.5%), and 2% of people followed another religion."}]},{"id":"test-5-1","name":"Test 5.1","group":"Chapter 5","questions":[{"q":"Members of the European Parliament (MEPs) are elected on the basis of:","o":["Personal achievements","Instant run-off","First past the post system (the candidate who gets the most votes)","Proportional representation"],"a":[3],"e":"Elections to the European Parliament use a system of proportional representation, where seats are allocated to each party in proportion to the total number of votes it has won."},{"q":"The North Atlantic Treaty Organisation is a group of European and North American countries that was created with which TWO purposes:","o":["Protect and promote human rights in those countries","Help each other if they come under attack","Maintain peace between all its members","Promote free trade amongst those countries"],"a":[1,2],"e":"NATO is a group of European and North American countries that have agreed to help each other if they come under attack. It also aims to maintain peace between all of its members."},{"q":"The small claims procedure is used in England and Wales for minor disputes for:","o":["Claims of less than £3,000","Claims of less than £4,000","Claims of less than £3,500","Claims of less than £10,000"],"a":[3],"e":"This procedure is used for claims of less than £10,000 in England and Wales, £5,000 using the simple claims procedure in Scotland, and £5,000 through the small claims process in Northern Ireland."},{"q":"Which of the following is the responsibility of the Chancellor of the Exchequer?","o":["Education","Agriculture","Economy","Defence"],"a":[2],"e":"Chancellor of the Exchequer is responsible for the economy."},{"q":"Which of the following statements is true?","o":["Proceedings in Parliament are broadcast on television and published in official reports","Proceedings in Parliament are not broadcast on television but published in official reports","Proceedings in Parliament are broadcast on the radio and not published in official reports","Proceedings in Parliament are broadcast on the radio only"],"a":[0],"e":"Proceedings in Parliament are broadcast on television and published in official reports called Hansard."},{"q":"The public can listen to debates in the Palace of Westminster from public galleries in the House of Commons but not in the House of Lords:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. The public can listen to debates in the Palace of Westminster from public galleries in both the House of Commons and the House of Lords."},{"q":"Which of the following organisations has no power to make laws but draws up conventions and charters, including the European Convention on Human Rights and Fundamental Freedoms?","o":["The UN Security Council","The Council of Europe","The United Nations","The European Union"],"a":[1],"e":"The Council of Europe is responsible for the protection and promotion of human rights in those countries. It has no power to make laws but draws up conventions and charters, the most well-known of which is the European Convention on Human Rights and Fundamental Freedoms, usually called the European Convention on Human Rights."},{"q":"Which Court deals with the most serious criminal cases, such as murder, in Scotland?","o":["The High Court","The Sheriff Court","The Court of Session","The Crown Court"],"a":[0],"e":"The most serious criminal cases in Scotland, such as murder, are heard at a High Court with a judge and jury."},{"q":"The leader of the opposition leads his or her party in pointing out what they see as the government’s (choose TWO options):","o":["Failures","Achievements","Strengths","Weaknesses"],"a":[0,3],"e":"The leader of the opposition leads his or her party in pointing out what they see as the government’s failures and weaknesses."},{"q":"What is the minimum age requirement to drive a mopped in the UK?","o":["16","18","20","21"],"a":[0],"e":"You need to be at least 16 years old to ride a moped, and there are other age requirements and special tests for driving large vehicles."},{"q":"How many countries formed the European Economic Community (EEC) when it was first created in 1957?","o":["Five","Six","Seven","Nine"],"a":[1],"e":"The European Union (EU), originally called the European Economic Community (EEC), was set up by six western European countries (Belgium, France, Germany, Italy, Luxembourg and the Netherlands) who signed the Treaty of Rome on 25 March 1957."},{"q":"What is donated blood used for?","o":["To help people with a wide range of injuries and illnesses","Blood cannot be donated in the UK","To carry out research with your DNA","To be stored in the hospital in case you need it in the future"],"a":[0],"e":"Donated blood is used by hospitals to help people with a wide range of injuries and illnesses."},{"q":"In which city is the Northern Ireland Assembly building, also known as Stormont, located?","o":["London","Belfast","Cardiff","Dublin"],"a":[1],"e":"In Northern Ireland elected members, known as MLAs, meet in the Northern Ireland Assembly at Stormont, in Belfast."},{"q":"What can living people donate that may help other people with injuries or illnesses (choose TWO options)?","o":["Blood","Saliva","A kidney","Their liver"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Living people can donate blood and a kidney."},{"q":"Where is the office of the MPs located?","o":["10 Downing Street","The House of Commons","The House of Lords","Buckingham Palace"],"a":[1],"e":"The MP’s office is located at The House of Commons, Westminster, London, SW1A OAA."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following statements is TRUE with regards to Magistrates and Justices of the Peace (JPs) in England, Wales and Scotland?","o":["They usually work unpaid","They do not need legal qualifications","They are legally qualified","They are paid"],"a":[0,1],"e":"In England, Wales and Scotland Magistrates and Justices of the Peace (JPs) usually work unpaid and do not need legal qualifications."},{"q":"The House of Commons is normally more independent of the government than the House of Lords:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. The House of Lords is normally more independent of the government than the House of Commons."},{"q":"In Northern Ireland members of the Legislative Assembly (MLAs) are elected on the basis of:","o":["Proportional representation","Personal achievements","First past the post system","Instant run-off"],"a":[0],"e":"﻿The Northern Ireland Assembly has 90 elected members, known as MLAs (members of the Legislative Assembly). They are elected with a form of proportional representation."},{"q":"How can MPs be contacted (choose TWO options)?","o":["By letter","By going to your local council and asking for an appointment","By phoning their constituency office","Through facebook"],"a":[0,2],"e":"MPs can be contacted by letter or telephone at their constituency office, or at their office in the House of Commons. In addition, many MPs, SMs and MSPs hold regular local ‘surgeries’, where constituents can go in person to talk about issues that are of concern to them."},{"q":"Does Britain have a written constitution?","o":["Yes","No"],"a":[1],"e":"The British constitution is not written down in any single document, and therefore it is described as ‘unwritten’. This is mainly because the UK, unlike America or France, has never had a revolution which led permanently to a totally new system of government."},{"q":"When were the United Nations set up?","o":["Before the Second World War","After the Second World War","Before the First World War","Before the War of the Roses"],"a":[1],"e":"The UN was set up after the Second World War and aims to prevent war and promote international peace and security."},{"q":"Where can you find copies of the ‘Hansard’?","o":["In the Guardian newspaper","In large libraries and at www.parliament.uk","In the bookshop","In any library"],"a":[1],"e":"Proceedings in Parliament are broadcast on television and published in official reports called Hansard. Written reports can be found in large libraries and at www.parliament.uk."},{"q":"Which court normally deals with cases of children aged 10 to 17 in England, Wales and Northern Ireland?","o":["The High Court","The Youth Court","The Magistrates’ Court","The Sheriff Court"],"a":[1],"e":"In England, Wales and Northern Ireland, if an accused person is aged 10 to 17, the case is normally heard in a Youth Court in front of up to three specially trained magistrates or a District Judge. The most serious cases will go the Crown Court."},{"q":"How many senior MPs are appointed by the Prime Minister?","o":["Almost 30","15","About 20","25"],"a":[2],"e":"The Prime Minister appoints about 20 senior MPs to become ministers in charge of departments."}]},{"id":"test-5-2","name":"Test 5.2","group":"Chapter 5","questions":[{"q":"Where is the official home of the Prime Minister?","o":["Buckingham Palace","Windsor Castle","10 Downing Street","21 Oxford Street"],"a":[2],"e":"The official home of the Prime Minister is 10 Downing Street, in central London, near the Houses of Parliament. He or she also has a country house outside London called Chequers."},{"q":"What name is given to the elected members of the European Parliament?","o":["AMs","MEPs","MPs","MSPs"],"a":[1],"e":"Elections for the European Parliament are held every five years. Elected members are called members of the European Parliament ( MEPs )."},{"q":"Which court deals with cases involving personal injury, family matters, breaches of contract and divorce in England and Wales?","o":["The Crown Court","The High Court","County Courts","The Sheriff Court"],"a":[2],"e":"County Courts deal with a wide range of civil disputes. These include people trying to get back money that is owed to them, cases involving personal injury, family matters, breaches of contract, and divorce. In Scotland, most of these matters are dealt with in the Sheriff Court."},{"q":"What is the name of the building where the Scottish Parliament meet?","o":["Holyrood","Senedd","Westminster","Stormont"],"a":[0],"e":"In Scotland the elected members, called MSPs, meet in the Scottish Parliament building at Holyrood in Edinburgh."},{"q":"Which court deals with minor criminal cases in England, Wales and Northern Ireland?","o":["The Justice of the Peace Court","The Magistrates’ Court","The High Court","The Crown Court"],"a":[1],"e":"In England, Wales and Northern Ireland, most minor criminal cases are dealt with in a Magistrates’ Court. In Scotland, minor criminal offences go to a Justice of the Peace Court."},{"q":"On which issues can the Northern Ireland Assembly NOT make decisions?","o":["Education","Defence","Agriculture","Health"],"a":[1],"e":"The Northern Ireland Assembly can make decisions on issues such as education, agriculture, the environment, health and social services. It cannot make decisions on defence."},{"q":"What is the first verse of the National Anthem of the UK ‘God save the King’?","o":["Long to reign over us","God save the King!","Long live our noble King!","God save our gracious King!"],"a":[3],"e":"The first verse of the National Anthem of the UK is ‘God save our gracious King!."},{"q":"Who forms the shadow cabinet?","o":["Senior opposition MPs appointed by the leader of the opposition","Junior opposition MPs appointed by the leader of the governing party","Junior opposition MPs appointed by the leader of the opposition","Local councillors"],"a":[0],"e":"The leader of the opposition appoints senior opposition MPs to be ‘shadow ministers’. They form the shadow cabinet and their role is to challenge the government and put forward alternative policies."},{"q":"People in the UK have to pay tax on any earnings from (choose THREE options)?","o":["Profits in self-employment","Income from property, savings and dividends","Valuable gifts","Taxable benefits"],"a":[0,1,3],"e":"People in the UK have to pay tax on their income, which includes: wages from paid employment, profits from self-employment, taxable benefits, pensions and income from property, savings and dividends."},{"q":"How many devolved administrations are there in the UK?","o":["2","3","4","5"],"a":[1],"e":"There are three devolved administrations in the UK, this include: the Welsh government, the Scottish Parliament and the Northern Ireland Assembly."},{"q":"Who is responsible for interpreting the law and ensuring that trials are conducted fairly?","o":["The government","The police officers","The judiciary","The Prime Minister and the MPs"],"a":[2],"e":"Judges (who are together called ‘the judiciary’ ) are responsible for interpreting the law and ensuring that trials are conducted fairly."},{"q":"How often do people who are 70 years old or over need to renew their driving licence?","o":["Every year","Every 2 years","Every 3 years","Every 5 years"],"a":[2],"e":"Drivers can use their driving licence until they are 70 years old. After that, the licence is valid for three years at a time."},{"q":"Where are the Welsh government and the Senedd based?","o":["In Cardiff","In Swansea","In Conwy","In Llandudno"],"a":[0],"e":"The Welsh government and the Senedd are based in Cardiff, the capital city of Wales."},{"q":"Which of the following is not a member of the European Union?","o":["Finland","Denmark","Sweden","Norway"],"a":[3],"e":"Norway is not a member of the European Union."},{"q":"In the UK, alcohol cannot be sold to anyone under the age of:","o":["16","18","20","21"],"a":[1],"e":"It is a criminal offence to sell alcohol to anyone who is under 18 or to buy alcohol for people who are under the age of 18. (There is one exception: people aged 16 or over can drink alcohol with a meal in a hotel or restaurant."},{"q":"To whom do new citizens have to swear or affirm loyalty as part of the citizenship ceremony?","o":["The Pope","The Prime Minister","The King","The Church of England"],"a":[2],"e":"New citizens swear or affirm loyalty to the King as part of the citizenship ceremony."},{"q":"How can you visit the UK Parliament (choose TWO options)?","o":["Arranging a tour through the visitor services","Writing to your local MP to ask for tickets","Queueing on the day at the public entrance","Contacting an MSP"],"a":[1,2],"e":"To visit the UK Parliament, you can write to your local MP in advance to ask for tickets or you can Queue on the day at the public entrance."},{"q":"Forced marriage is where one or both parties do not or cannot give their consent to enter into the partnership:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"True. Forced marriage is where one or both parties do not or cannot give their consent to enter into the partnership."},{"q":"Which of the following drugs is illegal in the UK?","o":["Cannabis","Cocaine","Ecstasy","All of the above are illegal"],"a":[3],"e":"Drugs such as heroin, cocaine, ecstasy and cannabis are illegal in the UK."},{"q":"What is the name of the building where the Northern Ireland Assembly meet?","o":["Westminster","Holyrood","Senedd","Stormont"],"a":[3],"e":"In Northern Ireland elected members, known as MLAs, meet in the Northern Ireland Assembly at Stormont, in Belfast."},{"q":"Which of the following countries is NOT a member of the Commonwealth?","o":["Uganda","Ghana","Malawi","Zimbabwe"],"a":[3],"e":"Zimbabwe is not a member of the Commonwealth."},{"q":"Who makes the decisions on government policies in the UK?","o":["The monarch","Only the Prime Minister can make the decisions on government policies","The Prime Minister and the Cabinet","The monarch and the Prime Minister"],"a":[2],"e":"Decisions on government policies are made by the Prime Minister and cabinet."},{"q":"How often is the electoral register updated?","o":["Every year","Every six months","Every two years","Every three years"],"a":[0],"e":"The electoral register is updated every year in September or October."},{"q":"What do you need to be able to drive a car in the UK?","o":["A driving licence, a valid motor insurance, a MOT certificate and the road tax","A driving licence and a valid motor insurance only","A driving licence and a MOT certificate only","A driving licence only"],"a":[0],"e":"To drive a car in the UK you need a driving licence, a valid motor insurance, a MOT certificate (for cars over three years old) and the road tax."}]},{"id":"test-5-3","name":"Test 5.3","group":"Chapter 5","questions":[{"q":"How many members does the Scottish Parliament have?","o":["60","90","120","129"],"a":[3],"e":"There are 129 members of the Scottish Parliament (MSPs), elected by a form of proportional representation."},{"q":"What does the term ‘coalition’ refer to?","o":["If no political party wins a majority, up to three parties may join and govern together","The candidate who gets the most votes is elected","If no political party wins a majority, two parties may join and govern together","If an MP dies or resigns, there will be a fresh election"],"a":[2],"e":"The government is usually formed by the party that wins the majority of constituencies. If no party wins a majority, two parties may join together to form a coalition."},{"q":"How much do you have to pay to visit the Palace of Westminster?","o":["£20","£5","£15","The entrance to the Palace of Westminster is free"],"a":[3],"e":"The entrance to the Palace of Westminster is free."},{"q":"Civil servants cannot stand for public office:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"True. Most citizens of the UK, the Irish republic or the Commonwealth aged 18 or over can stand for public office. There are some exceptions, including: members of the armed forces, civil servants, people found guilty of certain criminal offences."},{"q":"What is the main function of the Council of Europe?","o":["Protect and promote human rights","Promote international trade relationships","Maintain international peace","Recommend action when there are international crises and threats to peace"],"a":[0],"e":"﻿The Council of Europe has 47 member countries, including the UK, and is responsible for the protection and promotion of human rights in those countries."},{"q":"In which of the following areas has the Welsh government the power to make laws (choose four)?","o":["Education and training","Military defence","Economic development","Housing","Immigration","Health and social services"],"a":[0,2,3,5],"e":"The Welsh government has the power to make laws in 20 areas, including: education and training, health and social services, economic development and housing."},{"q":"How many members comprise the UN Security Council?","o":["5","10","15","25"],"a":[2],"e":"There are 15 members on the UN Security Council, which recommends action when there are international crises and threats to peace."},{"q":"Members in the Senedd can speak English and Welsh, but all publications of the Senedd must be in English:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. Members in the Senedd can speak either in English or Welsh, and all of the Senedd\\'s publications are in both languages."},{"q":"How many members does the Northern Ireland Assembly have?","o":["129","128","118","90"],"a":[3],"e":"The Northern Ireland Assembly has 90 elected members, known as MLAs (Members of the Legislative Assembly). They are elected with a form of proportional representation."},{"q":"Which of the following civil laws include disputes between landlords and tenants over issues such as repairs and eviction?","o":["Consumer law","Housing law","Debt law","Living law"],"a":[1],"e":"Housing law includes disputes between landlords and tenants over issues suck as repairs and eviction."},{"q":"How can you visit the Scottish Parliament?","o":["Arranging a tour through the visitor services","Contacting the Education Service","Contacting an AM","Contacting an MP"],"a":[0],"e":"To visit the Scottish Parliament, you can get information, book tickets or arrange tours through visitor services. You can also write to them at the Scottish Parliament, Edinburgh, EH99 1SP, telephone 0131 348 5200 or email sp.booking@scottish.parliament."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a duty of the police?","o":["Protect life and property","Protect the environment","Prevent disturbances","Prevent and detect crimes"],"a":[1],"e":"The job of the police in the UK is to protect life and property, prevent disturbances (also known as keeping the peace) and prevent and detect crime."},{"q":"How often are the elections for the European Parliament held?","o":["Every year","Every two years","Every four years","Every five years"],"a":[3],"e":"Elections for the European Parliament are held every five years."},{"q":"What do new citizens have to swear or affirm as part of the citizenship ceremony?","o":["They will remain in the UK","Loyalty to the King","Loyalty to the Pope","They will go to church at least once a week"],"a":[1],"e":"New citizens swear or affirm loyalty to the King as part of the citizenship ceremony."},{"q":"In which TWO cases may a person who has been summoned to do jury service be exempted from doing it:","o":["If they have a criminal conviction","If they have children","If they provide a good reason to be excused, such as ill health","If they have to go to work"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Everyone who is summoned to do jury service must do it unless they are not eligible: for example, because they have a criminal conviction or if they provide a good reason to be excused, such as ill health."},{"q":"What is the role of the shadow cabinet?","o":["To keep order during political debates to make sure the rules are followed","To challenge the government and put forward alternative policies","To point out what they see as the government’s failures and weaknesses","To control important public appointments"],"a":[1],"e":"The role of the shadow cabinet is to challenge the government and put forward alternative policies."},{"q":"If you are self-employed:","o":["You need to pay your own tax","Tax is automatically taken from your earnings by a specialised agency","You don’t need to pay tax","You only needed to pay tax on 50% of your earnings"],"a":[0],"e":"If you are self-employed, you need to pay your own tax through a system called ‘self-assessment’, which includes completing a tax return."},{"q":"What name is given to the elected members of the Northern Ireland Assembly?","o":["MSPs","MLAs","MPs","AMs"],"a":[1],"e":"Members of the Northern Ireland Assembly are known as MLAs (members of the Legislative Assembly)."},{"q":"Which senior MP is responsible for issues relating to crime, policing and immigration?","o":["The Home Secretary","The Foreign Secretary","The Chancellor of the Exchequer","The Prime Minister"],"a":[0],"e":"The Home Secretary is responsible for crime, policing and immigration."},{"q":"How can a person be protected from being forced into a marriage?","o":["By giving them a place to stay away from their family","Court orders can be obtained to protect a person from being forced into a marriage","By speaking to the person’s parents","There are no laws in the UK to avoid this"],"a":[1],"e":"Court orders can be obtained to protect a person from being forced into a marriage, or to protect a person in a forced marriage."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT classified as a criminal offence in the UK?","o":["Smoking in public places","Selling or buying drugs","Carrying a weapon","Unfair dismissal or discrimination in the workplace"],"a":[3],"e":"Unfair dismissal or discrimination in the workplace is classified as a civil offence."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT considered to be a main part of the British government?","o":["The monarchy","The European Council","The Prime Minister","The local government"],"a":[1],"e":"The European Council is not part of the British government."},{"q":"Who appoints the members of the Cabinet?","o":["The King","The Prime Minister","The Prince of Wales","The Magistrates"],"a":[1],"e":"The Prime Minister (PM) is the leader of the political party in power. He or she appoints the members of the cabinet and has control over many important public appointments."},{"q":"Which court usually deals with the MOST serious civil cases in Scotland?","o":["The Court of Session in Edinburgh","The Crown Court","The High Court","The Magistrates’ Court"],"a":[0],"e":"More serious civil cases – for example, when a large amount of compensation is being claimed – are dealt with in the High Court of England, Wales and Northern Ireland. In Scotland, they are dealt with in the Court of Session in Edinburgh."}]},{"id":"test-5-4","name":"Test 5.4","group":"Chapter 5","questions":[{"q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Forcing another person to marry is a civil offence","Forcing another person to marry is a criminal offence","Forcing another person to marry is not punished in the UK","Forcing another person to marry is common in the UK"],"a":[1],"e":"Forcing another person to marry is a criminal offence."},{"q":"Where do UK residents need to register their car or motorcycle?","o":["At the Home Office","At the Ministry of Transport (MOT)","At the Driver and Vehicle Licensing Agency (DVLA)","At their Local Council"],"a":[2],"e":"If you are resident in the UK, your car or motor cycle must be registered at the Driver and Vehicle Licensing Agency (DVLA)."},{"q":"Which court deals with serious criminal offences in Wales?","o":["The Sheriff Court","The Crown Court","The High Court","The Magistrates’ Court"],"a":[1],"e":"In England, Wales and Northern Ireland, serious criminal offences are tried in front of a judge and a jury in a Crown Court. In Scotland, serious cases are heard in a Sheriff Court with either a sheriff or a sheriff with a jury."},{"q":"When were Protection Orders to protect a person from being forced into a marriage, or to protect a person in a forced marriage introduced for Scotland?","o":["2007","2008","2010","2011"],"a":[3],"e":"Forced Marriage Protection Orders were introduced in 2008 for England, Wales and Northern Ireland under the Forced Marriage (Civil Protection) Act 2007. Court orders can be obtained to protect a person from being forced into a marriage, or to protect a person in a forced marriage. Similar Protection Orders were introduced in Scotland in November 2011."},{"q":"Since when have hereditary peers lost the automatic right to attend the House of Lords?","o":["1985","1995","1989","1999"],"a":[3],"e":"﻿Since 1999, hereditary peers have lost the automatic right to attend the House of Lords."},{"q":"What name is given to the elected members of the Welsh government?","o":["MSPs","SMs","MLAs","MPs"],"a":[1],"e":"There are 60 members of the Senedd (SMs) and elections are held every four years using a form of proportional representation."},{"q":"How often do the ministers that form the Cabinet meet to make important decisions about government policy?","o":["Every day","Weekly","Monthly","Every six months"],"a":[1],"e":"The cabinet is a committee which usually meets weekly and makes important decisions about government policy."},{"q":"How many countries are members of the United Nations?","o":["More than 190 countries","More than 150 countries","More than 140 countries","More than 180 countries"],"a":[0],"e":"The UK is part of the United Nations (UN), an international organization with more than 190 countries as members."},{"q":"Which of the following statements is true?","o":["The police force is a public service that helps and protects everyone","The police force is a private service that helps and protects everyone","The police force is a private service in charge of rescuing people from dangerous situations","The police force is a public service that works to avoid road accidents"],"a":[0],"e":"The police force is a public service that helps and protects everyone, no matter what their background or where they live."},{"q":"What is the system by which the income tax is automatically taken from the employee’s income by their employer and paid directly to HM Revenue & Customs (HMRC)?","o":["Self-assessment","“Pay As You Earn” (PAYE)","Tax return","Pension tax"],"a":[1],"e":"For most people, the right amount of income tax is automatically taken from their income from employment by their employer and paid directly to HM Revenue & Customs (HMRC), the government department that collects taxes. This system is called “Pay As You Earn” (PAYE)."},{"q":"How many local authorities are there in London?","o":["22","25","33","43"],"a":[2],"e":"London has 33 local authorities, with the Greater London Authority and the Mayor of London coordinating policies across the capital."},{"q":"Who is the head of the state in the UK?","o":["The Prime Minister","King Charles III","Prince William of Wales","Prince Philip, the Duke of Edinburgh"],"a":[1],"e":"King Charles III is the head of state of the UK."},{"q":"Members of the Scottish Parliament (MSPs) are elected on the basis of:","o":["Proportional representation","Personal achievements","Instant run-off","First past the post system (the candidate who gets the most votes)"],"a":[0],"e":"Members of the Scottish Parliament (MSPs) are elected by a form of proportional representation."},{"q":"The action of handing out leaflets in the street or knocking on people’s doors to ask for their political support is known as:","o":["Shadowing","Canvassing","Marketing","Persuasion"],"a":[1],"e":"The action of handing out leaflets in the street or knocking on people’s doors to ask for their political support is known as canvassing."},{"q":"Which of the following is a charity that works with old people?","o":["Shelter","Age UK","Crisis","Cancer Research UK"],"a":[1],"e":"Age UK is a charity that works with old people."},{"q":"How many members does the Council of Europe have?","o":["15","47","190","5"],"a":[1],"e":"The Council of Europe is separate from the EU. It has 47 member countries, including the UK, and is responsible for the protection and promotion of human rights in those countries."},{"q":"In the UK, Members of the Parliament (MPs) are elected on the basis of:","o":["Personal achievements","Instant run-off","First past the post system (the candidate who gets the most votes)","Proportional representation"],"a":[2],"e":"MPs are elected through a system called ‘first past the post’. In constituency, the candidate who gets the most votes is elected."},{"q":"People in the UK do NOT have to pay tax on:","o":["Income from expensive gifts","Income from savings","Income from property","Pensions"],"a":[0],"e":"People in the UK have to pay tax on their income, which includes: wages from paid employment, profits from self-employment, taxable benefits, pensions and income from property, savings and dividends."},{"q":"What Treaty established the European Economic Union in 1957?","o":["The Treaty of Rome","The Treaty of Vienna","The Treaty of Brussels","The Act of Union"],"a":[0],"e":"The European Union (EU), originally called the European Economic Community (EEC), was set up by six western European countries (Belgium, France, Germany, Italy, Luxembourg and the Netherlands) who signed the Treaty of Rome on 25 March 1957."},{"q":"When did hereditary peers lose the automatic right to attend the House of Lords?","o":["1979","1989","1999","2001"],"a":[2],"e":"Since 1999, hereditary peers have lost the automatic right to attend the House of Lords. They now elect a few of their number to represent them in the House of Lords."},{"q":"When did the King start reining?","o":["In 2020","In 2021","In 2022","In 2023"],"a":[2],"e":"The King has reigned since his mother’s death in September 2022."},{"q":"Members of the public are not allowed in Youth Courts, and the name or photographs of the accused young person cannot be published in newspapers or used by the media:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"True. Members of the public are not allowed in Youth Courts, and the name or photographs of the accused young person cannot be published in newspapers or used by the media."},{"q":"How do we call a set of principles by which a country is governed and which includes all of the institutions that are responsible for running the country and how their power is kept in check?","o":["Revolution","Constitution","Monarchy","Democracy"],"a":[1],"e":"A constitution is a set of principles by which a country is governed. It includes all of the institutions that are responsible for running the country and how their power is kept in check."},{"q":"﻿What is the National Anthem of the UK?","o":["God save the King","God save the Queen","United kingdoms","God save the land"],"a":[0],"e":"The National Anthem of the UK is ‘God Save the king’."}]},{"id":"test-5-5","name":"Test 5.5","group":"Chapter 5","questions":[{"q":"The Commonwealth has no power over its members and it cannot suspend their membership:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. The Commonwealth has no power over its members, although it can suspend membership."},{"q":"Which TWO of the following are environmental charities?","o":["Crisis","The National Trust","Friends of the Earth","PDSA"],"a":[1,2],"e":"The National Trust and Friends of the Earth are environmental charities."},{"q":"How often are elections held for the Senedd?","o":["Every three years","Every four years","Every five years","Every six years"],"a":[1],"e":"The Senedd has 60 Senedd members (SMs) and elections are held every four years using a form of proportional representation."},{"q":"What is the name given to the person appointed by local authorities to be the ceremonial leader of a particular council?","o":["Council candidate","Mayor","Peer","Magistrate"],"a":[1],"e":"Many local authorities appoint a mayor, who is the ceremonial leader of the council. In some towns, a mayor is elected to be the effective leader of the administration."},{"q":"What is the meaning of the term ‘first past the post’?","o":["In each constituency, the candidate who gets the most votes is elected","If no political party wins a majority, two parties may join and govern together","If an MP dies or resigns, there will be a fresh election","The Speaker will be chosen by other MPs in a secret ballot"],"a":[0],"e":"MPs are elected through a system called ‘first past the post’. In each constituency, the candidate who gets the most votes is elected."},{"q":"Which court deals with the MOST serious criminal cases in Scotland, such as murder?","o":["The Magistrates’ Court","The Sheriff Court","The Crown Court","The High Court"],"a":[3],"e":"In Scotland, serious cases are heard in a Sheriff Court with either a sheriff or a sheriff with a jury. The most serious cases in Scotland, such as murder, are heard at a High Court with a judge and jury."},{"q":"Who is the ceremonial head of the Commonwealth?","o":["The British King","The Pope","The President of the United States","The Prime Minister of the UK"],"a":[0],"e":"The King is the ceremonial head of the Commonwealth."},{"q":"Who is the chief officer of the House of Commons?","o":["The Prime Minister","The Speaker","The Archbishop of Canterbury","The leader of the opposition"],"a":[1],"e":"The Speaker is the chief officer of the House of Commons."},{"q":"What characterises the British constitution?","o":["It was written by the Prime Minister","It was written by the King","It is not written down in any single document, and therefore it is described as ‘unwritten","It was written by Henry VIII"],"a":[2],"e":"The British constitution is not written down in any single document, and therefore it is described as ‘unwritten’."},{"q":"Who forms the judiciary?","o":["The judges together","The Magistrates together","The MPs together","The judges and Magistrates together"],"a":[0],"e":"Judges (who are together called ‘the judiciary’) are responsible for interpreting the law and ensuring that trials are conducted fairly."},{"q":"Which court deals with minor criminal offences in Scotland?","o":["The Justice of the Peace Court","The Magistrates’ Court","The Sheriff Court","The Crown Court"],"a":[0],"e":"In Scotland, minor criminal offences go to a Justice of the Peace Court."},{"q":"If an MP dies or resigns, there will be a fresh election, which is known as:","o":["Hansard","First past the post","By-election","Coalition"],"a":[2],"e":"If an MP dies or resigns, there will be a fresh election, called a by-election, in his or her constituency."},{"q":"A person who has not paid enough National Insurance contributions will not be able to receive certain contributory benefits including (choose TWO options):","o":["Jobseeker’s Allowance","A full state retirement pension","Housing benefit","Working tax credits"],"a":[0,1],"e":"Anyone who does not pay enough National Insurance Contributions will not be able to receive certain contributory benefits such as Jobseeker’s Allowance or a full state retirement pension."},{"q":"How are life peers appointed?","o":["By the monarch on the advice of the Prime Minister","By the Prime Minister on the advice of the King","By the shadow cabinet","By the MPs"],"a":[0],"e":"Life peers are appointed by the monarch on the advice of the Prime Minister."},{"q":"In England, Wales and Northern Ireland, if an accused person is aged 18, the case will be heard in a Youth Court:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. In England, Wales and Northern Ireland, if an accused person is aged 10 to 17, the case is normally heard in a Youth Court in front of up to three specially trained magistrates or a District Judge."},{"q":"The UN was set up after the First World War and aims to prevent war and promote international peace and security:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. The UN was set up after the Second World War and aims to prevent war and promote international peace and security."},{"q":"What is played at important national occasions and at events attended by the King or the Royal Family?","o":["Folk music","The Beatles","The National Anthem","Opera music"],"a":[2],"e":"The National Anthem of the UK is ‘God Save the King’ is played at important national occasions and at events attended by the King or the Royal Family."},{"q":"How many members does a jury have in Scotland?","o":["15","10","14","16"],"a":[0],"e":"In England, Wales and Northern Ireland a jury has 12 members, and in Scotland a jury has 15 members."},{"q":"How can you visit the Northern Ireland Assembly (choose TWO options)?","o":["Contacting the Education Service","Contacting an MSP","Contacting an MLA","Arranging a tour through the visitor services"],"a":[0,2],"e":"There are two ways to arrange a visit to the Northern Ireland Assembly at Stormont. You can either contact the Education Service (details are on the Northern Ireland Assembly website at http://www.niassembly.gov.uk) or contact an MLA."},{"q":"Most citizens of the UK, the Irish republic or the Commonwealth aged 18 or over can stand for public office, except for (choose THREE options):","o":["People found guilty of certain criminal offences","People without a university degree","Civil servants","Members of the Armed Forces"],"a":[0,2,3],"e":"Members of the Commonwealth aged 18 or over can stand for public office. There are some exceptions, including: members of the armed forces, civil servants and people found guilty of certain criminal offences."},{"q":"On which of the following issues the Northern Ireland Assembly CANNOT make decisions?","o":["Health","Education","The Environment","Planning"],"a":[3],"e":"The Northern Ireland Assembly can make decisions on issues such as education, agriculture, the environment, health and social services. It cannot make decisions on planning issues."},{"q":"In which city is the Scottish Parliament building, also known as Holyrood, located?","o":["Edinburgh","Belfast","Aberdeen","Glasgow"],"a":[0],"e":"In Scotland the elected members, called MSPs, meet in the Scottish Parliament building at Holyrood in Edinburgh."},{"q":"What name is given to the elected members of the Scottish Parliament?","o":["MSPs","AMs","MLAs","MPs"],"a":[0],"e":"Elected members of the Scottish Parliament are known as Members of the Scottish Parliament (MSPs)."},{"q":"The public can listen to debates in the Palace of Westminster from public galleries located in:","o":["The House of Commons only","The House of Lords only","Both, the House of Commons and House of Lords","All of the above are false"],"a":[2],"e":"The public can listen to debates in the Palace of Westminster from public galleries in both the House of Commons and the House of Lords."}]},{"id":"test-5-6","name":"Test 5.6","group":"Chapter 5","questions":[{"q":"Which court deals with the most serious cases of children aged 10 to 17 in England, Wales and Northern Ireland?","o":["The High Court","The Youth Court","The Magistrates’ Court","The Crown Court"],"a":[3],"e":"In England, Wales and Northern Ireland, if an accused person is aged 10 to 17, the case is normally heard in a Youth Court in front of up to three specially trained magistrates or a District Judge. The most serious cases will go the Crown Court."},{"q":"When was the National Trust founded?","o":["1890","1895","1980","1910"],"a":[1],"e":"The National Trust was founded in 1895 by three volunteers."},{"q":"Who is married to the King of the United Kingdom?","o":["Kate, the Princess of Wales","Camila, the Queen Consort","Camila, the Princess of Wales","Kate, the Queen Consort"],"a":[1],"e":"The King is married to Camilla, the Queen Consort."},{"q":"What is the name of the building where the Senedd members meet?","o":["Westminster","Stormont","Holyrood","Senedd"],"a":[3],"e":"In Wales the elected members, known as SMs, meet in the Senedd building in Cardiff Bay."},{"q":"When did the ‘Habeas Corpus Act’ become law?","o":["In 1679","In 1669","In 1689","In 1709"],"a":[0],"e":"The Habeas Corpus Act became law in 1679."},{"q":"When was the Treaty of Rome signed?","o":["25th May 1957","25th March 1957","28th May 1957","28th March 1957"],"a":[1],"e":"The European Union (EU), originally called the European Economic Community (EEC), was set up by six western European countries (Belgium, France, Germany, Italy, Luxembourg and the Netherlands) who signed the Treaty of Rome on the 25th March 1957."},{"q":"The King is the head of state in the UK:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"How often are the general elections held in the UK?","o":["Every year","Every 2 years","Every 4 years","Every 5 years"],"a":[3],"e":"MPs are elected at a General Election, which is held at least every five years."},{"q":"What is the name of the building where the Northern Ireland Assembly members meet?","o":["Westminster","Holyrood","Senedd","Stormont"],"a":[3],"e":"The Northern Ireland Assembly building is known as Stormont."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a function of the House of Lords?","o":["Suggest amendments to existing laws","Check laws that have been passed by the House of Commons","Propose new laws","Cancel existing laws"],"a":[3],"e":"The House of Lords can suggest amendments or propose new laws, which are then discussed by MPs. The House of Lords checks laws that have been passed by the House of Commons to ensure they are fit for purpose. It also holds the government to account to make sure that it is working in the best interests of the people."},{"q":"How is the Speaker elected?","o":["By other MPs in a secret ballot","By the members of the House of Lords by proportional representation","By the members of the House of Commons in a public ballot","By the Prime Minister"],"a":[0],"e":"The Speaker is chosen by other MPs in a secret ballot."},{"q":"When was the Northern Ireland Parliament established for the first time?","o":["In 1922","In 1932","In 1925","In 1928"],"a":[0],"e":"The Northern Ireland Parliament was first established in 1922."},{"q":"What name is given to the system used to deal with children and young people who have committed an offence in Scotland?","o":["The Children’s Hearings System","The Youth Court System","The Sheriff Court for Young Children","The Young’s System"],"a":[0],"e":"In Scotland a system called the Children’s Hearings System is used to deal with children and young people who have committed an offence."},{"q":"What is the function of the UN Security Council?","o":["Recommend action when there are international crises and threats to peace","Recommend humanitarian action when there are natural disasters","Protect and promote human rights","Promote international trade relationships"],"a":[0],"e":"The UN Security Council recommends action when there are international crises and threats to peace."},{"q":"Which of the following charities works for the prevention of cruelty to children?","o":["PDSA","RSPB","NSPCC","Crisis"],"a":[2],"e":"The NSPCC is the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children."},{"q":"What does the term ‘Hansard’ relate to?","o":["Official reports were proceedings in Parliament are published","A popular British soap opera","A festival which celebrates the founding of the Sikh community","A public holiday"],"a":[0],"e":"Proceedings in Parliament are broadcast on television and published in official reports called Hansard."},{"q":"In Wales, the jury has to listen to the evidence presented at the trial and then decide a verdict of ‘guilty’, ‘not guilty’ or ‘not proven’ based of what they have heard:","o":["True","﻿False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. The jury has to listen to the evidence presented at the trial and then decide a verdict of ‘guilty’ or ‘not guilty’ based of what they have heard. In Scotland (not Wales), a third verdict of ‘not proven’ is also possible."},{"q":"A National Insurance number does on its own prove to an employer that you have the right to work in the UK:","o":["﻿True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. A National Insurance number does not on its own prove to an employer that you have the right to work in the UK."},{"q":"When driving in the UK, where do you need to display the tax disc which shows that the annual road tax has been paid?","o":["Anywhere inside the car","The windowscreen of the car","The wheel of the car","The boot of the car"],"a":[1],"e":"When driving in the UK, you must pay an annual road tax and display the tax disc, which shows that the tax has been paid, on the windowscreen."},{"q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["A jury is made up of members of the public who have applied to be part of it","A jury is made up of members of the public chosen at random from the local electoral register"],"a":[1],"e":"A jury is made up of members of the public chosen at random from the local electoral register."},{"q":"It is a civil offence to cause harassment, alarm or distress to someone because of their religion or ethnic origin:","o":["True","﻿False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. It is a criminal offence to cause harassment, alarm or distress to someone because of their religion or ethnic origin."},{"q":"Where can you get contact details for all your representatives and their parties (choose TWO options)?","o":["At bookshops","Your local supermarket","Your local library","www.parliament.uk"],"a":[2,3],"e":"You can get contact details for all your representatives and their parties from your local library and from http://www.parliament.uk."},{"q":"What is the Old Bailey?","o":["A Famous criminal Court","A Scottish drink","The name of a traditional Welsh cake","A British national park"],"a":[0],"e":"The Old Bailey is probably the most famous criminal court in the world."},{"q":"What is the relationship between the monarch and the government?","o":["Approve the government’s decisions on government policies","Advise, warn and encourage the government’s decisions","Advise, encourage and approve the government’s decisions","Advise and approve the government’s decisions"],"a":[1],"e":"The monarch has regular meetings with the Prime Minister and can advise, warn and encourage, but the decisions on government policies are made by the Prime Minister and cabinet."}]},{"id":"test-5-7","name":"Test 5.7","group":"Chapter 5","questions":[{"q":"The UK is a ‘constitutional monarchy’:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"Where can people facing domestic violence get help from (choose TWO options)?","o":["The NHS","The Citizens Advise Bureau","The Human Rights Commission","A solicitor"],"a":[1,3],"e":"A solicitor or the Citizens Advice Bureau can explain the available options to anyone facing domestic violence."},{"q":"People might be taken to court if they owe money to someone:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"How many members does a jury have in England, Wales and Northern Ireland?","o":["12","14","15","18"],"a":[0],"e":"In England, Wales and Northern Ireland a jury has 12 members, and in Scotland a jury has 15 members."},{"q":"What TWO tests do you need to pass to get a driving licence in the UK?","o":["A medical test","A knowledge test","A practical test","A physical test"],"a":[1,2],"e":"To get a UK driving licence you must pass a driving test, which tests both your knowledge and your practical skills."},{"q":"What is the role of the Speaker?","o":["To keep order during political debates to make sure the rules are followed","To point out what they see as the government’s failures and weaknesses","To control important public appointments","To challenge the government and put forward alternative policies"],"a":[0],"e":"The Speaker keeps order during political debates to make sure the rules are followed."},{"q":"Which of the following statements is FALSE regarding Youth Courts?","o":["The parents or carers of the young person being judged are expected to attend the hearing","Members of the public are not allowed in them","They are used for trials of young people aged 10 to 18","The name or photographs of the accused young person cannot be published in newspapers or used by the media"],"a":[2],"e":"They are used for trials of young people aged 10 to 17."},{"q":"Who is responsible for the creation of the European Convention on Human Rights and Fundamental Freedoms?","o":["The UN Security Council","The Council of Europe","The United Nations","The Commonwealth"],"a":[1],"e":"The Council of Europe has no power to make laws but draws up conventions and charters, the most well-known of which is the European Convention on Human Rights and Fundamental Freedoms."},{"q":"Members of the House of Lords may stand for election to the House of Commons and are eligible for all public offices:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. Members of the House of Lords may not stand for election to the House of Commons but are eligible for all other public offices."},{"q":"What does the following term ‘PDSA’ stand for?","o":["People’s Defence for Sick Animals","People’s Dispensary for Sick Animals","People’s Dispensary for Single Animals","People’s Defence for Single Animals"],"a":[1],"e":"PDSA’ stands for People’s Dispensary for Sick Animals."},{"q":"The Commonwealth is based on the core values of democracy, good government and the rule of law:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"How long does it take to donate blood?","o":["About half an hour","About an hour","About two hours","About 15 minutes"],"a":[1],"e":"Giving blood only takes about an hour to do."},{"q":"Where do local authorities get funding from (choose TWO answers)?","o":["The King","The central government","Donations made by local residents","Local taxes"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Local authorities are funded by money from the central government and by local taxes."},{"q":"What does the term MEPs stand for?","o":["Members of the European Parliament","Members of the Parliament","Magistrates of the European Parliament","Members of the European parties"],"a":[0],"e":"MEPs stands for Members of the European Parliament."},{"q":"In which of the following matters can the Scottish Parliament legislate (choose FIVE options)?","o":["Additional tax-raising powers","Economic development","Health","Education","Civil and criminal law","Immigration","Planning"],"a":[0,2,3,4,6],"e":"The Scottish Parliament can legislate on: civil and criminal law, health, education, planning and additional tax-raising powers."},{"q":"Drinking in public can be a criminal offence and you can be fined or arrested for it:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This stamens is true."},{"q":"Whose details are NOT listed in the Phone Book?","o":["The King","MPs, SMs and MSPs","MEPs","None of the above"],"a":[0],"e":"MPs, SMs and MSPs are listed in The Phone Book. The King is not."},{"q":"What is the name of the official reports where the Parliament’s proceedings are published?","o":["Domesday Book","Hansard","Parliamentary reports","Carta Magna"],"a":[1],"e":"Proceedings in Parliament are broadcast on television and published in official reports called Hansard."},{"q":"How many members does the Senedd have?","o":["40","50","60","75"],"a":[2],"e":"The Senedd has 60 Senedd members (SMs) and elections are held every four years using a form of proportional representation."},{"q":"Who deals with cases involving personal injury, family matters, breaches of contract and divorce in Scotland?","o":["County Courts","The Sheriff Court","The Crown Court","The High Court"],"a":[1],"e":"Civil disputes such as people trying to get back money that is owed to them, cases involving personal injury, family matters, breaches of contract, and divorce are dealt with in the Sheriff Court in Scotland."},{"q":"What name is given to the members of the House of Lords?","o":["Cabinet","Cavaliers","Peers","Housesteads"],"a":[2],"e":"Members of the House of Lords are known as peers."},{"q":"Magistrates and Justices of the Peace (JPs) are members of:","o":["The House of Commons","The Cabinet","The local community","The Parliament"],"a":[2],"e":"Magistrates and Justices of the Peace (JPs) are members of the local community."},{"q":"How can you visit the Northern Ireland Assembly (choose TWO options)?","o":["Contacting the Education Service","Contacting an MSP","Contacting an MLA","Arranging a tour through the visitor services"],"a":[0,2],"e":"There are two ways to arrange a visit to the Northern Ireland Assembly at Stormont. You can either contact the Education Service (details are on the Northern Ireland Assembly website at http://www.niassembly.gov.uk) or contact an MLA."},{"q":"For what sort of claims is the small claims procedure in Northern Ireland used?","o":["Claims of less than £3,000","Claims of less than £4,000","Claims of less than £5,000","Claims of less than £6,000"],"a":[0],"e":"The small claims procedure is used for claims of less than £10,000 in England and Wales and less than £3,000 in Scotland and Northern Ireland."}]},{"id":"test-5-8","name":"Test 5.8","group":"Chapter 5","questions":[{"q":"Which TWO of the following charities work with homeless people?","o":["Friends of the Earth","Shelter","Crisis","Oxfam"],"a":[1,2],"e":"﻿harities working with homeless people include Shelter and Crisis."},{"q":"Members of the Senedd (SMs) are elected every four years on the basis of:","o":["Proportional representation","Personal achievements","Instant run-off","First past the post system (the candidate who gets the most votes)"],"a":[0],"e":"The Senedd has 60 Senedd members (SMs) and elections are held every four years using a form of proportional representation."},{"q":"What is the Commonwealth?","o":["An association of countries that support each other and work together towards shared goals in democracy and development","An organization responsible for the protection and promotion of human rights in its member countries","An association of countries that promotes international peace and security","A group of European and North American countries that have agreed to help each other if they come under attack"],"a":[0],"e":"The Commonwealth is an association of countries that support each other and work together towards shared goals in democracy and development."},{"q":"When was the voting age reduced to 18 for men and women?","o":["1965","1969","1919","1929"],"a":[1],"e":"In 1969, the voting age was reduced to 18 for men and women."},{"q":"When was the Northern Ireland Parliament established for the first time?","o":["In 1922","In 1972","In 1998","In 1969"],"a":[0],"e":"The Northern Ireland Parliament established for the first time in 1922."},{"q":"What is the opposition?","o":["The second largest party in the House of Lords","The third largest party in the House of Commons","The second largest party in the House of Commons","The third largest party in the House of Lords"],"a":[2],"e":"The second largest party in the House of Commons is called the opposition."},{"q":"Which THREE of the following groups form part of the British government?","o":["The police","The cabinet","The local organizations","The judiciary"],"a":[0,1,3],"e":"In the UK, there are several different parts of government. The main ones are: the monarchy, the Parliament (the House of Commons and the House of Lords), the Prime Minister, the cabinet, the judiciary (courts), the police, the civil service and the local government."},{"q":"Who chairs the debates in the House of Commons?","o":["The shadow cabinet","The Speaker","The Prime Minister","The leader of the opposition"],"a":[1],"e":"Debates in the House of Commons are chaired by the Speaker."},{"q":"A woman who is violent towards her partner cannot be prosecuted in the UK:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. Anyone who is violent towards their partner – whether they are a man or a woman, married or living together – can be prosecuted."},{"q":"Many MPs, SMs and MSPs hold regular local events where constituents can go in person to talk about issues that are of concern to them. These are known as:","o":["Hansard","Local ‘surgeries’","Local ‘meetings’","Constituents ‘reunions’"],"a":[1],"e":"Many MPs, SMs and MSPs hold regular local ‘surgeries’, where constituents can go in person to talk about issues that are of concern to them."},{"q":"Which Act introduced the Forced Marriage Protection Orders for England, Wales and Northern Ireland in 2008?","o":["The Forced Marriage (Civil Protection) Act 2007","The Free Marriage (Civil Protection) Act 2007","The Forced Marriage (Civil Protection) Act 2011","The Free Marriage (Civil Protection) Act 2011"],"a":[0],"e":"Forced Marriage Protection Orders were introduced in 2008 for England, Wales and Northern Ireland under the Forced Marriage (Civil Protection) Act 2007. Similar Protection Orders were introduced in Scotland in November 2011."},{"q":"When is the electoral register updated?","o":["April or May","May or June","September or October","October or November"],"a":[2],"e":"The electoral register is updated every year in September or October."},{"q":"Where does the Scottish Parliament sit?","o":["In Glasgow","In St Andrews","In Edinburgh","In Aberdeen"],"a":[2],"e":"In Scotland the elected members, called MSPs, meet in the Scottish Parliament building at Holyrood in Edinburgh."},{"q":"Which of the following is a ceremonial role to be undertaken by the King?","o":["Opening the new parliamentary session every year","Visiting the major cities in the UK once every six months","Choosing the Prime Minister","Giving a speech on New Year’s Eve"],"a":[0],"e":"The King has important ceremonial roles, such as the opening of the new parliamentary session each year. On this occasion the King makes a speech which summarises the government’s policies for the year ahead."},{"q":"How long can you use your driving licence in the UK if you have a licence from another country?","o":["For up to 12 months","You need a UK driving licence","For as long as it is valid","For up to 2 years"],"a":[0],"e":"If you have a licence from another country, you may use it in the UK for up to 12 months."},{"q":"Which Court deals with the most serious civil cases in England, Wales and Northern Ireland?","o":["The County Court","The Crown Court","The High Court","The Sheriff Court"],"a":[2],"e":"More serious civil cases – for example, when a large amount of compensation is being claimed – are dealt with in the High Court of England, Wales and Northern Ireland."},{"q":"If you are arrested and taken to a police station, a police officer should:","o":["Make a copy of your ID and retain you until your lawyer arrives","Tell you the reason for your arrest and that you will be able to seek legal advice","Offer you a cup of tea","Explain the law to you and wait for your family to arrive"],"a":[1],"e":"If you are arrested and taken to a police station, a police officer will tell you the reason for your arrest and you will be able to seek legal advice."},{"q":"The UK is one of the five permanent members of the Security Council:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"Which of the following four changes did the Chartists NOT campaign for?","o":["For any man to be able to stand as MP","Secret ballots","Elections every six years","For MPs to be paid"],"a":[2],"e":"The Chartists campaigned for every man to have the vote, elections every year, for all regions to be equal in the electoral system, secret ballots, for any man to be able to stand as an MP and for MPs to be paid."},{"q":"Which of the following is a criminal offence?","o":["Selling tobacco to someone under the age of 18","Owing money to someone","Discrimination in the workplace","﻿Selling faulty goods or services"],"a":[0],"e":"It is illegal and a criminal offence to sell tobacco products (for example, cigarettes, cigars, roll-up tobacco) to anyone under the age of 18."},{"q":"Which court deals with serious criminal offences in Northern Ireland?","o":["The Sheriff Court","The Crown Court","The Magistrates’ Court","The High Court"],"a":[1],"e":"In England, Wales and Northern Ireland, serious criminal offences are tried in front of a judge and a jury in a Crown Court."},{"q":"Which political party does the Speaker represent?","o":["The Liberal Democrats","The Conservative party","The Labour Party","The Speaker does not represent any political party"],"a":[3],"e":"The Speaker is neutral and does not represent a political party, even though he or she is an MP, represents a constituency and deals with the constituents’ problems like any other MP."},{"q":"When was the Scottish Parliament formed?","o":["In 2000","In 2002","In 1999","In 1998"],"a":[2],"e":"The Scottish Parliament was formed in 1999."},{"q":"Who is the heir to the throne of the UK?","o":["Prince William","Prince Harry","Prince James","Prince Philip"],"a":[0],"e":"The King’s eldest son, Prince William (the Prince of Wales), is the heir to the throne."}]},{"id":"test-5-9","name":"Test 5.9","group":"Chapter 5","questions":[{"q":"Membership to the Commonwealth is compulsory for its members:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. Membership to the Commonwealth is voluntary."},{"q":"How many verdicts are possible in trials in Scotland?","o":["Two: ‘guilty’ or ‘not guilty’","Three: ‘guilty’, ‘not guilty’ or ‘not proven’","Three: ‘guilty’, ‘not guilty’ or ‘on hold’","Two: ‘guilty’ or ‘not proven’"],"a":[1],"e":"The jury has to listen to the evidence presented at the trial and then decide a verdict of ‘guilty’ or ‘not guilty’ based of what they have heard. In Scotland, a third verdict of ‘not proven’ is also possible."},{"q":"Which of the following charities works with sick animals?","o":["PDSA","NSPCC","Crisis","Age UK"],"a":[0],"e":"The PDSA is the People’s Dispensary for Sick Animals."},{"q":"Which of the following is the responsibility of the Home Secretary?","o":["Crime, policing and immigration","Managing relationships with foreign countries","Education and health","National and international defence"],"a":[0],"e":"The Home Secretary is responsible for crime, policing and immigration."},{"q":"What is the minimum age required for a citizen to stand for public office?","o":["16","18","21","25"],"a":[1],"e":"Most citizens of the UK, the Irish republic or the Commonwealth aged 18 or over can stand for public office."},{"q":"Who makes sure that the opposition has a guaranteed amount of time to debate issues which it chooses during political debates?","o":["The shadow cabinet","The Speaker","The King","Nobody"],"a":[1],"e":"The Speaker keeps order during political debates to make sure the rules are followed. This includes making sure the opposition has a guaranteed amount of time to debate issues which it chooses."},{"q":"Citizens of Liechtenstein can drive in the UK for as long as their driving license is valid:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"True. If your driving licence if from a country in the European Union (EU), Iceland, Liechtenstein or Norway, you can drive in the UK for as long as your licence is valid."},{"q":"What is the money raised from the Income Tax used for (choose THREE options)?","o":["Education","The National Grid system","Police and the Armed Forces","Roads"],"a":[0,2,3],"e":"Money raised from income tax pays for government services such as roads, education, police and the armed forces."},{"q":"When were the Forced Marriage Protection Orders introduced for England, Wales and Northern Ireland, which aimed at protecting a person from being forced into a marriage, or to protect a person in a forced marriage?","o":["2007","2008","2010","2011"],"a":[1],"e":"Forced Marriage Protection Orders were introduced in 2008 for England, Wales and Northern Ireland under the Forced Marriage (Civil Protection) Act 2007."},{"q":"In the UK, if the jury finds a defendant guilty, the judge decides the penalty:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"Which of the following countries is a member of the Commonwealth?","o":["Greece","Austria","Mexico","Bangladesh"],"a":[3],"e":"Bangladesh is a member of the Commonwealth."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a principle included in the European Convention of Human Rights?","o":["Prohibition of torture","Prohibition of immoral thoughts","Freedom of expression (speech)","Prohibition of slavery and forced labour"],"a":[1],"e":"Prohibition of immoral thoughts is not one of the principles included in the European Convention of Human Rights."},{"q":"A system of government where the whole adult population gets a say is known as:","o":["Monarchy","Republic","Communism","Democracy"],"a":[3],"e":"Democracy is a system of government where the whole adult population gets a say. This might be by direct voting or by choosing representatives to make decisions on their behalf."},{"q":"Which of the following statements is correct?","o":["Arranged marriages refer to marriages where both parties agree to the marriage","Arranged marriages refer to marriages where one or both parties do not give their consent to enter into the partnership"],"a":[0],"e":"Arranged marriages refer to marriages where both parties agree to the marriage."},{"q":"The devolved governments in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland have the power to legislate on any issues that affect them:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. Devolved governments in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland have the power to legislate on certain issues."},{"q":"What name is given to the members of the House of Commons?","o":["MPs","AMs","Peers","MEPs"],"a":[0],"e":"All of the elected MPs form the House of Commons."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a role of the King:","o":["To inaugurate important business in the UK","To receive foreign ambassadors and high commissioners","To entertain visiting heads of state","To make state visits overseas"],"a":[0],"e":"The King receives foreign ambassadors and high commissioners, entertains visiting heads of state, and makes state visits overseas in support of diplomatic and relationships with other counties."},{"q":"The Speaker is a neutral MP and does not represent a political party:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"True. The Speaker is neutral and does not represent a political party, even though he or she is an MP, represents a constituency and deals with the constituents’ problems like any other MP."},{"q":"How can a person facing domestic violence find a safe place to stay (choose TWO options)?","o":["Going to a hotel away from their home","Contacting the National Domestic Violence Freephone Helpline","Contacting the police","Contacting the hospital"],"a":[1,2],"e":"If you are facing domestic violence you can get help from the 24-hour National Domestic Violence Freephone Helpline on 0808 2000 247 at any time, or the police can help you find a safe place to stay."},{"q":"Who has control over policy and laws governing defence, foreign affairs, immigration, taxation and social security in Northern Ireland?","o":["The Irish government","The central UK government","The Northern Ireland Assembly","The monarch"],"a":[1],"e":"Policy and laws governing defence, foreign affairs, immigration, taxation and social security all remain under central UK government control. However, many other public services, such as education, are controlled by the devolved administrations."},{"q":"The devolved governments in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland cannot control policies and laws governing taxation and social security:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"True. Policy and laws governing defence, foreign affairs, immigration, taxation and social security all remain under central UK government control. However, many other public services, such as education, are controlled by the devolved administrations."},{"q":"In the UK, brutality and violence in the home is not a crime:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. In the UK, brutality and violence in the home is a serious crime."},{"q":"UK laws ensure that people are not treated unfairly in any area of life or work because of their age, disability, sex, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion or belief, sexuality or marital status:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."},{"q":"Since 1997, some powers have been devolved from the central government to give people in Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland more control over matters that directly affect them:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This statement is true."}]},{"id":"test-5-10","name":"Test 5.10","group":"Chapter 5","questions":[{"q":"How can you visit the Senedd?","o":["Contacting the Education Service","Contacting the Senedd Booking Service","Contacting an MP","Contacting an MLA"],"a":[1],"e":"To make a booking to visit the Senedd, you have to contact the Senedd Booking Service."},{"q":"In which of the following matters the Scottish Parliament CANNOT legislate?","o":["Health","Education","Immigration","Planning"],"a":[2],"e":"The matters on which the Scottish Parliament can legislate include: civil and criminal law, health, education, planning and additional tax-raising powers."},{"q":"Does Britain have a constitutional monarchy?","o":["Yes","No"],"a":[0],"e":"True. The UK has a constitutional monarchy."},{"q":"The devolved administrations of Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland each have their own civil service:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"True. The devolved administrations each have their own civil service."},{"q":"Carrying a weapon is a criminal offence, unless it is for self-defence:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. It is a criminal offence to carry a weapon of any kind, even if it is for self-defence. This includes a gun, a knife or anything that is made or adapted to cause injury."},{"q":"Prohibition of thought, conscience and religion is one of the principles included in the European Convention of Human Rights:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. Freedom of thought, conscience and religion is one of the principles included in the European Convention of Human Rights"},{"q":"Will a non-UK national living in the UK and looking for work, starting work or setting up as self-employed need a National Insurance number?","o":["Yes","No"],"a":[0],"e":"True. A non-UK national living in the UK and looking for work, starting work or setting up as self-employed will need a National Insurance number. However, you can start work without one."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a responsibility of the MPs?","o":["Scrutinise and comment on what the government is doing","Represent everyone in the constituency","Protect life and property","Debate important national issues"],"a":[2],"e":"MPs have a number of different responsibilities. They represent everyone in their constituency, help to create new laws, scrutinize and comment on what the government is doing and debate important national issues."},{"q":"The clock tower is named ‘Elizabeth Tower’ in honour of:","o":["Queen Elizabeth II’s Diamond Jubilee in 2012","Queen Elizabeth I’s Diamond Jubilee in 2012","Queen Elizabeth II’s Golden Jubilee in 2002","Queen Elizabeth I’s Golden Jubilee in 2002"],"a":[0],"e":"The clock tower is named ‘Elizabeth Tower’ in honour of Queen Elizabeth II’s Diamond Jubilee in 2012."},{"q":"The right to a fair trial is not included amongst the principles of the European Convention of Human Rights:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. The right to a fair trial is one of the principles of the European Convention of Human Rights."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT classified as a criminal offence in the UK?","o":["Selling alcohol to anyone under the age of 18","Selling or buying drugs","Carrying a knife","Unfair dismissal or discrimination in the workplace"],"a":[3],"e":"Cases of unfair dismissal or discrimination in the workplace are considered civil offences."},{"q":"The right to life and the right to liberty and security are some of the principles included in the European Convention of Human Rights:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"This is true."},{"q":"When are local elections for councillors held in most local authorities?","o":["In May","In March","In February","In June"],"a":[0],"e":"For most local authorities, local elections for councillors are held in May every year."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT protected by the laws of discrimination?","o":["Traditional customs","Disability","Pregnancy and Maternity","Religion or Belief"],"a":[0],"e":"UK laws ensure that people are not treated unfairly in any area of life or work because of their age, disability, sex, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion or belief, sexuality or marital status."},{"q":"Which of the following countries is NOT a member of the Commonwealth?","o":["Ghana","Cyprus","Greece","Sierra Leone"],"a":[2],"e":"Greece is not a member of the Commonwealth."},{"q":"In England, Wales and Scotland, most minor criminal cases are dealt with in a Magistrates’ Court:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. In England, Wales and Northern Ireland, most minor criminal cases are dealt with in a Magistrates’ Court. In Scotland, minor criminal offences go to a Justice of the Peace Court."},{"q":"When did the UK sign the European Convention of Human Rights?","o":["1940","1945","1950","1955"],"a":[2],"e":"The UK was one of the first countries to sign the Convention in 1950."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a devolved administration?","o":["The Scottish Parliament","The Senedd","The Irish Parliament","The Northern Ireland Assembly"],"a":[2],"e":"There are three devolved administrations in the UK, this include: the Senedd, the Scottish Parliament and the Northern Ireland Assembly."},{"q":"What was the name of the European Union when it was first established?","o":["European Political Union","Union of European Countries","European Economic Community","Europe United"],"a":[2],"e":"The European Union (EU), originally called the European Economic Community (EEC), was set up by six western European countries (Belgium, France, Germany, Italy, Luxembourg and the Netherlands) who signed the Treaty of Rome on 25 March 1957."},{"q":"Which Act incorporated the European Convention of Human Rights into UK law?","o":["The Discrimination Act 1995","The Equality Act 2010","The Freedom Act 2012","The Human Rights Act 1998"],"a":[3],"e":"The Human Rights Act 1998 incorporated the European Convention of Human Rights into UK law."},{"q":"Arranged marriages are not acceptable in the UK:","o":["True","False"],"a":[1],"e":"False. Arranged marriages, where both parties agree to the marriage, are acceptable in the UK."},{"q":"Who represents the UK to the rest of the world?","o":["The Prime Minister","Prince Harry","Prince William","The King"],"a":[3],"e":"The King represents the UK to the rest of the world."},{"q":"Who is responsible for managing relationships with foreign countries?","o":["The Chancellor of the Exchequer","The Foreign Secretary","The Home Secretary","The King"],"a":[1],"e":"The Foreign Secretary is responsible for managing relationships with foreign countries."},{"q":"If a husband forces his wife to have sex, he can be charged with rape:","o":["True","False"],"a":[0],"e":"True. Any man who forces a woman to have sex, including a woman’s husband, can be charged with rape."}]}];
